    20	
    21	Cael said nothing. He had learned on a hill to let a carter come to the end of his own load.
    22	
    23	"He's a lad and two mules. It's sixteen days of road, and I'd as soon he had somebody walking in front of him for the first six." The keeper tipped his stick at the gate-house wall. "Your boxes'd ride. There's room on the bed going out; it's coming home that she's full."
    24	
    25	"What's the hire?"
    26	
    27	"There's none. I've no paper for it and I'm not sending to a plank for some." He scratched his jaw with the stick's end. "You walked my hill for seventy and brought me a no. I'd have paid the seventy three times over in carts, by next thaw, if you'd brought me a yes to please me. I can find a bed for three boxes."
    28	
    29	So there it was. Cael went through it once, from habit, for the place where it cost something, and he could not find one. A column on a pasted leaf had ended in the wrong word, and the five of them had written it anyway, and for that a man was offering him wheels for six days. He had been paid for a great many things in his life, and most of them had been somebody's convenience. He did not think that he had ever before been done a kindness on the strength of a no.
    30	
    31	"I'll ask them," he said.
    32	
    33	"Ask them. He goes at first light whether or not."
