   110	
   111	By noon of the second day he hated the tape, as he had been told that he would.
   112	
   113	He could have borne the pain of it; he had carried pain before, on worse terms, and gone on. What he could not bear was that the thing was always right. He would reach for the water-skin, or turn his head to see what Lira had laughed at, or simply draw the breath that a hill wanted; and five bands of linen would close across his side a hair before the bone did, and tell him *there*. Every time, when he stopped to check it, the tape had known and he had not. A person can forgive an instrument for being wrong. He was learning that it was a good deal harder to forgive one for being right four thousand times a day.
   114	
   115	There was no arguing with it, and he could not bear a thing that he could neither argue with nor measure, so he measured it.
   116	
   117	A breath went in to a count of three before the tape spoke. On a level road he could take that breath at every fourth stride and never touch the edge. On a rise it wanted every third, and on the steepest of the valley's little hills every second, and there the breaths came so shallow and so quick that his head swam. He had always walked in fits. He had gone quick and then slow since he was a child on the lanes of Denvash, running to look at a thing and standing still to count it. He had watched a healer come down a mountain for a day and a half at one unbroken beat, and he had not been able to copy her for a hundred yards.
