     1	# Chapter 28 — Sixty-Two Cords
     2	
     3	That same night, in the second watch, Karis went back to the stone.
     4	
     5	She did not go alone, since Seln had made a rule and she had lost no time in seeing the sense of it. Lira had the watch with her. Lira sat down in the grass ten yards short of the place, with her back turned and her knife across her knees, to look at everything that was not a ring of stones; and Karis went on the last ten yards by herself, with a notebook, and a pencil, and no lamp.
     6	
     7	There could be no lamp. She had lit one in a yard two evenings ago and been absurdly glad of it, and she would not have set a point here for all the paper in Ternhall. She knelt down in the frozen grass on the western side of the stone and laid the notebook open on her knee. The stars were out. She could see the white of the page and not much else, and she resolved to write large, one line to a leaf, and sort it by daylight.
     8	
     9	First she called her sigil.
    10	
    11	She had been taught to do it at fourteen, by attending to a place behind the breastbone, and it had been years since she had needed to do it on purpose; it was simply there when she wanted it, as a clerk is there behind a counter. It came now. It hung at the edge of her sight where it had always hung, small and steady and yellow-white, and it had about it the particular quality of attention that she had never been able to describe to anybody who did not have one.
    12	
    13	She looked at it for a good while. Then she stood up, and stepped over the stone.
    14	
    15	It went out.
    16	
    17	There was no flicker in it, and no fading. She had half expected a lamp turned down, and it was a lamp that had never been in the room. She stood a stride inside the line with her heart going hard, and looked for the place behind her breastbone, and found the place. There was nothing in it.
    18	
    19	She stepped back, and it was there. It hung where it always hung, with the same steadiness, as though it had been waiting behind a door and had not noticed that the door was shut.
    20	
    21	Karis did it nine times.
    22	
    23	She counted them, and at each crossing she made herself attend to one thing only, as she would have made a student attend. The third time she watched for the instant, and it had no width that she could find. The fifth time she went over slowly, a hand's breadth at a time, to learn whether it dimmed; it did not, and then it was gone.
    24	
    25	The seventh time she tried to hold it. She fixed the whole of her will on that small bright mark and carried it forward as one carries a full cup, and it went out between one heartbeat and the next without the least sense that anything had been taken from her. That was the part she liked least. She had not been robbed. She had merely looked down and found her hands empty.
    26	
    27	After the ninth she knelt again, on the near side, and thought about emptiness, since she had lately become something of an authority.
    28	
    29	She had been empty for nine days. She had spent every point she owned on a mountain in eight seconds, and afterward she had not been able to strike a flint. But in all those nine days the sigil had been there. She had called it on the first night under the pale rock, and it had come, faithful as ever, and when she asked it for a point it had simply had nothing to give her. That had been a counter with a clerk behind it and an empty till.
    30	
    31	This was a different thing altogether. A stride beyond that stone she was three parts full; a healer had told her so with two fingers on her wrist. The till was heavy. There was no clerk, and no counter, and no room for them to stand in.
    32	
    33	*Empty: sigil present, reserve gone.* She wrote it large, by feel. *Here: reserve present, sigil gone.* She turned the leaf. *These are not the same condition and must not be entered under one word.*
    34	
    35	Then she set about the stride.
    36	
    37	It was the only measurement that she could take tonight, and she took it with the stubbornness of a person who has been given one tool. From the turn of the ruts to the stone it was eighty of Cael's strides, by his count, and his were a yard. She paced it herself, there and back, and her own count was a good deal larger, as it always was. She wrote both. She paced it a second time and was one stride out, and wrote that too, and the mean of the pair.
    38	
    39	She tried next to make the line finer than a stride, and could not.
    40	
    41	A hand held out across the stone did nothing at all; the sigil hung where it was. She had watched the healer stand so at dusk, for a long while, and had not asked her what she felt. An arm did nothing. But when she stood astride the stone itself, with one boot on either side, she had the sigil or had it not according to how she leaned, and Karis would not write down a lean. She put: *Narrower than one stride of mine. No finer figure tonight. Wants daylight, and a second person to watch my feet.*
    42	
    43	She sat back on her heels. The grass was stiff with frost, and beyond the stone the ground went down and up again to eleven shapes that she could only just tell from the sky.
    44	
    45	It was very tidy, and she made herself look at that, because it was what she was afraid of.
    46	
    47	Five people had walked up to this place in a row, an arm's length apart. She was an Ember, and Lira a Wind, and Brom an Iron Skin; there was a Shadow among them of a higher tier than any of the rest, and a Tide who had been trained in a healing house. Karis had sat at lectures on what an Arbiter was. She had copied out, in a restricted room, a good many pages on the subject, and on one point every page agreed: it was a person's own. It woke for that one person, on one morning, and named a single gift.
    48	
    49	And five of them had gone out on the same stride.
