   255	The Iron read was the quietest thing he owned. He had refused it on a fan and a lip and a bench, when it would have told him what he was being paid to learn, and each time he had refused because a boy's word could be checked by nobody.
   256	
   257	This was a different matter from a map. Six people were about to walk into a place where every one of them believed that all six were alike, and he was the only one who could find out whether that was so.
   258	
   259	He opened it.
   260	
   261	It came as it had always come, at once and without effort, like an eye that has been kept shut. For a pace and a half on every side he had the ground: the weight of the turf, and the frost standing in it, and a flat stone a foot down that he would never see. He had his own two boots, and the lean of his body over them. It was mass, and the way that mass meant to go, and nothing else.
   262	
   263	He was a pace short of the line. He walked.
   264	
   265	He had been certain, in the part of himself that kept accounts, of what would happen: a thing taken out of a Path would stop where Paths stopped. He had supposed it since a plank, as the others supposed it, and for the same reason, which was that no other sum came out even; and he had braced for the read to go from him as a count had gone from a healer's lips.
   266	
   267	It ran across the stride without a break.
   268	
   269	The turf under his left boot was outside the line, and the turf under his right was within it, and he had the weight of both as he had the weight of his own hands. Nothing dimmed. He took another step, and a third, and the ground went on telling him what it was.
   270	
   271	He stood still, three strides inside, with his heart going.
   272	
   273	Then he did the second thing. He had not been sure, a minute ago, that he would.
   274	
   275	He walked in, as a man goes who means to look at the ground that he will presently lead people over, without haste, twenty strides toward the ring. A grey boulder lay there in the grass, as high as his hip, and he walked straight at it. He kept his face to the east; if anyone at the stone had glanced his way, there was only a person with a notebook going to see what lay beyond a rock.
   276	
   277	At the twentieth stride he let Wind have his feet.
   278	
   279	It was a long way short of a burst. It was the smallest piece of what Lira's fighting had taught him, the lean that takes a body off a line a hair before the blow arrives; and he asked it for a foot. His weight went over without his deciding it. The boulder, which had been in front of him, was on his right hand, and he was past it, and the grass had hardly stirred.
   280	
   281	Anybody watching from behind would have seen a person walk round a rock, and no more.
   282	
   283	He stood on the far side with the tape drawn tight across his ribs and something going out of him into the cold air, small, as a breath goes out. He listened, and there was the wind, and the hiss of seed-heads, and far off toward the east a bird that got up and settled again. Nothing whatever came through the grass.
   284	
   285	He shut them both. He put Wind down, and closed the read as one closes a book, and they went back to where the other seven lay.
   286	
   287	He did not know why. He set that beside a healer's word from a hollow of shale, and added nothing to it, as she had added nothing. Five people had lost what they had at that stone, each in a different way, and one of them had asked with her whole body and been given a step. And twenty strides inside it, the things that he carried had answered him as they answered him anywhere.
   288	
   289	He walked back along the line, and stepped out over the first turf, and came down to the stone with the leaf in his hand.
   290	
   291	Karis took it from him, and read down the five rows, nodding at each. Then she laid it on her knee, and took the pencil, and ruled a sixth.
   292	
   293	"And yours?"
   294	
   295	She said it to the leaf. It was a clerk's question; she had a table with a row empty.
   296	
   297	He had perhaps a breath. He saw Lira, stamping her foot down into a boot the size of a bucket, with her face still stripped from that one step, and he saw a healer flexing her fingers; and he saw that the healer was the plainest reason of all, since she knew nothing of what he was.
   298	
   299	To tell it at that stone was to tell it to her, and he could not have said that the things he carried had answered him without saying what they were. There were nine, and every one of them had come out of a person.
   300	
   301	One had come out of the mild man who stood six feet off with his hands behind him, looking east. It was the very thing that Seln had reached for at this stone, not half an hour ago, and not found; and Cael had held it shut at every dusk for ninety-six days, so that nobody who might one day speak of it to a carter bound for a ferry should know where a boy had learned it. A healer spoke to every carter on seven roads. She would not have meant the least harm by it.
   302	
   303	That was not his alone to give away. It was a real reason, and he distrusted it for coming so readily to hand.
   304	
   305	"It was dark before," said Cael. "It's dark now."
   306	
   307	Both halves were true. He had looked at that place going over and coming back, and it had been the same dark. Karis wrote. He watched her pencil put the two halves down, one after the other, and he knew what she would take them to mean, and what the other four would take them to mean when they read her leaf; and that the whole was not the truth, though there was not a false word in it.
   308	
   309	It was the first thing of that kind that he had kept from any of them in a long while, and he had done it at a stone, at noon, with a pencil going.
   310	
   311	"The sun's over," said Lira. She was looking up, with her hand shading her eyes. "If we're going, it's now."

