     1	# Chapter 30 — The Far Lip
     2	
     3	Six of them went over the stone a little after noon, and the mule stayed where she was.
     4	
     5	Oryn had hobbled her on the western side, with a full net and the two panniers still roped on, and the long shape in its oiled cover lashed along the top of the pack as it had been since a mountain. The healer looked at that cover for a breath before she turned away. Then she walked across the line with her hands open at her sides, and Cael saw her fingers close once, hard, as the count went out of them.
     6	
     7	They took very little. Brom had the middle box on his back, with the chips in it and the paper, since whatever a surveyor had left would want carrying home in something. Karis had the long cord and the short one. There was the hedging bill, and Lira's knife, and Seln's, and a skin of water.
     8	
     9	Seln had the locked case as well, shut, on its strap across his chest. He had looked once at the mule's pack, and once at the stone, and had kept it on him, as he had kept it on every road. Everything else that they owned was either in a loft two days west or under the feet of a mule.
    10	
    11	"East, along the old line of the road," said Karis. "I want the cord laid from this stone to the middle, a length at a time, as we laid it round the edge. If I have my sum right, the tenth will end among those stones. If I have it wrong, I should like to be told so by the ground and not by a table."
    12	
    13	So they went in as they had gone round. Brom held one end at the stone, and Karis walked the other out until it came tight, and stopped; then he came up to her, and she went on. The other four walked behind, in file, in the track that the cord left. The grass stood to Cael's hip. It closed over each of them as they passed and did not quite rise again, and when he looked back from the third length there was a lane trodden through it, as straight as a ruled margin, with a grey stone and a small brown mule at the far end.
    14	
    15	He had his nine fragments as he always had them, and he held them. Wind leaned, since Lira was in front of him. Anchor had fallen quiet at the stake and had not asked since. He had opened nothing after a boulder, and he did not open anything now; but he walked those two hundred yards as the only one of the six who knew that he could have, and it was a strange thing to carry at the back of a file.
    16	
    17	At the eighth length the ground began to rise, gently, and the grass grew shorter. At the ninth it was at his knee, and he could see the whole of what stood on the swell in front of them.
    18	
    19	It was a floor, and it was round.
    20	
    21	It lay on the crown of the rise like the lid of a great jar, grey and pale and quite bare, and the short turf came up to its edge and stopped there as cleanly as a mown field stops at a road. Round its rim, a stride in from the edge, stood the eleven stones. He had counted them from three hundred yards and had the number right.
    22	
    23	They were grey, like the floor, and squared, each as thick through as a man's body. Not one of them was whole. The tallest stood twice Brom's height before it ended, and its neighbour came no higher than a knee, and between those two there was every height that a broken thing can have.
    24	
    25	"Tenth," said Karis.
    26	
    27	She had gone up onto the floor with the cord's end in her hand. It came tight while she was still walking, and she stopped where it stopped her. She was standing at the middle of the round, as near as his eye could tell, with the eleven stones in a ring about her at an equal distance.
    28	
    29	She did not say anything about her sum. She laid the end of the cord down very carefully on the grey stone, and set her notebook on it for a weight, and then she paced from where she stood to the rim, in four directions, counting. The four counts came out within a stride of each other.
    30	
