editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:1:# Chapter 18 — The Eighth
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:3:The first narrowing came at the thirty-third hundred, and Karis gave it a number of its own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:5:"One," she said, into his ear. "Of his eight. I shall keep them in a separate column, since they're what a carter will ask about first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:7:The hill had put down a foot there. A buttress of the grey slab came out of the face and ran right across the bench, and where it reached the edge it had left a shelf eleven feet broad where there had been twenty-five. It was room and to spare for a cart that the smith had cut to four feet between the wheels and five from hub to hub. Even so she looked different on it, with the wall at Brom's left elbow and nothing at all beyond the off wheel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:It was the seventy-sixth day, and they were making up for the one that Karis had spent on a rock. The second narrowing was nine feet, and the third nine and a half. The fourth was seven, on a pitch of two full hands to the yard, and there Lira walked backward in front of the off wheel for the whole length of it, laying a flat stone under the rim at every turn so that it should have something to bite.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:17:Overhead the rasping went on. The line had been above the second overhang at first light, and by noon it lay over the fourth narrowing, and they had not got ahead of it by a yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:21:"They're going where the moss hasn't been eaten," said Karis, who had heard, "and so, as it happens, are we. I shan't write *following*. I've nothing under it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:25:*Seventy-sixth day. Forty-seven lines. Every one says yes, and most of them on a condition. The herd is over us still.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:27:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:31:It came at the fifty-fourth hundred. The buttress there was not slab; it was a rib of darker stone, and it came out so far that the bench seemed to stop against it. Lira had gone round the corner at a walk and stood a long time out of sight. When she came back she held her two hands apart in front of her, a little wider than her shoulders, and then she shrugged.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:35:"Five feet nine," she said, when she had wriggled back. "At the narrowest, for four yards. The cart is five. That is a hand to spare beyond either hub, if she goes through exactly in the middle." She brushed grit from her chin. "And the outer edge is not a good edge. It's sound for the first foot. After that I think it's a crust."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:37:He went to look, since the line would be in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:39:The shelf under his boots was whole. But at the very rim, where the dark rib had weathered, the stone had come away underneath in a long hollow, and what was left on top was a lip no thicker than a plank, with daylight below it. A wheel that strayed nine inches to the right would be standing on that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:41:Something in him had found the lip before his eyes did. Anchor had asked at every sill and coping since a river, and at every one of seven narrowings, and it had never asked as it asked now. *There?* It showed him the place, under the crust, where a fixed point would sit: one point, set against the sound rock and told to stay, and that plank of stone would have carried a loaded wagon. It would have cost him nothing that he could not have paid by supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:43:He looked up the slope at thirty grey heaps, and thought of a page in his own book that had her three clauses on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:45:*No.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:47:It was the plainest refusal he had made on the mountain. The reason was above him, eating moss. But he noticed that he would have refused it without them. A point of his under that lip would have made the seventh narrowing safe for one cart on one morning, and then he would have had to write *yes* in a column that a ford-keeper was paying to believe; and the next wheel over it would have found only the crust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:49:"I want to measure it again," he said. "Myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:51:Karis looked at him. Then she held out the cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:53:"That's what the second hand is for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:55:He lay where she had lain, with the edge of the mountain under his breastbone and a long blue emptiness in front of his face, and he drew the cord tight from the foot of the rib to the last sound inch of the rim. He found the knot. It was five feet nine.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:57:He had expected nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:59:They took her through an inch at a time. Seln knelt at the off wheel with his cheek nearly on the rock, watching the rim against a scratch that Karis had made, and lifted one finger for every hand's breadth of travel. Cael had the shafts alone, so that there should be one pair of feet to steer by. It took a quarter of an hour to go four yards, and the only sounds were the dry hub and five people breathing through their mouths.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:61:"Cart: yes," whispered Karis, when the tail was clear. "This cart, with a man at each wheel who has nothing else in his hands. And under that: *a wagon of five feet six between the hubs, no.* I want him to have the number where the yes stops."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:65:Seln had said that he did not like the eighth, and when they came to it Cael saw why he had refused to say more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:67:It was wide. That was the first thing, and it was nearly a disappointment after the seventh; the shelf ran out from the wall a good nine feet, sound slab from the face to the rim, and the pitch was no worse than a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:71:He looked down into it. The sides went down smooth and close, without a ledge, and where they passed out of the light there was no floor that he could see. Karis let a pebble go, and it clicked from one wall to the other for a count of three and was still clicking when he lost it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:73:"It's clean," said Brom. He had got down on one knee and laid his right hand on the western edge, and then on the eastern. "Look at that. It's not crumbled. The frost's got into a joint and opened it like a book." He sat back. "That's the same height both sides, to a finger. If there was a plank across it you'd never know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:75:"We've a tail-board," said Lira. "And his seat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:77:"Elm, and three feet long, with a wheel on the middle of it? She'd go through them like bread." He shook his head. "There's nothing on this shelf to fill it with, and nothing to bridge it. A wheel that size'll drop a hand into a two-foot gap and come up against the far lip, and the far lip's a knife."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:79:"Then it stops here," said Karis. Her voice had gone thin. "At the fifty-ninth hundred. With the end in sight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:81:"I never said that." Brom was looking at the cart. "It wants carrying."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:85:"It's two feet. It's one stride."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:87:"You are not to lift. A woman with a needle told you so, and I wrote it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:89:"She told me not to pull, and that the wrist would say if I had. I carried a box up a fan on my back, and you looked at the wrist yourself, and it was dry." He got up. "A cart's a box with wheels on. It sits on me. There's nothing hanging from a hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:91:Karis opened her mouth and shut it. She turned to Cael, as people turned to him when there was a ruling to be given.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:93:He had it ready, and it was not the one that she expected. On any floor he had ever stood on he would have decided this himself, and in a breath, since the plan was always his. But the thing in front of him was a weight and a gap, and whether a back could take the one over the other; and there was a person on that shelf who had known such sums since he was twelve years old, and it was not Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:95:"It's a load," he said. "Loads are his. If he says it goes, it goes, and he tells us where to stand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:97:Brom gave him a slow look, as if a thing had been set in his arms that he had not thought to be handed. Then he nodded, and began to give orders, very low.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:99:The boxes went first, passed across the crack from two pairs of hands to two more. He had the cart emptied to her boards. He folded his coat into a pad and laid it on the back of his neck, and he had Seln and Cael step over the gap and take a shaft each from the far side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:101:"When I say, you lift the shafts to your shoulders, and you walk. Don't pull her. I'm bringing her. You're only keeping her nose up." He looked round. "She'll want to tip back when I've got her, and that tail-board'll swing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:103:He did not say what was to be done about that. He crouched, and went in under the bed between the wheels, backward, until the axle-tree lay across his shoulders on the folded coat. He set his hands on his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:105:"Now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:107:Cael lifted. He felt the shaft come light in his hands, and then lighter, and he looked back along it and saw the wheels leave the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:109:Brom had stood up under her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:111:He had not straightened altogether; he was bent like a man beneath a low beam, and the cords of his neck stood out, and every part of the cart that was not on two shoulders at the front was on his. It was the whole of her. Cael watched the two wrists, on the two knees, and waited to see Iron Skin start at them, as he had seen the grey start at a ferry and a wash whenever a weight came on suddenly. Thirty animals were scraping on the hill above, and there was a page in Karis's coat that said what a start might be worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:115:Brom took two short steps to the edge. Then he took one long one, with his left boot going out over two feet of nothing and coming down on the western lip, and the cart went over the crack on a man's back. The tail-board swung out on its chains as she tipped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:117:It did not strike. Lira had a hand under it on the near side, and Karis had the back of her wrist against it on the off, and they walked it across between them and laid it against the frame as one shuts a door on a sleeping house. Nobody had told either of them to be there. He had been looking at Brom, and had not seen them move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:119:The wheels came down on rock. Brom backed out from under her on his knees and stayed there, breathing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:123:"I've stopped arguing," said Karis. "I'm only counting now." She had his left arm turned to the light already. The cloth was dry at the wrist, and she let it go. "Eight. Nine feet. Broken across, clean, two feet wide, no bottom found. Cart: taken over on one man's back, with four to steady her." She stopped. "And then I shall write that a carter by himself does not cross it, and that two planks of oak might serve, eight feet long, and that I haven't tried them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:125:"Put that it didn't bang," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:129:"It's the best thing I've done all week. Put it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:133:Beyond the eighth the mountain let go of the bench at last, and it opened like a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:135:He had lived for five days on a shelf that he could have thrown a stone across. Now the wall on his left fell back in a long curve and came round again two hundred yards on, and between the wall and the drop there lay a bay forty yards deep, floored from side to side with fine loose shale that had come down the face and spread. The slope above it was the same roof-pitch of plates, but lower; the upper shelf ran across it not three hundred yards up, with thirty grey heaps along it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:137:Seln sat beside a fire that he had laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:139:He had chosen the eastern corner of the bay, hard against the eighth narrowing, where a slab leaned out from the wall and water ran down the rock behind it into a basin the size of a hat. The cart stood on sound stone there, and that was as well, for she would have floated on every yard of the bay's floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:141:"You'll have seen the rock," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:143:Cael had. Nobody could have missed it. Two-thirds of the way along the bay a blade of pale stone stood up out of the shale, quite alone, halfway between the foot of the face and the edge. It lay square across the fall of the ground, like a wall across a field, and it was as long as a barn and no thicker than a garden wall. At either end it was no higher than a man's waist, and in the middle it was twice his height, and the evening sun lay along its upper side and left the lower black.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:145:"I walked round it this afternoon. The shale is heaped against the upper side of it to the height of my chest, and it has gone round both ends, as water goes round a pier. On the lower side there is bare floor, a piece as large as this camp, with nothing lying on it at all." Seln turned his hands over to warm the backs. "That has not happened once. A slide has come down this face and gone round that rock a good many times, and never yet filled the place behind it. I should have slept there, if it had water. I do not care to carry a skin seventy yards across loose stone in the dark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:147:"A fin," said Karis. She was shading her eyes. "It's a harder bed, standing on its edge. I've seen it drawn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:149:Cael put it in the catalogue, with its length and its height and the bare floor on its lee side. He did not know what he would want it for, and by now that was no reason to leave a thing out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:151:Lira had not sat down. She had walked on along the wall, past the fin, to the place where the bay shut; and after he had drunk he went after her, in two stints, across shale that sighed and crept under his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:155:"That's it," she said. "That's his ledge."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:157:It ran out from her feet along the wall of the gorge, dead level, as straight as a plank laid on trestles. He judged it six feet wide, and in places eight. For most of its length a roof of rock leaned over it. It went on for three hundred yards, and there it came to a shoulder of the mountain, and beyond that shoulder was nothing but evening air, with a brown valley under it, and in the valley a pale thread that was a road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:159:"Three hundreds," he said. "That's all that's left of his table."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:161:"And then down the other side to a ford, and a man counts out seventy." She had her arms folded. Her right foot was set a little forward, on its whole sole, and she was looking along the ledge. "It's the best ground on the whole mountain. It's rock from end to end. There isn't a plate on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:163:"It's six feet wide."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:167:He said nothing against it. It was her work; she had chosen it on the first day, and he had written sixty-two lines on the strength of her feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:169:They went back to the fire. Brom had the bacon out, and Karis was holding the long leaf to the flames to read down her last column, and up on the slope the scraping had begun to slow as the cold came on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:171:He wrote with his back against the third box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:173:*Seventy-seventh day. The bay at the west end of the bench. Sixty-two lines, and Karis's table says yes to every one of them, with a reason. Eight narrowings. The seventh has a hand to spare. The eighth is broken clean across, two feet, and Brom took the cart over it on his back; his wrist is dry. The tail-board did not bang. Lira wants that entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:175:*In hand, forty-six. Seventy more at the ford.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:177:*The nine: held, the whole length of it. Anchor asked at the seventh as it has never asked. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:179:*The traverse holds. The map's good. Karis says the herd moves off declarations, which means the loudest thing on this mountain is us, and we've been quiet for five days. One more day. First honest job I've ever had that didn't have a fight at the end of it. Sleep.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:1:# Chapter 27 — The Rise
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:5:Two of the three boxes were to stay in the loft, and so two of the three box-ropes were free. Brom married them end to end with a splice that he worked by feel, and hung the whole length from a fence-post with a stone on its end to take the stretch out, while she stood by with her short cord, the one that was knotted at every foot. When the rope had done creaking she laid the short cord along it again and again, and at each length she tied in a scrap of red wool from the hold's mending basket. Cael watched her count. She told nobody the figure, and wrote it in her notebook with her shoulder turned, as a person writes down the weight of his own purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:7:"It's a cord now," she said, coiling it. "It was two ropes, and they are not to be thought of as ropes again until I have done with them. Nobody is to tie a box with this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:9:"What do I tie the box with?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:11:"You carry it. You have told me for a month that carrying is a different thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:13:The middle box stood open on the barn floor, and she had been at the others since before he woke. She had the lid off the third. He came up the ladder in time to see her lifting out the masonry chips, each in its twist of paper with a number inked on the twist, and laying them in a row on a sack; and when the last was out there was a flat canvas bag at the bottom of the box, sewn shut and sealed, with five thumb-marks in the wax.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:15:She looked at that for a breath. Then she put the lid on over it, and drove the pegs home with the heel of her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:17:"Those stay," she said. "The chips come. I have a piece of every sort of dressed stone that I could reach between Denvash and the Line, and a date for each, and if there is so much as a doorstep at this place, I mean to know which of them it matches."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:19:So the middle box went east on Brom's back, with the chips in it, and the lamp, and the calfskin and the printed sheet, and a quire of clean paper. He knelt and had it set on him, and passed a strap of plaited sacking over his chest, and stood up. He had refused to let anyone take a turn. Cael had seen that look on him at the edge of a moss. The only other thing that left the loft that morning was on a strap across Seln's chest, shut, as it had left every loft before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:21:Oryn was waiting at the east gate with the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:23:She had the two panniers on, and the hold's woman had roped a roll of six grey blankets across the pack, behind the long shape in its oiled cover, with instructions that they were to come back. The healer stood on the mule's far side from the hill. She had slept, by the look of her, and she gave the five of them one glance from her stride and a half, each in turn, and seemed to find nothing in it that wanted her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:25:"You'll walk at her pace," she said. "It's the only one I've got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:27:The bowman had come along the fence to that gate. It was not his; he kept the western one, and Cael had never seen him ten yards from his platform. He stood with his bow at the length of his arm and looked at the road going up the valley, and then at Lira, and he lifted two fingers from the stave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:29:Lira had a boot in each hand. One was the carter's left, which she had found standing outside the barn door at first light. She hung it on the mule's pack by its laces, with great care, against the other one going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:33:"It ends here," she said. "At about this fence, or an hour beyond; the plate isn't fine enough to say. I have carried this sheet for ninety-four days in case it should be of use, and from this gate on it is a clean piece of paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:35:She rolled it, and put it back in the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:37:They came to the fork an hour out. The valley divided there round a spur of hill, and one road went away up the left-hand water toward a smoke that he could just see. The right-hand branch climbed to a low place in the skyline and went over. It was a road like any other, two pale ruts with a ridge of grass between. There was nothing at its mouth to say what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:39:"That's the Fallow," said Oryn, and clicked her tongue to the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:43:He read the second letter at noon, on the far side of the hills, since it was a noon with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:45:They had halted on a level of short turf where a spring came out. Brom had been helped off with the box and lay on his back beside it with his arms spread. The others had gone to the water. Lira had not; she had sat down next to Cael, and stretched out both legs, and was looking at Hesk's book where it lay on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:47:"You've still got one," she said. "I can see the thread."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:49:"I was going to read it now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:51:"Read it to me. You read me his."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:55:He read it aloud, and it did not take long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:57:*Filed with your record. It reads: left in good standing, undefeated on paper. I've kept this ledger forty years and never had a line like it. The session still stands. The road still runs through Ardenmere. — V.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:59:Lira began to laugh before he had reached the end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:65:"No. I don't suppose she does." She took the leaf from him and read it through again herself, with her lips moving, and Cael watched her come to the end of it and go back two sentences. "She's got a book like the old man's, hasn't she? She writes down what happened. And she's put you in it as a thing that she's never had to write before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:67:"She had me in it before anybody would give me a number. I asked Pike once whether he'd heard of her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:71:"He'd never heard of the town."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:73:He had thought about that on the forty-ninth night, and it came back to him now on a turf bank two days past a fence. There was a woman in Ardenmere who kept a straight ledger of a fighting yard, and a one-armed man in a meadow who kept a straight book of a plank; and neither had the least idea that the other was alive. He was written in both. He did not think that any registry on the continent could have said as much of one of its own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:75:Lira had her finger on the last line but one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:79:He did not answer at once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:81:"I know what she's speaking of. I don't know what she means by saying it now, on a ledger leaf, to somebody who's past the Line." He took the paper back, and folded it along its two creases. "It's hers. I'd tell you if it were mine. I only keep it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:85:"I've been guessed at."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:87:She let that be. It was one of the things that she did, and that almost nobody else had ever done for him. She leaned back on her hands and looked up the road, where it ran on eastward over the turf, as straight as a ruled margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:89:"She puts the thing that decides it last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:91:He looked at the leaf. *The road still runs through Ardenmere.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:95:"He put Rooke last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:97:"He put what Rooke wouldn't say last. It comes to the same." He tied the thread again, in the knot that it had come in. "They've each of them written to tell me that a door's open, and neither would be seen holding it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:99:"That's two," said Lira. "And an old man who wants to hear that you've a roof. You've more people behind you than you had when you'd a bed to sleep in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:101:He put the leaf inside the book, beside the other. There was nothing left now with a seal on it. He did not mind that either, since both of them had turned out to be the kind of letter that keeps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:107:There was grass. That was nearly the whole of it. It began at the height of his ankle, on the turf by the spring, and by the middle of the afternoon it stood to his knee, and before dusk to his hip: pale stuff, the colour of dry straw, with a seed-head that hissed against his coat. No stock had cropped it. There was not a fence in sight, or a smoke, or a tree, only long slow swells of ground going away to a sky that seemed to begin at his boots. The wind came over it from the north with nothing in its way, and laid the whole of it over in one direction, like the nap of a cloth under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:109:"Why does nobody graze this?" said Brom. He had the box up again, and he spoke without turning his head. "There's feed here for a thousand head."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:111:"The holds either side won't put a beast on it," said Oryn. "I've asked. They don't give you a reason. They say it's fallow, as you'd say a field was resting, and then they talk about something else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:115:"Longer than anybody I've had my hands on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:117:They walked it quiet. That was the hold, as it was on any road, and here it needed no telling. Seln went fifty paces ahead and never once looked back. The ruts were plain enough; the grain carts had used them within the month, and here and there he found the mark of a small neat hoof, and beside it, on the low side, a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:119:He watched the healer walk, since it had become a habit. She kept the carter's pace. She was below the mule on every slope, though the slopes were so gentle here that he could not always have said which side was the low one, and she changed over without seeming to think. He set himself to her beat, and breathed to the tape, and for long stretches of that afternoon the tape had nothing to say to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:123:"One more night of that," said Seln, feeding it. "I should not light one tomorrow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:125:"Because of the dogs," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:127:"Because I do not know what is there, and a flame is seen a long way over grass." He sat back. "It is the same answer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:129:Oryn had taken off her boot to look at her heel. She pressed the strip flat with her thumb, and then, with the boot still in her hand, she looked across the fire at Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:131:"Give me that wrist. The one I did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:133:He gave it, and she laid two fingers on the inside of it and two on his neck, and her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:135:"That's there," she said. "It's sound, and it's yours, and I'd know it in the dark by now. I'll want it again the day after tomorrow, at this ring, so don't do anything to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:137:"What for?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:139:"To see whether I lose it." She let him go. "I can't tell whether my hands have stopped unless I've got them on something I know. I'm not putting them on a stranger's ribs at a place like that and guessing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:141:Karis had her notebook open, and her pencil was not moving. Cael knew what that stillness cost her. A primary source was sitting across a fire from her, pulling on a boot, and had been round the place thirty times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:143:"What do the carters say?" she asked. "I had it from you at the long house door, in a sentence. I should like it as they give it, if you'll tell me. I shan't ask twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:145:"They say a healer's no good there. That's the words. *You'll not want her in the ring; she's no good there.* I had it first from a carter at the first gate I ever mended at, and I took it that he meant I'd be frightened." Oryn laced the boot. "Then I had it from six more, in the same words, and not one of them a man to be frightened of a field. So it's a thing that was seen once, by somebody, and handed down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:149:"That's what it'd have to be. Nobody can tell me who, or when, or what was broken." She shrugged, heavily. "A hold keeps what it needs of a thing. It needed *don't*. It didn't need the rest, so the rest is gone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:151:Karis wrote that down, and underlined nothing. He saw her look at what she had written, and then at the dark beyond the firelight to the east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:153:"The day after tomorrow," she said, half to herself, "there will be six of us who saw it. I mean to write down who."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:157:They came to the rise late on the ninety-fifth day, with the sun low at their backs and their shadows going on ahead of them up the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:159:It was a long swell of ground, no steeper than a ramp, and the ruts climbed it in a straight line. Seln had stopped at the top. Cael saw him against the sky, a narrow dark figure with his hands behind him, and he did not move as they came up. When they stood beside him they saw why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:161:The road turned. He had walked it for two days as straight as a plumb-line, and here, at the crest, both ruts swung out to the left together, toward the north, in a wide easy curve, like a stream going round a rock; and a long way off, beyond the curve's far end, he could see them come back and go on eastward along the line that they had left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:165:On that second slope stood the ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:167:It was three hundred yards off, as near as he could judge. There were grey stones standing on end, close together, in a rough circle; he counted them, and had eleven. No two were of a height. One was as tall as two men, and the one beside it came no higher than a knee, and all of them ended in a break. Between their feet lay something pale and level that he could not make out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:169:"That's it," said Oryn. She had halted the mule. "I've never been nearer than these ruts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:173:"I should like to learn where it begins," said Seln, "before I lie down beside it. There is perhaps an hour of light."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:175:"Nothing declared," said Karis quickly. "By anyone. That's the hold, and it is my condition as well; I want to know what is felt, and I can't know that if somebody is doing something at the time." She was looking along the old line. "We go abreast, an arm's length apart, at a slow walk, so that we all come to it together. If you feel a thing, put your hand up, and say what it is like. Don't say anything before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:177:"And if we feel nothing?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:181:They left the mule at the turn, with her rein under a rock. Brom knelt out from under the box and set it beside her. Then the six of them spread across the old line, in a row, and Cael took the right-hand end. Lira was next to him, and then Brom, and Karis, and the healer; Seln had the far end, on the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:183:They walked east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:185:He counted strides, since somebody would want the number. And he did what a one-armed man had told them to do, though it had not been said to him. He turned his attention inward, to the place behind his breastbone where a small bright thing had gathered on the morning of his Kindling. He had gone looking for it a few times since, in the first months, on bad nights. He looked now. It was as it had always been: a dark place, like a niche in a wall where a lamp once stood lit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:187:*Seventy-eight. Seventy-nine.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:189:At the eightieth stride Lira's hand went up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:191:She had stopped dead, on both feet, as she had taught herself to stop on a gravel bar. Her head was a little on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:197:"Like a door closing behind me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:199:Cael looked along the row. The healer had not spoken at all. Both her hands had risen, half way, with the palms turned up and the fingers spread, as a person holds them out to learn whether it has begun to rain; and she was staring at them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:201:"My Arbiter's gone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:205:"Back up. Ten paces. Tell me when it comes back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:209:They stepped back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:211:"Back," said Lira; and Brom said it with her, and Karis, and the healer, and Seln himself, a breath behind the rest. It was five voices on the one word. Not one of them had taken a second step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:213:"Same stride. All of us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:215:Karis was looking down the row, from face to face. Then she went forward again by herself, a foot at a time, with her hand held out in front of her as though she expected to touch something; and at a certain place she stopped, and drew back her toe by the width of a hand, and put it out again. She bent, and felt about in the roots of the grass, and came up with a grey stone the size of a loaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:219:Cael had gone forward the eighty strides with them, and back the one. He stood now on the near side of a stone, with five people who had each lost a thing and had it given back, and nothing in him had altered at either step. The dark place was dark. He had looked at it going over and looked at it coming back, and he could not have told the two apart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:221:The nine fragments lay in him where they had lain since a ford. He was sure of that as well. When Lira's hand went up, Wind had leaned in his own feet to go with hers, as it had leaned every day for two years, and he had put it down without thinking; and that had been on the far side of where the stone now stood. Anchor was asking about the stone already. *There?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:225:And nobody had turned to him. He saw why, and it was the simplest thing in the world. All four of them knew what he was. They had known for years that a brass plate had waited eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and that nothing had spoken to him out of that dark since. A line that put out a lamp had nothing in him to put out. It had not occurred to one of them to ask whether he had felt it, any more than they would have asked a man without a purse whether he had been robbed. The fifth knew nothing of brass plates, and she was looking at her own two hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:227:So there were two reasons, and he looked at each of them squarely, standing in the long light. He did not know what he would be telling them. And he had not been asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:229:Neither one was a good enough reason by itself. He knew that. He kept silent on the pair of them together, and his own shadow lay out in front of him across a grey stone and on into the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:233:He wrote it at the stone, kneeling, while there was still light enough to see the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:235:*Ninety-fifth day. The Fallow road, at the rise. Eighty strides east of where the ruts turn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:237:*Quiet ground. Karis's Arbiter stopped at a line she can mark with a rock. So did Lira's, Brom's, Seln's, Oryn's — same line, same step. Mine didn't. Mine's been stopped since I was fourteen. I don't know what that means. Nobody asked. Tomorrow we go in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:239:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:241:He read it over. It was short, and it was set down as he would have set down a count, and under it he could feel the size of what he had not put.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:243:They made their camp at the turn of the ruts, eighty yards back from the stone, and there was no fire. Seln had chosen the place. It lay a little below the crest on the western side, so that a person lying down could not see the ring and could not be seen from it. They ate cold bread from Thornwater's oven, and the hold's blankets went round, and the mule stood with her tail to the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:245:"Two and two," said Seln, "and nobody alone. I shall take the first with whoever will have it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:247:"I will," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:251:"She doesn't know it's there," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:253:"She's nothing in her for it to take." Oryn had the rein, and she stood looking at her animal's four small hooves with an expression that Cael could not read. "I've walked her round this place thirty times, by the ruts, because I was told to. I never once thought to ask what she'd have done if I'd let her choose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:255:Shadow lifted then, as it lifted at every dusk. Cael felt it come up soft behind the other eight, offering to take him out of the light; and he told it no. It had come at the turn of the ruts as it came anywhere. He wondered whether it would have come a stride beyond the stone, and thought that he knew, and did not go to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:257:He walked up to the crest once more before he lay down, since he wished to see the place with the day going off it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:259:The sun was under the western hills. The grass had gone grey, and the eleven stones were black against the last of the sky, and all the long slope between had lost its colour and lay like ash. Only one thing in that whole country was holding the light. It was the pale level thing among the feet of the stones. He still could not have said what it was, at three hundred yards. It lay there like a pool, and the last light stood on it as light stands on water after the banks have gone dark; and it went on shining, faintly, for a long while after it should have stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:261:He was not the only one who had come up to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:263:The healer was at the stone. She stood on the near side of it, with her boots together, like a person at the edge of a pond. She had both arms stretched out in front of her at the height of her waist, so that her two hands were over the line and the rest of her was not, and the palms were turned down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:265:She kept them so. He did not go nearer, and he did not speak. He watched a square dark figure stand at a stone with its hands held out over nothing, as still as the uprights beyond, until the pale thing on the far slope had faded and there was no light left anywhere for her to see them by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:1:# Chapter 17 — Thirty Head
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:3:There were thirty of them, and he counted twice to be sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:5:Twenty-six were full grown. Four were half the size, with plates of a paler grey that had not yet gathered any green, and those four kept to the inside of the shelf, against the hill. Each of the grown animals was as long as the cart and a good deal broader. It stood low on four legs like the stumps of trees, and its back and both flanks were roofed over with horn, in slabs that overlapped as slates overlap on a house, the largest along the spine and each with its keel. Lichen grew on the old ones in rings. When one of them stood still, which was nearly always, it was a heap of the mountain's own stone, and his eye slid off it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:7:The head was small for such a body, and carried at the height of a man's knee. It ended in something like the beak of a tortoise, and with that the animal scraped the rock. He could hear it, now that he knew to listen: a dry patient rasping, thirty times over, like a yard full of masons dressing stone a long way off. Under that there was another sound, whenever one of them shifted its weight, for the plates moved on each other with a low clatter, as a slate roof settles when the sun goes off it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:9:"They're eating the hill," said Lira, close to his ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:11:"They're eating what grows on it," said Karis, who had come out from the buttress on her toes. "Look at the rock behind them. It's a different colour where they've been."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:13:It was. Along the upper shelf, east of the last animal, the stone was scoured pale in a band as wide as the shelf itself, and ahead of the first it was dark and furred. They were mowing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:17:There was none. No head was turned toward any other. Each of the thirty faced the rock in front of its own beak, and the gaps between them were so nearly equal that he could have laid Karis's cord in any one of them and found the same knot in the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:21:"The shelf does," said Cael. "For the next ten hundreds, by the look of it, and it's the only shelf."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:25:"He said also that the salt crews do it twice a year, and walk." Seln had appeared at the cart's tail, as he did. "I have been as far as the fifteenth of your hundreds this morning, and they did not lift a head for me. I should add that I made no request of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:27:Cael weighed it. Thirty animals stood three hundred and fifty yards up a slope of loose plates, and the five of them had a cart, and a rule in a one-armed man's words. To go back was to go down the fan, and to go on was to do for one afternoon what carters did every year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:29:"We go on," he said, "at a walk. Nobody calls a figure across the shelf; if you want me to hear a number, you come and say it in my ear."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:31:They did twelve more lines that day, and it was the quietest work he had ever done in company. Karis came to him with each figure as a clerk brings a paper to a desk. Brom leaned into his rope with his eyes on the cart's near wheel and never once on the hill, like a man passing a dog that he has been told will bite if it is stared at. Up on the face the rasping went on without a break, and each time Cael raised his eyes from the page the grey heaps were where they had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:33:At the twentieth hundred he looked up and found them behind his left shoulder, and he had not seen one of them take a step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:37:Seln had found the second overhang, and he had found it directly beneath the middle of the herd.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:39:"It is the only one in the next mile," he said, before anybody could remark on it. "I looked for another, and I should have preferred another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:41:It was deeper than the first, a long low cave of a place where a bed of softer stone had weathered back, and from its mouth the whole line of the upper shelf could be seen against the evening sky. He had laid a ring of stones on its floor, and put nothing in the ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:43:Karis stood looking at the ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:45:"I want a fire," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:49:"Hear me first. Pike's *loud* has three things in it, as the country's rule has, and I don't know which of them matters to those." She tipped her head at the roof, and at what was above it. "Of the three, a fire is the one I can try this evening and then take back. It's struck with a flint, it will be the size of my two hands, and if a head comes up, Seln puts his coat over it. A Path I can't take back, and a shout I'd sooner not try on purpose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:51:"And if it's the one?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:53:"Then we learn it with a fire that a coat can put out, and not next week with one we need."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:57:"Hands," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:59:There were five. Seln struck the flint, and Lira lay on her stomach at the mouth of the overhang and watched the upper shelf with her chin on her fists, since her eyes were the best of the five. A thread of smoke went up and flattened under the rock and crept out along it into the dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:61:"Nothing," she said, when the twigs had taken. "They're scraping. The third from this end has lain down; it folded up its front legs first, like a cow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:65:"One," said Karis, and wrote it. "Fire, lit and fed in plain sight at three hundred and fifty yards, and not a head. I shan't call it struck off for good. I call it struck off at that distance, and at that size."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:69:They ate hot food for the first time since the wash, and afterward Karis asked for the whole of the next day. She would do no hundreds; she would sit on a rock in front of that roof with the clock and watch the herd from first light until she could not see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:73:"It isn't a day off the map. It's the map. Your column says whether the shelf takes a cart, and why, and those are part of why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:81:Cael sat on her left with the notebook, and Lira lay on the rock at her right, on her stomach again. Brom stayed under the roof with the cart, and Seln had gone on westward before any of them woke. The sun had not yet reached into the gorge, and thirty heaps of grey stone lay along the upper shelf with frost on their backs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:83:"The east end is mine," said Karis, "and you have the west. Lira takes whichever of them does something. Nobody is to tell me what he thinks it means; I want what it did, and when."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:85:The first observation was the spacing, and she had it within the hour. He counted the intervals for her in lengths, since there was no means of taping them, and between every animal and the next there was room for one of them and half another; a calf and its dam made one place, and the gap was kept from the pair. When the sixth from his end had scraped out what was in front of it and moved up a pace, the seventh did the same a breath afterward, and the eighth after that. He watched the gap close and open again down eleven animals, like a pulse going along a rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:87:"They don't look at each other to do that," said Lira. "Their heads are down, and they can't see past their own beaks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:89:"Write that she said so," said Karis, "and the time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:91:The second thing took her until the sun was on the shelf. High on the face, a little west of the leading animal, a vein of white stone ran down the slope as broad as a door, and at the first hour by the clock the leader's beak had been a cart's length short of it. At the second hour the white vein was behind the leader's tail, and the whole line, thirty animals and all their gaps, lay forty yards farther along the mountain than it had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:93:"Did you see one of them walk?" she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:95:"I saw the sixth come up a pace, and twice more I saw a leg lift. I never saw the line go anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:99:The third thing came down on them out of the sky at about noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:101:The sun had been on the crags above the upper shelf for an hour, and whatever the frost had been holding there it let go. He heard a crack, a long way up, and then a hollow knocking that grew; and a rock the size of a keg came down the face in great slow bounds, turning over in the air, and went straight through the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:103:It passed between the ninth animal and the tenth. It struck their own shelf between them with a sound like a mallet on a cask, and burst, and he felt the blow come up through the slab under him. The larger half leaped out again and crossed the bench two hundred yards west of where they sat, and dropped into the gorge; and a good while afterward the far wall gave the whole of it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:105:Karis had not flinched, and her eyes had never left the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:107:"Lira."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:113:"Not a head, and that went by as close to them as I am to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:115:"A step, then, from the two it nearly hit, and a step isn't flight." Karis let out her breath. "Give me the notebook; I want to put this one in my own hand." She took it on her knee and wrote slowly, with her lips tight. "*Sound: a rock the size of a keg, striking their own shelf. Felt in the slab here. Two animals moved one step each. No other response.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:117:"That's two of the three," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:119:"That's two." She gave him back the book, and flexed her fingers. "And it's the two I could have guessed, since the carter lit his fire at the wash and nothing came. I'm only being told what I knew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:125:He had the west end, and he had it faithfully. His eyes were on the last animal of the line, a big one with a broken plate on its near shoulder, which stood a little beyond a rib of the hill, so that from where he sat he could see it and it could not have seen its own fellows at the eastern end. He had been counting the slabs along its spine to keep himself awake, and he had got to seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:129:It did so suddenly, and nothing on that shelf had moved so quickly in two days of his watching. Its off foreleg came up off the shelf and went down on the slope above, and the hind leg followed, and then both again, three steps, with a clatter of horn that reached him a moment later. Beside it the next did the same, and the next. He swept his eyes east along the shelf, and there was not one grey back in the whole line that was where it had been; all thirty had gone up the hill by the length of their own bodies, the calves scrambling inside them, and stood now on the open slope with their heads lifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:131:Then he heard it. It was a long grinding rush, like a load of slates shot off a cart, and it came from the east end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:133:"There," said Lira, who was up on her elbows. "Under the last one, at this end. Look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:135:A piece of the upper shelf had gone. He could see the scar, pale and sharp-edged, where a slab as big as a barn door had been lying along the lip, and the slab itself was sliding still, far down the face, breaking as it went. The easternmost animal stood above the place with its hindquarters low and its legs spread wide, for it had been standing on the thing when it went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:137:"What did you see?" said Karis. Her eyes were still on the hill, and her voice was very quiet, and it had something in it that made the hair lift on his arms. "Each of you, in order. Cael."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:139:"The west end went up, three steps. I was counting plates on the last animal, and it moved before I heard anything at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:143:"I'd swear to it. I heard its own horn first, and then the slide."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:145:"It can't see this end," said Lira. "There's a piece of hill in the way. I've been looking at that rib all day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:147:"Lira. What did you see?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:149:"The plate tipped, and I saw the edge of it lift. The one on it went down behind, as if somebody had kicked its legs away, and it scrabbled." She swallowed. "And they all went up. Karis, they all went up at once. Not one of them turned to see what had happened; they were already going."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:153:"None of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:157:"There wasn't. I've got good eyes, and I've watched a starting line since I was small. I know what it looks like when one goes and the rest follow, because you can see it run along the row, and this didn't run along." Lira sat up. "It was like one animal thirty carts long."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:161:"I saw the plate tip, and I began to count when it tipped. I saw them step in the same instant, both ends, as near as I can judge; and I had said *one*, and begun to say *two*, before the noise of it got to me here." She laid one finger on the glass. "It had three hundred and fifty yards to come to us. It had the best part of that again to go along the shelf to his animal, round a rib. His animal ought to have heard it a count after this one did, and it moved with the rest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:163:Up on the slope the thirty stood a little longer with their heads high. Then one beak went down to the rock, and another. They did not go back to the shelf they had left; they had begun to scrape where they stood, a body's length above it, and already the gaps between them were closing and opening, settling toward a length and a half.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:167:She had Lira's copy of the finding out of her coat before the last head was down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:169:It was the leaf with the struck dot on it. She laid it on her left knee, and the new page on her right, and sat between the two of them with a pencil in her fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:171:"I'm going to say it aloud, and I want it broken if it can be. Brom, come out here, and bring your objections."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:173:Brom came out from under the roof and crouched, enormous, at the edge of the slab.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:175:"At the ford, seven heads turned at once to a point the size of a seed. Something reached all seven together, across a river, and it wasn't the light, for six of them never turned for a hammer on steel." She touched the left-hand leaf. "Then they did a second thing. They waited for one of themselves to walk, and they followed it, *toward*. That's clause four: no leader, only whichever goes first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:177:She moved her finger to the other knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:179:"Here, something reached thirty together. It wasn't sight, for there was a rib of the hill between and their heads were down. It wasn't sound, for the far end moved before the sound could have got to it, and a rock made a greater noise at noon and shifted two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:181:"The ground," said Brom. "That slab going. They'd feel it in their feet, the same as I would."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:183:"I thought of the ground. The rock at noon struck their own shelf hard enough for me to feel it here, through this." She rapped the slab with her knuckle. "Two of them stepped. So it isn't a shake either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:187:"It was frightened," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:189:She had said it slowly, and she was looking at her own right foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:191:"It nearly went over. You could see it. Everything in it was trying to be somewhere else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:193:"Yes." Karis's eyes rested on her, and then on Brom. "I have watched Iron Skin start at your wrists when a rope snatched, and go down again, and you told me in a loft that something leaves you when it comes. A burst went off by itself on a gravel bar, in our first week, because a foot slipped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:195:"They've no Paths," said Brom. "They're cattle with roofs on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:197:"I don't say that they have. I say that I know of one thing in this country that reaches every animal of a kind in the same instant, through rock, and it's the thing those hounds turned to. I'm not entitled to invent a second until I've used up the first." She drew a line across the new page. "Whatever a frightened shale-back gives off, the herd has a sense for it, and that's the same sense, or its cousin. Now look what they did with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:199:"They went away from it," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:201:"Uphill, and all together, with no first animal to wait for. The hounds go to the thing, and these go from it." She wrote four words and underlined them. "*Same sense. Opposite answer.* And there is Pike's word to lay beside it, which is *loud*; and when we asked a ford what a carter meant by that, the answer was a Path."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:205:"*Shale-back. Seventy-fifth day. One: it does not answer fire at three hundred and fifty yards, nor sound, nor a blow on its own shelf. Two: it answers something given off by one of its own in fright, at once, the whole herd together, by moving away. Three: by that, and by the carters' rule, it will answer a declaration in the same way, and a declaration is a great deal larger than a frightened animal.*" She stopped. "*Derived. Not tested.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:207:"Why not tested?" said Brom. "You watched it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:211:"You could test it small," said Lira. "One of your points, a seed, for half a breath, as you did on the bar. That moved seven dogs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:215:"On a slope, flight is a landslide with legs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:219:"A frightened calf moved them three steps, and that was enough to finish what was left of that plate. If I'm right, I find out with the hill coming down on the cart. If they don't stir, I've learned nothing, since a seed might only have been too small; there is no size of that test that's worth its answer." She turned the pencil over. "So it stays derived, and I write that word on it, and anybody who reads it knows exactly how far to trust me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:221:Cael looked up at the grey heaps on the mountain, and he saw what the page meant for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:223:At the ford he had found a seam. He had sat on a gravel bar with four hurt people and gone over what he had counted, and it had yielded a first mover to give the pack, and a place to put a knife behind it. There was no seam in this. Nothing on that hill went first or could be led, and there was no cut dog; if those thirty ever ran, there would be no opponent to read, only a slope, and the single question of where a person ought to be standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:225:"Write it in yours, too. I'd like it in two hands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:227:It was Karis, and he looked round at her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:229:"The other one I gave you because I couldn't hold a pencil. This I can write myself, and I have. But it's derived, and a derived thing in one hand is an opinion." She was looking at the leaf, and not at Hesk's book, which she had never read. "You had the far end of the line. If you'll put down what you saw there, in your own words, then it's two people's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:231:He took out the book and wrote it, with the day at the head: the plates he had been counting, and the big animal stepping before any sound had reached it, and that he had looked for one that went first and found none. He wrote her three clauses under that, and her two words under the clauses. It was the first time that anyone had asked him to keep a finding because his hand would make it worth more, and he wrote it as small and as straight as he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:235:Seln came back at dusk, and they had a fire again, since one of the three had been struck off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:239:"The shelf narrows," he said. "I have been within sight of the end of it. For the next eight of your hundreds it is much as you have had it. After that there are places where the hill comes down to the edge and leaves less room, and I counted eight of them. The eighth I did not like."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:241:"What's wrong with it?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:243:"I should prefer that you looked at it with your own eyes, since it is a question about a load. I say only that it is wide enough." He turned his hands over. "Beyond the eighth there is a bay, with water, and beyond the bay a ledge, and at the end of the ledge the mountain stops. One sees a valley from there, and a road in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:247:"I take it so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:249:Lira had been lying on her back with her right foot propped on a box. She sat up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:253:"Six feet, in places. It is level, and it is rock. I did not go out on it." He considered her. "I thought that it was yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:257:Brom had said nothing since the slab. He had been working something out, and he brought it up now as he brought up a stone from a well, slowly and whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:259:"So it needn't be on purpose," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:261:They looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:263:"If she's right. A rope snatches, and the Skin starts before I've told it. Or somebody's foot goes." He kept his eyes on his own hands. "It only has to be a fright."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:265:"It hasn't gone off since that first week," said Lira, to the roof. "Not once. I'd a dog at my knee, and it didn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:271:"I know what you're saying. I've written that as well." Karis had the new leaf on her knee still, and she turned it to the firelight. "It's the reason I asked for two hands. If the page is right, then for as long as we are under them the hold isn't a discipline any more. It's the floor we're standing on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:273:The fire settled. Overhead, thirty beaks went on scraping at the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:275:"I'll run under the wall from now on," said Lira. "The whole way, where there's most rock under me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:1:# Chapter 24 — Under the Stone
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:3:The ferryman would not take silver for his rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:5:He said so on the western landing, with the scow rocking at his back and the far drum already groaning. He was the same long sour man who liked animals and nobody else, and he had stood with his arms folded through the whole of Brom's account: the wash, and the well, and a peeled log on a hill of plates, and at the last a gorge with a cart in it. Brom told it as he would have told a debt, in order, leaving nothing out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:9:"With a cart on top of it. I'd sooner say so here than have you find it out from the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:11:"Sixty yards of four-strand. I laid that rope up myself, in the cold term, with him turning the hook." He jerked his chin at his boy. Then he looked at Karis, who had the purse out on her knee, and his long face soured further. "Put that away. What'd I do with it? There's not a rope-walk nearer than the turf fire, and I can't draw a wagon out of a river with marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:13:"Then what do you want for it?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:15:"A rope. When you've one. I'll not put a figure on it, for a figure's a thing you can pay and walk off from."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:17:He turned his back on them and clicked his tongue at the far bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:19:Karis wrote it down as he had said it. *Owed: one rope, sixty yards, four-strand, to the ferryman. No price.* She put the purse away uncounted, and Cael thought that it was the first debt she had ever entered without a number beside it, and that she did not like the look of the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:21:They went over with the Oxhollow cart, a copper a wheel and a copper a beast out of the carter's own bag. Halfway across Brom lifted his head. In the round shed on the eastern bank something brown was walking its circle, steadily, with one white foot coming round and round into the light, and Brom watched that foot for the rest of the crossing with the face of a man who has seen a child of his doing well at a trade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:23:The meadow had thinned, for the cold had sent most of the tents home, and there were five fires where there had been a dozen; but the long eave was where it had always been, and under it twelve feet of tarred oak, and behind the oak a bald brown head bent over a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:25:Pike saw them from the landing road. He looked, twice over, at a strange cart with three boxes on it, and at a girl in one boot of her own and one the size of a bucket, and at how Cael held his left arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:27:"You're down," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:29:"We're down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:31:"You'd a cart when you went up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:33:"It's in a gorge," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:35:"So I'd heard, from a man who came in last night." Pike set his pen in its pot. "I'll say your line when I've read your paper. Read mine first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:39:*HOLD.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:41:It had had one name after it, that time. Under each of these there were five, set down in the order in which they had been spoken at the plank on the ninth day, and his own was the last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:43:"That's us," said Lira. Her voice had gone up. "That's our names. Who asked you to keep these?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:45:"Holds came to the ferry. Asked who. I said you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:51:"Who they ought to have. One's a bull wants bringing down off the top grazing before the snow shuts it, and he's killed a dog. The other's a ford that wants staking. They'd each of them heard a line said in this meadow about a river and five dead hounds." He blotted something. "I told them you were six days off on a hill. They said they'd wait a fortnight. After that they'll have Teague."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:55:He had been written on a good many boards. A woman in Ardenmere had chalked him up with a question mark, and a panel had posted him at a rate that it had scattered on purpose, and for two years a house had put his name on a card every week because a schedule told it to. Nobody had ever before gone to the keeper of a book and asked who was good, and been given him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:59:"A fortnight," said Lira. "They'd wait a fortnight, for us, with a bull loose." She looked round at the others, and her eyes were very bright. "He didn't say fast. Nobody said fast. They said *who*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:63:Karis had the paper out of her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:67:"He's marked it," he said. "And paid you there, by the paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:73:"He has it. He followed it with his finger from end to end, and then he said that his carts would go round, and went to his hearth for the bag."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:75:Pike nodded slowly. He opened the book at the leaf with five names, and set the flat stone on its upper corner, and turned his shoulder to the meadow. There were perhaps thirty people within hearing, and Cael saw the hide-woman among them, with her knife on her thigh, and under the ash an old carter who lifted his chin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:77:"High traverse, Oxhollow to Thornwater. Mapped. Delivered at the ford. Carts go round."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:81:"Cart lost. Crew of five, three hurt, none lost. Seventy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:83:It went down the meadow in that level carrying voice, past five fires. A woman looked at Lira's boot. A man by the nearest tent said something to his neighbour, low, and the neighbour looked at the strange cart, and then at the empty place beside the smith's shed where a hired one had stood. The Oxhollow carter had halted his mules at the edge of the grass to listen. At *carts go round* he had nodded slowly, like a person who hears his own errand read out to strangers; and at *cart lost* he looked at his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:85:"Is it wrong?" said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:87:He put it to all five, as he put it to anybody. Cael went through the line a word at a time, for the place where he could say so. *Mapped* was true, and *delivered*; carts would go round, for an old man with a tally-stick had said it in those words. There had been a cart, and there was none, and three of them had been hurt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:89:He looked for the thing that the line left out, and found it at once, and it was everything: a ledge that had shown no crack, and a page marked derived, and eight seconds of fire. None of that was in Pike's column, and he had known since a river bank that it would not be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:91:"No," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:93:Pike wrote, and the pen went to the horn pot and came back four times. When the line was on the page he did a thing that Cael had not seen him do before; he dropped the nib to the space beneath it and wrote four words more, smaller, and drew a short rule under them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:95:*less six, the cart.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:97:"Seventy's what the ford paid. Sixty-four's what the job was worth. That's what goes on the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:99:"Fair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:101:It was Brom who said it. He had come up to the oak, and he was looking at the small words under the line as he had looked at a smith's cut axle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:103:"It was his cart, and we brought him back a story about it. I'd have written it the same."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:107:Cael found that he did not mind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:109:He turned that over, since he had expected to. On the bank at Oxhollow he had written that he kept waiting to mind, and he had gone on waiting for six days of road. The line said that they had lost a cart, and it said so to thirty strangers, twice. And he stood under an eave with five bands of linen round his chest and felt only that a thing had been counted, by a person who counted everything alike; it was the seventh line under their names, and it was worth sixty-four, and both of those were so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:111:Karis had the purse in her hand, and she had not opened it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:113:"I want one thing straight, for my own page," she said. "The seventy is in here. I counted it at a ford, and it hasn't altered on the road. Earned stands at a hundred and thirty-two. What you have written under that line is another figure, and I don't believe that it is money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:115:"It's what I'd tell a hold you were worth for that job, if a hold came and asked me," said Pike. "The six you'll count out to a smith yourselves, before dark, if I know you. I only take it off where the meadow can see it go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:117:"Seven," said Lira, under her breath. She was counting on her fingers. "A boat, a fence, a wagon, a mile behind a mule. A ford. A well. A mountain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:119:"And a bull, if we want it," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:125:He had been reading the plank, as he read it every time, from the left-hand post to the right, and there were nine slips besides the two with their names on. He had sorted them and found nothing among them that weighed much, and his eye had come to rest, for want of anything better, on Pike's flat stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:127:It was a grey river stone the size of a loaf, and it lay on the upper corner of the open book, as it always lay. Something was under its far edge: a corner showed there, toward Pike's side of the oak, no wider than three fingers, and it was neither bark nor hide. It was paper, of a good cream weight, such as a clerk writes on at a desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:129:There was a curve of dust across the corner, and he looked at that for a breath before he understood it. The dust followed the rim of the stone exactly, a fine pale line, as a tide leaves its line on a beach; so the paper had not been slid there this morning. It had lain under that stone, in that one place, for long enough to take the stone's shape.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:133:Cael leaned a little over the oak. The writing ran away from him, toward the man with the book, and he read it upside down, as he had once read a bailiff's list on a counter in Denvash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:135:*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Fallow road. East of Thornwater; the second day; where the road leaves its line at the rise and comes back to it. One hundred marks (one hundred).*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:137:There was no hold's mark at its foot. There was a seal: a hook and a bar, in dark wax.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:139:He read it a second time, to be sure of the figure. Then he reached across the oak and drew the paper out from under the stone by its corner, and the dust-line broke. He turned the sheet round and held it out to Pike, in his fingers, saying nothing, as any crew holds out a slip that it wishes to hear said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:143:Cael had no other word for it. He had watched that face for eighty days, and it was never still; it looked at a thing and then looked again, and something in it went on working like a mill between the two looks. Now the one arm lay on the book and the brown eyes rested on a sheet of cream paper, and nothing in the face moved at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:147:"That's the Fallow Ring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:153:Beside Cael's shoulder a notebook shut with a small clap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:155:He did not need to look, for he knew whose it was, and he knew what was folded in the middle box, forty yards off, with two lines ruled under two of its words. Karis had heard those two words once before, dropped by a woman with a sack of barley on her hip, and she had asked nobody what they meant for thirty-two days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:157:"I have that noun," she said. Her voice was very careful. "I have had it since the fifty-eighth day, from a carter's wife, in three sentences, and I have had nobody to put a question to. You have just said it to me yourself, at your own plank. I think that I may ask now. What is it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:159:Pike laid the cream sheet down on the oak, face upward, and put his hand on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:161:"Quiet ground's where the sigil goes to sleep. Yours. Everyone's. You walk a road, and there's a place on it where your Arbiter stops — not slows; stops — and you walk on past and it's back. There's a handful of them. Along the void roads, all of them. Carters walk round them, and have as long as there've been carters. The dogs live in them. Stillhounds. Nobody's asked them why. Why any of it, nobody knows and nobody's needed to, because the rule's *don't go in*, and the rule's never once failed anybody who kept it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:163:It was the longest thing that Cael had ever heard him say, and he had said it once. He let it stand, without going back over a word of it, and that was stranger than what was in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:165:"That's what a carter knows. That's what I know. There's not a man on the ferry knows a word more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:169:Cael had heard *yours* and *everyone's*, and he had thought of a place behind his own breastbone, where a small bright thing had gathered once on a morning in a windowless room, and looked at him for eleven seconds, and gone out. It had been dark ever since, and he kept that thought where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:171:"You've seen it?" said Lira. "This ring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:173:"I've walked past the Fallow Ring two hundred times. Never once stepped over the line. Thirty years on the roads and I've never met a practitioner who did it twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:177:It was Karis, and it was the second of the four questions that she had swallowed in a cart-yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:179:"You'll know," he said. "Your Arbiter tells you. It stops."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:183:Karis went to the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:185:She walked the forty yards without hurrying, and climbed the wheel, and had the lid off the middle box; and when she came back she had a folded leaf in one hand and a roll of calfskin under her arm. She laid the leaf on the oak beside the cream paper. It was in Cael's own writing, three sentences with a question mark after the first, and under two of the words there were two ruled lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:187:Then she unrolled the hide, and held it open with her wrists, and put one finger down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:193:"He has drawn that. A road that leaves its line and comes back. He drew it for his own wheels, and never drew what was in the middle, since he had no business there. And she said that everybody knew it, and that nobody went in." She took the finger away. "I have had those two for a month and could do nothing with either. This is the third, and it has a price on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:195:She looked up at Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:199:"It's a paper with a hundred on it," said Pike. "I'd not call it more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:201:Cael looked at the three things lying side by side on the tarred oak. There was a leaf in his own hand that held a stranger's passing word, and a calfskin that a carter had drawn for his wheels over twenty-two years, and a cream sheet under a seal that he had first seen burned into the end of a cask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:203:"Why's it under your stone?" said Lira. "Everything else you've got is on a tack. You shout it at people."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:205:"I said it. I'm bound to say it; twice, the day it came, to the whole grass, with the price." He turned the cream sheet a little with one finger. "The broker's clerk brought it up from the landing the morning after the last sack, sealed as you see it, with a hundred marks in a bag, counted. I've had that seal on bales for thirty years. I never had it before on a slip that sends a crew two days past Thornwater."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:207:"And nobody took it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:209:"Nobody scratched. There were forty in this meadow that day, and when I'd done you could have heard the river. So I put the stone on it. A thing on a tack is a thing I'm offering."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:211:He said that last quite mildly, and Cael understood that he had been told something about the man. Pike wrote down whatever was put in front of him and read it to anyone; but he had found, in thirty years, one paper that he would not hang up between two fences and a wheel, and he had kept it where a person must go looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:215:A hundred marks was more than everything that the board had paid them in ninety days, the mountain included. It was to walk two days east of a hold where they were known, and pick up something that somebody had left lying, and carry it home. He could find no opponent in it. There was a ring on a road, and a rule with no reason given, and instruments, which were only things; and he heard himself thinking that, and distrusted it, since he had thought the same about a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:217:"What's a surveyor want out there?" said Brom. "On ground a carter won't put a wheel on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:219:"That I couldn't tell you," said Pike. "It's not a trade I've ever seen come over my ferry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:223:"The paper doesn't say. I've read it more than twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:225:Seln had not spoken at all. He stood at the plank's end, and he had not yet troubled with the words; it was the sheet itself that he studied, where it lay, as a joiner studies a board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:227:"I'll not mark it tonight," said Pike. "That's not a thing I'd say of a fence. Take it in with you, and put it on your boards, and look at it with a lamp. If you bring it me in the morning I'll say it and write your strokes." He pushed the paper across the oak with one finger, until it lay in front of Cael. "And if you don't, it goes back where it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:231:They paid the smith before the light went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:235:"Floated," the smith said, when it was done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:237:"To the edge, and over, with her shafts up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:239:"I'd cut that axle true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:241:"You had. She never once ran crooked. It wasn't the cart." Brom was quiet a little. "You kept my seat board, and I'm sorry for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:243:The smith held out his hand, and Karis counted six thick coins into it, and he shut his fist on them without looking down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:245:"A hundred and eight," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:247:It was done. Cael had carried that six since the seventy-first morning, as one carries a stone in a boot, and he waited to feel lighter and did not. The smith had gone back into his shed already, and presently the rasp began again. A cart was only a cart, and the man had been paid for it twice over; all the same there would be a clean place under that lean-to for a good while, and Brom stood looking at it until Karis took him by the sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:249:The account was square, as far as coin could square it. There remained a rope with no figure against it, and a line in a book that said *cart lost*, and neither of those could be paid off; they could only be added to, by whatever the five of them did next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:251:He came on Lira at the roof's edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:253:She was standing where the eave's shadow stopped, with her toes on the line of it, her own boot and the great one side by side, and she was looking east. The saddle road went up there, pale in the last light, toward a wash, and a ford, and a fence; and beyond the fence there was a road that none of them had walked, with a bend in it. The cold was coming up off the river behind them. Along the meadow the fires had drawn in to red, and somebody was calling a child home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:255:He stood beside her. The others had gone in, and through the open door he could hear a boy being told to hold a cloth taut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:259:He had not meant to say it aloud. It had been going round in him since the plank, a thing that he could not sort. A man had walked a road for thirty years, and each time that he came to a certain place he had gone round, and each time he had made a mark in his head. He had never once put his foot over, and he knew the number of his own refusals to the unit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:261:Lira did not turn her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:263:"So do you. I've never once heard you say never."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:265:He looked at her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:267:"He counts what he hasn't done," she said. "That's all that is. Two hundred times he didn't. You'd have gone round it the first time, and the second, and you'd have written both down. And the third time you'd have stood there with your book shut, working out what it would cost to look." She scuffed the great boot along the shadow's edge. "I've watched you price things since a lane in Ardenmere. You've said *not yet* to me, and *not here*, and *no* a hundred times. You don't say *never*. I don't think you know how."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:269:It was true, and he had not known it, and she had. He had refused nine things a stride at a time for ninety days, and thought himself a careful person; but a refusal that is made again at every stride is the opposite of a rule, for it is a door that somebody is holding shut with his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:271:"You want to see it," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:273:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:275:"So do I." She said it low, and she was still looking at the road. "He said your Arbiter tells you. It stops. I've spent three months not doing the one thing I'm good at, on purpose, a stride at a time, and it went off anyway when a ledge fell. I'd like to stand somewhere that I couldn't. I'd like to know what's left when it isn't me doing the *not*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:277:He thought of a question that stood in his book under the fifty-ninth day, in her words, with nothing written beneath it. She had asked what she was becoming if nobody was measuring, and he had not answered, and nobody had. He did not suppose that a ring on a road would answer it either. But it would tell her one thing plainly, at one stride, without a sheet or a panel or a coach; and he saw that she had heard that in the old man's three short sentences before any of the rest of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:1:# Chapter 16 — Floats
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:3:Karis took the grey thing on her two forearms, since her palms would not bear the weight of it, and held it up to the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:5:"It's horn," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:9:"It grew. Something laid this down a layer at a time, and carried it for years; there's lichen on it as old as any on that rock." She tipped it, and the pale underside showed, smooth and faintly hollowed. "And then it was shed, as a stag sheds. This lay on a back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:11:"A broad one," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:15:"It bears now," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:19:"I asked a question at the plank, two mornings ago, and I have carried the answer for a day and a half, since it concerned nothing that we had yet seen. I asked what a shale-back was, and whether a crew had ever come to harm by one." He inclined his head toward the horn. "He gave me the carters' rule first, and he gave it twice, and I shall give it as he did. 'Never be below them. Never be *loud* below them.'"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:21:"Below," said Lira. She glanced up the fan, and then along the edge of the shelf at the top of it, and her mouth went thin. "That's a bench. It's got a mountain on one side of it. Where else is there to be?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:23:"I put that to him also. He said that the salt crews go under them twice a year on the cold road, and walk, and are let alone. The animals are as mild as cattle. A man may pass beneath a herd all day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:27:"There he had two tales, and I asked for both." Seln looked at the ground while he told them, as he did when he was reading from memory. "The first is his own time. A salt string was going under a ledge herd on the cold road, and a block had come down across the track; and a Force at the tail of the string shifted it with his Path, since that was quicker than unloading. The hill came down, with the herd upon it. They found two mules."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:31:"The second he had from a carter, who had it from his father. Men used to come up a side water off the Thornwater road with carts, for roofing stone. It lies ready split at the foot of a great slide, and need only be loaded. One year they did not come back down, and the next year nobody went up to learn why." He lifted his eyes to the wheel-ruts under their feet, with the turf grown over them. "I had not supposed, until this morning, that I should be standing in the second tale."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:37:"He said *loud*. A carter says that of a Path, and of a shout, and I've heard one say it of a fire. Which did he mean?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:39:"I asked him that in those words. He said, the kind a man would not be on a road." Seln spread his hands a little. "He means all three, and he does not know which. It is the rule that Thornwater keeps, for the reason that Thornwater gave us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:41:Cael looked at the slope. Nothing on it moved. A small wind came down the gorge and stirred the heather, and across the water the far wall gave back the sound of the stream, late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:43:"The hold stands," he said. "No Path and no fragment, for any of us, here as on a road; that's not new. What's new is that until somebody can tell me which kind of loud, we keep every kind of quiet as well. Low voices. No iron on iron. And nobody goes under anything with a back until we've looked at it first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:45:"We're about to drag a cart up a hill of plates," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:47:"Then we'll do it when there's nothing above us. He's looked." He turned to Seln. "You have looked?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:49:"For a quarter of an hour. I should not say so otherwise."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:51:"Then how do we haul?" said Brom. "A rope wants calling. Somebody has to say when to heave and when to stand, or a man loses his fingers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:57:Brom walked out onto the fan, and it moved under him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:59:It did not slide. The whole surface gave a little, with a dry clinking, as a shingle beach gives under a boot, and settled again about his ankles. He stood in it with his arms out. Then he climbed ten yards, slowly, and each step sank and slipped back half its length, so that he seemed to be wading upstream.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:61:"It's loose to the depth of my arm," he said, when he came down. "Farther, maybe. There's no floor to it that I can find."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:63:They tried the cart loaded all the same, since he wished to see what it would do. Seln and Cael took the shafts and Brom went into his rope, and they put her at the slope and leaned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:65:She went up four yards. Then the wheels stopped turning. Cael felt it through the shaft before he saw it; the rims had sunk into the plates to the depth of a hand, and they were no longer rolling over the slope but pushing a wave of it ahead of them. Brom leaned harder, and the wave grew. The cart stood still for a breath, with three people straining at her. Then the stones under her let go all together, and she came back down the four yards with a long sigh of slate, a little sideways, and stopped where she had started.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:67:Brom stepped out of his rope and regarded her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:69:"She floats," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:71:"She what?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:73:"Floats. A wheel's for a road. It wants something hard under it to turn on. There's nothing hard in that." He pushed his boot into the fan and watched the plates close round it. "She'll not roll on it. She sits in it, like a boat in a pond, and she goes where it goes, and it only knows the one way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:75:"Then it can't be done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:77:"I never said that. You don't row a boat up a weir. You put a line on her bow from the top of the weir, and you lift her head, and you let her swim up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:79:He would have her empty first, and nobody disputed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:81:The boxes went up by hand, and it took what was left of the day. Seln and Cael slung the left-hand box between them from its two rope beckets and set off, and it was the worst climb that Cael had made since a scree above a valley with no name. He had nine strides. At the tenth the calf shut its fist, and he stood on a slope that crept under his boots, with half a box hanging from his right hand, and counted twenty while the gorge breathed cold on his neck. Seln waited beside him each time without a word, looking at the view, as a man waits for a door to be unlocked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:89:"It's carrying. It sits on me. There's nothing hanging off a hand." He did not turn his head, since the box would not let him. "The hold's woman said the wrist would be wet if I'd used it. Look at it when I come down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:91:Karis looked at it when he came down, and it was dry, and she wrote that as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:93:He took the third box after it, in the same manner, while the light went yellow on the far wall. Lira carried the stores, two steps and a slide, and would not be told that a bound ankle had no business on loose plates. By the last of her journeys the cart stood at the foot of the fan with nothing in it but its own seat board, and looked like a thing that had been robbed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:95:They slept that night at the top, on the end of the shelf, because she would not have three boxes left alone on a mountain and nobody cared to be the one to tell her that there was no one within a day's walk to steal them. It was too dark by then to see what they had climbed to. He lay with his back against the third box and heard the stream a long way down on his right hand, and a second stream, fainter, that was only the first one coming back from the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:99:The cart went up in the morning, by six stages, and Brom built every one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:101:He had found his weir. Islands of solid rock stood up out of the fan here and there, the bones of the hill showing through its skin, and a rope laid over bare rock would have sawed itself through in an hour. So he had Seln cut a length of red trunk, as thick as a thigh, and peel it. He bedded that across a notch in the first island, and laid the ferryman's rope over it, so that the log should turn and the rope not rub.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:One end went down to a bridle on the cart's axle-tree, where a pull from above would lift her nose. At the other end he put himself. He stood in a loop that went round his chest and under both arms, facing downhill, with his hands behind his back; and when Karis, up at the log, raised her arm, he walked down the mountain. Seln and Cael went down with him, hauling hand over hand, and for every yard that three people descended the cart rose one, nose first, half buried, with the plates parting round her and closing behind as water closes behind a punt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:105:"Hold," said Karis, in the low carrying voice that she kept for a reading-room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:107:They held. Lira scrambled across below the cart and wedged slabs under both wheels, and the rope was carried up to the next island, and the log after it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:109:The first stage went as he had planned it, and the second did not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:111:Along the gorge-ward edge of the fan there ran a gully, a chute as wide as a road, and the whole skin of the slope leaned that way, like a floor in an old house. Twenty yards into the second stage the cart began to lean with it. She went sideways, quite gently, a foot for every yard she climbed, floating off the line of the rope toward the chute; and up at the island the peeled log began to creep along its notch after her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:115:"Lira," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:117:She was there already. They had knotted the box-lashings into a second line and made one end fast to the cart's near side, and Lira had carried the other up the slope and to the left, away from the chute, to a boulder as big as a barrel. She sat down behind it now with both boots against the rock, and passed the line round her back, and took it in her two fists in front of her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:119:"It's a thing I can do sitting," she said. "Walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:123:Then the fan under the near wheel let go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:125:It was a small slip, a barrowful of plates running out from under the rim, and the cart dropped a foot and snatched. Cael saw the line take Lira. It lifted her off her seat and dragged her a yard up the boulder's face, with her heels scraping, and he saw what happened in her calves, because he had been watching for it for sixty days. Every sinew in her stood ready to be somewhere else. It was the burst, asking, with the whole of her Path behind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:127:In his own feet Wind leaned with hers, as it always did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:129:She sat down. She did it on purpose, hard, as one sits on a lid, and drove both boots against the rock again, and the line burned through her fists for a hand's breadth and stopped. The cart's nose came round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:133:He walked. Nine fragments had come up at the snatch, and he went on down the slope with the rope in his hands, putting them down one at a time. Compression had felt the jerk come along the hemp and had offered to take it through his frame into the hill. Anchor had been asking about the island since first light. *There?* A point fixed against that notch, and told to stay, would have been a better thing than any log; it would not have walked, or worn, or needed a man's weight to hold it. He owned the craft of it. He told it that the log was Brom's, and to leave it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:135:The Iron read was harder to refuse, because it offered the thing he was being paid to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:137:Within a pace and a half of his boots it would have given him every plate on that slope, and which way the whole loose skin meant to go, a breath before it went. He was a surveyor today, by the terms of a slip, and he carried an instrument that no surveyor west of the Line could have bought, and he kept it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:139:It was not only the hold. A reading taken with that thing could be checked by nobody, and Cael had lived since his Kindling under figures that could not be checked by the person they concerned. What Karis did with a knotted cord, any carter could do after her, at the same place, and get the same number or call her a liar. That was the map the man was buying. The other would only have been a boy on a hillside saying that he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:141:They brought her over the lip at noon. The last yard was rotten rock, and four of them lifted while Brom got his back under the tail and straightened his legs, with his hands on his knees and nowhere near a rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:147:He saw the bench properly for the first time with the cart standing on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:149:It was a road, or the stone that a road might have been made on. It ran west from the top of the fan along the wall of the gorge, as level from end to end as a shelf in a shop, thirty feet broad where they stood; and no hand, by the look of it, had ever cut it. The mountain rose on the left in a long grey slope of plates, steep as a roof, to a skyline that he had to lean back to find. On the right there was an edge, and beyond the edge blue air, and the far wall three hundred yards off with a white thread of water hanging on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:151:Only one thing was wrong with it. The whole shelf tipped outward, toward the drop, gently, like the top of a desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:153:"Pitched," said Brom, who had seen it at once. "Like a roof. Everything that falls on this from up there is meant to roll across it and go over." He set a pebble down. It lay still for a breath, and then it trundled away from him, quite slowly, the whole thirty feet, and dropped out of sight without a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:155:"Four, and a piece," said Karis, with Hesk's clock in her hand. "I heard it land, and then I heard it land again." She looked at the far wall with respect. "I shall have to take the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:159:"Why running?" said Brom. "She could walk it and see more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:161:"Because a thing that'll bear me running will bear her walking," said Lira. She was looking down the shelf, and not at anyone. "And if a piece of this is going to go, I'd sooner it went under me than under three boxes and the four of you tied to them. I can get off it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:163:Cael said nothing to that. He had stopped counting her runs on a gravel bar, and he did not begin again; but it was hard to hear her set her own ankle against a mountain as though it were a price she had already agreed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:165:Lira went, with her Path where she had kept it since the ford. She ran lightly, well in from the edge, and her right foot came down a shade behind her left, and he looked at the gorge until she was a small figure standing a stone on end. She walked back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:167:"Slab," she said. "Good slab, all the way. Dry. It rings a bit hollow about halfway, on the outside, so I kept in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:173:Karis measured where he stopped. She had her cord for the width, knotted at every foot. For the pitch she had made a thing out of the brass straightedge and a pebble hung on a thread, and she laid the brass across the slope and read off how far the thread hung from its mark. It took her the better part of a quarter of an hour, and she spoke each number once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:175:"One. Twenty-six feet. A hand and two fingers in the yard, outward. Slab; hollow at fifty on the outer third. No water. Cart: yes, keeping in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:179:Lira had come to look over his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:181:"How many of those will there be?" she said. "Before the other end."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:185:"Seventy lines." Lira began to laugh, low, mindful of the rule. "Seventy marks. He's being paid by the line." She pointed at the book in his hand. "You've written in one of those every night since a lane in Ardenmere, and nobody ever gave you a copper. Now there's a man with a ford who'll give you a mark every time you rule one off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:187:"It's her line," said Cael. "I only hold the pencil."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:189:"It's in your hand. That's whose it is; ask Pike."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:193:"What's the rate for a strap?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:199:"I am of no use at a shaft that she is not of more," he had said, with a small bow toward Lira. "I cannot run, or carry, or count by a clock. There is one part of a day's work on a road that I can do better than any of you, which is to go on in front and learn where a person would be glad to lie down. I should like to do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:201:"How shall we know the place?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:203:"I shall leave you word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:207:"Two more," said Brom, turning it over. "That's a man who's used to leaving things for people who mustn't be seen reading them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:209:They made the eighth as the light was failing. The shelf bent there round a buttress, and behind the bend the wall leaned out into a roof as deep as a room and twice as long, with a floor of dry grit under it. Nobody was in it. A second pebble lay in the middle of the floor, with nothing scratched on it at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:211:Seln himself was forty yards farther on, down on one knee at a place where water came out of the rock, and he was not filling the skin. He was looking at the wet ground beside the lower of two small pools.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:213:"I should drink from the upper one," he said, when they came up. He held both hands apart to show the width. "Something has used the other. It was heavy, and it had a foot as broad as that, with no claw to it, and it came down off the slope to do it and went back the same way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:217:"Not today, and not yesterday. I do not know the animal, and so I do not know how long its mark keeps." He stood, and wiped his knee. "The roof is dry. I have not slept under a better since a loft."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:219:They ate cold under it, and nobody proposed a fire. Brom let the tail-board down by its chains as gently as he could, and one link slipped and struck the frame, a small clear note, like a spoon on a cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:221:It came back off the far wall when he had already begun to apologise for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:223:They listened to it go. Up on the dark slope above them nothing whatever happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:225:"Well," said Brom, very low. "That's one thing it doesn't mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:227:"It's one thing that slope doesn't mind tonight, with nothing on it that we can see," said Karis. "I shan't write more than that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:229:He wrote his own page with his back to the wall, by the last grey light, since there was to be no lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:231:*Seventy-third day. The bench, at the end of the eighth hundred. The cart came up the fan in six stages, by Brom's reckoning and nobody's Path. Eight lines in Karis's table, and each of them says yes. Lira says that I am paid by the line.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:233:*By Pike's rule we are not to be loud under whatever lives on this hill. We have seen one piece of horn and one footmark.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:235:*The nine: held, on a hill where three of them would have been of use. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:237:There was ice on the kettle in the morning, a skin as thin as a leaf, the first that any of them had seen past the Line. Lira ran the ninth hundred with her breath smoking, and they drew the cart on after her with Brom leaning into his rope on the up-slope side. Wherever a gully came down the face it had laid a tongue of loose plates right across the shelf, and on those she floated. There he walked against the strap with his whole body slanted, like a man in a gale.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:241:Lira went out for the twelfth, and she did not come back at a run.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:243:He saw her stop, sixty yards off. She stood quite still. Then she set no stone on its end; she turned, and walked back toward them along the inside of the shelf, slowly, close under the wall, and her feet made no sound on the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:245:She did not speak when she reached them. She took Cael by the sleeve and drew him three steps out from under the buttress, and pointed with her chin, up and to the west.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:249:A quarter of a mile along, and far up the slope, another shelf ran across the face. It was narrower than theirs. Along it, for the length of a long field, the grey plates of the hillside lay in a row, humped and overlapping, each heap about the size of the cart; and there was a gap of bare rock between each heap and the next, and all the gaps were the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:251:He counted the heaps, since he could no more have helped it than breathing, and he had reached twenty-two when one of them lifted its head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:1:# Chapter 25 — Tenth
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:3:Pike wrote the wall out again that evening, from the top, on a clean length of sailcloth, and Lira counted the lines as they went up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:The room was warmer than Cael remembered it, and emptier. The cold term had sent half the crews home to their own fences, and the benches down both sides had room on them; but there was a fire in the great hearth that a man could have stood in, and it threw its light across the plaster where the old sheet had hung since the sixtieth day. A boy held the new cloth taut with both palms. The one hand moved over it, steadily, and went to the horn pot on the bench and came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:7:*Teague.* It stood by itself on the first line, as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:9:"Two," said Lira. "Three."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:11:She was not sitting. She stood in the middle of the floor with her arms folded and her weight on her own boot, and her lips moved at each name. The five of them had the boards nearest the door, where they had sat on the forty-ninth night, and on the boards between Brom's elbows lay a sheet of cream paper with a lamp beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:13:"Eight. Nine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:15:The pen went to the pot. Pike wrote five names on one line, in the order in which they had been spoken at a plank, and the boy shifted his hands down the cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:17:"Tenth," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:19:She said it quite quietly. Two lines that had stood over theirs since the day they came now stood under them, and the shingle pair were at the foot still. Cael looked for what the rung was made of, since he had learned in this room that it was always made of something. It was a mountain, less a cart. The wall had no place to say so. It said only that a hold which asked Pike who ought to walk a bad hill would now be given five names before it was given two others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:21:"We lost a cart and went up two," said Brom slowly. He had the jug, and he had not yet poured. "I'd not have reckoned it so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:23:"You'd have reckoned the cart," said Karis. "He reckoned the map as well. It's the same sum; you stopped halfway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:25:"Which two have we gone over?" Lira had come back to the boards at last. "I can't tell from here. Half of them are trades."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:27:"Then go and read it," said Pike, from the hearth, without turning his head. "I don't call out who's gone down. I write who's where."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:29:She went and read it, with her nose a hand's breadth from the wet ink, and came back looking thoughtful. One of the two was a crew of four who had brought a string of mules through the cold road's pass and lost none of them. She had seen them in the meadow, she said, and they were none of them young, and one had a wooden foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:31:"So that's what tenth is," she said. "It's just in front of a man with a wooden foot who never lost a mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:33:"I'd not sneer at him," said Brom. "I've lost a cart."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:35:"I wasn't sneering. I was working out how good you'd have to be to get past him by two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:37:Pike had sat down at last, on the hearth bench, under his own handwriting, with the ledger open beside him and a cup that somebody had put into his hand. Cael took his own cup and went over, as he had gone over once before to ask about a ledger-keeper in Ardenmere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:39:He had a question, and he had not yet found the words for it. It concerned the name at the head of the new sheet. A man had come in the night before, Pike had said at the plank, with news of a cart in a gorge; and for the whole of that man's time at the oak a sheet of cream paper had been lying under a river stone, where anybody who read a plank from end to end must come to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:41:Pike drank. He looked at the fire, and then across the room at the lamp on the boards by the door, and then down at the leaf beside him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:43:"Teague's nine up," Pike said, to the book, asked by nobody. Then, still to the book, at the same size, as if it were the next line and not a different thing: "He read that slip yesterday. Read it twice, same as the boy did. Put it back under the stone and took the fence. I'd have done the same, and I told him, and he said he knew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:47:He had read that man's feet in the silt of a ford, and his halts at five halting-grounds after it. He had watched him count three people on a sill before he looked at a room, and had been told by him on a river bank, in a level voice, what a mountain was worth. Now he had the man's answer to a hundred marks as well, at second hand, from a book-keeper who gave out nothing that he had not seen with his own eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:49:It was a reason against. He took it as one, with the name of the man who had given it; and he noticed that Pike had said what was done, and had not said why. He did not think that Pike had been told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:55:He did it on the eastern landing, with the scow's rail still under his hand. The spear was ashore already, and grounded. The boy had the two mules by one rein and was looking up the bank at the lower inn's chimney, where there would be something in a pot. The bow came off last, and stepped down onto the planks with the right foot, and stood on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:61:*HOLD.* It was in the square capitals, and it was not his. There were five names under it, and five again under the same word on the shingle beside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:63:He stood in front of them for as long as it took to be sure what he felt. He had asked for that word once, on a ford, and had it struck with one line while he was on a ledge with a string of salt; and he had never grudged the striking, since a hold cannot wait on a captain's convenience. Now two holds had come down to a ferry and asked a one-armed man who they ought to have. That was the board doing its work. If he minded it, he would have to mind arithmetic.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:65:He went on along the oak. There was nothing else on it that weighed, and he was turning away when he saw the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:67:Pike's stone lay on the book's corner, where it had lain for as long as Teague had been coming to that plank; and under its far edge there was the corner of a sheet of paper. A line of dust lay across the corner, fine and pale, drawn along the stone's rim. He had been four weeks up the north water, and that sheet had plainly lain there for most of them. A waybill does not gather dust under a stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:73:"Paper," he said. "It's been said. You weren't here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:75:"Then I'll read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:77:He was given it, as he had been given a struck slip on the fifty-seventh day. It was good paper, heavier than anything the holds wrote on. He read it from the first word to the seal, and then he began again at the top, more slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:79:He priced it as he would have priced a stage, a thing at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:81:The price came first, and it was a hundred, which was too much. A surveyor's kit was a thing that one man carried on a strap; he had seen such a kit once, years ago and a long way west. It was two days out to that rise and two home, on a road he knew, and nobody paid a hundred marks for four days and a case. But *too much* was only a reason if a man could say what it was too much for, and he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:83:The hirer was the broker, by the wax. Teague had carried that man's bales. He paid to the day, and argued every copper beforehand and none after; and he had never in his life sent a crew farther than his own landing. That was odd, and it was not wrong; a broker posts for whoever puts money in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:85:The ground he knew. He had been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and had looked across the grass each time at a row of grey stumps and kept walking. Every carter's child knew what was said of it. He had never put it to the proof, and he did not need a paper to tell him why not: he had three behind him, and in that place the spear would be a man with a spear, and the bow a man with a bad hip, and the boy a boy. He would not take them in for a hundred, or for twice a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:87:That was a reason not to take it. It was a good one, and it was his, and it would have done for any captain on the sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:89:But it was not what had stopped him at the second reading. Something in the paper itself was wrong, as a load is wrong on a mule a mile before the girth shows it. He went round it twice more, looking for the place. The witness was not named, and there was nobody at the far end to say that the things had ever lain there. The words were a clerk's. None of that was a reason either. He had taken worse paper than this from better men, and brought it home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:91:He could not find it. He knew only that he would not have liked to see one of his own carrying that sheet up the east road, and that he could not have told them why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:93:He laid it back under the stone himself. He set the corner on its own line of dust, as nearly as he could, and took his hand away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:95:"That's what I'd have done with it," said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:97:"I know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:99:It was all either of them said about it. There was a fence on the oak, four slips along: the axe-head hold's longer fence on the east road, forty marks, HOLD. Teague drew its tack and held it out, and Pike wrote his name to it before he shut the book and tucked it under what was left of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:101:Teague stood a breath longer in front of two slips with five names on each.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:103:They would be at the ferry tomorrow. He had kept a day ahead of them from Oxhollow without trying to, and he knew their pace, with a taped boy and a girl on one boot. The boy would read this plank as he had read a doorway, from one end to the other, leaving nothing out. He would find the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:105:Teague had said four things to him on a river bank, and every one of them had been counted before it was spoken. This was not a thing that could be counted. A captain did not walk up to another crew and hand it a feeling, as though a feeling were a figure; if he had, he would have been asked what it rested on, and rightly, and he would have had no answer but the hair on his own neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:107:So he said nothing, and it sat poorly with him. He went up the bank to find the boy, and whatever was in the pot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:111:They held the council once, at their own boards, with the lamp turned up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:113:Cael had set the cream sheet in the middle of the table, where all five could reach it, and he gave the order as he would have given it for any contract: each of them to speak, and nobody to answer until the last had done. Brom poured. He had allowed one jug, on the strength of a line said at a plank, and had made it plain that the paper under the lamp had not yet earned a second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:115:"Karis," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:117:She had her notebook shut under both hands, and she did not open it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:121:She nodded at the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:123:"He says that there is a place on a road where an Arbiter stops. He says it stops at a line, and that a person knows the line by her own. If that is so, then it can be found with nothing but feet, by anybody, and found again in the same place the next day by somebody else. I have been taught that an Arbiter cannot be tested at all; that it is the one thing every instrument we own takes for granted. I should like to stand where one fails." She drew a breath. "I would go for nothing. I say so in order that nobody should count my hand as a vote for the hundred."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:125:Cael had known her for two years, and in all that time he had heard her ask for a room, and for silence, and once for an hour more with a restricted shelf. He had never before heard her say what she wanted in front of four people and then decline to argue for it. She sat a little flushed, and looked at nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:127:"Brom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:129:Brom had turned the paper toward himself with one finger, and he had been doing a sum. Cael knew the look of it. He had worn that look over a wash and a fan and a cut axle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:131:"A survey kit's a cart's worth. Brass. A level, a chain, the rule. Nobody puts a cart's worth down and walks off from it unless they walked off fast." He looked at the jug. "That's not the reason. That's what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:133:He pushed the paper back to the middle of the table and picked up his cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:135:"Lira."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:137:She had sat down at last, sideways on the bench, with the great boot stuck out into the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:139:"I've walked the east road four times and I know every stone of it as far as that fence. Past the fence it's two days more, on ruts that the grain carts use, to a hill. Then it's whatever a surveyor leaves behind him, picked up, and brought back the same way." She looked round at them. "I'm saying it's a walk and a carry. I'm not saying it isn't the other thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:141:"The dogs live in them," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:143:"I heard him. I've been bitten by one; I've a foot to prove it." She shrugged, with some effort. "That's mine. A walk and a carry, and my eyes open."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:145:They were looking at him now. He was the nearest thing that they had to a captain, and it was his to say last but one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:147:He had a good many reasons. He could have said what he had said to Lira under the eave, that he wished to see it, and it would have been true. He could have said that a hold had asked for them by name, and that a crew which was asked for ought to find out what it was made of. There was a rope with no figure against it. There was a cart in a gorge, and a line in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:149:All of those were his, and he saw that each of them was a way of dressing the plain thing so that it should look better on him. The plain thing was that five people had a hundred and eight marks and the cold coming, and that this paid as much again for four days of road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:151:"It pays."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:153:Lira's eyes rested on him a breath longer than the words needed, and went back to the lamp. She did not say what else she knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:155:"Seln."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:163:"I have listened to four cases that turn upon what this says," he said. "I should like to add what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:165:He laid it down, and touched the left-hand edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:167:"There is a margin. It has been pricked, here and here, at even distances, with a wheel; that is done so that every sheet from a desk is ruled alike. No hold has such a wheel. The broker's clerk writes on whatever comes to hand." His finger moved to the head of the sheet. "*Recovery.* One word, set by itself, with a stop after it. That is how a class of business is named at the top of a paper that will afterward be put away with others of its class. A carter would have begun, *I have lost.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:171:"And the sum. It is written in words, and then again, in a bracket. A person is taught to do that who has been warned that a figure may be altered and a word may not. I was so taught, at a ruled desk, when I was not a great deal older than he is." The mild eyes came up. "The place is given as a description. A day, and a rise, and a road that bends. Every carter in that meadow has a name for it, and our friend at the plank used the name at once. Whoever wrote this either did not know it or was not permitted to put it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:177:"Inside the Line," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:179:"Inside the Line, at a desk, from a book of forms. I do not say whose desk. I say that it was not composed on that landing. It was copied there, or else it came over the water as it stands, with the money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:181:The only sound was the fire. Cael looked at a cream sheet that had lain a month under a river stone, and saw it as he had not seen it at the plank: a thing with a ruled margin, lying on an inn's boards in a country that kept no paper. He had walked east for ninety days, and here was a piece of the west on his own table, with a seal on it that told him nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:183:"It goes in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:187:"Is that you telling us to leave it?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:189:"That's not a reason not to go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:191:He gave them no more than that, and he sat back out of the lamplight, like a man who has handed in a report and does not mean to argue it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:193:Karis had opened the notebook after all. She was counting on the edge of a leaf, in small strokes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:195:"If we mark it in the morning, that is the ninety-first," she said. "The Oxhollow cart rests a day and goes east on the ninety-second, with our boxes, and we in front of it. That brings us to the fence on the afternoon of the ninety-third."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:197:She stopped, and Cael saw the stroke of her pencil stop with her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:199:Nobody at the table needed the sum done twice. A square woman had stood in a gateway on the eighty-fourth day, with a mule's cheek-strap in her hand, and had said nine days. The road to the Fallow Ring went through Thornwater, and by no arrangement of theirs it went through on the day that she had named.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:201:Brom was looking at his left wrist. Lira had her eyes on the great boot, and on what was taped inside it; she had asked for that ankle once, and been told what it was worth and to whom it was owed. Seln studied his cup. And Cael had stood by a well with a sentence ready, six days ago, and had found that he had no right to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:203:Not one of the five said that a healer might be brought. He watched four people not say it, each for a reason of his own, and he was prouder of them than he could have told them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:205:Karis wrote. She turned the leaf so that the lamp fell on it, and they could all read what she had put.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:209:"Hands," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:211:There were five. He drew the cream sheet toward him, and folded it once along its old fold, and put it inside Hesk's book for the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:215:*Ninetieth day. Lowmarch. The seventh line is said: seventy in the purse, sixty-four on the wall. Tenth. The smith is paid, and we have a hundred and eight. A rope is owed to the ferryman, with no figure. Two holds have asked for us by name, and will wait a fortnight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:217:*Twelve weeks. Seven contracts. Pike says there's ground out here where the Arbiter goes quiet, and nobody goes in twice. Karis has been waiting her whole life for a sentence like that. Seln says the contract was written by someone from inside. I say it pays. Two days east, through Thornwater, on the day the healer said. For twelve weeks nothing out here has read me. I'd like to see what a place that reads nothing does with that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:219:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:225:Pike took it without a word. He read it twice, though he could have said it in his sleep, and then he turned his shoulder to the meadow and gave it out: the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the rise, and the hundred. It was the first time in a month that those words had been heard on that grass. He said the whole of it a second time. Then he said *taken*, and five names, and dipped his pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:229:Teague was there. He stood a little way out from the eave, on the trodden grass, with his thumbs in his belt, and his three behind him at their distances: the spear, grounded, and the bow on the good leg, and the boy with a heel of bread. That made four. The Oxhollow carter had come up from his mules and stood chewing, which was five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:231:The hide-woman had her arms folded. Under the ash the old carter had lifted his chin at the first word and was holding it there. Two men in tarred aprons had stopped with a bundle of shingles between them; and the boy who took the standing-money stood on one leg beside a tent-rope, with his bag forgotten in his hand. That was ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:233:The eleventh was at Pike's elbow. He was a thin young man with ink on his cuffs and a ledger under his arm, and he had come up from the landing at a trot when he was sent for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:235:"You're the witness for the hirer," said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:237:"I am told so." The clerk looked at the five as if they had been bales of a kind that he had not handled before. "My master had it by the last sack, sealed, with the sum counted in a bag and a line of direction. He was to post it and to lodge the price. Whatever is brought down, I am to see it at the landing and put our mark to the slip, and it goes west by the next boat." He swallowed. "I don't know what the things are. I was not told who wants them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:239:"Nobody's asked you," said Pike, and wrote five short strokes at the foot of the cream paper, side by side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:241:"What if they're not there?" said Lira. "The things. Somebody else might have picked them up. It's been lying under your stone a month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:243:"Then you come down and tell me so, and I say that." Pike was blowing on the strokes to dry them. "I write what was done. There's a hundred in my box against his mark on this paper, and if his mark never goes on it, the box stays shut. I've nothing in the book that pays a crew for walking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:247:"You could. You've read my lines. Every one of them says what came back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:249:It was done in the time that it takes to lace a boot. Cael waited for something to come from the trodden grass, and nothing came. The man with the leather on his wrist had watched the pen make its strokes. He did not nod, by so much as the width of a finger, as he had nodded on a river bank; he stood with his eyes on the paper until it was folded, and then they moved to Cael, and rested there, and told him nothing that he could have written down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:253:"The two holds," he said to Pike. "With our names. We'll be nine days on this, if it goes as it's written. If we're not down in their fortnight, don't keep a bull waiting on us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:255:"I'd not have. I told them a fortnight, and after that the top of the sheet." Pike blotted the strokes. "It's good of you to say it where he can hear."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:257:He had not said it for that. But he saw, from the corner of his eye, that the boy with the bread had stopped chewing, and was looking at his captain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:1:# Chapter 19 — The Loud Thing
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:3:There was frost on the bay at first light, and Brom wanted the cart across it before the sun came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:5:"It's stuck," he said, low, with his boot on the shale. The plates did not creep under him as they had the evening before; they held, with a small dry crackle. "The cold's glued it. She'll roll on this for an hour, and by noon she'll float on every yard of it. I'd as soon have her on the far side waiting for the word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:7:So they had broken their camp in the grey, without a fire, and drawn her out from Seln's corner onto the open floor. Seln had the near shaft and Cael the off. Brom walked against his rope on the left, toward the hill, and Karis went at the off wheel with her cord looped over her shoulder and the long leaf rolled in her coat. The narrow wheels cut two clean lines in the rime. The hub ticked, and the far wall gave it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:9:Lira had gone on ahead. She had not eaten. She had wound the linen round her right ankle by the first light there was, and tried her weight on it, and walked away along the foot of the wall with her arms swinging loose. By the time the cart was a hundred yards out she was a small dark figure at the place where the bay shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:11:He watched her stop there and set her feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:13:He did not count. He had the book open against the shaft, with the leaf turned to the sixty-third line, and he had written the number and nothing after it. Above them the slope was in shadow still. Thirty grey heaps lay along the upper shelf with white on their backs, and not one of them had yet begun to scrape.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:15:It was the last morning of the job, and he let himself know it. Three lines remained of a table that had sixty-two. After the ledge there was a shoulder, and a hillside going down into a brown valley, and a man at a ford who would count seventy marks into Karis's purse. He had walked a mountain for seven days with nine things shut in him, writing down what a cord and a clock and a girl's feet could tell him, and not one line of it had wanted anything that he was. He thought that he would be sorry to come down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:17:"It is very quiet," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:19:"That's the frost," said Brom. "They'll not eat till the sun's on the moss. Nor would I."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:25:"She's off," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:27:She was. She went out along the ledge under its roof of rock, close in, and from that distance there was nothing to show that one foot came down later than the other; she looked like a thing that the mountain had been keeping that shelf for. He made himself look at the fin. It stood ahead of them and a little to the right, pale in the half light, with the frozen shale banked along its upper side, and he judged that they were thirty yards short of its nearer end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:29:"Stand here," said Brom. "This'll do. There's slab under this bit; I can feel it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:31:They set the shafts down. Karis took the cord from her shoulder and began to free the first knot, since the ledge would want measuring as soon as it had been run. Seln straightened his back with both hands. It was very still. The stream was only a breath of sound from far below, and the cold had laid a quiet on the hill that Cael had not heard there on any other morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:33:He looked west again in time to see the ledge go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:39:A length of the shelf simply tilted outward, thirty yards beyond the place where Lira was running, as a table-leaf drops when its bracket is drawn. Then the piece nearer to her did the same. She was on the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:43:It was Wind, and it was hers, and it was the whole of it. He had not heard that sound from her since a painted floor at Norhold, and she had never, there or anywhere, let it go so hard. She was not on the falling stone. She was twenty feet east of it, in the air, and then she was standing on sound ledge with both arms flung wide. Behind her a hundred feet of shelf was going down into the blue with a roar that grew as it fell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:45:Wind stood up in his own feet to answer hers. He put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:47:Her declaration crossed the gorge. Its crack went with it. He had time to see Lira turn her head toward the mountain, and then the far wall gave it back, late, as clear as a whip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:49:Thirty heads came up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:53:"Karis," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:55:"I see them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:57:They went. He had watched them for five days and had never seen one of them hurry, and they went along that shelf now like barrels loosed down a ramp, all thirty, shoulder behind shoulder, with the calves between; and the clatter of their plates reached him across three hundred yards of slope like hail on a slate roof. They were running from her. They were running east, along a lip of rock that had dropped a slab under one frightened animal at a walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:59:It held them for as long as he could have counted four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:61:Then the shelf began to come away in front of the leaders. He saw the edge of it sag, and a grey back lurch outward, and after that there was no shelf and no line, only the whole upper face of the bay beginning to move. The animals had turned downhill, since downhill was the only way that the ground was going. They came in a front, eighty yards wide and three or four deep, and the mountain's loose skin came with them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:63:He did the sum as he would have done it on any floor. It was three hundred yards of roof, and they were carts with legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:67:"There. Thirty seconds. Lee side."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:69:Nobody asked him what he meant. Seln had Karis by the elbow before the second word and was running her over the rimed shale as one runs a child across a road. Brom ripped the rope over his head. Cael ran, and for a wonder the leg ran with him; the ground was level and frozen, and he had twenty strides in him before it thought to complain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:71:They came round the eastern end of the rock, where it was no higher than his belt, and into the black strip behind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:73:It was just as Seln had said. The wall stood over them, twice Brom's height in the middle, and at its foot there was bare clean floor, twelve feet deep. On the far side of it the shale lay banked to a man's chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:77:"It will stop the stone," she said. "That's what it's stopped before. It won't stop them. That bank's a ramp, and there are thirty of them, and they'll come straight over the top of this and down on us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:79:"Can you turn them?" said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:81:She did not answer at once. He saw her eyes go to her coat, where a leaf was, and come back to the slope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:83:"Derived," she said. "I've nothing else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:89:"Cart's gone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:91:He said it as he would have told somebody that a horse was lame. Then he ran back for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:95:He went like a bull at a gate, and Karis made a small sound and did not call him back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:97:She had no breath to spare for it. She had both hands lifted a little in front of her, the palms with their pink lines turned toward the hill, and she had begun to do the thing that Ternhall had taught her before she was fifteen. *Points first, always. Joins last.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:99:Cael could not see them. Nobody could; a set point was a promise and gave no light. But he had watched her lay them for two years, in a whitewashed room and on a district floor, and each one had always cost her the same small dip of the right hand, as a person dots a page. He counted the dips, and followed her eyes. The first went down eleven paces up the slope, straight above the middle of the wall; the second eight paces from that, down and to the left; and then the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:103:Thirty yards off, Brom let the tail-board fall. It banged, and nobody was there to stop it. He dragged out the left-hand box and got it onto his shoulder, and the middle one after it onto the other. Then he turned with a hundredweight on either side of his head and came back over the cracking shale at a trot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:107:Cael had known it as he ran, and had kept it out of his mind until the others were behind the rock. The third was the right-hand one. It had a third of everything that Karis had ever copied in it, and at the bottom, under the masonry chips, a canvas bag with five thumbs in the wax.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:109:"Twenty," said Seln quietly. He was counting as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:111:Cael went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:113:He heard Seln say his name, once, and then he was round the end of the wall and out on the open floor with the mountain coming down on his right hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:115:He gave it no more than a breath's looking, and that was enough. The front had come a third of the way, and it was no longer grey; it had a brown smoke of dust standing over it, and out of the smoke came the sound, a deep steady grinding with the hail of the plates on top. He passed Brom going the other way, and Brom's eyes went to him and to the cart and he did not waste his wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:117:The cloth at Brom's left wrist had gone dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:119:The box was heavy and its beckets were stiff with frost. He got it to the edge of the bed and his right shoulder under it, as he had seen it done, and straightened. The cart moved beneath his hand as he let go of her, like a boat that has been pushed off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:121:He ran with it. It was thirty yards, and he had a count of ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:123:All nine of them were up. He had never felt them so, on the stretch or at the ford; there the offers had come one behind another, and here they came in a single shout. Wind wanted his feet and had the whole distance measured. Storm showed him the air over the bay, torn already, as a lane. He put them down as he ran, without words, since there was no time for words, and they got up again at every stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:125:The slope lit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:127:He saw it at the edge of his left eye and could not help turning his head. Across the face of the mountain, above the pale rock, a line of fire had stood up out of the shale. It ran from a point high over the wall's middle down and outward on either hand, straight as two rules, yellow-white, the height of a man's knee; and it was sixty yards from end to end. She had joined them. Nine points and eight channels, no join longer than eight paces, laid on frozen stone in the time it takes to lace a boot, and every one of them burning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:129:He felt her discharge go over him like the door of a furnace opening. He had stood near that Path in archives and annexes for two years. It had never been a quarter of this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:131:The front met it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:133:He could see the animals now, the foremost, with their heads down and their plates flying; and he saw them feel it. The middle of the line broke. The ones dead above the arrowhead's point flung themselves left and right as if the ground there had been snatched away, and the ones beside them went with them, and in the space of two breaths a front eighty yards wide had torn down the middle into two streams. Eleven paces above the rock there was nothing coming at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:135:A bird over the bay would have seen it plainly: the pale rock lying east and west across the floor, and above it an arrowhead of fire, point up the hill, with one foot burning a little beyond each end of the rock. The herd had broken on that point into two streams, one pouring down outside the eastern foot and one outside the western, and everything between the two feet was in shelter. Cael was coming from the east with a box, so the eastern stream was the one that could reach him; Lira was coming off the ledge from the west, and the western was hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:137:He was eight yards short of the wall's end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:139:He was outside her line. Its eastern foot stood a little ahead of him and uphill, burning, and the eastern stream was pouring down past the end of it, and he was in the edge of where that stream was going to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:143:Compression had the thing measured already. It had waited since a river for a blow worth taking, and it showed him how: a plated shoulder at a run, met square, and sent down through his frame into the slab beneath the shale. He would have stood. Anchor offered him the other way. *There.* One fixed point against the rock under his left boot, and told to stay, and a boy with a box would have been a post that a river goes round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:147:It would have gone off in front of animals that were running from one already. They were pouring away from her fire, down and east; if a second loud thing stood up in their road, the leaders would check, and the rest would pile on them from behind, and a stream that is dammed goes over its bank. Its bank was sixty yards of fire held by one small person who had never held a third of it. They would come back through her line and over the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:149:The whole split stood on Karis. One more loud thing would turn it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:151:*No*, he told the pair of them, and he gave it his left side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:153:He had learned that from Lira on a bar of gravel: the side that is not wanted afterward. He turned the box away, and the innermost animal of the stream came by him at the length of an arm. It did not so much as see him. Its shoulder caught him below the left armpit as a cart's tail catches a man in a lane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:157:Then he was in the air, going the way he had chosen, with both arms still round the box. He came down on his left leg in the shale at the foot of her fire, and slid, and the edge of a plate opened his calf along the old line as a thumb opens a pod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:159:There were hands in his coat. They were Seln's, and then Brom's, and he was hauled over the low end of the rock on his back, with the box on his chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:161:He lay where they put him and counted, since it was the one thing he could do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:163:She was above him, at the wall's end, on her feet. Her two hands were out in front of her and they shook as a rope shakes under a load. Her face was as white as paper and quite calm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:165:*Five*, he counted. *Six.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:167:The noise was on both sides of them, a river in flood going by either end of a pier. Plates were coming over the top of the wall in the middle and bursting on the floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:171:At the eighth second Karis sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:173:She did it all at once, like a puppet whose strings have been let go, with her back against the rock and her hands open in her lap. The light went off the mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:177:It did not matter. The last of them were by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:179:He got himself onto his right elbow, with a care that he had never before had to take over breathing, and looked out past the low end of the wall to the east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:181:The stream had spread as it reached the level. He saw the backs of perhaps a dozen animals, wheeling on the bay's floor in a fog of dust, with the shale still running between their legs like water; and beyond them, alone, in the place where five people had stood a minute ago, the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:185:Brom had named it at the foot of a fan, and Cael had heard the word a dozen times since, and he had not understood it until now. The whole apron under her had let go. It was moving toward the edge in one piece, without haste, as ice goes out on a river, and she rode on it with her wheels buried to the hubs and her canvas still roped. She turned once, slowly, as a leaf turns in an eddy. Her shafts swung round and lifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:189:He did not hear her land. He heard the slide going over after her, tons of it, in a long hissing fall; and after that, at intervals, three or four heavier sounds, dull and separate, that were not stone and not a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:191:The far wall gave all of it back, late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:193:Nothing in the hollow moved. Dust came down on them out of the brown air as flour comes down in a mill, and settled on the boxes, and on Karis's open hands. Above the wall the face of the bay was a different mountain. The roof of plates had gone from a strip eighty yards wide, scoured to the pale slab beneath, and along both edges of that strip the stone was sliding still, a plate at a time, with a sound like somebody clearing a table in another room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:195:Out on the level the animals had stopped. They stood about in twos and threes among the wreck of their own hill, with their sides heaving and their heads low; and already, he saw, they had begun to shuffle a little apart from each other, feeling for a length and a half.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:201:"I couldn't see," he said. "I heard something go. I wouldn't swear to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:203:"Heard." Her lips shaped the rest. "Not sworn. Write that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:205:"When I can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:209:"Carried," he said, to nobody. "Both of them. It was the lifting them out did it. I pulled."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:211:Seln was on his feet and whole, with grey dust in his hair. He had gone to the western end of the rock and was standing there very still, looking out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:213:Cael counted the hollow. It was a habit, and it was all that he had. There was a large man, kneeling. There was a small one, sitting. There was a mild one, at the end of the wall. There were three boxes, left and middle and right, and one of them was lying on his own legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:217:"Lira," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:219:Seln had not turned his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:221:"Here," he said. "She is here. Come when you can, and not before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:223:He could, after a fashion. He got his back against the stone and worked along it, a foot at a time, with his left arm clamped to his side and his left leg leaving a mark on the clean floor. The middle of the wall went by over his head, tall and pale. It took him as long as it had taken thirty animals to come down a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:225:She was three strides beyond the western end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:227:She had almost reached it. She lay on her front in the grey spill of the other stream, with one arm stretched toward the rock and her cheek on the shale, and from the knees down he could not see her. A plate of the mountain lay across her legs. It was as long as a door and as thick as his wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:229:Her eyes were open, and they were looking at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:233:She had liked the ledge from the moment she saw it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:235:She would not have told the boy so. He would have written it down. But she had stood at the end of that bay in the evening light with her arms folded, looking at three hundred yards of clean level rock, and something in her had sat up, as it used to sit up at the sight of a chalked lane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:237:It was not the same thing, and she knew that. Nobody was going to call a time. There was no slate, and there was no girl in the next lane to be beaten by a tenth. She would run to the far end of a shelf and come back and say *slab, dry, six feet*, and a boy with a pencil would write it in a column. A carter she would never meet would put a wheel there or not, on her word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:241:That had surprised her more than the mountain. For a week after a fire above a wash she had gone about with a question in her like a stone in a boot, the one she had put to four people and nobody could answer. She had not got an answer. She had got a job. Each morning there was a hundred yards of ground that nobody alive had tested, and she was the one who went onto it first, at speed, to learn whether it would bear; and when she walked back and said that it would, four people and three boxes moved forward onto the place where her feet had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:245:So she bound the ankle in the dark, on the seventy-eighth morning, and ate nothing, as she had eaten nothing before a card. The foot was what it was. It bent less than the other and later, and she had stopped being angry with it somewhere on the bench and begun simply to allow for it, as one allows for a wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:247:She set her feet at the ledge's mouth, and went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:249:The rock was a gift. It was dry under its roof, and rimed along the outer half where the roof stopped, so she kept in, close under the wall, as she had promised a fire that she would. Her left foot struck and her right came after it. She had the count in her legs. Forty. Fifty. The gorge went by on her right hand, blue and enormous, and she gave it none of her attention; she was reading the shelf five yards ahead of her toes, for cracks and for colour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:251:She saw no crack. She would say so afterward to anyone who asked, and nobody ever did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:253:At eighty the wall on her left hand moved away from her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:255:That was how it seemed. She had been running a hand's breadth from it, and between one stride and the next there was a gap there the width of her arm, with daylight in it. The shelf under her had come off the mountain in one piece and was leaning out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:259:She had been told at Fenmark that a burst was a choice, and at Norhold she had made a rank of choosing well. For sixty days on gravel bars and roads she had refused it a stride at a time, on purpose, and she had been proud of how good she had got. Not one of those refusals was anywhere near her now. The ground went, and her body did what a body with Wind in it does when the ground goes, and it did not send up to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:261:The air broke round her. She felt it in her teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:263:Then there was rock under both feet, and she was standing on it, twenty feet back, with her arms out and her heart going like a mill. Behind her the ledge roared down into the gorge. She had not chosen where to come down, either. Her feet had found sound stone under the roof by themselves, and stopped dead on it, as she had spent a month on a river bar teaching them to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:267:Then the far wall answered her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:269:It was her own burst, coming home. It crossed three hundred yards of empty air and came back from the cliff late and clean, and it did not sound like a thing she had done. It sounded like a whip in somebody else's hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:271:She understood it then, in the pit of her stomach, before she had turned her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:273:There was a page with a comma struck out of it. She had been lying on a slab when it was written. *It will answer a declaration in the same way.* She had asked for a test, a seed, half a breath; and a small pale person had looked up a hill and declined.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:275:She looked up the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:277:Thirty heads were lifted, all of them, turned away from her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:281:She ran.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:283:There was nothing else to do with what she had done. The cart was a toy on the white floor of the bay, with four specks beside it, and one of the specks had its arm out. She could not hear what it said. She did not need to, for the arm was pointing at the long pale rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:285:It was sixty yards of ledge to the bay and forty more of shale to the wall's near end, and she had run farther than that to catch a dog.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:289:It said it at the second stride, quite reasonably. It had just saved her life and it could not see why it was not wanted again. Twenty feet at a time, five times, and she would be behind that stone before the first of them was halfway down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:291:*No.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:293:She knew what a second one was. She had sat in a loft and heard it laid out with a charred stick. *Never two at once.* A burst would crack over that bay like the first, and thirty animals that were running from one loud thing would have another to run from, and it would be going off beside the only shelter on the mountain. She had been the loud thing once this morning without being asked. She would not be it a second time by choice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:295:She refused it at the third stride and the fourth. It was harder than it had been at the ford, where she had only been afraid of a dog.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:297:The right foot came down late. She allowed for it. The ledge poured back under her, and the hill on her left was no longer a hill; it was brown, and moving, and the sound of it came down ahead of it and pressed on her ears. She came off the rock onto the shale and it broke under her like the crust on snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:301:A small figure stood at the wall's far end with its hands out. She saw it across the whole length of the pale rock, no bigger than a doll, and she knew whose hands they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:303:The mountain caught fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:305:She had watched those points go down in barns and annexes and on the end wall of a whitewashed room, a dozen at a time, each no bigger than a barley grain. She had seen them joined in threes, and once, at a sitting, sixteen. She had never seen a road. It stood up across the whole face above the rock, two straight burning lines that met in a point high on the slope, as wide as a street and as long as a field; and the animals coming down on it split like a log under a wedge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:311:She was outside the line. She saw that with great clearness. Ten yards more would put her under the rock, and she did not have ten yards; she had perhaps six, and a foot that was a beat behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:315:She told it no, and ran the six yards on her feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:317:Something the size of a cart went behind her so close that the wind of it turned her half round. She kept her legs. She was three strides from the stone, with her arm already out for it, when the shale that the thing had brought down with it took her ankles from under her like a wave on a beach, and she went onto her face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:319:She felt the plate come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:321:It was not quick. It slid up over her calves on the moving stone as a lid slides onto a box, and settled, and the weight of the hill behind it leaned. Her left leg was in a hollow and it only pinned. Her right was lying across something hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:325:After that she lay with her cheek on the mountain and watched the stream go by. It took a long time and no time. Grey backs passed at the level of her eyes, and stones ran over her outstretched hand without hurting it, and none of it seemed to have much to do with her. The fire went out. She noticed that, and thought, *She's done*, and was sorry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:327:Then it was quiet, and the gorge was giving something back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:329:She could not have said how long it was before the boy came. He came along the lower side of the rock, on his feet and hardly so, with one arm shut against his ribs and his left leg dragging; and he was the colour of the dust. He got down beside her by letting the wall take him. His eyes went over her a piece at a time, as they always did. They reached the plate, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:331:She had one thing to say, and this time she did not pick it with any care.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:333:"That was me," said Lira. Her mouth was full of grit. "The loud thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:335:He did not tell her that it was not. She watched him not tell her. He had never once made her a present of a thing that was untrue, at a fire or anywhere, and she saw him go through what had happened in its order, as he would have gone through a bout.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:337:"It was the ledge. The ledge went. Then it was you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:339:She turned that over, with her face on the stone. It had the ledge in it, first, where the ledge belonged. It had her in it second, and it did not leave her out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:341:"Then it was me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:1:# Chapter 23 — Round by the Ferry
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:5:Cael stayed where he was, on the gravel at the water's edge, with the book shut on his knee and his left arm held in against five bands of linen. Six days ago the side of a mountain had come down on five people and a cart, with a herd of frightened beasts on top of it, and all five had walked off; he had not yet got used to counting them and finding five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:7:It was the start of something, and so he took the inventory, as he took it at the start of anything. Three ribs were broken on the left side, low; they had been taped on the eighty-first day, and the tape was to come off on the ninety-ninth and not before. The calf was bound in a healer's cloth and was closing by itself, as his hurts did. Lira had a shin that was four days old, and an ankle that no healer on this road was going to mend, and one boot. Brom's left wrist wore a linen cuff, and he was to hang nothing from it until the middle of the eighty-seventh day. Karis had been told to ask nothing of herself for three days more, and she was asleep against a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:9:There were three boxes, and they stood on the bare ground by a gate-house wall. That was the item he liked least. A hundred and sixteen marks lay in Karis's purse, and six of them were a smith's. The plank where those six would be paid was six days down a road, and each box weighed as much as a grown sheep. He had one man who was allowed to carry, and four who were not fit to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:13:It was the keeper. He let himself down onto a flat stone by stages, with the notched hazel across his knees, and he looked at the river for some time before he spoke, like a man who has all his life counted what crossed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:15:"I've a cart goes out at first light," he said. "For grain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:19:"Where else is there grain? I'd meant him to go over the top, when the dry spell came, and be home in six days." The brown eyes came round. "You've told me what's over the top. So he goes round by the one-armed man's ferry, the same as his father went, and that's eight days out and eight home, and the frost won't wait on me while I think about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:21:Cael said nothing. He had learned on a hill to let a carter come to the end of his own load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:23:"He's a lad and two mules. It's sixteen days of road, and I'd as soon he had somebody walking in front of him for the first six." The keeper tipped his stick at the gate-house wall. "Your boxes'd ride. There's room on the bed going out; it's coming home that she's full."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:27:"There's none. I've no paper for it and I'm not sending to a plank for some." He scratched his jaw with the stick's end. "You walked my hill for seventy and brought me a no. I'd have paid the seventy three times over in carts, by next thaw, if you'd brought me a yes to please me. I can find a bed for three boxes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:29:So there it was. Cael went through it once, from habit, for the place where it cost something, and he could not find one. A column on a pasted leaf had ended in the wrong word, and the five of them had written it anyway, and for that a man was offering him wheels for six days. He had been paid for a great many things in his life, and most of them had been somebody's convenience. He did not think that he had ever before been done a kindness on the strength of a no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:31:"I'll ask them," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:33:"Ask them. He goes at first light whether or not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:35:He asked them in the yard, by the well, with the light gone grey. Brom looked at three boxes and then at his own left wrist, and Cael watched him weigh a road of six days against a rule of three, and put his hand up. Lira's was up already. Seln lifted two fingers from his folded arms. They had to wake Karis for hers, and she raised it before her eyes were properly open and asked afterward what she had voted for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:37:"Wheels," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:41:"His. They go round."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:45:They lay that night in the keeper's cart-shed, under a heap of empty grain sacks that smelled of last year's barley, since their own blankets were at the bottom of a gorge. It was warmer than a bay of shale had been. He lay on his right side, where the ribs hung, and listened to four people breathing, and counted nine days to a fence in a valley where a healer meant to put her hands on him again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:49:They went through the ford at first light on the eighty-fifth day, and nobody spoke until the far bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:51:The carter was a long young man with a narrow sleepy face and the largest feet that Cael had ever seen on anybody. He was the keeper's sister's son. He had two grey mules, and a cart a foot wider in the bed than the one that lay in the gorge. In the six days that followed he said perhaps forty words, and most of them were to the mules. The red oxen drew his wheels over for nothing, since the wheels were the hold's own. He walked through behind them with his breeches rolled to the thigh and his boots hung round his neck by their laces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:53:The five waded after. It was a hundred yards of gravel under a foot of quick water, cold enough to make the bones of his feet ache. The tape told him where the edge was whenever a stone turned under him. He kept his breath shallow and his eyes on the cart's tail. Seln waded a little upstream of him. He had taken up the strap of the locked case by two holes before he stepped in, so that it rode high between his shoulder-blades, well clear of the river; it had been on him on every road since their own cart was sold, and he had declined, mildly, to let it ride on another man's bed with the boxes. Three boxes sat on it under a sheet of patched canvas, left and middle and right, with the corner that was worn white turned outward. On top of them, roped down by her own instructions, sat Karis, who had been lifted up there like a sack and had made no objection.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:57:They were a day old. The dew had softened their edges, but the gravel gave way there to a strip of grey silt, and the silt had kept everything. Four people had come up out of the river in single file, each a crew's length behind the one in front, and two mules after them on one rein. He crouched, as far as five bands of linen would let him, and read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:59:The first prints were a man's, of the middle size, set down evenly. Beside the right foot, here and there, was a small round dent, where the haft of a hand-axe had swung at his thigh and touched ground on the slope. The second walker had grounded a spear at every step. The third set of marks was shorter on the left side than on the right by the width of two fingers, every stride, and no deeper for it; whoever made them had been favouring that hip for so long that the body no longer argued. The fourth were the smallest feet of the four and went in the deepest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:61:"That's the boy," said Lira, over his shoulder. She was standing on one boot and one bare foot, with her arms wrapped round herself. "He's carrying more than the others. Look how his heels go in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:65:"Somebody ought to stop him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:67:"I should think somebody does, about once a day." He stood up carefully. "They went up this bank at dusk, with the light going and a river to get over, and there isn't a scuffed print among the four of them. Nobody turned to look back. Nobody stepped out of the line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:69:He had not expected to enjoy it. But there was a pleasure in reading a thing well made, and he had it now. The difference was that he did not want to beat this one. He had no notion what he wanted from it; the man had told him to be good enough to be glad of, and Cael was reading the silt to learn what *good enough* walked like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:71:The carter had halted his mules on the bank and was waiting, with his sleepy face turned toward them. He had seen Lira's foot. He looked at it for some time, and then he went round to the cart's tail and took down a pair of boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:73:They were his spare ones. Each was as long as Cael's forearm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:77:It was the first thing that anybody had heard him say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:79:"For the one?" said Lira. "I've only the one foot wants it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:81:He considered that, and shook his head slowly. "They're a pair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:83:"I've got a left. Look at it; it's a perfectly good left. What am I to do with yours?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:85:"Keep it by."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:89:The carter thought about it with real care, and the mules stood, and the river went past.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:91:"The other one going," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:95:"Two marks," she said. "Karis."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:97:"I heard." The purse came down over the cart's side in a small pale hand. "I shall want it understood that this is the board's money and it has bought a boot and a half's worth of leather that nobody can wear. In hand, a hundred and fourteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:99:Lira packed the right one. It took the whole of a spare shirt, torn in strips and wound round her taped foot until the foot was the size of the boot. When she stood up in it she was two fingers taller on that side than on the other. She walked a few steps, and stopped, and walked a few more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:101:"It's like wearing a bucket," she said. "I can't feel the ground through it at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:103:"Can you feel where your foot is?" said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:107:"Yes. That's her tape. I'd know where it was in a barrel." She tied the left boot to the cart's tail by its laces, where it hung all the way to Lowmarch, turning slowly, like a sign over a shop. "Don't anybody tell me what it looks like."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:113:He could have borne the pain of it; he had carried pain before, on worse terms, and gone on. What he could not bear was that the thing was always right. He would reach for the water-skin, or turn his head to see what Lira had laughed at, or simply draw the breath that a hill wanted; and five bands of linen would close across his side a hair before the bone did, and tell him *there*. Every time, when he stopped to check it, the tape had known and he had not. A person can forgive an instrument for being wrong. He was learning that it was a good deal harder to forgive one for being right four thousand times a day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:115:There was no arguing with it, and he could not bear a thing that he could neither argue with nor measure, so he measured it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:117:A breath went in to a count of three before the tape spoke. On a level road he could take that breath at every fourth stride and never touch the edge. On a rise it wanted every third, and on the steepest of the valley's little hills every second, and there the breaths came so shallow and so quick that his head swam. He had always walked in fits. He had gone quick and then slow since he was a child on the lanes of Denvash, running to look at a thing and standing still to count it. He had watched a healer come down a mountain for a day and a half at one unbroken beat, and he had not been able to copy her for a hundred yards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:119:He tried it now beside the near mule, since there was nothing else to do and six days in which to do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:121:The mule did not hurry and did not stop. Its near fore came down, and he put his left foot down with it; four of those, and a breath, to the count of three; four more. Whenever he ran ahead of the beat the tape caught him, and whenever he fell behind it the cart's wheel came up at his heel. Between those two there was a place the width of a plank where neither of them touched him, and by the afternoon of the eighty-sixth day he could stay on the plank for a mile together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:123:It was a small thing to be proud of. He entered it all the same. In two years of other people's fights and eighty-six days of road, nobody had ever taught him anything that he had so little wished to learn, and it had been taught him by a strip of pitch and a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:125:Lira walked at the off wheel, on the other side of the cart, and she counted nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:127:He knew, since he could hear her. She had been told a month, and it was plain that she had taken the month as she had taken the mule, as a thing agreed to and not to be discussed. She set the great boot down on its whole sole at every step and looked at the country. There was a good deal of it to see. The road went south down a broad valley of alder and rough pasture, with the grey mountain shrinking behind their left shoulders, and twice a day it forded some small brown water where the mules drank below the crossing. She named nothing and timed nothing. Once, in the middle of the second afternoon, she said across the cart that she had never before walked anywhere slowly enough to see what was growing on a wall, and that it was mostly moss.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:129:Karis slept. She woke to eat, and to ask which day it was and write the number down, and then she folded herself up again between two boxes with Seln's coat over her and was gone. Cael watched her do it and was easier each time. On the mountain she had looked at her own hands before she looked at anything else, and by the third morning on this road she had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:131:Seln found the sleeping places, and gave no report of how.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:135:He walked behind the cart with his hands hanging, as he had walked behind another cart up a saddle, and every little while he turned his left wrist over and looked at the cuff. He ate his bread with the right hand. When the carter's off mule cast a shoe-nail in the middle of the morning and stood on three legs, it was Seln who held the hoof, and Brom stood by with both arms folded so tightly that the knuckles showed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:137:At the noon halt he cut a hazel rod from the roadside, a yard long and as straight as he could find, and pushed it upright into the turf of the halting-ground. Then he sat down on a chock-stone in front of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:139:"What's that for?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:141:"The middle of the day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:143:"You can see the middle of the day. It's up there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:145:"I can see near enough. She didn't say near enough." He had his eyes on the rod's shadow, which lay along the grass to the north, as long as his hand and growing shorter. "She said a week from the middle of the eightieth. I was there. I saw where the rock's shadow lay when she took her hands off it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:147:So they ate, and the shadow drew in. Nobody spoke of it, but Cael noticed that nobody strayed far either. The carter chewed, and looked from the rod to the big man and back, with the mild puzzlement of a person watching the customs of a foreign hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:149:It took the better part of a quarter of an hour. The shadow came in to the width of three fingers and stood there, and stood, and then, by a hair that Cael would not have sworn to, it was on the other side of the rod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:151:"That's the week," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:153:He untied the cuff with his right hand and his teeth. Under it the seam was pale and dry, from the heel of the hand to the middle of the forearm. He bent the wrist down and brought it back. Then he folded the linen in four and put it inside his shirt, on the left, where he kept a dead slip with six words on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:155:"Well?" said Lira. "Aren't you going to lift something? You've been looking at that cart for three days like a dog at a door."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:161:"They're on a bed, where they ought to be. I'd only be taking one down to put it up again." He stood, and pulled the rod out of the ground, and laid it across his knee as though to break it; then he seemed to think better of that and slid it under the cart's canvas. "She gave me a week, and I've kept it to the hour. I'll not spend the first minute of the next one showing off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:163:Cael had half risen to say something, and he sat down again. He had been on the point of telling Brom that the week was out, and that he was free of it. He saw now that it would have been like telling a man the price of something he had made himself. Nobody had kept that week but Brom. He could finish it in whatever way he pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:169:The valley had flattened and gone wet. For two hundred yards and more in front of them there was no pasture and no alder, only black water standing in sheets among yellow reed, with tussocks between as big as kneeling sheep; and across the whole of it somebody had laid a road. It was made of logs. They lay side by side, crosswise, each as thick as a man's leg and twice as long as the cart was wide, bedded on brushwood and sunk to half their depth. They ran away over the moss in a grey ribbed strip, like the backbone of something very long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:171:"How old is that?" said Karis. She had sat up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:173:The carter lifted one shoulder. "My father's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:179:Brom had gone forward onto the first of the logs. He stood on it, and on the next, and then he came to one that was darker than its neighbours and put his heel down hard, and the heel went in. He drew it out with a sucking noise. The wood under the bark was the colour of wet bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:181:"One in ten," he said. "Maybe one in six. They've gone soft from the middle, where you can't see it, and they'll bear a man. I'd not put a loaded axle on them." He looked back at the carter. "What did you weigh, coming this way last time?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:183:"Never came. He did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:187:It was the fan again, Cael thought, and he was glad to see that it was the fan made small; there was no slope in it and nothing overhead. But somebody had to learn which logs were sound before a wheel was trusted to any of them, and there was only one way that the five of them had ever done that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:191:"Walking," she said. "I'm allowed to walk. She said it'd walk in a week as well as the other, and that was eight days ago."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:193:"She said no running for a month," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:195:"I know what she said; I was under her hands when she said it. This is a flat road, at a walk, in a boot the size of a trough. Somebody mark them behind me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:197:She went out along the logs. She did not run them, and Cael, who had watched her run sixty-two hundreds of a mountain, found it stranger to see her walk these. She set the packed boot down in the middle of each log and leaned on it, and waited, with her arms a little out from her sides; and at every one that gave she said "Soft," without turning her head, and stepped over. Seln followed three logs behind with a lump of chalk that the carter kept for his tallies, and drew a cross.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:201:Then they emptied the cart. The mules were led over one at a time, picking their way between the chalk marks with their ears laid back. After them the cart itself went across with her bed bare, the carter at her head, and Seln and Cael each at a wheel to turn it by the spokes whenever the rim came up to a cross. He could do that much. It was done at the height of his hip and it asked nothing of his ribs that the tape would not allow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:205:Brom looked at them. He had stood so in front of a cart in a wash once, Cael remembered, and in front of two hundred and sixty yards of scree, working out a weight. He was not working anything out now. He walked up to the middle box, with the corner that was worn white, and bent his knees, and took hold of it by its two rope beckets, one in each hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:207:He did not swing it to his back. He stood up with it in front of him, held against his chest in both arms, as a man carries a thing through a doorway; and for a breath he only stood there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:209:His left hand had been in a sling and a cuff, by turns, since a river on the fifty-third day. That made thirty-five days. Cael watched the two wrists, as he had watched them under a cart on the eighth narrowing, and no grey started at either. There was a hundredweight of copied paper on them and they were the colour of wrists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:213:He carried it the whole way over. He came back for the left-hand one, and then for the right; and when Seln stepped forward for the third, since three crossings of that moss was a long way under a box, Brom shook his head and took that too. The carter watched him go by each time with a hundredweight in his arms and a look of deep content on his plain face, and plainly could not make out what he was seeing. Carrying boxes was only what a big man was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:215:"He's pleased about something," the carter said at last, to Cael. It was the longest speech of the six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:217:"He's been waiting eight mornings to pick that up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:219:"It's a box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:221:"Yes," said Cael. "It is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:225:They made their last camp on the evening of the eighty-ninth day, on a halting-ground at the top of a long hill, with a river shining below them in the dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:227:It was Lowmarch's river. He knew its colour, and the way it bent. They had come back to it from the west, by a bank that he had never stood on; and somewhere down there in the dark was a round thatched shed with a drum in it, and a horse walking a circle that had once drawn their cart across a province. Seln had a fire going with his flint. The carter sat at it as a matter of course, and held his enormous feet to the warmth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:229:Karis was awake. She had been awake since noon, sitting up between the boxes with her notebook on her knee, and she had the colour back in her face that six days of sleep could give it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:231:"Tomorrow is the ninetieth," she said. "The plank, and a smith. I have a slip in my coat with a horned head on it, and I want to hear what he does with a job that ended in a no." She turned a leaf. "And Thornwater on the ninety-third. That's four days, and two of them are road."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:233:"We haven't said we're going to Thornwater," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:235:"She said nine days, and he said nine days back to her. I was there; I had my eyes shut, but I was there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:237:"He didn't say we'd come. He said the number."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:241:Lira looked across the fire at Cael, and he kept his eyes on the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:243:"You've been reading that crew the whole way down," she said. "At every halt. I've watched you go round the stones."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:245:"They don't light a fire."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:249:"Five halting-grounds, and not a new ash on one of them. The chock-stones are where they found them. They eat cold, and they sleep in a row with the mules at the windward end, and in the morning there's nothing to show for it but where the grass lay down." He turned a page back to look at what he had put. "We've had a fire every night since the ford, on the strength of a finding. He's had none for longer than I've known what a hold was, on the strength of never having needed one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:251:"That's not better," said Lira. "That's only older."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:253:"I didn't say it was better. I said that I could read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:255:Brom had the carter's pot between his knees, since their own kettle was at the bottom of a gorge, and he was scouring it with a handful of grass, slowly, with his lips moving. Cael knew that look as well. He was getting two speeches ready, one for a ferryman and one for a smith, and he was putting the worse of them first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:257:Cael had the day's lines to put down, and they were short ones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:259:*Eighty-ninth day. The west bank, a morning short of the ferry. The boxes rode from Oxhollow on the keeper's grain cart, for nothing, since the map said no. In hand, a hundred and fourteen; a boot. Six owed to the smith and a rope to the ferryman.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:261:*Mine: I breathe to the tape and walk to a mule. Brom: the week kept, to the hour, and three boxes carried over a moss. Lira walks. Karis is awake.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:263:*A crew went down this road a day ahead of us and never left a mark out of its line. I read them at every halt. They'll have been at the plank tonight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:265:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:267:He blotted it. Down in the valley a single light had come out, low, by the water, and he thought that it was most likely the lower inn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:1:# Chapter 26 — White Paper
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:5:There were nine pieces, and the meadow had come up from its five fires to hear them; it stood about the eave in a loose half ring, leaning a little each time the one hand went into the canvas. The smith had one. The hide-woman had none this month, and went away with her jaw set, and a fold of bark tied with red thread was held up for the ferryman, twice, until a boy ran with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:7:"*Cael,*" said Pike. He was frowning at a sheet folded very small. "And under it, *past the Line.* I've had that direction once before." He looked over the heads, twice. "I've the one Cael."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:9:Cael went up for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:13:So he walked back down the grass with two letters, and thirty people watched him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:15:He knew the first by its writing before he had it fairly in his fingers. It was a narrow upright hand, every letter the same height, and he had read it on a yard's rota slates for two years, and once at the foot of a bout sheet. The second was a square of stiff paper ruled faintly in red, folded in four and tied with a waxed thread. It was a leaf out of a ledger, and he knew of only one person who would have cut a page from her own book in order to write to him on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:17:He had sent one letter west to her, on the fourth day, by a courier with a split lip, and it had carried no place to answer to; to the other he had sent none at all. Both of them had written into the air, as an old man in Denvash had, and the air had brought both to the one plank that there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:19:"Well?" said Lira. She had come up on his left. "Aren't you going to open them?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:21:"Not here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:23:"The river, then. You went down to the river with the last one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:25:"I had the last one read in a quarter of an hour, three times over, and then there was nothing left of it." He turned the red-ruled square in his fingers. "I've had one letter in ninety days. Now I've two, and four days of road in front of me. I'm going to have one of them at the wash tomorrow, at the noon halt. I'll keep the other for a noon with nothing in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:27:Lira stared at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:29:"You're rationing your post."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:31:"He's quite right," said Brom, with feeling. "I cut a loaf and a half into fifteen on a mountain, and it was the only thing that week that came out even. A letter's no different. You don't eat it all the day it comes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:33:"It's a letter. It doesn't go off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:35:"It does, though," said Cael. "The first reading goes off. You only get the one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:37:He put them both inside Hesk's book, between the leaves, where a cream sheet had lain the night before. It was a small thing to have decided; but he had gone a long time with nothing in front of him except what he was holding down, and he liked the weight of two unbroken seals a good deal more than he would have liked knowing what was under them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:They spent the rest of that day getting ready for a road. The Oxhollow carter had his off mule at the smith's for a shoe. Brom went over the three box-ropes a hand's breadth at a time, since they were all the rope that the five now owned. Seln was gone from the meadow between the mail and dusk, and came back with pale dust on his boots, and said nothing; and Cael, who had made a promise at that plank, looked at the dust and then at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:43:They were at the wash by noon of the ninety-second day, with the cart behind them and the saddle behind the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:45:It lay as it had lain on four other crossings, dry and red, thirty yards from lip to lip, with a ramp cut down either side and the thorn stump standing on its eastern edge. The carter took his mules through at a walk and halted them on the far flat to blow, and the five sat down among the chock-stones where they had twice made a camp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:49:"Aloud," said Lira. "If it's his. I want to hear how he sounds on paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:51:"It's mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:53:"I know whose it is. I stood on a yard with him for two years, and I'd like to know he's well, and you'll only give me the half of it afterward in your own words." She pulled her knees up. "Read it as it's written."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:55:He looked at the others. Karis had her chin on her fists, and Brom had stopped with the bread halfway out of the bag; and Seln, a little apart as always, had turned his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:63:*The Crown yard has a new cohort captain. The intake came up the bluff a fortnight ago, forty-one of them, and they've put a third-year over them who was a second-year when you left and whose name you would not know, so I'll not spend the ink. He is fair. He counts slower than you did. They stand where he puts them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:65:*Withrow's tenure holds. That is the whole of the sentence. I asked whether there was a longer one and was told there was not, in a way that meant there had been, for a week.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:67:*A query came down to the registry from somewhere above it. Bracken would not say where and I did not ask twice. It wanted former enrollees' last known direction of travel. Bracken answered it. He gave them a map reference to the Line and nothing past it — which is true, and which Bracken enjoyed being true. He told me so at the plaque wall with his hands behind his back, and then told me he had not told me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:69:*I still lose to you every time I run it. I have stopped running it in the yard.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:71:*Rooke says the yard's quieter. He didn't say it was worse. He also didn't say it wasn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:73:*— E.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:75:A small wind came down the wash and lifted the corner of the sheet, and it was some little time before any of them found a word to put after that narrow upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:77:"Good."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:81:"What is?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:83:"Bracken. A map reference to the Line and nothing past it. Somebody up a stair wanted to know which way we'd gone, and he gave them a place that everybody already knew we'd been, and wrote it out properly, I expect, with a number." She was grinning. "I never liked him. I'd like to shake his hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:87:Brom had put the bread down, and for a little he only looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:89:"He's quoted Rooke," he said at last. "That's Rooke to the life. He'd not tell you a thing was good, and he'd not tell you it was bad; he'd tell you what he'd seen, and you could carry it home and find out. *Quieter.* I'd like to know which of us he meant."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:91:"All of us, I should think," said Lira. "You broke more benches than I did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:93:Cael had read the last lines but two again, to himself. *I still lose to you every time I run it.* He could see the bout as it had gone, on sixty feet of sprung oak, with a blade's edge declared against him in earnest and nothing held back on either side; and he could see a tall, exact person walking it through afterward in an empty yard, alone, looking for the place where it turned. Something in Cael stirred at the thought, quick and bright, and asked *Edge?* of the nearest arris, which was the lip of the wash. He told it no. It was Ephram's, that question. He carried it for him, unspent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:95:"Read me the fourth again," said Seln. "The query."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:97:Cael read it. Seln heard it out with his eyes on the ground, as he listened to anything that he meant to keep, and at the end he nodded once, and asked for nothing more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:99:"Does it bear?" said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:101:"I do not know yet. When I do, I shall say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:105:He did not join them. It would have been very easy, and he had been joined to things himself on no better evidence, by people with seals. Two points could be ruled together by anybody. He would wait for a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:109:Karis had the printed sheet out that evening, for the first time since a shelf above a valley with no name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:113:"Come and look," she said. "I want it seen by more eyes than mine. I have carried this for ninety-two days and not opened it once, since I knew what was on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:115:They stood round it, and it was a handsome thing. The registry had printed it from a plate, in black, on a sheet as long as Brom's arm, and the whole of a continent was there: every district with its bound ruled round it, and every chartered road a firm double line, and the towns in three sizes of letter by their standing. Cael found Ardenmere, and a river he had crossed on a bridge, and a bluff above Ostrand with a small square on it. Somebody had written *ridge* beside a pass in the east, in pencil, and a little farther on, *Shelf; a pine; water at 36*, in the same small exact hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:117:A hand's breadth past that pencilled word, the plate ran out of things to say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:119:"There's the Line," said Karis. Her finger went down a ruled stroke that crossed the sheet from top to bottom. "And here is what they have printed east of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:121:It was a river or two, laid in without their bends, and a scatter of hills, drawn all alike; and it was seven roads. They left the Line at seven places down the length of the sheet, and each was drawn as a row of short dashes, with no name beside it; and each ran out into the white paper for the length of a thumb, or a finger, and stopped. There was no town at the end of any of them. The dashes simply came to an end, as a person's voice does who has forgotten what he meant to say, and beyond them the sheet was clean to its margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:123:"Seven," said Lira. "Which is ours?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:125:"I make it the third from the top, by where it crosses the first water. They have drawn the crossing and left out the ferry. They have left out Lowmarch." Karis put her little finger on the last dash of the third road. It ended in nothing, three fingers east of the river. "That is about the saddle. By this sheet we walked off the end of everything that is known the first time we climbed out of that meadow, and we have been standing on blank paper ever since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:127:"He called them that," said Brom. "The old man, at the plank. Void roads. I didn't know what he meant by it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:129:"It is a clerk's word," said Seln. He had come up behind them. "An entry is void when nothing has been written against it. The road is granted; it is on the sheet. It has no particulars. I should think that a carter was shown one of these once, by somebody who had come over with it in his coat, and asked what the dashes were, and went home with the answer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:131:Karis had turned to the hide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:135:"Now his," she said. "I have been over this again since the plank, and I have found two things that I had read wrongly. I took them for his thrift."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:137:She showed them. The first they knew: it was the double line that left its straight, two days beyond Thornwater, and went round a patch of bare skin and came back, and she had ringed the patch in pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:139:"That is the only place where one of his roads walks round a blank. I said so on the fifty-eighth day, and it is still so. But he draws a hill with short hatching, and water with three wavy strokes. Look here, south of the ring with the bar, a day down and a little aside. He has hatched hills all across this corner, close as thatch, and in the middle of the hatching there is a round place the size of my thumbnail where he lifted the stick."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:141:There was. Cael would have taken it for a thin place in the hide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:143:"And here, a long way south-east, among his water. Strokes all round, and a round of bare skin in the midst, with no road within a day of it." She had ringed both. "Three blank circles. One I can source, since a one-armed man has named it. The other two are a man lifting his hand from a skin. I have written *guess* beside each, and I shall not call them anything else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:145:"Could you rule a line through them?" said Lira. "You've got that brass thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:147:"On this? No. It is a map of days. He has drawn a day's haul the same length uphill and down, and bent his roads to fit the skin. I could lay an edge across it and learn something about the calf." She settled back on her heels, and her eyes went from the one to the other, the black print and the brown hide, lying side by side on a carters' halting-ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:151:She rolled the printed sheet up again before the dew could reach it. Cael noticed that she rolled the calfskin second, and more slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:155:They came up to Thornwater's gate an hour after noon on the ninety-third day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:157:The ford had been only a ford. Its white stones ran out along the lip of the sill to the bar, a yard apart, without a gap, and there was nothing on the gravel but a wagtail; he had looked down the bar to its tail all the same, and at the bend below, where two had gone on the fifty-third evening and not been seen since. They took the stretch at noon, a mile of pale grass with the frost still lying in its roots and a white stone standing in it, and he walked it with his hands at his sides and nine things shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:159:On the platform the bowman lifted two fingers from his stave. Lira put up the great boot, an inch, and set it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:163:It was only a plank on two kegs. A small brown mule stood beside it with her pack still on and her head down, and on the pack, lashed along its top, lay a long shape in an oiled cover. Astride one end of the plank sat a carter whom Cael had not seen before, a heavy man with a grey stubble, who held his right hand out in front of him, palm upward, as a person holds a hand out for coin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:165:A square woman in a brown coat sat on the other end, and she had the hand between both of hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:167:Her eyes were shut and her lips were still, and the sweat had come out along her cropped hair in the cold; and Cael understood that she had come through that gate herself not long before them, and had sat down to this without so much as slipping a pannier. She had owed it for fifteen days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:169:They stood and waited, and it was not long. She drew a slow breath and opened her fingers, and the carter turned his palm to the light. There was a pink seam across it from the root of the thumb to the heel, dry and shut. He closed the hand, and opened it, and closed it again on nothing, as a man tries a tool that has come back from the smith.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:171:"That's one," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:173:She said it to the fence. Then her eyes came round, tired and level, and went over the five of them from a stride and a half, a single time, and stopped on Cael's left side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:175:"Fifteen days. Since the break."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:177:He had not opened his coat. She had got it from how he stood, and from a sum that she had plainly done on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:179:"Breath stops at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:181:It was what she would want first, and so he gave it first: the tape was where she had laid it, and he had learned the edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:183:"Ninth day. I said nine. You've counted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:185:"So did you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:187:"I count days. It's the only thing I do count." She got up off the plank by pushing on her knees. "I eat now, and I'm on my back two hours, and then there's a yard of people. You can tell me where you're going while I've a bowl in my hand. Five don't walk up this valley in the cold to keep an appointment about three ribs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:189:He told her at the long house door, with the steam of a barley broth going up between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:191:He did not make a sentence of it. He gave it as he would have given Pike a line: a paper off the plank, under a broker's seal; something to be fetched from a place on the Fallow road, two days east, at a rise; out by the east gate at first light. He said what the place was called, and what Pike called it, and he asked her for nothing. He had no more right to that sentence than he had had at a well, and he found that it was easier to leave unsaid the second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:193:Oryn ate, and heard him out, and scraped the bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:195:"The ring," she said. "I know it. I've been round it thirty times, with the mule, by the ruts." She set the bowl on the sill. "You broke on the seventy-eighth. It's twenty-one days by the rule, and I don't lift a tape on a guess, so that's the ninety-ninth. The long one wants a morning with nothing else in it. That's the hundredth. I'd meant to sit at this gate's table from tonight until then; the grain's moving, and that bench fills as fast as I empty it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:"Two days out. Two back. Whatever you spend there. That has you at this gate again by the ninety-ninth, if nothing goes wrong, and I've walked behind you people long enough to know what *if* is worth." She held up a thick finger. "That's one reason. I want a patient whole and still for the long reading, and I've seen what your crew brings home from a road. I'll not have him back here on the ninety-ninth with something new broken, and wait three weeks more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:203:"The other's mine." Oryn looked at her own right hand. "Every carter on my round has told me the same thing about that place, for thirty rounds. That a healer's hands are no good in it. Not one of them's seen it. They had it from their fathers. I've let it go by me all that time, since I'd no business in there and a list to keep." Her mouth went hard. "I don't like a thing about my own hands that I've only been told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:207:"Six gates wait on me the same days, whether I spend them at this table or behind you on that road. I know who's at every one. It's this bench that goes short, and I'll pay it when I come back through." Then she looked straight at Cael, as she had looked at him once across a hollow of shale. "You're a stop. I've added you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:209:He could find nothing to say that was the size of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:211:"Don't make it a long stop."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:215:He stood at the long house door with an empty bowl on the sill beside him. She had a round of seven gates, and she was late at every one of them already, and nobody had asked this of her; and she had just spent five days of it on a road that every carter she had ever mended told her to keep off. He had wanted to say the sentence for nine days. He was glad now, with his whole heart, that he had left it for her, since from him it would have been a request, and from her it was a decision.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:219:From the loft door, that evening, with his legs hanging over the yard, he watched the healer work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:221:It was the place where he had written on his first stay at Thornwater, and on his second. Two of the boxes stood behind him among the barley sacks, and the third, with the corner that was worn white, waited by the ladder's head for the morning. Down in the yard the Oxhollow carter was backing his mules to an empty bed. He would load grain at first light and go home by the ferry, sixteen days in all, and he had said goodbye to nobody; but Cael had seen him stand for some time looking at a boot that hung from his cart's tail, and then untie it, and carry it over, and stand it carefully outside the barn door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:223:The steading's woman had given the healer her back room without being asked; and when Karis inquired whether that was the custom, she had looked at her as at a person asking whether the well had water in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:225:"It's hers when she's here," the woman said. "It's nobody's when she's not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:227:There was a bench under its window, and by dusk there were seven people on it, three of the hold and the rest come in with the east carts, and one of them a child. Oryn came out when the shadow of the fence had crossed the well, and washed to the elbow, and went along them as he had seen her go along eight at Oxhollow; and she chose two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:229:That was no longer new to him, and he did not count her lips; he watched the yard instead, where nobody stood about. He had seen a healing house's corridor once, from a bed, and it had been full of people waiting to be told their turn by tier. Here the five whom she had passed over got up from the bench without complaint and went back to what they had been doing, and the woman of the hold set a lamp on the back room's sill, unlit, and a bowl beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:231:Oryn glanced at the lamp, and then across the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:233:"You. Ember."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:235:Karis was on the well's coping with her notebook. She looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:237:"I told you a week, on a mountain, and three days on top of it at a ford. That ran out on the eighty-seventh, and this is the ninety-third. Have you asked it for anything since?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:239:"No."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:243:"Because I have been on roads, where one does not. And because—" Karis stopped. She did not often stop in the middle of a sentence. "Because I looked for the place, the first night, and it was gone. I had sooner not look again and find it gone a second time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:245:"That's honest." Oryn jerked her head at the sill. "This is a yard, and I want a light. Come here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:247:Karis got down, and crossed the yard with her hands held a little away from her sides, as she had carried them when they were bound, and stood before the lamp. Cael saw her draw a breath. Then her right hand made the smallest dip that he had ever seen it make, as a person sets a full stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:249:A seed of yellow stood on the wick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:251:It was no bigger than a grain of barley; it caught, and the flame came up round it, ordinary and smoking, and the seed was gone into it, and Karis let out her breath in something that was not quite a laugh. He had last seen her do that across sixty yards of mountain, with her hands shaking like a rope under load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:253:"Somebody has sewn the pocket back into the coat," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:255:Oryn had two fingers on her wrist already, and two on her neck, and her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:257:"Three parts full. It'll be the whole by the end of the week." She let go. "You spent it to the last copper once. Don't do that again where I'm not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:261:Cael sat on in the loft door until the cold drove him in. Tomorrow there would be an east gate, and a road with a bend in it; and for the first time since he had crossed the Line, six people would go out by a gate together where there had always been five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:1:# Chapter 22 — Nine Days
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:3:The table stood under a lean-to roof against the gate-house wall, and nobody in Oxhollow had put so much as a cup on it for twenty-five days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:5:Cael could tell that from the wood. It was a slab of ash on two trestles, long enough for a man to lie on, and it had been scrubbed so often that the grain stood up pale and clean, and round its feet the earth was swept. A bench ran along the wall beside it. Eight people were sitting on the bench when the five came up from the ford with seventy marks, and it was plain that some of them had been there since the mule was first seen on the valley road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:9:"There's people waiting," said Lira, to the woman who kept the gate. "There's a boy on that bench with his arm in a scarf."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:11:"She's a rule. She'll not lay a hand on anybody till she's had a meal and two hours on her back, not if the hold was burning." The woman said it with a kind of pride. "They've waited on her twenty-five days. They'd sooner wait two hours more and have the one who can count to three."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:13:She came down when the shadow of the palisade had reached the well. She washed her hands to the elbow in a bucket, and then she went along the bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:15:It was the thing he had seen her do under a rock, and he understood it better for seeing it done eight times. She stopped in front of each of them at her stride and a half and looked. Then she put two fingers on a wrist, or a hand on the hurt place, and her lips moved to ten, and she passed on to the next without a word. An old man with a cough. A child on a lap. A woman who held her left forearm away from her body, wrapped in a cloth that had been wet and dried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:19:"You," she said to the woman. "Then him, with the foot. Then the boy. The rest of you I'll see after, and none of you is going to die of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:21:Cael sat on an upturned trough with his back against a post and watched the whole of it, since the tape would not let him do much else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:23:The arm was a scald, a week old, and it had gone bad. He saw a red line running up the inside of it from the wrist toward the elbow, and the woman's eyes were too bright. Oryn laid her on the ash and put both hands round the forearm above the cloth, and shut her eyes, and it was as it had been with Lira; there was nothing to see but a square brown woman breathing slowly, and the sweat coming out along her cropped hair. It took half an hour. When she lifted her hands the red line was a faint pink thread, and the woman on the table had begun to cry quietly from relief.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:27:The foot belonged to a man who drove the oxen, and one of them had stood on it. The boy had gone off a hay-rick onto his shoulder. She set that bone with her thumbs before she mended it, and told him first that he would mind it, and he bit his scarf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:29:After the third she did not get up at once. She let herself down onto the end of the bench, a little at a time, with one hand on the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:33:"And those?" said Lira. She was on the trough beside Cael, with her taped foot on her knee, and she tipped her head at the five still sitting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:35:"A tooth, which the smith can draw. A cough that's had him every cold term for twenty years. The child's fat, and it's cross, and there's nothing the matter with it." The healer's voice had gone hoarser. "I've looked at every one. That's what the bench is for. It isn't the ones I mend who need me to come on the day. It's the ones I have to tell from the others."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:39:"Does it do anything else? Tide. Could you fight with it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:41:Oryn was slow to answer, and Cael saw her mouth go hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:43:"There's one thing, and you'll hear of it from somebody, so hear it from me. A hand on a wrist, for a breath, at the moment the other one means to declare; and whatever was coming doesn't come. I'm not opening a way then. I'm shutting one." She looked at her right hand as though it were a tool that she kept in the bottom of a chest. "It costs me a day's mendings, and the next day's, and it mends nobody. I've done it. I'd give a good deal not to know how."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:49:"You. Up here, and your shirt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:51:He got onto the ash by stages. The light was going yellow, and the others had drawn in without being asked; Karis sat on the end of the bench with her notebook shut, and Brom stood behind her, and Seln was a darker place against the gate-post. Lira had not moved from the trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:55:He counted with her. It was the second time, and he knew now what to watch for. At six a crease came between her brows. At ten she left the hand where it was and shifted her thumb, a little, from one rib to the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:57:"They're knitting," she said. "Six days, and they've done the work of ten." She pressed, very lightly. "That's not what I mind. I've known bone knit fast in the young. What I mind is that when a rib knits, I can feel the thing that's doing the knitting. It's there in a goat. And these are closing up under my hand, as neat as you like, with nothing doing it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:59:She took the hand away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:61:"Give me your wrist. The right one; there's nothing wrong with that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:63:He gave it. She laid two fingers on the inside of it, and two of the other hand on the side of his neck below the jaw, as she had laid them on Karis, and this time she shut her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:65:He lay still on the scrubbed wood and looked at the underside of a turf roof. Nine things lay in him as quiet as he had ever known them; he had held them for eighty-four days, and it had become very like breathing. He could not tell what her fingers were reaching for. He knew only that none of the nine had stirred, and that if one had, he would have felt her feel it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:67:It went on a long time past ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:69:"No," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:73:"That's three times. The first I thought it was the break; a bad one can shout over everything. So I went where you're whole. There's nothing wrong with that wrist, or that neck, and there's still nothing I can read." She looked at Karis, who was the one writing it down, or would be. "It's like putting my hands in a river in the dark. Something's moving. I can't find the banks, so I can't read it, so I can't touch it. That's the rule. I didn't make it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:75:"What are the banks?" said Karis. Her voice was not quite steady, and Cael did not think that the healer could know why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:77:"Everybody's got them. What moves in a person moves in a bed, the same bed all their life, and the shape of the bed is their Path. I put my hands on you and I felt the banks before I felt anything else, and I said Ember, and I was right. I'd know a Wind from a Skin with a glove on." She jerked her chin at the table. "In him it's moving, and it hasn't a side anywhere that I can find."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:79:Nobody in the yard breathed. Lira's eyes had come round to Cael, and Brom's, and he kept his own on the turf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:83:"They're the same thing, done twice as hard. That's the short one, and a healing house calls it the surface reading. Hands on the hurt, or on a wrist, and a count of ten. It tells me what's broken and where it's standing, and it costs me nothing I'd notice; I did eight of them along that bench." Oryn leaned on the edge of the ash. "There's a long one, which they call the deep reading. Both hands, and all of me, and it isn't a count, it's minutes. I go through the whole of how a person's built, as you'd go through every room of a house with a lamp. It costs what a mending costs. And the one under my hands has to lie still for it, and be whole, since a break shouts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:87:"I don't run it on a road. I've not the day to spare for it, and he's three ribs short of whole." She turned back to Cael, and for the first time since a rock he saw something in her face that was neither tiredness nor arithmetic. It was appetite. "When you're not broken, I run the long one. It won't read you either. I want to feel it not read you properly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:91:"Get down," said Oryn. "I've three things to ask you, and then I'm done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:99:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:107:"I'd have laid money on it." She pulled a buckle through. "Has a healer ever got anything from you? A Tide, anywhere, with hands on?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:111:"No."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:113:"No." She said it after him, with a kind of grim content, as a carter says the weight of a load he has guessed right. "That's the visit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:115:She did the rest of them in the time it took to load a mule. She looked at Lira's foot, and at how the long strip crossed in front, and said that it would do. She had Brom's wrist out of its cuff and into it again. She told Karis to eat, and to ask nothing of herself for three days more, not so much as a spark to see by. To Seln she gave a nod, as one gives it to a man of one's own trade, and Seln returned it exactly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:119:He had been building it since the halting-ground. *We go south in the morning, six days, to a town with a board. There are five of us, and in one month we have been bitten, and broken, and emptied, and the nearest hands were a day and a half down a mountain. Come south.* It was a reasonable sentence. He could have priced it for her: what a crew would pay, and what the board paid a crew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:121:He looked at the table. It was bare again already, and somebody had swilled it down; it would stand so for twenty days, scrubbed, with its bench against the wall, and on the twentieth there would be people sitting there before it was light. There were six more like it. There was a child with a chest, three stops on, and a carter with his hand in a rag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:123:She had told him what late was. He would have been asking her to be late to all of it at once, and to call that a wage.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:125:He did not say it. He had no right to the sentence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:127:She had the cheek-strap in her hand. The gate stood open on the north side, and beyond it the road went up a long brown hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:129:"Thornwater's on my route in nine days. You'll be on the board by then. Come find me. I want to read you when you're not broken."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:131:"Nine days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:133:She nodded, once. Then she clicked her tongue to the mule, and went out under the lintel at a carter's pace, on the low side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:137:Oryn walked out of that gate with her hands in the pockets of a dead man's coat, and she kept them there for the first mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:139:She did not often do that. A healer's hands are her eyes, and she carried hers open, as a rule, swinging, where the air could get at them. This evening they felt as they had felt once in her first year on a house's staff, when she had been set to wash a hundred bandages in a trough in the cold and had lost her fingers to the wrist; she could move them, and they would not tell her anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:143:She had spent three mendings at the table since noon, the whole of a Tide's reserve for a day, and every one had been what it should be. The scald had stood in the arm like water behind a board. The ox-man's foot had been four small bones and a great deal of noise. She had gone into each of them as she went into a house she knew, and found what was stopped, and opened it, and come out tired. That was her trade. She had not once, since the morning her Path woke, laid a hand on a living body and had it come back to her empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:145:Three times, now. Once on a mountain, over three broken ribs, and twice on a scrubbed table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:147:She was not a person who guessed. She had been taught by a woman who struck her knuckles for guessing, and she had come to be glad of it. So she went over what her hands had found, as she would have gone over a patient for a second healer, and she kept strictly to that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:149:There was bone. Bone answered to anyone; she had felt the breaks as a blind man feels a step. There was movement, past the bone. She was sure of that, and it was what kept her fingers stupid. A dead thing she could have understood. This was more alive than most, and it ran, and she had felt it running; and she could no more have said which way, or between what, than she could have drawn the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:151:*No banks.* She had told the small pale one so, since the small pale one was writing it down. It was the truest thing she had, and it was not much.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:153:She tried her hands on the mule, at the first turn of the hill, as a person tries a lamp that has gone out once. She laid her palm on the warm brown neck, under the mane, and counted. It was all there. A beast's bed is broad and shallow and runs slow, and she had felt this one's every day for thirty rounds; it told her of a sound heart, and a near hind hoof that would want the smith inside the month. Her hands were as they had always been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:155:That ought to have been a comfort, and it was only half of one. If the fault was not in the instrument, then it lay in the thing the instrument had been put to, and she had no place to keep a thought of that kind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:157:The road went up the hill in a long slant, and she went up it below the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:159:Four people were coming down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:161:She knew the first of them, as anybody on those roads did. It was Teague, off the north water, with a string of two mules behind him and his three; and she gave them what she gave everything on legs, which was a look from a stride and a half as they drew level. A spear, carried upright and grounded at each step, by somebody who never hurried. A boy who was all wrists. And a bow, on the back of one who walked a hair short on the left, at the hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:163:Her fingers woke for that. She had not touched it, and she did not need to. It was old, ten years or more; the joint had been out and gone back in by itself, a little wrong, and grown a seat there. She knew what it would cost to unmake that. She knew, too, that the captain had stopped his walk by half a pace to let her see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:165:"You were looked for up the water," he said. "On the eighty-first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:167:"I was late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:169:He let that be the whole of it. He had a level voice. "Is it a thing you'd do?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:171:"Not on a road. Not in a night." She did not soften it; he was not a man one softened things for. "It's two days of me and a week lying down after, and I'm five days behind. Ask me on a round when I'm not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:173:He nodded. He had asked, and been told the price, and she saw him put it by. She gave him a nod of her own, as one gives it to a man who has not wasted one's time, and went on up the hill below the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:177:She was at the next gate on the eighty-sixth, five days after she had said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:179:It was the hold that had given her the coat. She did there what she always did. She ate, and slept her two hours, and walked the bench; and at the end of it sat a man whose shoulder had come out of its place a week before, hauling a calf. His brothers had put it back for him, kneeling on his chest, since there was nobody else. It was in. It was not right. She spent two on it that should have been one, and she did not need to be told whose five days those were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:181:There was a woman on that bench, too, with a breast-fed child that would not thrive, and an old herdsman who wished to be told that the pain in his side was nothing. She told the one what to do and the other the truth. Neither of them cost her a mending, and both of them had needed her on the day; and that was the part of the round that nobody at a healing house would have understood, for a house counted what its hands did, and never what its eyes had spared them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:183:She gave that hold a night and a morning, and went on. They had put a loaf and a cold fowl in the off pannier without being asked, as they did every round, and the smith's girl had looked at the near hind hoof. It was how the coat had come to her. Nobody at any of the seven gates had ever spoken of a price, and nobody had ever let her leave with less than she came with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:185:The route was the only thing she had ever made. She thought of that on the road, where there was time. She had not been given it. There had been no round when she crossed the Line, only a rumour in a ferry-town that the holds had no hands; and she had gone to the first gate, and mended what was there, and asked where the next fence was. It had been four holds, the first year. She had put the fifth on in her third round, and the sixth for an old woman's sake. The seventh she had added because of a child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:187:So she knew how a stop was put on. It was not done lightly. Every stop was two days, and every two days was a day that six other gates waited; she had sat on a chock-stone with a pencil in her teeth, the night she added the seventh, and worked it through until the round came out at twenty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:189:The child was at the next gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:191:She got there on the eighty-ninth evening, and she did not eat first. That was a rule broken, and she knew it, and she went straight through the yard to a house under a pear tree and put two fingers on a small wrist and two on a neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:193:Her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:195:The banks were where they had always been, narrow and clear, a little ragged on the left side where the breath came hard in the cold. It was running. It was no worse than it had been twenty-five days ago, and perhaps a shade better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:199:It had been nothing. She had been five days late, and for five days she had not known, and it had been nothing, this round. She ate the whole bowl. Her hands had told her the truth as easily as they ever had; they had gone in and come out full, and she sat and looked at them in the firelight with a relief that she would not have owned to anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:201:It was not her hands, then. She had half thought, on the hill out of Oxhollow, that something in them might have begun to fail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:203:It was he.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:205:She left that hold at first light on the ninety-first, with two days of road in front of her and the mule going well. And somewhere on the first of those days, between a ford and a stand of the red trees, she found that she had been doing a sum for the better part of a morning without having set herself to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:207:Three ribs, broken on the seventy-eighth. Twenty-one days to knit, in anybody. By what she had felt under her hand they would be sound in him some days sooner. But she could not read him, and so she could not know it, and she had never in her life lifted a tape on a guess. It would be the ninety-ninth, then, by the rule. The long one wanted him whole and still, and it wanted the better part of a day out of her besides, so that the hundredth was the first morning on which she could run it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:209:She would be at Thornwater on the ninety-third, and he would be fifteen days from the break. She would have her two days at that gate, and the carter's hand, which she owed, and whatever was on the bench; and on the ninety-fifth the round would take her out by the far road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:211:The hand troubled her more than she liked. A split across the palm is a small thing on the day, and she had left it clean and wrapped, and told the man to keep it out of a rein. He would not have kept it out of a rein. He had a load for Thornwater and one pair of hands, and he had given her the news of a late crew when he might have held his tongue and had his mending. By the ninety-third that hand would be fifteen days old. She owed it to him as she owed nothing else on the round, and she meant to pay it before she sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:213:So he would be whole four days after she had gone. She would be two gates on by then, and the round would not bring her back to Thornwater for twenty days more; and a crew off a board does not keep whole for twenty days. He would be on some other road, and as like as not broken in some other place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:215:She walked a mile on that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:217:She was not a person who wanted things. She had wanted one thing, once, which was to keep a list in the right order, and she had got it, and it had filled her days so full that there was no room left over. She had not thought that there was anything else her hands were hungry for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:219:They were hungry for this. She had spent her life reading what people were built like, and she had been proud of never meeting a body she could not go into. Now she had met one. She did not want to mend him; there was nothing to mend that a strip of linen could not see to. She wanted to lay both hands on him, whole and still, for as long as it took, and feel exactly how it was that he could not be read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:225:She took it out of the round. It was five days from the ninety-fifth to the hundredth, and she went through them one at a time, as she went along a bench. Four she could spend at Thornwater's own table without lying to anybody. The grain was moving; carters came through that yard from every hold up the valley, and a bench that filled twice in two days would fill again in four. They would make her late at six gates all the same, and she knew every one of the people she would be keeping waiting. But they were work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:227:The fifth was not. On the hundredth she would mend nobody. She would spend a mending's worth of herself on a boy with nothing wrong with him, to learn a thing that would help no one on her route; and no hold owed her that day, and she owed it to none of them. If it was to be had, it must be added.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:231:Then she clicked her tongue to the mule, and went on at the same pace as before, on the low side, toward a gate that was still a day's walk off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:237:They were behind him on the hill. The spear had not changed pace for the stop. The boy had both mules by one rein and was looking back up the road after the woman, with his mouth a little open, as he looked after everything. The bow had stood square on both legs for as long as the healer's eyes were on that hip, and now the weight had gone over to the right again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:239:Three. Whole, and fed at noon, and one of them no better off than a week ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:241:They had done two holds' work in four weeks, a drain cut and a byre roofed, and there was nothing in either that would be talked of at a plank. He did not mind that. Most of what kept a crew at the top of a sheet was work of that kind, done to the day and brought home without a mark on anybody; a man who wanted his lines to be stories did not keep his people long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:243:It was the bow's hip that he minded, and he put it first, since it was the cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:245:He had timed the second of his two slips to end at the upper hold on the eighty-first day. That was the day the healer's round should have brought her to its gate. He had said nothing of it to the bow; he had only found a day's more work on a byre roof that might have waited, and stayed. She had not come, and nobody at that gate could say why. On the hill she had given him no more than that she was late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:247:He did not blame her. A healer on a round is not late for nothing, and he had never known this one's reasons to be poor ones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:249:But it was a day he had spent and a thing he had hoped for, and he could not set it down against five names. Pike had struck a ford off his hold and given it to them; that had cost nothing, because he had chosen the pass himself. The healer's lateness was not theirs on any ground he had read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:251:The road came round the shoulder of the hill, and Oxhollow lay under him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:253:He read the yard through the open gate as he went by it, without turning his head more than a walking man does.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:255:There was no cart. That was the first thing, and it was the largest. He had heard on the road that they had hired the smith's, and there were three boxes standing against the gate-house wall on the bare ground, and a crew that has wheels does not stand its goods on the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:257:The mild one was by the gate-post. Teague found him at once, this time, and was pleased with himself for a breath, until he saw that the man had been looking at him since the shoulder of the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:259:The big one sat on the well's coping with a box-rope coiled over his knee. His left wrist had a cuff of clean linen on it, tied with a knot that Teague knew, since he had seen it on his own people. Mended, then, and under orders. The girl was on a trough. She had one boot, and the other foot was bare and strapped, crosswise, by somebody who had been shown how. It was on its whole sole. He had read those feet in an inn as the quickest thing in the room, and he would not have laid a copper on them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:261:The small pale one was asleep sitting up, with her head on a box and a book shut under her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:265:The old keeper was on his bench by the stone house, with a tally-stick, and he gave Teague the rest in the time it took two mules to pass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:267:"It's a no," he said. "Over the top. I've paid seventy to be told so, and I'd pay it again. They put their own cart in my gorge finding out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:271:"All five. On their feet, the most of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:277:He had four things to say to that boy. He priced each of them, on the bank, as he would have priced a stage.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:279:The first was the two jobs, set side by side with the same number on each. It would cost him nothing, and it would tell the boy that he was being read, and by what. That was fair. The boy had read him across a room and not troubled to hide it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:281:The second cost Teague something, and he had known that he would pay it since the fifty-seventh day. The ford had been good work. To say so aloud was to say that the slip had gone where it ought, and that he had no claim left on it, even in his own head. It was true, and a true thing was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:283:The third would land hardest, and he turned it over longest. He did not mean it unkindly. A cart was not a carter. Nobody lay in that gorge. But a crew that went out with another man's wheels and came back without them had done a thing that the board would enter, and it was better that they heard the word for it from him, on a river bank, than read it on a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:285:The fourth was the only one that he might have kept. It gave something away. It told five people at the bottom of a sheet that the name at the top expected to stand on a road beside them before the cold term was out; and it set a term on that, which was his to set, since he had three behind him who would be standing there too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:287:He looked back, once. The spear. The boy. The bow, on the good leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:289:He decided to say it. A man who meant to share a contract owed the other crew the warning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:291:The boy was sitting on the gravel at the water's edge, alone, with a book on his knee. He held one arm in a little against his left side, and under the shirt, when he breathed, something drew a line across his ribs. There was a healer's tape on him. That meant ribs; and ribs, by her rule, were three weeks under linen, counted from whichever day the mountain had done it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:293:Teague still could not tell what he was. A Force stood one way and a Wind another, and there was no Path in the set of that boy that a man could name, and no want of one. He went down the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:299:He had known who it would be. A crew had come down the north road at a walking pace, in single file, without a word passing among them; and the man in front had a band of black leather laced on his right wrist and a hand-axe in his belt. He came to a stand a stride and a half from where Cael sat. It was the second time that day that somebody had chosen that distance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:301:He gave no name, and he wanted none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:303:"You cleared Thornwater's ford with five and lost a cart on the high bench with five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:305:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:307:It was the first word that Cael had ever said to him. There was no other to put with it. He could have told the man about a ledge that showed no crack, and a page marked *derived*, and sixty yards of fire held for eight seconds by a person who had never held a third of it. All of that was true, and all of it was his to know and nobody's to plead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:309:"The ford was good work. The bench was a cart."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:311:There was no contempt in it. He said it as Pike said a line, level, so that it could be called wrong if it was wrong; and he waited, as Pike waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:313:Cael went through it for the place where he could say so. He thought of a smith in Lowmarch, and six marks owed, and a tail-board that had not banged. He thought of what a ford-keeper had counted out, and what the ford-keeper had said he was paying for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:315:He had no argument. The man had not said that they had failed. He had said what the bench was, on a board that wrote down outcomes, and it was that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:317:"The board's going to have you and me on the same contract inside a season. Be good enough that I'm glad."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:319:He nodded, the width of a finger. Then he walked on into the river, and his three came after him, each at the same distance from the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:321:They crossed without a sound. Cael watched it as he would have watched a thing well made. The one with the spear went second and grounded it at every step. The one with the bow walked a little short on one side and kept the pace regardless. A boy of sixteen or so brought the two mules through on a slack rein, and not one of the four looked down at the water. They had kept the hold on roads since before he knew there was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:325:*Eighty-fourth day. Oxhollow, at the ford. The map is delivered: sixty-three lines, and the last one says no. Seventy marks, counted. Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and sixteen. Six owed to the smith for a cart that is in a gorge.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:327:*Mine: three ribs taped, twenty-one days from the seventy-eighth; the calf bound in her cloth. Lira: a leg that is new, an ankle that is taped and will not be mended. Brom: mended, and not to pull. Karis: empty, and filling. Seln: nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:329:*The nine: held, on a mountain and off it. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:331:*Teague priced us. He's right about the cart. Oryn read me three times and got nothing three times and wants a fourth. Two people out here measuring me, and neither of them has a seal. I keep waiting to mind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:335:On the far bank four people and two mules were going up out of the river into the dusk, quiet, a crew's length apart. He turned his head the other way. The north road climbed the brown hill behind the hold in one long slant, and a mile up it, small and steady, there was a mule, and on the low side of the mule a woman, walking at a pace that she could keep until dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:1:# Chapter 20 — Who's Worst
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:3:They got the plate off her with a hedging bill and a man's back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:5:Brom would not have Cael touch it, and for once there was no arguing with him, since Cael could not have lifted a kettle. So Seln drove the ash haft of the bill under the slab's edge and bore down on it, and Brom knelt in the shale and got his right shoulder beneath the corner that rose. He kept the left arm folded against his chest. The stone came up by the breadth of a hand, grating, and the whole slope behind it stirred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:7:"Now," said Brom, through his teeth. "Whoever's got her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:9:Cael had her wrists. It was all that was left for him to do, and he could do it sitting, with his back against the pale rock. He drew, and she pushed with her elbows, and she came toward him over the plates a foot at a time with her face shut like a fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:11:The left leg came out whole, and the right came out without its boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:13:He saw the boot for a breath, down in the dark under the slab, with the lace still tied. Then Brom let the corner go, because he had to, and the plate settled, and the shale ran in round it and over it with a long sigh. By the time the dust had gone there was nothing in that place but hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:15:"That was a good boot," said Lira. Her voice was very small. "I'd only just got it to stop rubbing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:17:They carried her round the end of the wall onto the bare floor. Nobody remarked on the leg until she was lying flat, and then there was no need to. Below the knee the stocking was whole, and under it the shin took a small wrong turn, a hand's breadth down, where no shin has a joint.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:19:"It's not come through," said Brom. He had looked at a good many hurts on a good many yards, and he said it as the one thing in sight that was better than it might have been. "I'll not pull it straight, for I don't know how, and I'd do it badly. But it wants keeping as it is, till there's somebody who does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:21:He chose the splints himself, and they were the mountain's own; he found two plates in the bank above the wall, each as long as her shin and no thicker than a slate, and he laid one down either side of the leg. They had no canvas to tear, for the canvas had gone with the cart. Cael's coat had lost its left sleeve at a river. He got the knife into the seam of the right one, and Seln cut that as well, and Brom bound the two plates on with it in three places, one-handed, with Seln's fingers for the knots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:23:"There," he said. "You've a roof on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:25:"Like them," said Lira, and shut her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:27:Cael took his inventory then, because it was the start of something, and because he could not think what else to do with the fear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:29:There were three ribs. He knew the number without touching them; he had heard it. They were low on the left side, and they let him breathe in to a certain depth and then they stopped him, quite firmly, as a hand stops a door. The calf had opened along the line of the old bite. Seln had taken off his own neckcloth and bound it, and the cloth was already dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:31:Brom's forearm had parted at the wrist for the length of a finger, where it had parted once before above a wash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:33:Karis sat where she had sat down, and she had spoken twice since then and no more. When Seln went to her and put his coat round her shoulders she let him, and her eyes followed his hands as though they were a long way off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:35:There were three boxes, and Brom's older ash wedge lay beside them. In the third, under the masonry chips, there was a bag that had not been opened since the twelfth day, and it would buy nothing whatever on this shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:37:Then he counted what they had to eat, and that took less time. Brom had slung the bread bag on his own back that morning, from habit, and it held a loaf and a half and the hard end of the bacon. Seln had his flint, a twist of salt, and a water-skin a third full. Everything else had been on the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:41:Karis could not light the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:43:It was her own, the small brass reading lantern that lived in the left-hand box with her pens, and when the dusk came down she asked for it. They were the first words she had said since morning. Seln set it in front of her with the flint and steel beside it, and she took one in each hand, as she had taken them a thousand times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:45:Her hands would not do it. He watched her bring the steel down on the flint, and there was no strength in the stroke at all; it slid off, and no spark came. She tried again, with no better result, and the third time the flint dropped out of her fingers onto the floor, and she sat looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:47:"I've set nothing, and I want that understood," she said. "I didn't try a point; I only looked for the place I'd have set one from." She turned her right hand over. "It's like feeling in a coat for a pocket, and finding that somebody has taken the pocket out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:49:"It'll come back," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:51:"You don't know that, and I don't know it either. I've never spent it all before; nobody at Ternhall would have let me." Her voice had no fright in it, and that was worse than fright. "Nine points and eight channels, held for eight seconds across sixty yards. I should be able to give you the cost of that to a decimal. I can't find the figure. I can't find where I keep figures."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:53:Brom picked up the flint and lit the lamp for her, with two strokes, and set it where the light fell on her knees. She thanked him. Then she lay down on her side with her back to the wall, in Seln's coat, and was asleep before he had drawn his hand away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:55:It was a cold night, and they had no blankets; the frost came down out of a clear sky, and they lay close under the rock in a row, the hurt ones in the middle, and kept one another warm as cattle do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:57:Cael did not sleep much. He could not lie on his left side, and on his right the ribs hung, and so he sat against the stone and watched the bay by starlight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:59:The herd was still there, though he could not have said how many of it. They stood here and there on the level, among the spilled shale, grey on grey, and through the first hours of the dark he heard them moving, a few slow steps and a clatter of horn and then quiet. By the middle of the night the sounds had spread themselves out along the foot of the face into something very like a line, and toward morning one of them began to scrape.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:61:They had lost their hill and some of their number, and by the next light they would be mowing what was left. He could not hate them for it. They had run from a noise, as anything runs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:65:"It's still coming," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:69:"A bit at a time. I've been counting them, and that's eleven since the lamp went out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:73:"Yes." She thought about it. "It's a different hurt from the ankle. That one was a thing I'd done, and this is only a thing that's there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:75:"Karis turned thirty animals with a page she'd not tested," said Lira presently. "And Brom carried two boxes he's not allowed to lift. And you went back for the third and stood under one of those things with your hands shut. I saw the marks where you came down." A plate slid, somewhere above. "I ran away from it, and I was the only one who did what I was supposed to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:79:"I know what I did. I'm saying it's a queer morning when the one who made the noise is the one who kept the rule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:81:"That's twelve," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:89:He had the book, for Hesk's satchel had been on his shoulder when the mountain came, as it was always on his shoulder, and it had gone under him when he fell and taken no harm. He opened it once, in the grey of the morning, and found the page with the last entry. It ended on the word *Sleep*, and above that he had written that the job had no fight at the end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:91:He read it through, and he was not ashamed of it, for it had been true when the pencil made it; but he could find no sentence to set beneath it that was a thing counted, and he would not put down one that was only a thing felt. He shut the book, and that day got no line either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:93:Karis made no move toward her notebook either. She slept, and woke to drink, and slept again; and each time she woke she looked at her two hands before she looked at anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:97:He had taken charge of it without a word, and he made a ceremony of the dividing that was plainly meant to give five people something to watch. He cut the loaf and a half into fifteen with Lira's long knife, on the lid of the middle box, and laid the pieces out in three rows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:99:"That's today, and tomorrow, and the day after. Five a row." He pointed with the knife. "The bacon's for whoever's walking. I've done it in my head six ways and that's the one that comes out even."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:101:"And the day after that?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:105:It was Seln who would walk, and they all knew it. He was the only one of the five with two legs, two hands and his wits about him, and he had spent the first night without a coat, saying nothing of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:107:He went for water first. The seep was at the bay's eastern corner, a hundred and thirty yards from the rock, and every yard of the way lay under the stripped face, where the plates were still coming down singly, with no warning but a small dry tick. Cael watched him cross it without once hurrying. He stopped whenever a slide began, and judged its line, and stood still until it had gone by, sometimes within a few feet of him; and he came back in the rain with the skin full and grey grit to his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:109:"I should not send anybody else," was all he said. "It wants a kind of patience that is unpleasant to learn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:111:In the afternoon he went the other way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:113:He was gone three hours. When he came round the end of the wall the light was failing, and he sat down on his heels in front of Cael, and Cael understood that he was being given a report, unasked, since it bore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:115:"The ledge is whole for eighty yards from the bay," said Seln. "Beyond that it has gone, for thirty-one paces; I counted them, since I had to place a foot for each. What is left there is not a shelf. It is a lip of rock, where the shelf was rooted in the wall; four feet wide at the best of it, and tilted outward, and covered with what fell on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:119:"It went for me, and I crossed it twice. Some of the stones on it are bedded and some are waiting to leave, and I could not tell the one from the other by looking; so I tried each with my weight before I trusted it, and I scratched a mark on every one that bore." He took out the narrow dull knife and showed its point, which was bright. "There are forty-four. A careful person may go over on those, facing the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:121:"A person with a leg in slates?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:123:"No."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:127:Seln considered Cael, and did not soften it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:129:"One at a time, perhaps, with a hand on his belt. Not this week." He put the knife away. "Beyond the lip the ledge is sound again to the shoulder, and from the shoulder a track goes down, an old one, steep. I went a little way. There is a road in the valley and I saw nobody on it. I judge it a day and a half to the road for a man alone, and I cannot say how far along the road to a roof."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:131:So it was settled as Brom's bread had settled it. Seln would go down at first light with the bacon, and bring back whatever he found that had hands. The four would stay under the rock. Nobody said aloud how many rows there were on the lid of the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:133:Lira's leg was hot that night. Cael felt it through the stocking when he reached across in the dark to find the water for her; the skin between the two slates burned like a stone that has lain in the sun, and she was shivering with her teeth shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:135:"Don't count them tonight," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:137:"I'm at six," said Lira. "It keeps my mind off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:141:It was Lira who knew first, and she knew it by ear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:143:It was the middle of the eightieth day. The rain had gone through, and Cael had got himself propped at the western end of the rock, where he could watch the ledge; he had been watching it since Seln went out along it in the first grey light and passed from sight where the shelf was broken. In six hours he had seen nothing move on it but a bird.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:145:"That's iron," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:149:"On rock. Listen. Something's coming along that shelf with shoes on, and nothing that lives on this hill wears shoes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:151:He heard it then, a small hard clink, and another, as even as a clock. A little after that Seln came round the bend of the ledge, under its roof. He was alone, and he walked no faster than he ever did; but he came straight over the shale to the end of the wall, and for once he gave his report before he had sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:153:"There is a healer behind me, with a mule. I did not fetch her. I met her below the shoulder at sunrise, already on her way up, and she has not altered her pace since for anything that I could tell her about a leg." He looked at Lira's. "She said that she would see it for herself, and that it was a quarter of an hour either way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:155:The clinking stopped beyond the wall's end. Somebody said one word to an animal; and then a woman was standing at the corner of the rock, a stride and a half from where Lira lay, with a cheek-strap in her fist and a brown mule's long head at her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:157:Her eyes went over the five of them a single time, and it did not take her as long as it takes to count five. He saw them go to the slates on a leg, and to a dark cloth at a wrist, and to the way that he himself was sitting, with one arm shut against his side; and they rested longest on Karis, who had nothing wrong with her that could be seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:161:It was a low voice, and a little hoarse, like a voice that was not much used on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:163:She had not asked who they were, or what had happened to them, or how five people came to be lying under a rock at the top of a mountain with three boxes. Cael noticed that. He had been met at gates and counters and tables for as long as he could remember, and at every one of them the first question had been what he was; this woman had walked a day and a half up a hill to put a different one, and it was the only one she appeared to want answered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:165:"Her," said Brom, and pointed at Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:167:"I'm fine," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:169:"You're not. I can see your leg from here." The woman put the cheek-strap into Seln's hand without troubling to see whether he would take it. "Water, somebody. I've had these on a mule since before it was light, and I'll not put them on that as they are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:171:Brom poured for her out of the skin. She washed to the wrists, rubbing hard, and it was while she did so that Cael read her; she had read him first, which did not often happen to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:He began with the hands, since she was holding them out. They were broad and brown, and the nails had been cut as short as nails can be. She was not tall, but she was square in the shoulder, and she wore a man's coat of some thick brown stuff that had been made for a bigger person and taken in. Her hair was cropped as short as a boy's. He thought that she was perhaps ten years older than himself, and he could not have said why he was sure it was not more. On top of the mule's pack, lashed along it in an oiled cover, there was something with the shape of a crossbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:179:"I did," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:181:"They've kept it. That's all they were asked to do." She glanced up at Lira's face, as one looks at the head of a horse before going round behind it. "Name's Oryn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:183:"Are you a healer?" said Lira. Her teeth were knocking together. "A woman at a gate said there wasn't one. She said the nearest walked a route."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:185:"Tide. Healer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:187:"She said the rest of you keep to the country where somebody ranks you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:189:"Most do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:195:"Iron. Four, if anybody's keeping a book up here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:197:"Nobody is," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:203:She took off the second slate, and then she put both hands round the leg and shut her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:205:Her lips moved. Cael watched them, having nothing else to do, and found that she was counting; she went to ten, slowly, and stopped, and opened her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:207:"It's broken through, clean, a hand below the knee," she said. "The one bone, and the other's whole. The ends have slipped by each other the width of my thumb. I have to bring them back to where they belong before I mend it, or I mend it short, and you walk on that for the rest of your life." She looked at Brom. "You. Behind her. Take her under the arms and don't let her come with me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:211:"That's the part you'll mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:213:She said it to Lira, plainly, as a ferryman says that the next step is wet. Then she set one hand above the place and one at the ankle, over the dirty linen, and leaned back with her whole weight, steadily, as one draws a bucket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:215:Lira made one sound, and it was not loud. Thirty yards off, out on the level, a beaked head came up and went down again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:217:"There," said Oryn. "That's it over. You'll not get worse than that from me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:219:She did not let go. She shifted her hands until both of them lay round the shin where the wrong turn had been, with the fingers overlapping, and she settled on her heels, and her face went quiet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:221:There was nothing to see. He had half expected a light, or a colour, as there was grey in Iron Skin and yellow in a point; and there was a square brown woman kneeling in the dust with her hands on a girl's leg, breathing slowly. A minute went by, and another, and the only thing that changed was that the sweat came out on her forehead, though the air was cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:223:"What are you doing?" said Karis. She had got up on one elbow. "I want it described, for I can't write, but I can remember till I can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:229:"What she mends with. Everybody's got it. It goes round you like water round a mill, and when you break a bone it does the knitting, if you leave it be for six weeks." Oryn spoke in pieces, between breaths, with her eyes shut. "At a break it stands, since it can't get by, and it piles up, and that's the heat you felt in this last night. I find where it's standing. Then I open the way with mine, and hers goes through, and I hold it open while it does the six weeks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:233:"All of them. That's what I pay. It doesn't come from nowhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:235:Lira's face went a colour that was not white, and she said "Cold," not to anyone, and then "No. It's going along," and put her head back and shut her eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:237:"That's right," said Oryn. "Lie still and let it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:243:Lira lifted her head to see it. She moved the toes of her right foot, in the torn stocking, all five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:245:Oryn did not get up. She turned herself about on the floor, slowly, and let herself down with her back against the rock, a little at a time, as a carter sets down a load whose weight he has known for years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:247:"That's one," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:249:"One what?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:251:"One mending. A Tide has a reserve, like a purse, and it's spent at the rate of what it mends. Mine holds three in a day, if I've slept under a roof and somebody's fed me, and on a road it holds two." She wiped her face with her sleeve. "After that the reserve's gone, and I'm a woman with a mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:253:"Could you do his from where you sit?" said Lira. She was looking at Brom's wrist. "So you needn't move."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:255:"Hands on. That's the only way it works."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:257:She was unlacing her own right boot as she spoke. She drew it off, and the stocking after it, and turned the foot to look. The heel had been rubbed raw in a patch the size of a coin, and over the patch there was a strip of some stiff linen stuck down smooth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:259:"And I can't do it to me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:265:She ate before she touched anybody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:267:It was her own bread, out of the mule's off pannier, a dark close loaf from some hold's oven, and she broke it in six and gave five pieces away before she bit into hers. There was a cheese as well, and four onions. Brom looked at the three rows on the lid of the box, and then at the pannier, and swept the rows back into his bag without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:269:Then she had his arm across her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:273:"Twenty-seven days. I counted the stitches in and I counted them out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:275:"Then it should have closed in a month and hasn't, because you've pulled on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:277:"I carried," said Brom. "Mostly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:279:"You pulled this week. I can feel where." She had her hands round the wrist already, and it took her a quarter of the time that the leg had taken. When she let go there was a seam on it, pink and dry, from the heel of the hand to the middle of the forearm, and it lay closed along its whole length. "That's two, and that's my day. You'll want telling what it's good for, and I'll tell you tomorrow, when I can think."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:281:She went to Karis last of the three, on her knees, and read her as she had read the leg, with two fingers on the inside of a thin wrist and two on the side of the neck. Her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:285:"I know the word. I didn't know that it could be felt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:287:"It's like a well you drop a stone in, and hear it land on the bottom dry." She sat back. "What did you do?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:289:Nobody answered. Then Seln lifted his chin at the slope above the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:291:The shale still lay banked there, in the shelter of the rock, where neither stream had carried it off; and across it, from a point high up down to either hand, ran two straight black lines. The plates along them had been burnt to the colour of iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:293:Oryn looked at that for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:297:"Eight seconds," said Cael. "I counted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:299:"Hm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:303:"That isn't mine to mend, for nothing's broken. It fills again or it doesn't, and it fills quicker asleep and fed. Don't ask it for so much as a spark for a week." She got to her feet with a hand on the wall, and her eyes went to Seln. "Those were your scratches, on the lip."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:305:"They were."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:307:"They were right. I put the mule's feet on four of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:309:Seln inclined his head. He looked, Cael thought, like a man who has had a report read by somebody competent, and it had been a long time since that had happened to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:311:"Can I stand on it?" said Lira. She had been flexing the foot all this while, a little at a time, with the wonder of a person who has been given back a thing she had already begun to grieve for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:313:Oryn did not answer at once. She leaned over and set her thumb on the place where the wrong turn had been, and pressed, hard, with her eyes on Lira's face and not on the leg. Lira's face did nothing whatever.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:315:"Tomorrow, with somebody's arm," she said then. "It's knit, and it's sound, but it's new, and new bone's like new bread; it wants a day before you cut it. It'll walk in a week as well as the other. You'll not run on it for a month, and I can see that you were about to ask."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:317:"I wasn't," said Lira, who had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:323:"You sat up to watch me come in," she said. "So you weren't worst. Coat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:325:He got it open with one hand. She knelt on his left, and her fingers went in under the shirt and lay along his side, low, where the three small sounds had been; they were warm and very steady, and her lips began to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:327:He counted with her. *One. Two.* It was the first time since he crossed the Line that anybody had laid a hand on him to learn what he was. *Three. Four.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:331:Her left hand stayed where it was. The right she put out behind her, without turning her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:333:"Wrist," she said. "Anybody's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:335:It was Brom who gave her one, the left, with its new seam. She closed two fingers on it, and her lips moved as far as three, and she let it go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:339:She brought the hand back and laid it on his side beside the other, with the fingers of both spread wide, and shut her eyes; and this time she did not count at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:341:He watched her face, and he knew what it was doing, for he had seen a brass plate in a district hall wait eleven seconds over a boy of fourteen before it lit. He had seen assessors look at a panel with that same small stillness, and a hooded seat, and a clerk with a form that had no box for him. It was what an instrument looked like when it reached for the thing it was made to read, and closed on air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:343:She was not an instrument, and that was the difference. She took her hands away at last and sat back on her heels, and she looked straight at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:345:"Your ribs are broken," she said. "I can feel that they're broken. I can't feel *you*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:349:"Whatever you are, I can't read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:351:He waited for the next thing, since he knew the order in which it came. First the instrument failed, and then somebody asked him what he was, and wrote the answer down, and the answer became a word on a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:353:The question did not come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:355:"I've never said that to anyone. Tape them. They'll heal on their own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:357:She was frowning at his side, as at a sum that had come out oddly, and she spoke the rest as though she were reading it off the bone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:361:"Yes," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:363:"That's not an answer," Oryn said. "That's a *finding*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:365:He knew that word. It was Karis's, and she kept it for a thing that had been looked at carefully and written down with nothing added. This woman's hands had come back empty twice, and she had only said so, and put a name on the emptiness that made no claim about him at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:367:She got up and fetched clean cloth from her pannier, and a flask; and she unwound Seln's neckcloth from his calf and washed what was under it, and bound it again in her own linen, tight and neat. She put no hand round it to count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:369:"I can't mend what's in you, since I can't find where it's standing," she said. "So you get what a carter gets. Cloth, and tape in the morning." She knotted the end. "A man at the foot of this hill told me the mountain had come down two days back, and that a crew off the Lowmarch plank had gone up it with a cart and not come off the other side. He said you were late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:371:"We were," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:373:It was dusk before Cael opened the book, and it was easier than it had been in the rain. He had five things that were counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:375:*Eightieth day. Lira's leg is straight. The boxes are ours. The cart is in the gorge. A healer came up the road because a carter said we were late.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:377:He looked at that, and added what belonged with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:379:*Some of the herd went over the edge after the cart. How many: heard, not sworn, which is Karis's wording. She cannot hold a pencil again, and will not be able to set a point for a week.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:381:*The healer put her hands on my ribs and could not read me. She told me so to my face, and asked nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:383:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:1:# Chapter 21 — Thirty-One Paces
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:5:It was grey in the hollow still. He had got to his feet by the wall, as he did every morning, to learn what the night had done to him, and he had drawn in air to say something to Brom. At a certain depth, a little short of where the ribs had stopped him the day before, a hand seemed to close across his left side and hold it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:7:It was the tape. She had put it on him by the lamp, before the light came and before she had so much as eaten. There were five bands of linen, three fingers wide, each drawn from his breastbone round under the arm to the spine in a single pull, while he stood with his left arm on his head and no air in him at all. He had supposed, while she did it, that he was being splinted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:9:"That's all it does," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:11:She had seen him find it. She was winding what was left of the strip back onto its peeled stick, and she spoke across him to Karis, who was sitting up this morning in Seln's coat with her notebook open on her knee and no pencil in her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:13:"I want you to have that right, if you mean to write it. It mends nothing, and it doesn't hold the bone, for you can't hold a rib; it's got to move, or he stops breathing. People take it for a splint, and it isn't one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:15:"What's on it?" said Karis. "It smells like a pine wood."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:17:"Pitch, and mutton fat, and a thing I get from a hold that keeps bees. It sticks to skin, and it comes off again when I say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:19:"Then what is it for?" said Lira. She was propped against a box with her right leg stretched out in front of her, bare from the knee, and she had not stopped looking at it since she woke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:21:"It tells the ribs where the edge is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:23:Oryn tucked the roll into her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:25:"He'll go to draw a full breath, or turn to pick something up, and that pulls before the bone does, so he stops there. He'll have learned it by tonight, and he'll hate it by noon tomorrow, and it stays on till I take it off." She looked at him at last. "Twenty-one days from the morning they went, and I want to be the one who lifts it. Can you count that far?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:27:"He can count anything," said Brom. "It's stopping him that's the work."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:29:"Why tape for him," said Karis slowly, "and hands for her? You had two mendings yesterday and you've two this morning, and a rib is a bone like a shin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:31:Cael had been waiting for that question, and he saw that the others had too, for Lira's eyes came up from her leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:33:"Because I cannot mend what I can't read." The healer said it without heat. "I read her leg at a count of ten and I knew what it was, and how she was built round it, and where the thing that knits had stopped; and then I knew where to put mine. I had my hands on him for three times as long and I got bone. I'd have got that much with my eyes shut, off a dead sheep." She showed her palms, briefly. "Past the bone there's nothing I can lay hold of. I'd be pushing in the dark, and I don't do that to anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:35:She turned to Brom then, and had his left arm out of its sleeve before he could offer it. The seam was dry and pale. She wound a strip of plain linen round the wrist, four turns, no tighter than a cuff, and tied it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:37:"That's not holding anything either. It's closed, and it's sound, and for a week it's the weakest thing on you, so this is to remind the hand." She tapped the cloth. "It'll take what sits on it. It won't take what hangs from it. No rope, no rail, and no hauling anybody up anything; and if you find it shut on something that's pulling away from you, let go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:39:"A week from yesterday, at the middle of the day," said Brom. "I'll know the hour."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:49:Cael stood at the end of the sound rock with the others and saw where a girl had been running three days ago. The shelf stopped as if it had been bitten. Beyond the break there was only the wall of the gorge, and along that wall a scar where the fallen stone had been rooted: a strip of raw pale rock four feet wide, tipped outward like the sill of a window, and heaped with broken plates. It ran on for thirty yards or so, and then the ledge began again, grey and level under its roof, as though nothing had happened. Under the scar there was a great deal of air, and he looked at that once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:53:"There are forty-four," said Seln, "and they are not in a line. Do not go by the look of a stone; go by the mark. Several without one look a good deal better than their neighbours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:55:He went over first, to stand at the far end, and he went as he did everything, without seeming to hurry and without a glance at the drop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:59:"You're on the wrong side," said Lira. "If she slips she goes that way, onto you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:61:"If she slips with me on the inside, she goes that way with nothing to stop her. This way she's got something to lean on." Oryn clicked her tongue. "She's a mule, and she'd sooner lean than fall. Most things would."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:63:They went out onto it together, the brown beast with her near flank brushing the wall and the square woman at her shoulder on the open side, a boot's length from the rim. The mule set each small hoof down and tried it. The woman's eyes were on the scratches; and where a marked stone lay under the animal's line she moved its head a little with her hand, and the foot came down there. At the far end Seln received the cheek-strap, and the boxes came off, and she brought the mule back as steadily as she had taken it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:65:"Now you," she said to Lira. "Up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:67:"I can walk it. You said tomorrow, with an arm, and it's tomorrow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:69:"I said you could stand. That leg's a day old; it'll bear you on a floor, and I'll not have it asked to catch you on that." She had made a step of her two hands already. "Up, and sit loose, and keep your knee off the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:71:Lira got up. She sat the pack-saddle as she would have sat a fence, sideways, with her new leg hanging and her bare foot turned in; and she fixed her eyes on the roof of rock over the far ledge and kept them there while she was taken across between a wall and a healer. When Seln lifted her down she said something short to him that Cael could not hear, and Seln bowed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:77:He said it across the gap, to her, before she had opened her mouth. Oryn considered the box, and the rope that went round it and over his chest, and his two hands, which were open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:81:It took Cael a breath to understand that she had agreed with him. Brom understood it at once. He stood up under the box like a man who has had a long account settled in his favour, and went over the lip sidelong, breast to the wall, placing his big boots on the scratches as daintily as a cat on a shelf of plates.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:83:Seln came back for Karis. He put two fingers through the back of her belt and walked behind her, and she went across with her lips moving; and when she reached the sound ledge she turned round and studied the scar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:85:"Thirty-one," she called softly. "I make it the same."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:87:Then there was nobody left on the near side but himself. He had known that it would be so, and had not let himself think of it. He set his left hand on the wall and his right foot on the first scratch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:89:The tape spoke at once. He had reached out a little farther than a man reaches who is walking on a floor, and the five bands drew across his side and said *there*; so he took the breath shallower and the reach shorter, and it let him be. He learned that he could go so, at half a stride, with his face to the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:91:Anchor asked, as he had expected. Here was a rim thirty yards long with a broken boy on it. *There? There?* Each stone under his boot was a place where a point might be fixed and told to stay, and at each one he said no, and moved his foot to the next mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:93:There was something else that wanted to help him, and it was harder to turn away. The Iron read would have given him every plate on that lip within a pace and a half: which was seated on rock and which on its fellow, and which meant to go. He would have known the ground as he knew his own weight. He had refused it on a fan, when all that hung on it was a map.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:97:A broad slab lay directly before his right foot, level, as big as a hearthstone, and there was no mark on it. The mark was on the stone beyond, a mean tilted thing no wider than his boot. To reach that he must stretch, and the tape had already told him what it thought of stretching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:99:He stood still, with the gorge breathing cold up his back, and he knew what he was choosing between. On the one hand was what he could see for himself. On the other was a line the length of a finger, cut with the point of a knife by a mild man who had come this way in the rain and stood on forty-four stones, one at a time, to learn which would have him. Cael had not asked for that report. He had been given it, since it bore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:101:He stretched. The tape drew, hard, and three ribs said what they had to say, and his right boot came down on the mean stone and it did not move. Behind him, as his weight left the edge of the broad slab, it turned over like a page and went out into the air without a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:103:At the thirty-first pace there was a hand in his coat, and it was Brom's, the right one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:107:"So everybody says." Oryn was looking at his colour, and then at his side. "You breathed to the tape the whole way, which is more than most do. Most don't believe it till they've cried out once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:109:He turned round, since the others had. From the western ledge the whole bay lay open behind them. He could see the pale rock standing across its floor, and the two black lines above it that came to a point, and the wide scoured strip of the hill; and along the foot of the face, in a row, with a length and a half between each, the shale-backs were mowing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:111:He counted them, as he had not been able to before, and there were twenty-five. That was five fewer than thirty, and it was not the same thing as saying that five were dead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:115:They went down the western side of the mountain for the rest of that day and half of the next, and he had a great deal of time in which to watch the healer walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:117:The track had been cut for mules. It left the shoulder by a notch and dropped in long slants across a hillside of brown grass and broken rock, turning back upon itself at every hundred yards, and the valley came up to meet them so slowly that the pale thread of the road seemed no nearer at dusk than it had at noon. Going down was worse for him than the level had been. Each step jarred, and the tape told him so, faithfully, some four thousand times; and he catalogued her, since it took his mind from his side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:119:She never hurried and she never stopped, and that was the first item. She had the carter's pace so deep in her legs that he thought she could not have walked at any other. On the steepest of the slants she shortened her stride and kept the beat, and where the track ran level she lengthened it again, and the time between one footfall and the next did not change by a hair. He tried to walk at it himself and could not, for he went in fits, quick and then slow, as he had always gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:121:The second item was the side she kept. On every slant she was below the mule. When the track turned, and the fall of the hill went over to the other hand, she passed behind the animal's tail without breaking step and came up on the low side again; and she did it at every turn of that hillside without once seeming to consider which side was the low one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:127:"Two rounds. It closes when I'm three days at a hold, and I'm never three days at a hold."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:131:"I can tape it." Oryn pulled the lace tight. "I told you on the hill. Tide runs from the healer into somebody else, and there's nowhere in me to run it from. A healer with a broken leg lies in a barn six weeks like anyone, and hopes there's another healer on the next road. There isn't, out here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:133:Lira rode. She had been put up on the pack-saddle at the shoulder, on top of the panniers, between two boxes, and for the first hour she sat as stiff as a person on a throne that she expects to be taken away. She had not been carried anywhere since she could walk. She looked straight in front of her, over the long brown ears, and when the mule stopped to consider a turn she looked at the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:135:"How is she?" said Brom, on the second slant. He was walking at the tail with the third box on his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:139:"The mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:141:"I've not noticed a mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:143:"You're on one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:145:"I'm on a saddle. I was told to get on a saddle, and I did, and I've nothing to say about what's under it." She shifted her weight, with some care. "It's very sure-footed, for a saddle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:147:"She's got a kind eye," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:149:"I wouldn't know. I can't see her eye from up here," said Lira, and then she shut her mouth, and Brom walked on behind with a look of deep content.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:151:They slept that night beside a seep of good water, halfway down, in a fold of the hill where somebody had once built a wall of loose stones against the wind. Seln had found it, and he had a fire lit when they came up, and enough dead heather gathered to keep it till morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:157:The track had come down by then onto the last long slant above the valley, and Lira's right foot hung from the pack-saddle at about the height of the healer's shoulder, bare, and wound with the strip of linen that it had worn since a meadow. Oryn went beside it for the better part of a mile. Cael, two paces behind, saw her eyes go to it and come away, and go again, as a person's go to a thing that somebody else has done badly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:161:"I did," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:165:Lira did not answer for the length of one turn of the track. Then she asked, from the back of a mule, over the heads of four people, and Cael could hear what it cost her to do it so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:167:"It's the cords, and not the bone. A woman with a barn bound it to a stave, four weeks back. She said that it would set stiff, and that a healer would have had it straight in an hour." Her voice was very level. "Can you mend it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:169:Oryn kept her pace. She put up her left hand and took the heel in her palm as it swung, and went on so, with another person's foot in her hand and her lips moving; and it took her a good deal longer than ten. She turned the foot outward. It went a little way, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:171:"So I would have, that week," she said. "It's knit now. It's knit wrong, short, and across itself; but it's knit, and it's strong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:175:She did her sum aloud, walking, as plainly as Pike did his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:177:"To put that right I'd have to take it back to the day it went, and undo the whole of what's grown, and then bring it up again straight. That's two mendings, or three; I'd not know till I was in it. Then you're on your back for a week while it takes." She let the foot go. "I've two in me on a road. There's a table at that ford that hasn't seen me for twenty-five days, and I don't know who's lying on the bench beside it. It could be nobody. It could be a man with his chest stove in, who's been waiting on me since the day I went up your hill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:179:She shook her head, once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:181:"That's a tape and a season. It'll set."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:183:Lira looked straight in front of her, over the long brown ears, and Cael watched her take it. He had seen her take a result before, on a painted floor, with a sheet of figures to tell her how far she had fallen short. There was no sheet here. There was a square woman with tired eyes, walking below a mule, who had spent what she had on the leg above that ankle, and had said to her face what the rest was worth and to whom it was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:187:"Then show me the tape," said Lira, a quarter of a mile farther on. "Properly. What's on it I did myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:189:"At the halt. And when I've shown you, you'll take it off and do it, twice. I'm not going to be there every morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:191:They halted at noon, on a floor of turf beside a stream, with the road in sight half a mile off, two pale ruts going north-west; and there she showed her. It was a craft, like any other, and Cael watched it as he would have watched a new guard. One turn went round the shin above the ankle, for an anchor; then a strip ran from the inside of that, down under the heel, and up the outside, pulled hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:193:"Always that way, up the outside. That's the side it went over on, so that's the side that's slack, and you're lending it a cord." She laid a second beside the first, and then a long one that went over the top of the foot and under the arch and crossed itself in front, like a figure of eight. "That one stops it rolling." Her thumb went on up the shin, of its own accord, and pressed along the new bone. "And this is ahead of where I'd have looked for it. You've good legs. Somebody's taken trouble with them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:195:"A good many people," said Lira. "They kept lists."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:199:Lira stood, on turf, with her weight on both feet. She rocked forward onto her toes and back, and something went over her face that was a long way from joy and yet was on the road to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:201:"It doesn't bend any more than it did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:203:"No, and it won't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:205:"But I know where it is." She tried it again. "It's been a beat behind since the river. I'd put it down and not be sure of it till it was down, and now I can feel the edge of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:207:"That's all tape ever does," said Oryn. "Take it off. It's your turn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:213:It was the second night down, and the first on which nobody was too cold or too sore to talk. Seln had the fire, and Brom had made a bread of the healer's flour on a hot stone; it had come off black on both faces, as his bread did, and everyone ate it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:215:Karis had been studying the healer across the flames for some time, with her notebook on her knee. She could hold a pencil again, for as long as it took to write a line, and she had spent her line that evening on the foot of the long leaf. Cael had seen it: *Sixty-three. The ledge. Gone, for thirty-one paces. Cart: no.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:217:"How long is your round?" she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:219:"Twenty days," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:223:"Round. Two at a hold, near enough, and the road between, and the road's the part that doesn't move."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:227:"Seven. I've been about them thirty-odd times, and I stopped counting rounds a while back, for what I count is days late." She turned the heel to the fire. "I've been late eleven times, and this is twelve."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:229:"That's us," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:231:"That's a carter at the foot of a hill who had a split hand, and told me while I was looking at it that a crew was late off the top. I said I'd do his hand that evening, and then I went up to you." There was no reproach in it. "He's got it in a rag still. He hauls to Thornwater, and I'll find him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:233:"What do they pay you?" said Lira. "The holds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:235:"They feed me, and they feed her." She nodded at the mule. "One of them shoes her. One gave me this coat, the year I came, off a peg that nobody was using any more. I get a bed for two nights and a table to work at, and nobody's put a coin in my hand since I crossed the Line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:239:"The day." Oryn looked into the fire. "I said I'd be at a gate on a day. There's a child three stops on with a chest that I listen to every round, and most rounds it's nothing. One round it won't be nothing; and if I'm five days late for that one, then that's what five days cost."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:243:"That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:245:Karis had waited as long as she was able.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:247:"But you keep a record," she said. "You must. Seven holds, and every one with its sick; what you found, and what you did, and what you said you'd do next time. I should like to see how you've ruled it, for I've never seen a healer's book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:249:"I've not got one. No ledger, and no roll."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:251:Karis's mouth came open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:255:"I remember who I've had my hands on," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:259:"That's the book. A healer who needs a page to know who's owed has stopped looking at them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:261:Karis sat with her pencil lifted over nothing, and Cael saw the two halves of her at war: the archivist, who could not bear a thing unwritten, and the researcher, who knew a primary source when it sat down across a fire from her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:263:"I have a rule about not asking," she said at last, rather faintly. "I made it at a fence, and I've never wanted so much to break it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:265:"Ask what you like. I'll not write the answer down for you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:267:It was Lira who asked. She had her taped foot stretched to the fire beside the healer's bare one, and she had been quiet since noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:269:"You're Iron, Rank Four; you said so on the hill. That's three above me, and I was thought good." She was picking the words. "A Tide of that rank could sit in any city on the continent and have people come to her. Why are you walking round seven fences with a sore heel?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:273:"A healing house keeps a list, every morning, of who's waiting and for what. Do you know what order it's in?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:275:"Worst first," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:277:"That's the order I keep. A house keeps it by tier, with Gold at the top, if there's a Gold, and then Silver, and so down. There were six of us on the staff, and I got what the four above me didn't want." She held her right hand out, palm down, at a level. "A Bronze comes in from his practice yard with a bruise the size of an egg, and he's seen that hour. There's a carter on the bench outside with his foot gone under a wheel, and he's no tier at all. He's seen when the list's done; and the list is never done, so mostly he gets up and goes home on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:279:She let the hand fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:281:"In the city I healed Bronzes with bruises. Out here I set legs. I'm not going back and I'm not stopping. The route is the only thing I own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:285:"Did you mind?" she said. "Leaving it, and not being told any more what you were."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:287:"I know what I am. I'm twelve times late." Oryn reached for her boot. "Go to sleep. That leg knits at night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:289:Cael lay awake a while after the others. He had supposed, for eighty days, that he was the only person past the Line who had walked out from under a measure, and that the rest had merely been born beyond it. Here was another. She had not been thrown out, and she had not run; she had looked at a list that was kept in the wrong order, and gone to find a place where she could keep it in the right one, and all she had asked of that place was a road to do it on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:291:He had a book, and she had a route, and he did not think that either of them owned much else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:297:The road had brought them along a valley for a day and a half, through alder and rough pasture, with the mountain standing over their right shoulders the whole way. From below it was a long grey wall with a notch in it, and he had looked back at the notch a good many times. On the evening of the eighty-third day he had seen a line of smoke ahead, and in the morning a fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:299:The hold stood on the far bank, in a hollow of the hill, behind a palisade of split logs with a gate in it, and between the gate and the water there was a low stone house with a turf roof. The ford itself was a hundred yards of pale gravel under a foot of quick water, wider than any he had crossed past the Line. On the near side a yoke of red oxen stood in the shallows with a chain between them, chewing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:301:"For the wheels," said Oryn. "He hooks on and draws them through, at a copper a wheel, and nothing for feet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:305:A man had come out of the stone house. He was old, and bent, and brown as a nut, and he had a hazel stick in his hand with notches cut all down one edge. He watched them cross as he would have watched a cart cross, counting. He nodded to the healer as to somebody who was owed that and more; and then his eyes went along five people in torn clothes, and a mule, and three boxes, and came back to Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:307:"You'll be the Lowmarch crew that took my paper. A carter said the one-armed man had given it to five." He looked past them, at the empty road, and at the river. "Where's your cart?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:309:Pike had said that he would ask it at his gate, and Cael had had the answer ready for six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:313:The old man took that in without any change of face. Then he jerked his head at the door of the stone house, and they followed him in, all but the healer, who had gone on up the bank toward the palisade with the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:315:It was one room, with a hearth and a bench and a scrubbed board under the window. Karis unrolled the long leaf on the board, and weighted its four corners with the old man's own tally-sticks, and stood back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:317:He could not read. Cael saw that in the first breath, from where the brown eyes went; they passed over the six columns to the two ruled lines down the middle of the leaf, and to what was drawn between them, and a thick cracked finger came down on the foot of a fan and began to climb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:321:"A wheel floats on it," said Brom. "It wants a man on a rope, up the hill side, leaning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:325:"Hollow under," said Lira. "Keep in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:327:He went on. They told him each mark as his finger reached it, in a word or two, as one names the stops on a road; and Karis, at his elbow, read him the number of each narrowing from her separate column. He stopped a long while at the seventh. He stopped longer at the eighth, where Cael had drawn a short black bar across both lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:329:"Two feet, clean through," said Karis. "He took our cart over it on his back." She touched Brom's sleeve. "No carter crosses that by himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:335:"It isn't there any longer," said Cael. "It went on the morning we came to it. There's a lip of rock thirty-one paces long that a mule can be led over, by somebody who knows how, and there's no road." He put his own finger on the margin, where Karis had written small. "And that's the herd. They're shale-backs, on the face over the bay. If anybody uses a Path beneath them, they run, and the hill comes with them, and that's how your cart went into the gorge. She's written that it's derived."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:337:"I have written that it was derived on the seventy-fifth day, and used on the seventy-eighth," said Karis. "I haven't written that it's tested. The ledge went and the declaration followed in the same breath; that's two causes in an accident, not one cause in a test."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:341:"Then they go round," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:343:He went to the hearth and lifted a stone out of it, and brought back a leather bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:345:"That's worth seventy to know before the first cart's in the gorge. I'd sooner pay it for this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:347:"There's one in it," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:349:"Aye, and it's yours." The brown eyes were not unkind. "I'd have sent three of mine over that top at the next dry spell, with a Force walking behind to shift what fell, for he's the only strong back I've got. I'll not, now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:351:He counted it onto the board in tens, seven heaps of thick worn silver, beside the foot of the fan. Then he took a burnt stick and made his mark at the bottom of Pike's slip, pressing hard: a horned head, broad between the horns, over three wavy strokes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:353:Karis had the purse open in her two hands, for she could hold it now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:355:"Earned, a hundred and thirty-two," she said. "In hand, a hundred and sixteen, and six owed to a smith."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:357:"Sixty-three lines," said Lira. She was leaning in the doorway on her taped foot, looking at the heaps. "And seventy marks. That's a mark a line, and seven over. What are the seven for?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:359:"For the last line," said Karis. "It's the only one that says no."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:1:# Chapter 30 — The Far Lip
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:3:Six of them went over the stone a little after noon, and the mule stayed where she was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:Oryn had hobbled her on the western side, with a full net and the two panniers still roped on, and the long shape in its oiled cover lashed along the top of the pack as it had been since a mountain. The healer looked at that cover for a breath before she turned away. Then she walked across the line with her hands open at her sides, and Cael saw her fingers close once, hard, as the count went out of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:7:They took very little. Brom had the middle box on his back, with the chips in it and the paper, since whatever a surveyor had left would want carrying home in something. Karis had the long cord and the short one. There was the hedging bill, and Lira's knife, and Seln's, and a skin of water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:9:Seln had the locked case as well, shut, on its strap across his chest. He had looked once at the mule's pack, and once at the stone, and had kept it on him, as he had kept it on every road. Everything else that they owned was either in a loft two days west or under the feet of a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:11:"East, along the old line of the road," said Karis. "I want the cord laid from this stone to the middle, a length at a time, as we laid it round the edge. If I have my sum right, the tenth will end among those stones. If I have it wrong, I should like to be told so by the ground and not by a table."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:13:So they went in as they had gone round. Brom held one end at the stone, and Karis walked the other out until it came tight, and stopped; then he came up to her, and she went on. The other four walked behind, in file, in the track that the cord left. The grass stood to Cael's hip. It closed over each of them as they passed and did not quite rise again, and when he looked back from the third length there was a lane trodden through it, as straight as a ruled margin, with a grey stone and a small brown mule at the far end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:He had his nine fragments as he always had them, and he held them. Wind leaned, since Lira was in front of him. Anchor had fallen quiet at the stake and had not asked since. He had opened nothing after a boulder, and he did not open anything now; but he walked those two hundred yards as the only one of the six who knew that he could have, and it was a strange thing to carry at the back of a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:17:At the eighth length the ground began to rise, gently, and the grass grew shorter. At the ninth it was at his knee, and he could see the whole of what stood on the swell in front of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:21:It lay on the crown of the rise like the lid of a great jar, grey and pale and quite bare, and the short turf came up to its edge and stopped there as cleanly as a mown field stops at a road. Round its rim, a stride in from the edge, stood the eleven stones. He had counted them from three hundred yards and had the number right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:23:They were grey, like the floor, and squared, each as thick through as a man's body. Not one of them was whole. The tallest stood twice Brom's height before it ended, and its neighbour came no higher than a knee, and between those two there was every height that a broken thing can have.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:27:She had gone up onto the floor with the cord's end in her hand. It came tight while she was still walking, and she stopped where it stopped her. She was standing at the middle of the round, as near as his eye could tell, with the eleven stones in a ring about her at an equal distance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:29:She did not say anything about her sum. She laid the end of the cord down very carefully on the grey stone, and set her notebook on it for a weight, and then she paced from where she stood to the rim, in four directions, counting. The four counts came out within a stride of each other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:31:"Ten cords," she said at last. Her voice was not altogether steady. "I worked it at the stone from the round, with a pencil. And I have walked it now with a rope, from the edge, in a straight line, and there is not a full stride between the two. I have never in my life had a figure come home like that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:33:Nobody had stepped onto the floor after her. Five people stood on the turf at its western edge, in the track of the cord, and looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:37:It was Lira who got the rest of them onto it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:39:She had not moved from the turf. She stood at the end of the trodden lane with her head tipped back, looking at the nearest of the tall stones, and she was frowning as she frowned at a card.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:41:"There's writing on that," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:43:Karis turned round on the grey as though she had been stung, and after that they all went up together, in a rush, and Cael could not have said afterward how he came to be on the floor. He was standing in front of an upright twice his own height, with the tape drawn tight across his side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:45:Round the foot of the stone, a hand above the floor, there ran a band a hand wide, and in the band there were lines. They were sunk in the grey to the depth of a fingernail, fine and even, some straight and some hooked, some crossing others at a slant; they went right round and met themselves. He walked to the next stone, the one no higher than a knee, and it had its band too. All eleven had. Cael looked for a letter anywhere in it, and found none that he knew, and he could not have sworn that they were letters at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:47:"Whose hand's that?" said Lira. "You've got every sort of alphabet there is, in those boxes. You copied half of them out yourself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:49:Karis was down on her knees already, with her nose a hand's breadth from the band. She went the whole way round the stone so before she answered, and when she got up there were two spots of colour high on her cheeks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:51:"None I own. And I own more than the registry prints."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:53:"Then copy it," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:55:"I mean to have every stroke of it. But the floor first, while the sun is high enough to measure by. A stone will keep." Her pencil was out already. "Pace it for me, rim to rim, through where the cord ends. I want yours, since yours are yards."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:57:It was only then, with the first rush over, that he noticed what was under him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:59:It was cold; he felt it through his soles, and it was a deeper cold than the turf's. It gave back no sound whatever. Lira had come across it in one boot of her own and one the size of a bucket, and Brom with a hundredweight on his back, and neither of them had rung. The low sun came over from the south-west and threw the shadows of eleven stones in long bars to the far side, and between the bars the grey shone a little, dully, as a slate shines that has been wetted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:61:He paced it from west to east, past her notebook, and had twenty-six strides. He paced it again from south to north, and had twenty-six. By the time he came back with the second figure, nine twists of paper lay opened in a row along the lid of the middle box, each with its chip set back in it and a small cross pencilled on the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:63:Karis and Brom had the long cord stretched across the whole width by then, to learn whether the thing was level; and it was the cord that found the edge for them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:65:"She won't lie down," said Brom, from the western side. He had his end drawn to the ground outside, and the cord stood clear of the grey for a yard in from the brink. "There's a rise on it. Look here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:67:Cael came and looked. All along the brink, as far as he could follow it to either hand, the floor turned up and rolled over on itself, smooth, with no corner anywhere, so that its crest stood a finger above the grey within and a hand above the turf outside. He had stepped over that a quarter of an hour ago and never marked it. A person running would have had to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:69:"A lip," said Karis, and wrote the word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:71:Something stirred in him at that rolled crest and asked its one question, as it asked of every arris and sill. Blade wished to know whether the thing was an edge; and for once Cael could not have told it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:73:They held the cord a hand high after that, from crest to crest, and she went along beneath it on her knees with the short one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:75:"Level," she said, at the far side. "The same gap the whole way over, to the thickness of a knot." She had the brass straightedge, with its pebble on a thread, and she laid it toward each quarter of the floor in turn. "And no pitch. I laid this brass on a mountain for seven days, and on every hundred of it the thread hung off its mark by so many hands. Here it hangs true whichever way I point it. A threshing floor is not as flat as this. A table is not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:77:"What is it, though?" Brom had let the box down beside her nine crossed twists, and he crouched with one big hand spread on the grey. "It's not slab; slab's got a grain. It's not laid, either. I can't find a course."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:79:"I am most of the way to telling you what it is not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:81:She tipped what was left of the chips into her lap, thirty of them or more, and sorted them by eye into three heaps on the floor. There were the honey-coloured stones, such as the walls of Ostrand were built of. There were the dark close ones, like a district hall's, and a pale kind with a sparkle in the break. She chose the freshest-broken from each heap and held the three against the grey together, in the slanting light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:83:"No." She let them fall. "Not the colour, and not the grain; it has no grain at all. I have a piece of every sort of dressed stone between Denvash and the Line in this box, with a date to each, and not one family of them comes near it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:87:He put both palms on the floor. It was smooth, and very slightly rough under the skin, like the shell of an egg. He moved his hands outward over it as far as his ribs would let him, feeling for the thing that every floor he had ever knelt on possessed somewhere: a crack, or a line where one piece met the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:89:His fingers found none. He knew that fingers could be wrong on a surface so close, and that the others were all looking at three small stones in a researcher's lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:91:He opened the read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:93:It was the same thing that he had done at the line, done now for work, and he did not count it a third. For a pace and a half round his knees, and a pace and a half straight down, he had the weight of what he was kneeling on, and it was one thing. There was no bed under it, and no joint through it, and no place where it changed. The short upright stood within his reach, and he took in the foot of that as well. It went down into the floor as a horn goes into a skull, and he could find no line between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:97:He had known since a hill of plates what he thought of a reading that nobody else could check, and it had not altered. So he took the pencil and wrote in Karis's table, under her *level*, the only part of what he knew that any one of them could have got with a hand and a pair of eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:99:*No seam.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:101:"Nor at the foot of the stones?" said Karis. She had read it upside down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:103:"Not that I can find."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:105:"Then I shall write that two of us looked." She put it down. "It is a poor sort of catalogue. *Round. Level. No seam. Matches nothing.* I have written four things about it and three of them are that something is absent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:109:She began on the bands when the sun was a little past its height, and it took the rest of the afternoon. She would not copy by eye, since she said that an eye puts in what it expects to see. She laid a clean sheet against a band, and had Lira hold it flat with both hands, and rubbed the side of her pencil over it until the lines came up white out of the grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:111:She took a sheet to a stone. She numbered each in its corner, and wrote beside the number how high the stone stood and which way it faced; and Brom carried them to the box one at a time, on the flat of his two hands, as he would have carried a tray of eggs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:115:"There is nothing here," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:117:He had let them work. Cael had seen him go about the floor, in no hurry, with his hands behind him and his eyes down; he had been to the foot of each of the eleven stones, and all round the outside of the lip, and twice he had gone off into the grass and come back. Now he stood at the middle, by the cord's end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:119:"I have been looking for what we were sent to fetch. A case, I supposed, or a tripod, or a chain. A peg. The mark where a peg had been." He turned one hand over. "There is nothing on this floor but ourselves, and nothing at the foot of any stone. I have walked a round in the grass at twenty yards from the lip, and another at forty. I find our own track, coming in from the west. I find nothing that a person has set down, or dropped, or driven."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:121:"It's been five years," said Brom. "By your stake."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:123:"It has. Leather would be gone, and wood. Brass would not, nor iron. And I should expect to see where a thing had lain, on turf that has never been grazed." He looked at Cael. "I do not say that nothing was ever left. I say that I cannot find it, and that I have looked as well as I know how."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:125:Cael had expected a good many things of this place, and that had not been among them. He stood on a grey floor with the paper's second sentence going through his head: instruments, and abandoned, and a site. Somebody had sat at a ruled desk and written that, with the sum in words and again in a bracket, and had sent it over a river with a hundred marks in a bag. It had lain a month under a stone. A captain had read it twice and put it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:129:"He said he'd write what was done," said Lira slowly. She had her two hands spread on a sheet of paper against the ninth stone. "The old man. If the clerk's mark never goes on it, the box stays shut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:131:"Then it stays shut," said Brom. "We've walked four days for a line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:135:Cael looked at the sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:137:It had come round into the west while they worked, and it stood now above the hills that they had crossed two days ago. He put his arm out straight and laid his hand along the skyline, fingers together, and then his other hand on top of that. The lower edge of the sun sat on his upper knuckle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:139:Two hands. He had said it at the eastern runs that morning, with a print in front of him, and Seln had said that he would hold him to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:143:He weighed it. He had set a limit, and he knew what a limit was worth that gave by a quarter of an hour the first time it was leaned on. He also knew that nine sheets out of eleven was a thing she would carry for the rest of her life. And the hillside was as empty as it had been at noon. No bird had got up. Nothing had stirred the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:145:"The two," he said. "No more. Seln, go round the far side once, by the east, and see that we've missed nothing under the lip. When you're back, we're in that lane and walking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:149:"I did. I'm spending a quarter of an hour that I told you I wouldn't, and I want it entered against me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:151:Seln gave the small bow that he gave to an order he disliked, and went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:153:Oryn had sat down on the western lip with her boot off. She had watched the whole afternoon from there, pressing the strip on her heel now and then with her thumb, and she had not once asked what any of it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:155:"It's a queer thing," she said, to nobody. "I've been on a great many floors with people on them. This is the first where I couldn't have told you which of you was tired."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:157:"I feel thirteen," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:161:"I do, though. That's what it's like. The year before my Kindling I used to run the lanes at Fenmark against the big girls, with nothing in me, just legs, and I'd forgotten there was ever such a person." She shifted her palm for the pencil. "I didn't think that I'd meet her again. I'm not sure I mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:165:Karis finished the eleventh sheet at the quarter-hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:167:She numbered the last corner and rolled the sheet, and for once she did not stop to look at what she had got. Brom had set the box at the foot of the tallest stone, on the eastern side, an hour ago, to keep ten sheets of paper out of the north wind. He knelt there now with his back to it and reached behind him for the strap, and Cael went to steady the weight onto his shoulders, since that was a thing that could be done with one arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:169:"There's no ground under this bit," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:171:He was looking down past his own knee. The box stood a yard from the lip; and beyond the lip, on that side, the turf that came up to the floor's edge everywhere else was not there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:173:Cael leaned out over the rolled crest. The slope fell away to the east more steeply here, and at some time the earth beneath the rim had slumped and been carried off, so that the grey stone stood out over nothing, like the eave of a roof. Thigh-deep below it there was bare ground, trodden as hard as a threshing floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:175:There was a print in it, directly under his face. It was as broad as a palm, without a claw, and its edge was sharp where it had broken the morning's frost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:177:He had time to know what it was. Then something shifted, four yards along the foot of the overhang, and it was Seln. He was down on one knee at the border of the trodden earth, where he must have been for some minutes, with one hand lifted a little from his side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:179:"I had been about to call you," he said, very low, without turning his head. "I find that I do not care to raise my voice in this place. Bring the others round by the turf, and bring them quietly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:181:They came, all of them, and stood where the ground still was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:185:He went over what lay before that mouth a piece at a time, as he went over everything. The prints lay one upon another, hundreds deep, so that it was only at the margins that he could tell a single foot. To one side there was a heap of bones, deer for the most part, cracked and grey. There was scat, old and new. Tufts of coarse hair had caught on the underside of the stone lip, at the height of a low grey back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:189:There were a good many such places. He could see the first four. They went back into the dark in a row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:191:Nothing was in them. He checked that twice. The hollow was empty, as far as light went, and no sound came out of it, and the wind brought him only the smell of cold earth and something rank and dry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:193:Seln had not risen, and he had touched nothing. He was reading, with his head a little on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:195:"This morning," he said, very low. "Before we had laid the first cord. More than six. I should say eight, or nine; they have gone over each other's marks and I cannot do better." He pointed, without lifting his hand from his knee, at the newest prints. They led away from the mouth, down the slope, to the east. "Out. All of them, and none back since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:197:"Then where are they?" said Lira. It was hardly a breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:199:"I do not know. I have seen no animal on this hillside since the sun rose, and I have been looking for nothing else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:201:Brom had come up on Cael's other side, with the box on his back. He was looking at the bones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:203:"Why here?" he said. "There's a thousand hollows in a country like this. There's a whole mountain two days west with caves in it. Why would a thing with teeth choose to lie under the one floor that the carters won't walk on?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:205:Seln did not turn his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:209:It lay on the cold air. Cael heard it, and so did the rest, and he watched it reach each of them in turn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:211:A stillhound hunted what went out of a person. Every hold in that country kept its Paths in its pockets on a road because of it; and here was a place, a quarter of a mile across, where a Path could not be taken out of a pocket by anybody. A deer that wandered over that line was a deer. A carter who did was a man with a stick. And five people who had cleared a ford with a declaration and a knife were standing at the door of the den with a hedging bill and two knives, and a healer whose hands had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:213:Lira was looking west.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:215:They all were. He saw their heads turn, one after another, across twenty-six strides of grey floor and the eleven stones, to the lane that the cord had left in the grass. It ran away from them down the slope, straight, pale in the low light. The grey stone at its end was too small to see. He knew where it was. From the middle of the floor it lay two hundred and twenty yards to the west, and they were standing on the wrong side of the floor from it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:217:Then they looked at the sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:221:Then four of them looked at Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:223:She stood a little apart, with her broad hands hanging open. She had told them at noon what they had in her, on this side of a stone, and she met their eyes one after another and did not pretend that it had altered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:225:And then, all five, they looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:227:He had the ruling. He was the nearest thing that they had to a captain, and it was his to say which way and how fast. That was the whole of what was in those five faces. Not one of them was asking him anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:229:He stood with it. *Nothing that walks in can fight.* It was true of five of the six. He had known since a boulder that it was not true of the sixth, and he had told nobody, and he was the person they were all waiting on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:235:It took him less time than it took Brom to shift the box higher on his shoulders and draw the strap tight. He had always written at the start of a thing. He did not know how to begin one without it, and he thought that this might be the last quiet minute of the day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:237:*Ninety-sixth day. The Fallow Ring, at the eastern lip of the floor. Sunset.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:239:*The circle is perfect. The stakes are the registry's. The dogs are inside. Everyone's Path dies at the line and mine didn't, and I've told nobody, and I'm going to need to think about why I haven't. Not tonight. Tonight we're inside the one place on the continent where a stillhound can't lose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:241:He looked at what he had put.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:243:There was a line that came next. He had written it at the foot of every entry since a shelf above a valley with no name, and for the last forty-three days it had ended in the same word. His pencil went to the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:245:He did not write it. It would not have been true, and he did not yet know what would be. He shut the book on an entry that stopped short, for the first time since he had crossed the Line, and put it away in Hesk's satchel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:247:Something lifted in him then, soft, behind the other eight. It came as it came at every dusk, in a meadow or on a road or at the turn of a carter's ruts. It was offering to take him out of the light. He stood ten cords and more inside the line, on the far side of a floor where a Shadow of fifteen years had found nothing to lift, and it came up in him exactly as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:249:He told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:251:The sun was half gone. Along the foot of the eleven stones the floor had turned the colour of milk. Lira had drawn her knife, and Karis had the last sheet rolled inside her coat, and the healer was lacing her boot with quick hard pulls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:253:Seln had not yet got up off his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:1:# Chapter 28 — Sixty-Two Cords
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:5:She did not go alone, since Seln had made a rule and she had lost no time in seeing the sense of it. Lira had the watch with her. Lira sat down in the grass ten yards short of the place, with her back turned and her knife across her knees, to look at everything that was not a ring of stones; and Karis went on the last ten yards by herself, with a notebook, and a pencil, and no lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:7:There could be no lamp. She had lit one in a yard two evenings ago and been absurdly glad of it, and she would not have set a point here for all the paper in Ternhall. She knelt down in the frozen grass on the western side of the stone and laid the notebook open on her knee. The stars were out. She could see the white of the page and not much else, and she resolved to write large, one line to a leaf, and sort it by daylight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:11:She had been taught to do it at fourteen, by attending to a place behind the breastbone, and it had been years since she had needed to do it on purpose; it was simply there when she wanted it, as a clerk is there behind a counter. It came now. It hung at the edge of her sight where it had always hung, small and steady and yellow-white, and it had about it the particular quality of attention that she had never been able to describe to anybody who did not have one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:15:It went out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:17:There was no flicker in it, and no fading. She had half expected a lamp turned down, and it was a lamp that had never been in the room. She stood a stride inside the line with her heart going hard, and looked for the place behind her breastbone, and found the place. There was nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:19:She stepped back, and it was there. It hung where it always hung, with the same steadiness, as though it had been waiting behind a door and had not noticed that the door was shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:She counted them, and at each crossing she made herself attend to one thing only, as she would have made a student attend. The third time she watched for the instant, and it had no width that she could find. The fifth time she went over slowly, a hand's breadth at a time, to learn whether it dimmed; it did not, and then it was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:25:The seventh time she tried to hold it. She fixed the whole of her will on that small bright mark and carried it forward as one carries a full cup, and it went out between one heartbeat and the next without the least sense that anything had been taken from her. That was the part she liked least. She had not been robbed. She had merely looked down and found her hands empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:29:She had been empty for nine days. She had spent every point she owned on a mountain in eight seconds, and afterward she had not been able to strike a flint. But in all those nine days the sigil had been there. She had called it on the first night under the pale rock, and it had come, faithful as ever, and when she asked it for a point it had simply had nothing to give her. That had been a counter with a clerk behind it and an empty till.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:31:This was a different thing altogether. A stride beyond that stone she was three parts full; a healer had told her so with two fingers on her wrist. The till was heavy. There was no clerk, and no counter, and no room for them to stand in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:33:*Empty: sigil present, reserve gone.* She wrote it large, by feel. *Here: reserve present, sigil gone.* She turned the leaf. *These are not the same condition and must not be entered under one word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:37:It was the only measurement that she could take tonight, and she took it with the stubbornness of a person who has been given one tool. From the turn of the ruts to the stone it was eighty of Cael's strides, by his count, and his were a yard. She paced it herself, there and back, and her own count was a good deal larger, as it always was. She wrote both. She paced it a second time and was one stride out, and wrote that too, and the mean of the pair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:39:She tried next to make the line finer than a stride, and could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:41:A hand held out across the stone did nothing at all; the sigil hung where it was. She had watched the healer stand so at dusk, for a long while, and had not asked her what she felt. An arm did nothing. But when she stood astride the stone itself, with one boot on either side, she had the sigil or had it not according to how she leaned, and Karis would not write down a lean. She put: *Narrower than one stride of mine. No finer figure tonight. Wants daylight, and a second person to watch my feet.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:43:She sat back on her heels. The grass was stiff with frost, and beyond the stone the ground went down and up again to eleven shapes that she could only just tell from the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:45:It was very tidy, and she made herself look at that, because it was what she was afraid of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:47:Five people had walked up to this place in a row, an arm's length apart. She was an Ember, and Lira a Wind, and Brom an Iron Skin; there was a Shadow among them of a higher tier than any of the rest, and a Tide who had been trained in a healing house. Karis had sat at lectures on what an Arbiter was. She had copied out, in a restricted room, a good many pages on the subject, and on one point every page agreed: it was a person's own. It woke for that one person, on one morning, and named a single gift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:51:Her pencil had come down on the page. She looked at the point of it, resting on white paper in the starlight, and she knew exactly what kind of sentence it was about to begin. It would have had *as though* in it. It would have said what the thing was like; and a likeness, set down in a notebook at midnight, is a hypothesis that has not yet had the honesty to call itself one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:55:*Five rows,* she wrote instead, and ruled them by touch. *K. L. B. S. O. Each: gone at the stone, going east. Back at the stone, coming west. One stride. Observed by all five at once on the 95th; by me alone, nine times, that night.* And under that, because she kept that sort of account as well: *I am frightened by how well it agrees. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:57:There were six of them on that road, and she had ruled five rows. She considered that. It was right as far as it went; one could hardly enter the stopping of a thing that had stopped years ago, on a brass plate, in front of witnesses. She left a space at the foot of the leaf all the same. She had no idea what she expected to put in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:67:She gave them the procedure at first light, with the frost smoking off the grass, and she gave it once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:69:"We are going to walk round it," she said. "I want to know what shape it is and how far about, and I can't see it, so I shall have to find it sixty times with my feet. Lira goes a stride inside, the whole way. She is the instrument. She says nothing unless it comes back to her, and then she says *out*, and steps in again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:71:"Why me?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:73:"Because yours sits nearest the skin of any of us. You had your hand up before the rest of us had finished the stride." Karis shook out the long cord, with its scraps of red wool. "I walk on the line itself, with this end. Brom has the other, at the last mark. When the cord comes tight between us I stop, and find the line under me, and that is a station. Brom cuts a turf there with the bill and turns it over, so that we leave a black mark in the grass at every cord. Then he comes up to me, and we do it again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:75:"And I'm in front," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:77:"You are in front, reading whatever is on the ground, and you will stand where I wave you. He writes." Her pencil pointed at Cael. "The number of the station, and one figure more, which I'll give him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:79:"What do I do?" said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:81:"You keep the stone. Somebody must be standing at the place where we began, so that we know it when we come round to it from the other side. And I should like one of us to be outside the whole time, with the mule, where an Arbiter is an Arbiter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:85:They set off northward, with the ring on their right hands and the ruts some forty yards away on their left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:87:It went slowly at first. Lira walked with her arms a little out from her sides and a look of deep concentration, like a person carrying a bowl filled to the brim; twice in the first cord she said "Out," sharply, and stepped to her right. Karis felt her way along beside her. At the cord's end she stopped, and shuffled a hand's breadth inward, and a hand's breadth out, and stood still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:89:"Here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:93:"One," said Cael, and wrote it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:97:"That is where the next mark would fall if the line ran straight," she said. "It won't. Watch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:99:They walked the third cord. Lira kept to her stride inside, and Karis to the line, and when the cord came tight Karis was standing well to the right of Seln's wand. She had her short cord out already, and she laid it on the grass from the wand to her own boots and read the knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:101:"Six feet and a half. Inward. Write that beside the three."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:103:He wrote it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:105:It was the third line of a new table, and it was of another kind from the sixty-three that he had written on a mountain. There a cord and a clock had shown what anybody could check. Here the only instruments were two people's Arbiters, going out and coming back; he had none to offer, and so he held the pencil for the people who had, and it surprised him a little that he did not grudge it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:109:It was Brom who found the iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:111:He had come up to cut the third turf, and he set the bill's point where Karis's heels had been and leaned on the haft; and there was a sound that did not belong on that hillside, steel on iron, a short dull clink. Brom took the bill away as if it had burnt him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:113:"There's a thing in the ground," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:115:It stood a boot's width from the place where she had stopped, and the grass had hidden it. When Brom parted the stems with the side of his hand they all saw it: a square bar of iron, as thick as two fingers, driven straight down into the turf so that a hand's length of it stood clear. It was brown with rust and flaking, and its head had been beaten a little flat, and spread at the edges, by something heavy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:117:Lira had come out across the line to look, and she crouched and tipped her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:119:"There's a mark on it. On this side. And figures."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:121:The mark was stamped into the iron, a thumb's breadth below the head, and Cael knew it before the rust let him see the whole of its shape. He had seen it painted over a hearth in a wool-factors' counting room, and cut in a lintel at the last station on a western road; it was printed in the corner of the sheet that lay rolled in the middle box, back at the turn of the ruts. Under it somebody had punched two numerals.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:125:She had gone very still, with the cord's end in her fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:127:"That's the registry's stamp," said Lira. "That's theirs. What's it doing in a field two days past a fence that isn't on their map?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:129:Seln had come back down the line with his wand, and he stood over the stake and looked at it without bending. His mild face told Cael nothing whatever, and by now Cael knew that for a kind of news.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:131:Something in Cael had turned toward the thing as well. Anchor asked now, more quietly than it had ever asked, as one craftsman takes note of another's work. *There.* It was not a question. Here was a point that somebody else had fixed, and told to stay, and it had stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:133:He put that down, and looked at what was left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:135:He had laid two things side by side at a wash four days ago, and had refused to rule a line between them for want of a third. A query had come down to a registry from somewhere above it, asking which way certain people had gone. A paper had come over a river with a hundred marks, in the hand of somebody who had learned to write at a ruled desk. And here, on the edge of a place where a carter would not put a wheel, was a bar of iron with a registry's stamp on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:139:"Don't touch it," said Karis. Her voice shook a little, and then steadied. "Nobody is to pull at it, or clean it. I have three stations and I want sixty. It has stood there a good while without us and it will stand until we come round." She turned her back on it with a visible effort. "Brom. Cut the turf a foot short, and I'll allow for the foot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:141:"You're leaving it," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:143:"I am finishing a count. If I stop at every wonder on this hillside we shall be here at dusk with half a shape, and I had rather have a whole one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:145:They went on. Cael walked the fourth cord with his head turned back over his shoulder. The bar stood in the grass where they had left it, brown and small, beside a square of black turf; and he thought that it was a strange thing to have crossed a Line, and a mountain, and ninety-six days of road, and to be met at the far end of all of it by two numerals that somebody at a counter had signed for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:149:By the tenth station she no longer needed the wand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:151:"It is in my wrist," she said. She was standing with the cord's end held out before her, and as Cael watched she swung it to the right, without looking down, and stopped. "There. Lira?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:153:"In," said Lira, a stride beyond her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:155:He laid the short cord down all the same, since a wrist is not an instrument, and wrote what the knot said. He had written that figure nine times already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:157:They went round the north side with the ruts beside them. He could see where the carts had gone, forty yards off, keeping the same distance from the line the whole way, as exactly as if the carters had had a cord of their own; and he supposed that in a sense they had. A man who walked in front of a mule here for twenty-two years, with a Path in him, would have learned to the yard where he ceased to be comfortable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:159:From the north the ring looked different. The eleven uprights had closed up and overlapped, and between two of the tallest he had his first clear sight of what lay among them. It was a floor, grey and pale and perfectly level, raised a little above the grass like the top of a low table; and the morning light lay on it without a shadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:161:The ruts came back to their old line on the eastern side, and went away from them over the next swell. Seln was waiting there, a cord ahead, and he was down on one knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:163:"Here," he said, when they came up. "And there, and beyond that tussock. I should like all of you to see it before it is trodden."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:165:There were paths in the grass, and Cael would not have found them. They were no wider than his two hands, and the stems had not been broken, only pressed aside, low down, by something that went under them rather than through. Three of them crossed the line within twenty yards of one another. Where the nearest ran over a patch of bare ground there was a print in the frost-softened earth, as broad as a palm, with no mark of a claw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:167:"Going in?" said Lira, very low. "Or coming out?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:169:"Both. That one is this morning's, and it points inward. This is older, and it goes out. They use these as a hold uses its gate." Seln had not risen. "I cannot give you a number from a path. I can say that it is not one animal, or two. And I can say that I have gone the whole of the north side and not found a single run. They come and go by the east."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:171:"He told us they lived in them," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:173:"He did. I had hoped that he was repeating something." Seln stood, and dusted his knee. "I have seen no animal. I should like that entered exactly. I have seen where they walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:175:Cael looked at the print, which was the breadth of the ones beside a white stone in a valley, and of the ones on a gravel bar; and then he looked at the sun, which was a third of the way up the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:177:"We go in at noon," he said. "Not before; it's the hour they've never once been seen abroad, on a stretch or at a ford. And we're out again and on the west side of that stone with the sun two hands above the hills. Whatever isn't done by then isn't done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:179:"Two hands," said Seln. "I shall hold you to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:181:They took the south side at a better pace, since by now each of them had the work by heart. Lira drifted out twice more and said so. Brom's turfs lay behind them in a long curve, black on the pale grass; and when Cael looked back along them from the fiftieth, he could see the curve closing on itself like a hoop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:183:From the south he had the whole of it in one view, for the first and only time. The grey stone was away on his left hand, with a small brown mule beside it. Beyond it the ruts swung out round the north in their wide curve. The floor lay in the middle of everything, with its eleven broken stones, and on the far side, where no turf had been cut, there was the low place in the grass where Seln had knelt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:191:They had come up from the south, with the grey stone in plain sight ahead and a square brown figure standing beside it with a mule. Karis reached the end of the sixty-second cord, and stopped, and found the line, and Brom cut his turf; and the stone was still some way off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:197:She stood there with her eyes shut, and her lips moved for some time. Cael knew what she was doing, since she had the length of that cord in feet, to the knot, in a notebook that nobody else had read, and had paced her own stride twice against his in the dark. He would have given a good deal to check the sum; he had only her figures for the half of it, and so he waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:201:"Round a field," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:203:"Round a field, and home to the stone that it started from." She opened her eyes. "I have one offset written sixty-two times in his hand. There is only one shape that will do that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:213:It lay in the air a breath. Lira was looking at the stones, and Brom had the bill over his shoulder and was counting his turfs. Oryn, who had stood at that stone since first light, was watching five people come toward her along a line that she could not see. Nobody took the words up, and Karis did not seem to look for anyone to do so. She had entered a property of the figure, and she bent to tie the cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:215:Cael heard it. He put it with the three other things, a little apart from them, since it was of another kind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:219:Cael went after him, and they stood over the iron together. In the full light it was plainer than it had been: the square bar, and the spread head, and the stamp. Seln crouched now, without laying a finger on it, and looked at the rust, where it had lifted in leaves, and at the turf round the bar's foot, which had grown up close against the metal on every side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:221:"Five years in the weather. A cold term either side."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:225:"From rust, and from the grass. It has had time to forget that anything was done to it." He tilted his head to bring the stamp into the light. "I have seen iron of this pattern given out across a counter, and signed for. A man answers for what he takes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:227:"Thirty-one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:229:"I do not know what the number counts, and I shall not guess." Seln rose. He looked from the stake to the ring, and back along the line of turfs to the stone, as he would have looked over a room that somebody had left in a hurry. "It stands a boot's width from where she stopped. She found that place with her feet, this morning, knowing nothing. Whoever drove this found the same place to the same boot's width."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:231:"With a cord," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:233:"With a cord, or a chain. That is what I should like you to carry away." He nodded at the iron. "You drive them where you've measured to. Not where you've measured from."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:235:Cael looked at it. A stake marked the far end of a measure; somebody had stood at another place, with the other end of a line in his hand, and had paid it out until it reached exactly here.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:237:He looked along the only line there was. It ran from the stake's foot, across two hundred yards and more of pale grass, to the middle of eleven stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:1:# Chapter 29 — A Stride Inside
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:5:"Smallest first," she said. She had ruled a fresh leaf into five rows and given it to Cael, with the pencil. "A one-armed man has told us that nothing can be declared in there, and five of us felt our Arbiters go, and I believe both. I believed in a ledge as well. There are prints in the grass on the far side of this field, and I am not going to learn that he was mistaken by having Lira crack the sky open a quarter of a mile from whatever left them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:7:"I'd not crack it on purpose," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:9:"You did not crack it on purpose the last time. That is rather my point." Karis looked round at them. "So I go first, with one point, unlit. If it sets, I shall know it, and nobody else will, and we stop there and think. If mine does not, then Brom. Then Seln. You are last of the four, and you go only if the three of us have had nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:11:"And outside the line nobody tries anything," said Seln. "It is a road. I should like that kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:15:The sun stood as high as it would go at that time of year, pale and small, and their shadows lay short on the frosted grass. They had gathered on the western side of the grey stone, with the turn of the ruts at their backs and the ring two hundred yards and more in front. Oryn had the mule by the cheek-strap a few yards behind the rest, and her free hand tucked into her armpit for warmth; she had watched the whole of the morning from that stone without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:17:Cael looked at the leaf that he had been given. There were five rows on it, each headed with a letter, and his own was not among them. That was as it should be, since he had been handed the pencil for the very reason that there was nothing of his to enter. He had kept the count at a great many trials of other people, and had thought himself used to the seat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:19:Karis stepped over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:21:Cael saw her shoulders tighten as her foot came down, and ease again; she had done this nine times in the dark, she had told them at first light, and it plainly did not grow pleasanter. She stood a stride inside the line, with her back to the ring, and her eyes went slowly along the slope in front of her from left to right, as he had seen them go over a district floor before a bout.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:23:"I can see them," she said. "Every place. I could put sixteen down on that bank and tell you where each would sit, to the hand. That is not altered in the least."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:25:"That's only knowing how," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:27:"It is knowing how, and it is still here; I want it written that it is still here." Her right hand had come up. "Now I am going to set the first, eleven paces off, by that tuft with the frost on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:29:The hand dipped, the small exact dip of a person dotting a page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:33:"No," said Karis. She tried it a second time, and a third, quickly. "No. It has not set. A lecturer would say that the declaration did not render. And that is not the whole of it. When I am empty and try to set one, I feel myself reach into the purse and close my fingers on nothing. Here I am not even reaching. I am three parts full, and I can't find the hand to reach with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:41:Karis thought about that with real care.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:45:She stepped back over. He saw the colour come into her face as the foot came down, and her eyes go, for an instant, to something at the edge of her sight that nobody else could see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:47:"Back," she said. "First row. Write it as I gave it: every place seen; none set; no cost."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:49:He wrote it, and looked at it a breath longer than a line needed. He had sat in a whitewashed room for the better part of a year while that small exact person laid points on a wall for him to study, and he carried one of them still, the fifth of his nine; and he had not known until this noon that there was a place anywhere where her hand could make that dip and have nothing come of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:55:He had done that on purpose, Cael thought, so that all of them should be able to see his forearms: the left with its pale dry seam from the heel of the hand to the middle, and the right, which had never had a mark on it. He stood a stride inside and held them out a little before him, with the palms down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:57:For forty-three days he had refused that Path, and Cael had watched him do it on a ferry, and in a wash, and under a cart on a crust of rock. He had seen the grey start at those wrists whenever a load came on suddenly, and each time he had seen Brom put it down again, like a man pressing a lid onto a pot; it had been the hardest kind of work, and it had been done every hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:59:Now, for the first time since a river, Brom told it to come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:61:"Skin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:63:He said it aloud, in his ordinary voice, as he might have called a dog that was lying a yard off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:65:They waited, and the big brown wrists stayed the colour of wrists. He turned them over to look at the insides, and turned them back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:67:"Well," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:71:"What's it like?" said Lira. "You said a door, yesterday. Closing behind you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:73:"It's that. The door's shut, and I'm on the wrong side of it from where I keep the thing." He let his arms fall. "It's queer. I've not used it since the ford, and I'd have told you I'd got used to going without. But I always knew where it was. I could feel it in the next room, as you can feel a fire through a wall. There's no next room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:75:He looked about him, and his eye fell on the middle box, which sat on the grass just outside the line where he had put it down. He leaned across the stone and took it by its two beckets, and lifted it over, and stood holding it against his chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:81:"Weight. I wanted to know whether a hundredweight was a hundredweight in here, with nothing behind me." He set it down inside the line, quite gently. "It is. So that's what I've got. Write that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:83:"I am to write that a box weighs the same."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:87:Cael wrote it. He wrote *Skin called aloud; none*, and under it, since the row was Brom's, *lifted a hundredweight; no difference*; and it seemed to him that of the two findings the second was the one that Brom would take home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:89:Seln had been standing at the end of the row with his hands behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:91:"I shall be brief," he said. "I have very little to show."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:93:He stepped across, and for a breath he only stood, with his eyes half shut. Cael knew what he was reaching for, since he carried a piece of the same thing himself, sealed, and had felt it lift in him at every dusk for two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:95:"There is nothing to lift," said Seln. "I looked for it as one feels for a banister. It is not that it resists. It is not there to be asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:97:"Then you're only a man in a coat," said Lira. "Like the rest of us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:99:"I have been only a man in a coat for a great deal of my working life. Will you all look at the mule for a count of three, and then look back?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:101:It was an odd request, and they did it. The mule was regarding a tussock; Cael counted three, and turned his head, and the place where Seln had stood was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:103:There was the grass, and the short noon shadows, and a stride beyond the stone a patch of bent stems where two boots had been. Lira made a small sound. She had the best eyes of the five, and Cael saw them go the whole length of the slope and come back with nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:105:"Four yards to your right," said a mild voice. "And down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:107:He was crouched low against a tussock of the pale grass, side on to them, with his face turned down into the crook of his arm and his hands drawn up into his sleeves. He had put himself to the south of them, where the low sun came from behind him into their eyes. There was no Path in it, and no trick that a child could not have understood once it was shown; and it had still taken five people, all of them looking for him, the better part of a breath to find a man in a dark coat on an open hillside at noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:109:He stood up and brushed the frost from his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:111:"I've a knife and fifteen years. It was never most of what I do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:113:"That isn't a trial of anything," said Karis. But she was looking at the bent grass, and at where he had got to, and her pencil hand had twitched. "I suppose that it is a trial of you. Third row. *Path sought; none. Practitioner not found for a count of two.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:115:"A count of three," said Seln, coming back over the stone. "I should not like the record to flatter you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:119:Lira went last of the four, and she did not make a joke of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:121:She sat down on the grass outside the line and took off the great boot, and unwound the whole of a spare shirt, and stood up on her own taped foot with the frost under it. She was to run on nothing for a fortnight yet, and nobody reminded her of it; this was one stride, and she had plainly weighed it against a healer's month before she touched the lace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:123:"I want all of it," she said. "Not a lean. I'm going to ask for the whole thing, the way I'd ask on a card with a panel watching, and I'm going to ask along the line, that way, north. So if the old man's wrong I come down in grass and not on top of one of you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:127:"I know what it'll be. I heard it come back off a mountain." Lira's face was pale. "It went off by itself, that morning. I never told it to. I've got to walk into a place with dogs in it on this foot, and I'd like to know, before I do, whether it can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:129:There was no arguing with that. Cael had the ruling, and he gave it with a nod, and she stepped over the stone and turned to face north, with the ring on her right hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:131:He did not watch her go. If a one-armed man had been wrong for thirty years, the answer to it would come out of the grass on the eastern side, a quarter of a mile off, where three runs crossed a line; and somebody had to have his eyes there when the air broke. He fixed them on that place and kept them on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:133:So it was his own feet that told him. Wind stood up in them to go with hers, harder than it had leaned in three months, and he put it down; and in the same instant the grass behind his shoulder hissed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:He turned his head. She was a yard and a half along the line from the place where she had planted herself, with the left foot forward and the right drawn up beside it, looking down at the pair of them on the frost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:139:"That was a step," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:141:She said it to her feet. Then she looked round at the row of them, and her eyes were very wide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:143:"I asked for all of it. I went up onto my toes and let the whole of both legs go, the way I'd go on a card, and I didn't stop it. I've stopped it every day for three months, just there, with my heels off the ground. I never touched it this time. Brom saw; he'd have seen if I'd held any back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:145:"She went like a hare off its form," said Brom. "For a yard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:147:For an instant Cael thought that she was going to weep, and then he saw that it was not that at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:149:"It's in me. It's not in the air."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:153:"I'd always thought of it as mine. They told me at Fenmark that it was a thing I *did*, and I believed them, and I was proud of doing it well. And every piece of the doing's still here. I can feel it. I could do that step again exactly." She set her right foot forward on its whole sole, and drew it back. "There's just nothing out there to take hold of."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:155:"Come out," said Oryn. "You've a bare foot on frost."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:159:"Oh," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:163:"Back." She had her hand flat against her own breastbone. "I felt it at the stone last night, but I wasn't looking then. I was looking this time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:167:Lira frowned, and hunted for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:169:"It doesn't come on like a lamp. It's just there, and it's where it was, like a clock that stopped and started again without losing the time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:171:Karis had Cael write that down as it was spoken, the whole of it, in the fourth row. She read it over his arm when it was done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:173:"That is the best of the four," she said. "It is the only one with a measurement in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:177:"I've spent three months not doing it," she said, to the boot. "I thought that was hard. It's nothing to standing there and asking with everything I've got and being given a step." She looked up at Cael. "I know what's left now. When it isn't me doing the *not*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:181:"Legs," said Lira. "And they're good ones. Somebody took trouble with them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:185:"Now me," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:187:She had given the mule's rein to Seln, and she came up to the stone at her own pace, rubbing her hands together, and looked at Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:189:"The wrist. The one I did, that I read at the fire."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:191:He held it out. She took it between both of hers, as she had taken a carter's palm on a plank at Thornwater, with two fingers of her right hand laid along the inside where the pulse was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:193:"This is how it goes," she said. She was speaking to Karis, since Karis was the one who would have it written. "It's the short reading; it costs me nothing. I'll have it running, and I'll count aloud so that you can hear where I am. We go over together, him and me. Then back. Then I'll want a third, and I'll tell you what when I've had the two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:197:"Nobody has that I ever met. That's why I walked two days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:199:Her lips began to move, and this time there was sound in them. "One. Two. Three." She nodded to Brom, and the two of them stepped over the stone together, the big man and the square woman, hand in wrist. "Four—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:201:It stopped. It did not trail away; it stopped as a word stops when a door is shut on the speaker. She stood a stride inside the line with Brom's forearm between her palms, and her mouth was a little open, and there was no count in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:203:Cael had seen that look once before, on a mountain, over his own ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:207:They stepped back, and her lips moved at once. "One. Two." She went all the way to ten this time, with her eyes shut, and Cael saw her shoulders come down as the last of the count went by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:209:"That's there. It's his, and it's sound, and I could tell you what he had for breakfast." She opened her eyes. "And a stride that way I'd my hands on a piece of warm meat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:211:"Is it Brom?" said Karis. "Forgive me; I must ask it. His Arbiter stopped at that stone, and yours did. If the thing that you read is his current, and his current is shaped by his Path, then perhaps there was nothing in him to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:213:"That's the third." Oryn had not let go of the wrist. "You. Stand where you are, this side. Give me the arm across."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:Brom stood on the western side of the stone, where he was an Iron Skin with his Path in the next room. He stretched out his left arm. Oryn kept her two hands on it, and she stepped over the stone by herself, so that she stood inside and he outside, with the mended wrist held between them across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:217:Her lips did not move at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:219:She stayed so for a count of twenty, and Cael kept it for her, since she could not. She shifted her fingers on the wrist, and pressed, and shifted them again; then she let go of Brom with her left hand, and bent, still holding him with the right, and laid the left on her own shin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:223:"Nothing. Not nothing in him. Nothing in my hands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:225:The wind went over the grass. Nobody at the stone had anything to put beside that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:227:"He's standing out there with everything he owns. I had it not a minute ago. And I can't so much as feel that he's alive, except that he's warm." She looked down at her two broad palms, as she had looked at them in a hollow of shale, and this time there was no puzzlement in her face. "They told me, and I never checked. Thirty rounds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:233:She stepped out. The count came back to her lips before her second boot was down, and she let it run to ten on Brom's wrist, with something like greed, and only then released him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:235:"So you'll all hear this, and you'll hear it once," said Oryn. "I'm coming in with you. I said I'd see him whole to the ninety-ninth, and I can't do that from a stone. But understand what you've got. If one of you puts a leg through something in there, I can pull it straight. I can splint it, and I can carry my end of you. I can't mend it. Not a scratch. Not till you're this side of that." She pointed at the stone with her chin. "Whatever happens in there happens to six people with no healer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:237:Cael looked at her as she said it. She had walked two days up an empty road to learn that the one thing she owned in the world, besides a route, would be taken out of her hands at a stone; and having learned it she was going in regardless, with her sleeves pushed back, to be of whatever use a pair of ordinary hands could be. He had not asked her to come. He did not think that he would have had the face to ask it, knowing what a stride had just cost her, and he saw that she would have come all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:239:"Fifth row," said Karis, in a small voice. "As she said it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:243:For a quarter of an hour after that, nobody at the stone had an eye to spare for anybody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:245:Cael watched it happen. Five people had each lost a thing, a stride from where they stood, and had it handed back, and every one of them had turned inward to look at it. Oryn was hobbling the mule, with her lips moving in a count that she did not seem to know she was keeping. Brom knelt by the middle box. Karis sat with her eyes shut, and Lira walked up and down on the great boot, a little apart, and Seln stood looking east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:247:There were five rows to be ruled fair before the leaf went into a box. He took it a cord along the line to the north, to Brom's first turf, where there was a flat place to kneel; and nobody marked his going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:249:He had seen that it would be so. He had been waiting for it since first light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:253:The finding in his book said that the hounds answered what went out of a person; and a fragment answering went out of him, as he had felt it go at a ford, where one burst had drawn three. That was the cost, and he set it down first. Against it there was the hour, at which no hound on any road had ever been seen abroad, and there was the choice of what to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:257:This was a different matter from a map. Six people were about to walk into a place where every one of them believed that all six were alike, and he was the only one who could find out whether that was so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:259:He opened it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:261:It came as it had always come, at once and without effort, like an eye that has been kept shut. For a pace and a half on every side he had the ground: the weight of the turf, and the frost standing in it, and a flat stone a foot down that he would never see. He had his own two boots, and the lean of his body over them. It was mass, and the way that mass meant to go, and nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:263:He was a pace short of the line. He walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:265:He had been certain, in the part of himself that kept accounts, of what would happen: a thing taken out of a Path would stop where Paths stopped. He had supposed it since a plank, as the others supposed it, and for the same reason, which was that no other sum came out even; and he had braced for the read to go from him as a count had gone from a healer's lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:269:The turf under his left boot was outside the line, and the turf under his right was within it, and he had the weight of both as he had the weight of his own hands. Nothing dimmed. He took another step, and a third, and the ground went on telling him what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:275:He walked in, as a man goes who means to look at the ground that he will presently lead people over, without haste, twenty strides toward the ring. A grey boulder lay there in the grass, as high as his hip, and he walked straight at it. He kept his face to the east; if anyone at the stone had glanced his way, there was only a person with a notebook going to see what lay beyond a rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:277:At the twentieth stride he let Wind have his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:279:It was a long way short of a burst. It was the smallest piece of what Lira's fighting had taught him, the lean that takes a body off a line a hair before the blow arrives; and he asked it for a foot. His weight went over without his deciding it. The boulder, which had been in front of him, was on his right hand, and he was past it, and the grass had hardly stirred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:281:Anybody watching from behind would have seen a person walk round a rock, and no more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:283:He stood on the far side with the tape drawn tight across his ribs and something going out of him into the cold air, small, as a breath goes out. He listened, and there was the wind, and the hiss of seed-heads, and far off toward the east a bird that got up and settled again. Nothing whatever came through the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:285:He shut them both. He put Wind down, and closed the read as one closes a book, and they went back to where the other seven lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:287:He did not know why. He set that beside a healer's word from a hollow of shale, and added nothing to it, as she had added nothing. Five people had lost what they had at that stone, each in a different way, and one of them had asked with her whole body and been given a step. And twenty strides inside it, the things that he carried had answered him as they answered him anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:289:He walked back along the line, and stepped out over the first turf, and came down to the stone with the leaf in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:291:Karis took it from him, and read down the five rows, nodding at each. Then she laid it on her knee, and took the pencil, and ruled a sixth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:295:She said it to the leaf. It was a clerk's question; she had a table with a row empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:297:He had perhaps a breath. He saw Lira, stamping her foot down into a boot the size of a bucket, with her face still stripped from that one step, and he saw a healer flexing her fingers; and he saw that the healer was the plainest reason of all, since she knew nothing of what he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:299:To tell it at that stone was to tell it to her, and he could not have said that the things he carried had answered him without saying what they were. There were nine, and every one of them had come out of a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:301:One had come out of the mild man who stood six feet off with his hands behind him, looking east. It was the very thing that Seln had reached for at this stone, not half an hour ago, and not found; and Cael had held it shut at every dusk for ninety-six days, so that nobody who might one day speak of it to a carter bound for a ferry should know where a boy had learned it. A healer spoke to every carter on seven roads. She would not have meant the least harm by it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:303:That was not his alone to give away. It was a real reason, and he distrusted it for coming so readily to hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:305:"It was dark before," said Cael. "It's dark now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:307:Both halves were true. He had looked at that place going over and coming back, and it had been the same dark. Karis wrote. He watched her pencil put the two halves down, one after the other, and he knew what she would take them to mean, and what the other four would take them to mean when they read her leaf; and that the whole was not the truth, though there was not a false word in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:311:"The sun's over," said Lira. She was looking up, with her hand shading her eyes. "If we're going, it's now."

