editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:57:Cael broke the wax, and read it as it was written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:75:A small wind came down the wash and lifted the corner of the sheet, and it was some little time before any of them found a word to put after that narrow upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:93:Cael had read the last lines but two again, to himself. *I still lose to you every time I run it.* He could see the bout as it had gone, on sixty feet of sprung oak, with a blade's edge declared against him in earnest and nothing held back on either side; and he could see a tall, exact person walking it through afterward in an empty yard, alone, looking for the place where it turned. Something in Cael stirred at the thought, quick and bright, and asked *Edge?* of the nearest arris, which was the lip of the wash. He told it no. It was Ephram's, that question. He carried it for him, unspent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:95:"Read me the fourth again," said Seln. "The query."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:97:Cael read it. Seln heard it out with his eyes on the ground, as he listened to anything that he meant to keep, and at the end he nodded once, and asked for nothing more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:121:It was a river or two, laid in without their bends, and a scatter of hills, drawn all alike; and it was seven roads. They left the Line at seven places down the length of the sheet, and each was drawn as a row of short dashes, with no name beside it; and each ran out into the white paper for the length of a thumb, or a finger, and stopped. There was no town at the end of any of them. The dashes simply came to an end, as a person's voice does who has forgotten what he meant to say, and beyond them the sheet was clean to its margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:135:"Now his," she said. "I have been over this again since the plank, and I have found two things that I had read wrongly. I took them for his thrift."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:139:"That is the only place where one of his roads walks round a blank. I said so on the fifty-eighth day, and it is still so. But he draws a hill with short hatching, and water with three wavy strokes. Look here, south of the ring with the bar, a day down and a little aside. He has hatched hills all across this corner, close as thatch, and in the middle of the hatching there is a round place the size of my thumbnail where he lifted the stick."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:35:"So I'd heard, from a man who came in last night." Pike set his pen in its pot. "I'll say your line when I've read your paper. Read mine first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:65:It had come down a mountain inside her shirt and six days along a valley, and it was soft at the folds. Pike laid it on the oak and put two fingers on it, and read it to himself from the careful writing at the head to the foot, where five strokes stood side by side over a horned head and three wavy lines; and then he read it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:83:It went down the meadow in that level carrying voice, past five fires. A woman looked at Lira's boot. A man by the nearest tent said something to his neighbour, low, and the neighbour looked at the strange cart, and then at the empty place beside the smith's shed where a hired one had stood. The Oxhollow carter had halted his mules at the edge of the grass to listen. At *carts go round* he had nodded slowly, like a person who hears his own errand read out to strangers; and at *cart lost* he looked at his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:125:He had been reading the plank, as he read it every time, from the left-hand post to the right, and there were nine slips besides the two with their names on. He had sorted them and found nothing among them that weighed much, and his eye had come to rest, for want of anything better, on Pike's flat stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:131:Every other slip that Pike had was on a tack, and he read them all out to anybody who was frying dough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:133:Cael leaned a little over the oak. The writing ran away from him, toward the man with the book, and he read it upside down, as he had once read a bailiff's list on a counter in Denvash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:139:He read it a second time, to be sure of the figure. Then he reached across the oak and drew the paper out from under the stone by its corner, and the dust-line broke. He turned the sheet round and held it out to Pike, in his fingers, saying nothing, as any crew holds out a slip that it wishes to hear said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:161:"Quiet ground's where the sigil goes to sleep. Yours. Everyone's. You walk a road, and there's a place on it where your Arbiter stops — not slows; stops — and you walk on past and it's back. There's a handful of them. Along the void roads, all of them. Carters walk round them, and have as long as there've been carters. The dogs live in them. Stillhounds. Nobody's asked them why. Why any of it, nobody knows and nobody's needed to, because the rule's *don't go in*, and the rule's never once failed anybody who kept it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:191:She read the paper's third sentence over to herself, with her lips, and her finger had not moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:211:He said that last quite mildly, and Cael understood that he had been told something about the man. Pike wrote down whatever was put in front of him and read it to anyone; but he had found, in thirty years, one paper that he would not hang up between two fences and a wheel, and he had kept it where a person must go looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:223:"The paper doesn't say. I've read it more than twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:13:The middle box stood open on the barn floor, and she had been at the others since before he woke. She had the lid off the third. He came up the ladder in time to see her lifting out the masonry chips, each in its twist of paper with a number inked on the twist, and laying them in a row on a sack; and when the last was out there was a flat canvas bag at the bottom of the box, sewn shut and sealed, with five thumb-marks in the wax.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:27:The bowman had come along the fence to that gate. It was not his; he kept the western one, and Cael had never seen him ten yards from his platform. He stood with his bow at the length of his arm and looked at the road going up the valley, and then at Lira, and he lifted two fingers from the stave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:43:He read the second letter at noon, on the far side of the hills, since it was a noon with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:49:"I was going to read it now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:51:"Read it to me. You read me his."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:55:He read it aloud, and it did not take long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:65:"No. I don't suppose she does." She took the leaf from him and read it through again herself, with her lips moving, and Cael watched her come to the end of it and go back two sentences. "She's got a book like the old man's, hasn't she? She writes down what happened. And she's put you in it as a thing that she's never had to write before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:107:There was grass. That was nearly the whole of it. It began at the height of his ankle, on the turf by the spring, and by the middle of the afternoon it stood to his knee, and before dusk to his hip: pale stuff, the colour of dry straw, with a seed-head that hissed against his coat. No stock had cropped it. There was not a fence in sight, or a smoke, or a tree, only long slow swells of ground going away to a sky that seemed to begin at his boots. The wind came over it from the north with nothing in its way, and laid the whole of it over in one direction, like the nap of a cloth under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:163:Straight in front, where the old line would have run, there was only grass. It lay over in the wind, unbroken, down a gentle slope and up another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:167:It was three hundred yards off, as near as he could judge. There were grey stones standing on end, close together, in a rough circle; he counted them, and had eleven. No two were of a height. One was as tall as two men, and the one beside it came no higher than a knee, and all of them ended in a break. Between their feet lay something pale and level that he could not make out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:181:They left the mule at the turn, with her rein under a rock. Brom knelt out from under the box and set it beside her. Then the six of them spread across the old line, in a row, and Cael took the right-hand end. Lira was next to him, and then Brom, and Karis, and the healer; Seln had the far end, on the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:199:Cael looked along the row. The healer had not spoken at all. Both her hands had risen, half way, with the palms turned up and the fingers spread, as a person holds them out to learn whether it has begun to rain; and she was staring at them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:207:Seln had not raised his voice. He stood at the north end of the row with his hands at his sides, for once, and Cael had never before heard him give an order as though he expected to be obeyed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:215:Karis was looking down the row, from face to face. Then she went forward again by herself, a foot at a time, with her hand held out in front of her as though she expected to touch something; and at a certain place she stopped, and drew back her toe by the width of a hand, and put it out again. She bent, and felt about in the roots of the grass, and came up with a grey stone the size of a loaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:221:The nine fragments lay in him where they had lain since a ford. He was sure of that as well. When Lira's hand went up, Wind had leaned in his own feet to go with hers, as it had leaned every day for two years, and he had put it down without thinking; and that had been on the far side of where the stone now stood. Anchor was asking about the stone already. *There?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:241:He read it over. It was short, and it was set down as he would have set down a count, and under it he could feel the size of what he had not put.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:243:They made their camp at the turn of the ruts, eighty yards back from the stone, and there was no fire. Seln had chosen the place. It lay a little below the crest on the western side, so that a person lying down could not see the ring and could not be seen from it. They ate cold bread from Thornwater's oven, and the hold's blankets went round, and the mule stood with her tail to the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:253:"She's nothing in her for it to take." Oryn had the rein, and she stood looking at her animal's four small hooves with an expression that Cael could not read. "I've walked her round this place thirty times, by the ruts, because I was told to. I never once thought to ask what she'd have done if I'd let her choose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:27:"Then go and read it," said Pike, from the hearth, without turning his head. "I don't call out who's gone down. I write who's where."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:29:She went and read it, with her nose a hand's breadth from the wet ink, and came back looking thoughtful. One of the two was a crew of four who had brought a string of mules through the cold road's pass and lost none of them. She had seen them in the meadow, she said, and they were none of them young, and one had a wooden foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:39:He had a question, and he had not yet found the words for it. It concerned the name at the head of the new sheet. A man had come in the night before, Pike had said at the plank, with news of a cart in a gorge; and for the whole of that man's time at the oak a sheet of cream paper had been lying under a river stone, where anybody who read a plank from end to end must come to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:43:"Teague's nine up," Pike said, to the book, asked by nobody. Then, still to the book, at the same size, as if it were the next line and not a different thing: "He read that slip yesterday. Read it twice, same as the boy did. Put it back under the stone and took the fence. I'd have done the same, and I told him, and he said he knew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:47:He had read that man's feet in the silt of a ford, and his halts at five halting-grounds after it. He had watched him count three people on a sill before he looked at a room, and had been told by him on a river bank, in a level voice, what a mountain was worth. Now he had the man's answer to a hundred marks as well, at second hand, from a book-keeper who gave out nothing that he had not seen with his own eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:55:He did it on the eastern landing, with the scow's rail still under his hand. The spear was ashore already, and grounded. The boy had the two mules by one rein and was looking up the bank at the lower inn's chimney, where there would be something in a pot. The bow came off last, and stepped down onto the planks with the right foot, and stood on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:57:Three, and whole. That hip had had six days of road since a healer told him the price of it, and the bow had not once asked to shorten a stage.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:59:He sent the boy to the smith's with the mules and went up to the plank by himself, as he always did, before he had so much as washed. Pike was taking in his book for the night. Teague read the oak from the left-hand post, a slip at a time, and at the second slip he stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:75:"Then I'll read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:77:He was given it, as he had been given a struck slip on the fifty-seventh day. It was good paper, heavier than anything the holds wrote on. He read it from the first word to the seal, and then he began again at the top, more slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:85:The ground he knew. He had been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and had looked across the grass each time at a row of grey stumps and kept walking. Every carter's child knew what was said of it. He had never put it to the proof, and he did not need a paper to tell him why not: he had three behind him, and in that place the spear would be a man with a spear, and the bow a man with a bad hip, and the boy a boy. He would not take them in for a hundred, or for twice a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:103:They would be at the ferry tomorrow. He had kept a day ahead of them from Oxhollow without trying to, and he knew their pace, with a taped boy and a girl on one boot. The boy would read this plank as he had read a doorway, from one end to the other, leaving nothing out. He would find the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:141:"The dogs live in them," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:205:Karis wrote. She turned the leaf so that the lamp fell on it, and they could all read what she had put.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:217:*Twelve weeks. Seven contracts. Pike says there's ground out here where the Arbiter goes quiet, and nobody goes in twice. Karis has been waiting her whole life for a sentence like that. Seln says the contract was written by someone from inside. I say it pays. Two days east, through Thornwater, on the day the healer said. For twelve weeks nothing out here has read me. I'd like to see what a place that reads nothing does with that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:225:Pike took it without a word. He read it twice, though he could have said it in his sleep, and then he turned his shoulder to the meadow and gave it out: the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the rise, and the hundred. It was the first time in a month that those words had been heard on that grass. He said the whole of it a second time. Then he said *taken*, and five names, and dipped his pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:229:Teague was there. He stood a little way out from the eave, on the trodden grass, with his thumbs in his belt, and his three behind him at their distances: the spear, grounded, and the bow on the good leg, and the boy with a heel of bread. That made four. The Oxhollow carter had come up from his mules and stood chewing, which was five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:247:"You could. You've read my lines. Every one of them says what came back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:Oryn had hobbled her on the western side, with a full net and the two panniers still roped on, and the long shape in its oiled cover lashed along the top of the pack as it had been since a mountain. The healer looked at that cover for a breath before she turned away. Then she walked across the line with her hands open at her sides, and Cael saw her fingers close once, hard, as the count went out of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:He had his nine fragments as he always had them, and he held them. Wind leaned, since Lira was in front of him. Anchor had fallen quiet at the stake and had not asked since. He had opened nothing after a boulder, and he did not open anything now; but he walked those two hundred yards as the only one of the six who knew that he could have, and it was a strange thing to carry at the back of a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:61:He paced it from west to east, past her notebook, and had twenty-six strides. He paced it again from south to north, and had twenty-six. By the time he came back with the second figure, nine twists of paper lay opened in a row along the lid of the middle box, each with its chip set back in it and a small cross pencilled on the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:81:She tipped what was left of the chips into her lap, thirty of them or more, and sorted them by eye into three heaps on the floor. There were the honey-coloured stones, such as the walls of Ostrand were built of. There were the dark close ones, like a district hall's, and a pale kind with a sparkle in the break. She chose the freshest-broken from each heap and held the three against the grey together, in the slanting light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:89:His fingers found none. He knew that fingers could be wrong on a surface so close, and that the others were all looking at three small stones in a researcher's lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:91:He opened the read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:101:"Nor at the foot of the stones?" said Karis. She had read it upside down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:125:Cael had expected a good many things of this place, and that had not been among them. He stood on a grey floor with the paper's second sentence going through his head: instruments, and abandoned, and a site. Somebody had sat at a ruled desk and written that, with the sum in words and again in a bracket, and had sent it over a river with a hundred marks in a bag. It had lain a month under a stone. A captain had read it twice and put it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:151:Seln gave the small bow that he gave to an order he disliked, and went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:167:She numbered the last corner and rolled the sheet, and for once she did not stop to look at what she had got. Brom had set the box at the foot of the tallest stone, on the eastern side, an hour ago, to keep ten sheets of paper out of the north wind. He knelt there now with his back to it and reached behind him for the strap, and Cael went to steady the weight onto his shoulders, since that was a thing that could be done with one arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:189:There were a good many such places. He could see the first four. They went back into the dark in a row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:191:Nothing was in them. He checked that twice. The hollow was empty, as far as light went, and no sound came out of it, and the wind brought him only the smell of cold earth and something rank and dry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:207:"They live in here because nothing that walks in can fight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:235:It took him less time than it took Brom to shift the box higher on his shoulders and draw the strap tight. He had always written at the start of a thing. He did not know how to begin one without it, and he thought that this might be the last quiet minute of the day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:21:He chose the splints himself, and they were the mountain's own; he found two plates in the bank above the wall, each as long as her shin and no thicker than a slate, and he laid one down either side of the leg. They had no canvas to tear, for the canvas had gone with the cart. Cael's coat had lost its left sleeve at a river. He got the knife into the seam of the right one, and Seln cut that as well, and Brom bound the two plates on with it in three places, one-handed, with Seln's fingers for the knots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:55:It was a cold night, and they had no blankets; the frost came down out of a clear sky, and they lay close under the rock in a row, the hurt ones in the middle, and kept one another warm as cattle do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:91:He read it through, and he was not ashamed of it, for it had been true when the pencil made it; but he could find no sentence to set beneath it that was a thing counted, and he would not put down one that was only a thing felt. He shut the book, and that day got no line either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:99:"That's today, and tomorrow, and the day after. Five a row." He pointed with the knife. "The bacon's for whoever's walking. I've done it in my head six ways and that's the one that comes out even."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:171:Brom poured for her out of the skin. She washed to the wrists, rubbing hard, and it was while she did so that Cael read her; she had read him first, which did not often happen to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:221:There was nothing to see. He had half expected a light, or a colour, as there was grey in Iron Skin and yellow in a point; and there was a square brown woman kneeling in the dust with her hands on a girl's leg, breathing slowly. A minute went by, and another, and the only thing that changed was that the sweat came out on her forehead, though the air was cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:267:It was her own bread, out of the mule's off pannier, a dark close loaf from some hold's oven, and she broke it in six and gave five pieces away before she bit into hers. There was a cheese as well, and four onions. Brom looked at the three rows on the lid of the box, and then at the pannier, and swept the rows back into his bag without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:281:She went to Karis last of the three, on her knees, and read her as she had read the leg, with two fingers on the inside of a thin wrist and two on the side of the neck. Her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:309:Seln inclined his head. He looked, Cael thought, like a man who has had a report read by somebody competent, and it had been a long time since that had happened to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:341:He watched her face, and he knew what it was doing, for he had seen a brass plate in a district hall wait eleven seconds over a boy of fourteen before it lit. He had seen assessors look at a panel with that same small stillness, and a hooded seat, and a clerk with a form that had no box for him. It was what an instrument looked like when it reached for the thing it was made to read, and closed on air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:349:"Whatever you are, I can't read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:381:*The healer put her hands on my ribs and could not read me. She told me so to my face, and asked nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:73:"That's three times. The first I thought it was the break; a bad one can shout over everything. So I went where you're whole. There's nothing wrong with that wrist, or that neck, and there's still nothing I can read." She looked at Karis, who was the one writing it down, or would be. "It's like putting my hands in a river in the dark. Something's moving. I can't find the banks, so I can't read it, so I can't touch it. That's the rule. I didn't make it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:77:"Everybody's got them. What moves in a person moves in a bed, the same bed all their life, and the shape of the bed is their Path. I put my hands on you and I felt the banks before I felt anything else, and I said Ember, and I was right. I'd know a Wind from a Skin with a glove on." She jerked her chin at the table. "In him it's moving, and it hasn't a side anywhere that I can find."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:87:"I don't run it on a road. I've not the day to spare for it, and he's three ribs short of whole." She turned back to Cael, and for the first time since a rock he saw something in her face that was neither tiredness nor arithmetic. It was appetite. "When you're not broken, I run the long one. It won't read you either. I want to feel it not read you properly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:129:"Thornwater's on my route in nine days. You'll be on the board by then. Come find me. I want to read you when you're not broken."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:149:There was bone. Bone answered to anyone; she had felt the breaks as a blind man feels a step. There was movement, past the bone. She was sure of that, and it was what kept her fingers stupid. A dead thing she could have understood. This was more alive than most, and it ran, and she had felt it running; and she could no more have said which way, or between what, than she could have drawn the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:161:She knew the first of them, as anybody on those roads did. It was Teague, off the north water, with a string of two mules behind him and his three; and she gave them what she gave everything on legs, which was a look from a stride and a half as they drew level. A spear, carried upright and grounded at each step, by somebody who never hurried. A boy who was all wrists. And a bow, on the back of one who walked a hair short on the left, at the hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:207:Three ribs, broken on the seventy-eighth. Twenty-one days to knit, in anybody. By what she had felt under her hand they would be sound in him some days sooner. But she could not read him, and so she could not know it, and she had never in her life lifted a tape on a guess. It would be the ninety-ninth, then, by the rule. The long one wanted him whole and still, and it wanted the better part of a day out of her besides, so that the hundredth was the first morning on which she could run it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:219:They were hungry for this. She had spent her life reading what people were built like, and she had been proud of never meeting a body she could not go into. Now she had met one. She did not want to mend him; there was nothing to mend that a strip of linen could not see to. She wanted to lay both hands on him, whole and still, for as long as it took, and feel exactly how it was that he could not be read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:237:They were behind him on the hill. The spear had not changed pace for the stop. The boy had both mules by one rein and was looking back up the road after the woman, with his mouth a little open, as he looked after everything. The bow had stood square on both legs for as long as the healer's eyes were on that hip, and now the weight had gone over to the right again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:243:It was the bow's hip that he minded, and he put it first, since it was the cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:245:He had timed the second of his two slips to end at the upper hold on the eighty-first day. That was the day the healer's round should have brought her to its gate. He had said nothing of it to the bow; he had only found a day's more work on a byre roof that might have waited, and stayed. She had not come, and nobody at that gate could say why. On the hill she had given him no more than that she was late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:249:But it was a day he had spent and a thing he had hoped for, and he could not set it down against five names. Pike had struck a ford off his hold and given it to them; that had cost nothing, because he had chosen the pass himself. The healer's lateness was not theirs on any ground he had read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:253:He read the yard through the open gate as he went by it, without turning his head more than a walking man does.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:259:The big one sat on the well's coping with a box-rope coiled over his knee. His left wrist had a cuff of clean linen on it, tied with a knot that Teague knew, since he had seen it on his own people. Mended, then, and under orders. The girl was on a trough. She had one boot, and the other foot was bare and strapped, crosswise, by somebody who had been shown how. It was on its whole sole. He had read those feet in an inn as the quickest thing in the room, and he would not have laid a copper on them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:275:So he had it, and there was nothing in it that a man could not have read off a yard and a keeper. They had cleared a ford with five, in a way that nobody on those roads had cleared one before, and they had walked a mountain and brought back a true answer; and they had lost a cart doing it, and come down with three of them marked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:279:The first was the two jobs, set side by side with the same number on each. It would cost him nothing, and it would tell the boy that he was being read, and by what. That was fair. The boy had read him across a room and not troubled to hide it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:283:The third would land hardest, and he turned it over longest. He did not mean it unkindly. A cart was not a carter. Nobody lay in that gorge. But a crew that went out with another man's wheels and came back without them had done a thing that the board would enter, and it was better that they heard the word for it from him, on a river bank, than read it on a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:287:He looked back, once. The spear. The boy. The bow, on the good leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:293:Teague still could not tell what he was. A Force stood one way and a Wind another, and there was no Path in the set of that boy that a man could name, and no want of one. He went down the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:321:They crossed without a sound. Cael watched it as he would have watched a thing well made. The one with the spear went second and grounded it at every step. The one with the bow walked a little short on one side and kept the pace regardless. A boy of sixteen or so brought the two mules through on a slack rein, and not one of the four looked down at the water. They had kept the hold on roads since before he knew there was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:331:*Teague priced us. He's right about the cart. Oryn read me three times and got nothing three times and wants a fourth. Two people out here measuring me, and neither of them has a seal. I keep waiting to mind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:333:He read the last of it over, and it stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:47:Five people had walked up to this place in a row, an arm's length apart. She was an Ember, and Lira a Wind, and Brom an Iron Skin; there was a Shadow among them of a higher tier than any of the rest, and a Tide who had been trained in a healing house. Karis had sat at lectures on what an Arbiter was. She had copied out, in a restricted room, a good many pages on the subject, and on one point every page agreed: it was a person's own. It woke for that one person, on one morning, and named a single gift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:99:They walked the third cord. Lira kept to her stride inside, and Karis to the line, and when the cord came tight Karis was standing well to the right of Seln's wand. She had her short cord out already, and she laid it on the grass from the wand to her own boots and read the knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:197:She stood there with her eyes shut, and her lips moved for some time. Cael knew what she was doing, since she had the length of that cord in feet, to the knot, in a notebook that nobody else had read, and had paced her own stride twice against his in the dark. He would have given a good deal to check the sum; he had only her figures for the half of it, and so he waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:219:Cael went after him, and they stood over the iron together. In the full light it was plainer than it had been: the square bar, and the spread head, and the stamp. Seln crouched now, without laying a finger on it, and looked at the rust, where it had lifted in leaves, and at the turf round the bar's foot, which had grown up close against the metal on every side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:57:They were a day old. The dew had softened their edges, but the gravel gave way there to a strip of grey silt, and the silt had kept everything. Four people had come up out of the river in single file, each a crew's length behind the one in front, and two mules after them on one rein. He crouched, as far as five bands of linen would let him, and read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:99:Lira packed the right one. It took the whole of a spare shirt, torn in strips and wound round her taped foot until the foot was the size of the boot. When she stood up in it she was two fingers taller on that side than on the other. She walked a few steps, and stopped, and walked a few more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:113:He could have borne the pain of it; he had carried pain before, on worse terms, and gone on. What he could not bear was that the thing was always right. He would reach for the water-skin, or turn his head to see what Lira had laughed at, or simply draw the breath that a hill wanted; and five bands of linen would close across his side a hair before the bone did, and tell him *there*. Every time, when he stopped to check it, the tape had known and he had not. A person can forgive an instrument for being wrong. He was learning that it was a good deal harder to forgive one for being right four thousand times a day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:161:"They're on a bed, where they ought to be. I'd only be taking one down to put it up again." He stood, and pulled the rod out of the ground, and laid it across his knee as though to break it; then he seemed to think better of that and slid it under the cart's canvas. "She gave me a week, and I've kept it to the hour. I'll not spend the first minute of the next one showing off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:249:"Five halting-grounds, and not a new ash on one of them. The chock-stones are where they found them. They eat cold, and they sleep in a row with the mules at the windward end, and in the morning there's nothing to show for it but where the grass lay down." He turned a page back to look at what he had put. "We've had a fire every night since the ford, on the strength of a finding. He's had none for longer than I've known what a hold was, on the strength of never having needed one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:253:"I didn't say it was better. I said that I could read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:263:*A crew went down this road a day ahead of us and never left a mark out of its line. I read them at every halt. They'll have been at the plank tonight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:5:It was grey in the hollow still. He had got to his feet by the wall, as he did every morning, to learn what the night had done to him, and he had drawn in air to say something to Brom. At a certain depth, a little short of where the ribs had stopped him the day before, a hand seemed to close across his left side and hold it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:33:"Because I cannot mend what I can't read." The healer said it without heat. "I read her leg at a count of ten and I knew what it was, and how she was built round it, and where the thing that knits had stopped; and then I knew where to put mine. I had my hands on him for three times as long and I got bone. I'd have got that much with my eyes shut, off a dead sheep." She showed her palms, briefly. "Past the bone there's nothing I can lay hold of. I'd be pushing in the dark, and I don't do that to anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:35:She turned to Brom then, and had his left arm out of its sleeve before he could offer it. The seam was dry and pale. She wound a strip of plain linen round the wrist, four turns, no tighter than a cuff, and tied it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:43:Cael let his shirt down over five bands of linen. It was a strange thing to have somebody else's judgment fastened round his chest. He had always priced his own hurts, alone, and decided what each would bear, and he had been wrong often enough to carry the marks. This one had been priced for him by a person who could not read him, and she had put the price where he would feel it at every breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:93:There was something else that wanted to help him, and it was harder to turn away. The Iron read would have given him every plate on that lip within a pace and a half: which was seated on rock and which on its fellow, and which meant to go. He would have known the ground as he knew his own weight. He had refused it on a fan, when all that hung on it was a map.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:109:He turned round, since the others had. From the western ledge the whole bay lay open behind them. He could see the pale rock standing across its floor, and the two black lines above it that came to a point, and the wide scoured strip of the hill; and along the foot of the face, in a row, with a length and a half between each, the shale-backs were mowing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:151:They slept that night beside a seep of good water, halfway down, in a fold of the hill where somebody had once built a wall of loose stones against the wind. Seln had found it, and he had a fire lit when they came up, and enough dead heather gathered to keep it till morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:157:The track had come down by then onto the last long slant above the valley, and Lira's right foot hung from the pack-saddle at about the height of the healer's shoulder, bare, and wound with the strip of linen that it had worn since a meadow. Oryn went beside it for the better part of a mile. Cael, two paces behind, saw her eyes go to it and come away, and go again, as a person's go to a thing that somebody else has done badly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:317:He could not read. Cael saw that in the first breath, from where the brown eyes went; they passed over the six columns to the two ruled lines down the middle of the leaf, and to what was drawn between them, and a thick cracked finger came down on the foot of a fan and began to climb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:327:He went on. They told him each mark as his finger reached it, in a word or two, as one names the stops on a road; and Karis, at his elbow, read him the number of each narrowing from her separate column. He stopped a long while at the seventh. He stopped longer at the eighth, where Cael had drawn a short black bar across both lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:9:Lira had gone on ahead. She had not eaten. She had wound the linen round her right ankle by the first light there was, and tried her weight on it, and walked away along the foot of the wall with her arms swinging loose. By the time the cart was a hundred yards out she was a small dark figure at the place where the bay shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:27:She was. She went out along the ledge under its roof of rock, close in, and from that distance there was nothing to show that one foot came down later than the other; she looked like a thing that the mountain had been keeping that shelf for. He made himself look at the fin. It stood ahead of them and a little to the right, pale in the half light, with the frozen shale banked along its upper side, and he judged that they were thirty yards short of its nearer end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:43:It was Wind, and it was hers, and it was the whole of it. He had not heard that sound from her since a painted floor at Norhold, and she had never, there or anywhere, let it go so hard. She was not on the falling stone. She was twenty feet east of it, in the air, and then she was standing on sound ledge with both arms flung wide. Behind her a hundred feet of shelf was going down into the blue with a roar that grew as it fell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:45:Wind stood up in his own feet to answer hers. He put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:115:He gave it no more than a breath's looking, and that was enough. The front had come a third of the way, and it was no longer grey; it had a brown smoke of dust standing over it, and out of the smoke came the sound, a deep steady grinding with the hail of the plates on top. He passed Brom going the other way, and Brom's eyes went to him and to the cart and he did not waste his wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:123:All nine of them were up. He had never felt them so, on the stretch or at the ford; there the offers had come one behind another, and here they came in a single shout. Wind wanted his feet and had the whole distance measured. Storm showed him the air over the bay, torn already, as a lane. He put them down as he ran, without words, since there was no time for words, and they got up again at every stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:181:The stream had spread as it reached the level. He saw the backs of perhaps a dozen animals, wheeling on the bay's floor in a fog of dust, with the shale still running between their legs like water; and beyond them, alone, in the place where five people had stood a minute ago, the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:245:So she bound the ankle in the dark, on the seventy-eighth morning, and ate nothing, as she had eaten nothing before a card. The foot was what it was. It bent less than the other and later, and she had stopped being angry with it somewhere on the bench and begun simply to allow for it, as one allows for a wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:249:The rock was a gift. It was dry under its roof, and rimed along the outer half where the roof stopped, so she kept in, close under the wall, as she had promised a fire that she would. Her left foot struck and her right came after it. She had the count in her legs. Forty. Fifty. The gorge went by on her right hand, blue and enormous, and she gave it none of her attention; she was reading the shelf five yards ahead of her toes, for cracks and for colour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:259:She had been told at Fenmark that a burst was a choice, and at Norhold she had made a rank of choosing well. For sixty days on gravel bars and roads she had refused it a stride at a time, on purpose, and she had been proud of how good she had got. Not one of those refusals was anywhere near her now. The ground went, and her body did what a body with Wind in it does when the ground goes, and it did not send up to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:287:Wind said *now*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:313:*Now*, said Wind, for the last time, and it was not reasonable any longer. It was begging.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:317:Something the size of a cart went behind her so close that the wind of it turned her half round. She kept her legs. She was three strides from the stone, with her arm already out for it, when the shale that the thing had brought down with it took her ankles from under her like a wave on a beach, and she went onto her face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:33:"No," said Karis. She tried it a second time, and a third, quickly. "No. It has not set. A lecturer would say that the declaration did not render. And that is not the whole of it. When I am empty and try to set one, I feel myself reach into the purse and close my fingers on nothing. Here I am not even reaching. I am three parts full, and I can't find the hand to reach with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:47:"Back," she said. "First row. Write it as I gave it: every place seen; none set; no cost."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:87:Cael wrote it. He wrote *Skin called aloud; none*, and under it, since the row was Brom's, *lifted a hundredweight; no difference*; and it seemed to him that of the two findings the second was the one that Brom would take home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:89:Seln had been standing at the end of the row with his hands behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:113:"That isn't a trial of anything," said Karis. But she was looking at the bent grass, and at where he had got to, and her pencil hand had twitched. "I suppose that it is a trial of you. Third row. *Path sought; none. Practitioner not found for a count of two.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:133:So it was his own feet that told him. Wind stood up in them to go with hers, harder than it had leaned in three months, and he put it down; and in the same instant the grass behind his shoulder hissed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:141:She said it to her feet. Then she looked round at the row of them, and her eyes were very wide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:171:Karis had Cael write that down as it was spoken, the whole of it, in the fourth row. She read it over his arm when it was done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:175:Lira wound the shirt on again, with fingers that were not quite steady, and pulled the great boot over it, and stamped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:189:"The wrist. The one I did, that I read at the fire."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:211:"Is it Brom?" said Karis. "Forgive me; I must ask it. His Arbiter stopped at that stone, and yours did. If the thing that you read is his current, and his current is shaped by his Path, then perhaps there was nothing in him to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:225:The wind went over the grass. Nobody at the stone had anything to put beside that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:227:"He's standing out there with everything he owns. I had it not a minute ago. And I can't so much as feel that he's alive, except that he's warm." She looked down at her two broad palms, as she had looked at them in a hollow of shale, and this time there was no puzzlement in her face. "They told me, and I never checked. Thirty rounds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:239:"Fifth row," said Karis, in a small voice. "As she said it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:255:The Iron read was the quietest thing he owned. He had refused it on a fan and a lip and a bench, when it would have told him what he was being paid to learn, and each time he had refused because a boy's word could be checked by nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:265:He had been certain, in the part of himself that kept accounts, of what would happen: a thing taken out of a Path would stop where Paths stopped. He had supposed it since a plank, as the others supposed it, and for the same reason, which was that no other sum came out even; and he had braced for the read to go from him as a count had gone from a healer's lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:273:Then he did the second thing. He had not been sure, a minute ago, that he would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:275:He walked in, as a man goes who means to look at the ground that he will presently lead people over, without haste, twenty strides toward the ring. A grey boulder lay there in the grass, as high as his hip, and he walked straight at it. He kept his face to the east; if anyone at the stone had glanced his way, there was only a person with a notebook going to see what lay beyond a rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:277:At the twentieth stride he let Wind have his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:283:He stood on the far side with the tape drawn tight across his ribs and something going out of him into the cold air, small, as a breath goes out. He listened, and there was the wind, and the hiss of seed-heads, and far off toward the east a bird that got up and settled again. Nothing whatever came through the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:285:He shut them both. He put Wind down, and closed the read as one closes a book, and they went back to where the other seven lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:291:Karis took it from him, and read down the five rows, nodding at each. Then she laid it on her knee, and took the pencil, and ruled a sixth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:295:She said it to the leaf. It was a clerk's question; she had a table with a row empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:307:Both halves were true. He had looked at that place going over and coming back, and it had been the same dark. Karis wrote. He watched her pencil put the two halves down, one after the other, and he knew what she would take them to mean, and what the other four would take them to mean when they read her leaf; and that the whole was not the truth, though there was not a false word in it.

