/bin/zsh -lc "rg -n -i '\\b(Oryn|Teague|Pike|Lowmarch|Thornwater|Oxhollow|Millrace|Fallow|stillhound|shale-back|Quieting|Ephram|Withrow|Vell|Ostrand|Denvash|Ardenmere|Fenmark|Ternhall|Norhold|Rooke|Bracken|Reydan|Gault|Vastin|Dellenmoor|Halcenvane|Greyvane|Velmere|Auremont|Rhagen|Compression|Anchor|Storm|Tide|Shadow|Ember|Wind|Iron Skin|Kindling|Arbiter|sigil|quiet ground|void road|Line)\\b' editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-{16..30}.md" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:3:Pike wrote the wall out again that evening, from the top, on a clean length of sailcloth, and Lira counted the lines as they went up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:7:*Teague.* It stood by itself on the first line, as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:15:The pen went to the pot. Pike wrote five names on one line, in the order in which they had been spoken at a plank, and the boy shifted his hands down the cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:19:She said it quite quietly. Two lines that had stood over theirs since the day they came now stood under them, and the shingle pair were at the foot still. Cael looked for what the rung was made of, since he had learned in this room that it was always made of something. It was a mountain, less a cart. The wall had no place to say so. It said only that a hold which asked Pike who ought to walk a bad hill would now be given five names before it was given two others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:27:"Then go and read it," said Pike, from the hearth, without turning his head. "I don't call out who's gone down. I write who's where."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:37:Pike had sat down at last, on the hearth bench, under his own handwriting, with the ledger open beside him and a cup that somebody had put into his hand. Cael took his own cup and went over, as he had gone over once before to ask about a ledger-keeper in Ardenmere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:39:He had a question, and he had not yet found the words for it. It concerned the name at the head of the new sheet. A man had come in the night before, Pike had said at the plank, with news of a cart in a gorge; and for the whole of that man's time at the oak a sheet of cream paper had been lying under a river stone, where anybody who read a plank from end to end must come to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:41:Pike drank. He looked at the fire, and then across the room at the lamp on the boards by the door, and then down at the leaf beside him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:43:"Teague's nine up," Pike said, to the book, asked by nobody. Then, still to the book, at the same size, as if it were the next line and not a different thing: "He read that slip yesterday. Read it twice, same as the boy did. Put it back under the stone and took the fence. I'd have done the same, and I told him, and he said he knew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:49:It was a reason against. He took it as one, with the name of the man who had given it; and he noticed that Pike had said what was done, and had not said why. He did not think that Pike had been told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:53:It had been the evening before, the eighty-ninth, with the five still a camp short of the river, when Teague counted his three off the ferry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:59:He sent the boy to the smith's with the mules and went up to the plank by himself, as he always did, before he had so much as washed. Pike was taking in his book for the night. Teague read the oak from the left-hand post, a slip at a time, and at the second slip he stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:63:He stood in front of them for as long as it took to be sure what he felt. He had asked for that word once, on a ford, and had it struck with one line while he was on a ledge with a string of salt; and he had never grudged the striking, since a hold cannot wait on a captain's convenience. Now two holds had come down to a ferry and asked a one-armed man who they ought to have. That was the board doing its work. If he minded it, he would have to mind arithmetic.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:67:Pike's stone lay on the book's corner, where it had lain for as long as Teague had been coming to that plank; and under its far edge there was the corner of a sheet of paper. A line of dust lay across the corner, fine and pale, drawn along the stone's rim. He had been four weeks up the north water, and that sheet had plainly lain there for most of them. A waybill does not gather dust under a stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:71:Pike's eyes went to the stone and came back, twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:83:The hirer was the broker, by the wax. Teague had carried that man's bales. He paid to the day, and argued every copper beforehand and none after; and he had never in his life sent a crew farther than his own landing. That was odd, and it was not wrong; a broker posts for whoever puts money in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:85:The ground he knew. He had been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and had looked across the grass each time at a row of grey stumps and kept walking. Every carter's child knew what was said of it. He had never put it to the proof, and he did not need a paper to tell him why not: he had three behind him, and in that place the spear would be a man with a spear, and the bow a man with a bad hip, and the boy a boy. He would not take them in for a hundred, or for twice a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:93:He laid it back under the stone himself. He set the corner on its own line of dust, as nearly as he could, and took his hand away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:95:"That's what I'd have done with it," said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:99:It was all either of them said about it. There was a fence on the oak, four slips along: the axe-head hold's longer fence on the east road, forty marks, HOLD. Teague drew its tack and held it out, and Pike wrote his name to it before he shut the book and tucked it under what was left of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:101:Teague stood a breath longer in front of two slips with five names on each.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:103:They would be at the ferry tomorrow. He had kept a day ahead of them from Oxhollow without trying to, and he knew their pace, with a taped boy and a girl on one boot. The boy would read this plank as he had read a doorway, from one end to the other, leaving nothing out. He would find the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:105:Teague had said four things to him on a river bank, and every one of them had been counted before it was spoken. This was not a thing that could be counted. A captain did not walk up to another crew and hand it a feeling, as though a feeling were a figure; if he had, he would have been asked what it rested on, and rightly, and he would have had no answer but the hair on his own neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:113:Cael had set the cream sheet in the middle of the table, where all five could reach it, and he gave the order as he would have given it for any contract: each of them to speak, and nobody to answer until the last had done. Brom poured. He had allowed one jug, on the strength of a line said at a plank, and had made it plain that the paper under the lamp had not yet earned a second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:123:"He says that there is a place on a road where an Arbiter stops. He says it stops at a line, and that a person knows the line by her own. If that is so, then it can be found with nothing but feet, by anybody, and found again in the same place the next day by somebody else. I have been taught that an Arbiter cannot be tested at all; that it is the one thing every instrument we own takes for granted. I should like to stand where one fails." She drew a breath. "I would go for nothing. I say so in order that nobody should count my hand as a vote for the hundred."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:147:He had a good many reasons. He could have said what he had said to Lira under the eave, that he wished to see it, and it would have been true. He could have said that a hold had asked for them by name, and that a crew which was asked for ought to find out what it was made of. There was a rope with no figure against it. There was a cart in a gorge, and a line in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:177:"Inside the Line," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:179:"Inside the Line, at a desk, from a book of forms. I do not say whose desk. I say that it was not composed on that landing. It was copied there, or else it came over the water as it stands, with the money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:195:"If we mark it in the morning, that is the ninety-first," she said. "The Oxhollow cart rests a day and goes east on the ninety-second, with our boxes, and we in front of it. That brings us to the fence on the afternoon of the ninety-third."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:199:Nobody at the table needed the sum done twice. A square woman had stood in a gateway on the eighty-fourth day, with a mule's cheek-strap in her hand, and had said nine days. The road to the Fallow Ring went through Thornwater, and by no arrangement of theirs it went through on the day that she had named.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:207:*Thornwater, ninety-third. The healer.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:215:*Ninetieth day. Lowmarch. The seventh line is said: seventy in the purse, sixty-four on the wall. Tenth. The smith is paid, and we have a hundred and eight. A rope is owed to the ferryman, with no figure. Two holds have asked for us by name, and will wait a fortnight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:217:*Twelve weeks. Seven contracts. Pike says there's ground out here where the Arbiter goes quiet, and nobody goes in twice. Karis has been waiting her whole life for a sentence like that. Seln says the contract was written by someone from inside. I say it pays. Two days east, through Thornwater, on the day the healer said. For twelve weeks nothing out here has read me. I'd like to see what a place that reads nothing does with that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:219:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:223:He gave the paper back to Pike in the morning, across the oak, before the mist was off the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:225:Pike took it without a word. He read it twice, though he could have said it in his sleep, and then he turned his shoulder to the meadow and gave it out: the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the rise, and the hundred. It was the first time in a month that those words had been heard on that grass. He said the whole of it a second time. Then he said *taken*, and five names, and dipped his pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:229:Teague was there. He stood a little way out from the eave, on the trodden grass, with his thumbs in his belt, and his three behind him at their distances: the spear, grounded, and the bow on the good leg, and the boy with a heel of bread. That made four. The Oxhollow carter had come up from his mules and stood chewing, which was five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:233:The eleventh was at Pike's elbow. He was a thin young man with ink on his cuffs and a ledger under his arm, and he had come up from the landing at a trot when he was sent for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:235:"You're the witness for the hirer," said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:237:"I am told so." The clerk looked at the five as if they had been bales of a kind that he had not handled before. "My master had it by the last sack, sealed, with the sum counted in a bag and a line of direction. He was to post it and to lodge the price. Whatever is brought down, I am to see it at the landing and put our mark to the slip, and it goes west by the next boat." He swallowed. "I don't know what the things are. I was not told who wants them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:239:"Nobody's asked you," said Pike, and wrote five short strokes at the foot of the cream paper, side by side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:243:"Then you come down and tell me so, and I say that." Pike was blowing on the strokes to dry them. "I write what was done. There's a hundred in my box against his mark on this paper, and if his mark never goes on it, the box stays shut. I've nothing in the book that pays a crew for walking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:245:"So we could go four days and come home with a line and no money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:253:"The two holds," he said to Pike. "With our names. We'll be nine days on this, if it goes as it's written. If we're not down in their fortnight, don't keep a bull waiting on us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:255:"I'd not have. I told them a fortnight, and after that the top of the sheet." Pike blotted the strokes. "It's good of you to say it where he can hear."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:13:That column had been growing more careful since morning. On the first afternoon every line of it had said a plain *yes*, and now each *yes* dragged a condition after it, like a cart with a pole lashed under its corner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:15:Brom leaned on his rope through all of it, and Cael thought that he had never seen him so content. He had stood idle in a yard for five days and in a meadow for eleven. Here he had found a way of putting his whole weight to use that a woman with a needle could not have forbidden, and he had found it by himself. Iron Skin had not once risen to meet a load on that shelf. Cael had his own things to keep down there, for Compression rose whenever the rope snatched, and Wind leaned at every yard that Lira ran, and he paid the rent on both fragments and spent neither.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:17:Overhead the rasping went on. The line had been above the second overhang at first light, and by noon it lay over the fourth narrowing, and they had not got ahead of it by a yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:27:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:37:He went to look, since the line would be in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:41:Something in him had found the lip before his eyes did. Anchor had asked at every sill and coping since a river, and at every one of seven narrowings, and it had never asked as it asked now. *There?* It showed him the place, under the crust, where a fixed point would sit: one point, set against the sound rock and told to stay, and that plank of stone would have carried a loaded wagon. It would have cost him nothing that he could not have paid by supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:111:He had not straightened altogether; he was bent like a man beneath a low beam, and the cords of his neck stood out, and every part of the cart that was not on two shoulders at the front was on his. It was the whole of her. Cael watched the two wrists, on the two knees, and waited to see Iron Skin start at them, as he had seen the grey start at a ferry and a wash whenever a weight came on suddenly. Thirty animals were scraping on the hill above, and there was a page in Karis's coat that said what a start might be worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:177:*The nine: held, the whole length of it. Anchor asked at the seventh as it has never asked. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:3:Karis made her long cord along Thornwater's fence in the first grey light, with Brom to do the knots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:17:"Those stay," she said. "The chips come. I have a piece of every sort of dressed stone that I could reach between Denvash and the Line, and a date for each, and if there is so much as a doorstep at this place, I mean to know which of them it matches."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:21:Oryn was waiting at the east gate with the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:39:"That's the Fallow," said Oryn, and clicked her tongue to the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:57:*Filed with your record. It reads: left in good standing, undefeated on paper. I've kept this ledger forty years and never had a line like it. The session still stands. The road still runs through Ardenmere. — V.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:67:"She had me in it before anybody would give me a number. I asked Pike once whether he'd heard of her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:73:He had thought about that on the forty-ninth night, and it came back to him now on a turf bank two days past a fence. There was a woman in Ardenmere who kept a straight ledger of a fighting yard, and a one-armed man in a meadow who kept a straight book of a plank; and neither had the least idea that the other was alive. He was written in both. He did not think that any registry on the continent could have said as much of one of its own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:75:Lira had her finger on the last line but one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:81:"I know what she's speaking of. I don't know what she means by saying it now, on a ledger leaf, to somebody who's past the Line." He took the paper back, and folded it along its two creases. "It's hers. I'd tell you if it were mine. I only keep it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:91:He looked at the leaf. *The road still runs through Ardenmere.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:93:"So does Ephram."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:95:"He put Rooke last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:97:"He put what Rooke wouldn't say last. It comes to the same." He tied the thread again, in the knot that it had come in. "They've each of them written to tell me that a door's open, and neither would be seen holding it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:105:The Fallow road ran east for the rest of that day, and it was the emptiest country that he had ever walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:107:There was grass. That was nearly the whole of it. It began at the height of his ankle, on the turf by the spring, and by the middle of the afternoon it stood to his knee, and before dusk to his hip: pale stuff, the colour of dry straw, with a seed-head that hissed against his coat. No stock had cropped it. There was not a fence in sight, or a smoke, or a tree, only long slow swells of ground going away to a sky that seemed to begin at his boots. The wind came over it from the north with nothing in its way, and laid the whole of it over in one direction, like the nap of a cloth under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:111:"The holds either side won't put a beast on it," said Oryn. "I've asked. They don't give you a reason. They say it's fallow, as you'd say a field was resting, and then they talk about something else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:129:Oryn had taken off her boot to look at her heel. She pressed the strip flat with her thumb, and then, with the boot still in her hand, she looked across the fire at Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:145:"They say a healer's no good there. That's the words. *You'll not want her in the ring; she's no good there.* I had it first from a carter at the first gate I ever mended at, and I took it that he meant I'd be frightened." Oryn laced the boot. "Then I had it from six more, in the same words, and not one of them a man to be frightened of a field. So it's a thing that was seen once, by somebody, and handed down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:159:It was a long swell of ground, no steeper than a ramp, and the ruts climbed it in a straight line. Seln had stopped at the top. Cael saw him against the sky, a narrow dark figure with his hands behind him, and he did not move as they came up. When they stood beside him they saw why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:161:The road turned. He had walked it for two days as straight as a plumb-line, and here, at the crest, both ruts swung out to the left together, toward the north, in a wide easy curve, like a stream going round a rock; and a long way off, beyond the curve's far end, he could see them come back and go on eastward along the line that they had left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:163:Straight in front, where the old line would have run, there was only grass. It lay over in the wind, unbroken, down a gentle slope and up another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:169:"That's it," said Oryn. She had halted the mule. "I've never been nearer than these ruts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:175:"Nothing declared," said Karis quickly. "By anyone. That's the hold, and it is my condition as well; I want to know what is felt, and I can't know that if somebody is doing something at the time." She was looking along the old line. "We go abreast, an arm's length apart, at a slow walk, so that we all come to it together. If you feel a thing, put your hand up, and say what it is like. Don't say anything before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:181:They left the mule at the turn, with her rein under a rock. Brom knelt out from under the box and set it beside her. Then the six of them spread across the old line, in a row, and Cael took the right-hand end. Lira was next to him, and then Brom, and Karis, and the healer; Seln had the far end, on the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:185:He counted strides, since somebody would want the number. And he did what a one-armed man had told them to do, though it had not been said to him. He turned his attention inward, to the place behind his breastbone where a small bright thing had gathered on the morning of his Kindling. He had gone looking for it a few times since, in the first months, on bad nights. He looked now. It was as it had always been: a dark place, like a niche in a wall where a lamp once stood lit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:201:"My Arbiter's gone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:221:The nine fragments lay in him where they had lain since a ford. He was sure of that as well. When Lira's hand went up, Wind had leaned in his own feet to go with hers, as it had leaned every day for two years, and he had put it down without thinking; and that had been on the far side of where the stone now stood. Anchor was asking about the stone already. *There?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:225:And nobody had turned to him. He saw why, and it was the simplest thing in the world. All four of them knew what he was. They had known for years that a brass plate had waited eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and that nothing had spoken to him out of that dark since. A line that put out a lamp had nothing in him to put out. It had not occurred to one of them to ask whether he had felt it, any more than they would have asked a man without a purse whether he had been robbed. The fifth knew nothing of brass plates, and she was looking at her own two hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:229:Neither one was a good enough reason by itself. He knew that. He kept silent on the pair of them together, and his own shadow lay out in front of him across a grey stone and on into the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:235:*Ninety-fifth day. The Fallow road, at the rise. Eighty strides east of where the ruts turn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:237:*Quiet ground. Karis's Arbiter stopped at a line she can mark with a rock. So did Lira's, Brom's, Seln's, Oryn's — same line, same step. Mine didn't. Mine's been stopped since I was fourteen. I don't know what that means. Nobody asked. Tomorrow we go in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:239:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:243:They made their camp at the turn of the ruts, eighty yards back from the stone, and there was no fire. Seln had chosen the place. It lay a little below the crest on the western side, so that a person lying down could not see the ring and could not be seen from it. They ate cold bread from Thornwater's oven, and the hold's blankets went round, and the mule stood with her tail to the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:249:The mule had settled the matter of the line for herself an hour before. She had been left with her rein under a rock, and she had drawn it out and grazed her way down the old road; and when Oryn went to fetch her she was standing with her forefeet on one side of the grey stone and her hind feet on the other, pulling at the grass, as easy as a beast in its own paddock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:253:"She's nothing in her for it to take." Oryn had the rein, and she stood looking at her animal's four small hooves with an expression that Cael could not read. "I've walked her round this place thirty times, by the ruts, because I was told to. I never once thought to ask what she'd have done if I'd let her choose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:255:Shadow lifted then, as it lifted at every dusk. Cael felt it come up soft behind the other eight, offering to take him out of the light; and he told it no. It had come at the turn of the ruts as it came anywhere. He wondered whether it would have come a stride beyond the stone, and thought that he knew, and did not go to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:263:The healer was at the stone. She stood on the near side of it, with her boots together, like a person at the edge of a pond. She had both arms stretched out in front of her at the height of her waist, so that her two hands were over the line and the rest of her was not, and the palms were turned down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:19:"I asked a question at the plank, two mornings ago, and I have carried the answer for a day and a half, since it concerned nothing that we had yet seen. I asked what a shale-back was, and whether a crew had ever come to harm by one." He inclined his head toward the horn. "He gave me the carters' rule first, and he gave it twice, and I shall give it as he did. 'Never be below them. Never be *loud* below them.'"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:31:"The second he had from a carter, who had it from his father. Men used to come up a side water off the Thornwater road with carts, for roofing stone. It lies ready split at the foot of a great slide, and need only be loaded. One year they did not come back down, and the next year nobody went up to learn why." He lifted his eyes to the wheel-ruts under their feet, with the turf grown over them. "I had not supposed, until this morning, that I should be standing in the second tale."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:39:"I asked him that in those words. He said, the kind a man would not be on a road." Seln spread his hands a little. "He means all three, and he does not know which. It is the rule that Thornwater keeps, for the reason that Thornwater gave us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:41:Cael looked at the slope. Nothing on it moved. A small wind came down the gorge and stirred the heather, and across the water the far wall gave back the sound of the stream, late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:77:"I never said that. You don't row a boat up a weir. You put a line on her bow from the top of the weir, and you lift her head, and you let her swim up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:111:Along the gorge-ward edge of the fan there ran a gully, a chute as wide as a road, and the whole skin of the slope leaned that way, like a floor in an old house. Twenty yards into the second stage the cart began to lean with it. She went sideways, quite gently, a foot for every yard she climbed, floating off the line of the rope toward the chute; and up at the island the peeled log began to creep along its notch after her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:117:She was there already. They had knotted the box-lashings into a second line and made one end fast to the cart's near side, and Lira had carried the other up the slope and to the left, away from the chute, to a boulder as big as a barrel. She sat down behind it now with both boots against the rock, and passed the line round her back, and took it in her two fists in front of her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:121:They walked. The line came taut across the slope and the cart's head swung, a little, back toward the island.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:125:It was a small slip, a barrowful of plates running out from under the rim, and the cart dropped a foot and snatched. Cael saw the line take Lira. It lifted her off her seat and dragged her a yard up the boulder's face, with her heels scraping, and he saw what happened in her calves, because he had been watching for it for sixty days. Every sinew in her stood ready to be somewhere else. It was the burst, asking, with the whole of her Path behind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:127:In his own feet Wind leaned with hers, as it always did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:129:She sat down. She did it on purpose, hard, as one sits on a lid, and drove both boots against the rock again, and the line burned through her fists for a hand's breadth and stopped. The cart's nose came round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:133:He walked. Nine fragments had come up at the snatch, and he went on down the slope with the rope in his hands, putting them down one at a time. Compression had felt the jerk come along the hemp and had offered to take it through his frame into the hill. Anchor had been asking about the island since first light. *There?* A point fixed against that notch, and told to stay, would have been a better thing than any log; it would not have walked, or worn, or needed a man's weight to hold it. He owned the craft of it. He told it that the log was Brom's, and to leave it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:137:Within a pace and a half of his boots it would have given him every plate on that slope, and which way the whole loose skin meant to go, a breath before it went. He was a surveyor today, by the terms of a slip, and he carried an instrument that no surveyor west of the Line could have bought, and he kept it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:139:It was not only the hold. A reading taken with that thing could be checked by nobody, and Cael had lived since his Kindling under figures that could not be checked by the person they concerned. What Karis did with a knotted cord, any carter could do after her, at the same place, and get the same number or call her a liar. That was the map the man was buying. The other would only have been a boy on a hillside saying that he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:185:"Seventy lines." Lira began to laugh, low, mindful of the rule. "Seventy marks. He's being paid by the line." She pointed at the book in his hand. "You've written in one of those every night since a lane in Ardenmere, and nobody ever gave you a copper. Now there's a man with a ford who'll give you a mark every time you rule one off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:187:"It's her line," said Cael. "I only hold the pencil."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:189:"It's in your hand. That's whose it is; ask Pike."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:231:*Seventy-third day. The bench, at the end of the eighth hundred. The cart came up the fan in six stages, by Brom's reckoning and nobody's Path. Eight lines in Karis's table, and each of them says yes. Lira says that I am paid by the line.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:233:*By Pike's rule we are not to be loud under whatever lives on this hill. We have seen one piece of horn and one footmark.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:235:*The nine: held, on a hill where three of them would have been of use. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:237:There was ice on the kettle in the morning, a skin as thin as a leaf, the first that any of them had seen past the Line. Lira ran the ninth hundred with her breath smoking, and they drew the cart on after her with Brom leaning into his rope on the up-slope side. Wherever a gully came down the face it had laid a tongue of loose plates right across the shelf, and on those she floated. There he walked against the strap with his whole body slanted, like a man in a gale.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:3:The table stood under a lean-to roof against the gate-house wall, and nobody in Oxhollow had put so much as a cup on it for twenty-five days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:13:She came down when the shadow of the palisade had reached the well. She washed her hands to the elbow in a bucket, and then she went along the bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:23:The arm was a scald, a week old, and it had gone bad. He saw a red line running up the inside of it from the wrist toward the elbow, and the woman's eyes were too bright. Oryn laid her on the ash and put both hands round the forearm above the cloth, and shut her eyes, and it was as it had been with Lira; there was nothing to see but a square brown woman breathing slowly, and the sweat coming out along her cropped hair. It took half an hour. When she lifted her hands the red line was a faint pink thread, and the woman on the table had begun to cry quietly from relief.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:39:"Does it do anything else? Tide. Could you fight with it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:41:Oryn was slow to answer, and Cael saw her mouth go hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:53:Oryn laid her hand along his left side, over the five bands of linen, and counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:69:"No," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:77:"Everybody's got them. What moves in a person moves in a bed, the same bed all their life, and the shape of the bed is their Path. I put my hands on you and I felt the banks before I felt anything else, and I said Ember, and I was right. I'd know a Wind from a Skin with a glove on." She jerked her chin at the table. "In him it's moving, and it hasn't a side anywhere that I can find."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:83:"They're the same thing, done twice as hard. That's the short one, and a healing house calls it the surface reading. Hands on the hurt, or on a wrist, and a count of ten. It tells me what's broken and where it's standing, and it costs me nothing I'd notice; I did eight of them along that bench." Oryn leaned on the edge of the ash. "There's a long one, which they call the deep reading. Both hands, and all of me, and it isn't a count, it's minutes. I go through the whole of how a person's built, as you'd go through every room of a house with a lamp. It costs what a mending costs. And the one under my hands has to lie still for it, and be whole, since a break shouts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:91:"Get down," said Oryn. "I've three things to ask you, and then I'm done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:107:"I'd have laid money on it." She pulled a buckle through. "Has a healer ever got anything from you? A Tide, anywhere, with hands on?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:129:"Thornwater's on my route in nine days. You'll be on the board by then. Come find me. I want to read you when you're not broken."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:137:Oryn walked out of that gate with her hands in the pockets of a dead man's coat, and she kept them there for the first mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:143:She had spent three mendings at the table since noon, the whole of a Tide's reserve for a day, and every one had been what it should be. The scald had stood in the arm like water behind a board. The ox-man's foot had been four small bones and a great deal of noise. She had gone into each of them as she went into a house she knew, and found what was stopped, and opened it, and come out tired. That was her trade. She had not once, since the morning her Path woke, laid a hand on a living body and had it come back to her empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:149:There was bone. Bone answered to anyone; she had felt the breaks as a blind man feels a step. There was movement, past the bone. She was sure of that, and it was what kept her fingers stupid. A dead thing she could have understood. This was more alive than most, and it ran, and she had felt it running; and she could no more have said which way, or between what, than she could have drawn the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:161:She knew the first of them, as anybody on those roads did. It was Teague, off the north water, with a string of two mules behind him and his three; and she gave them what she gave everything on legs, which was a look from a stride and a half as they drew level. A spear, carried upright and grounded at each step, by somebody who never hurried. A boy who was all wrists. And a bow, on the back of one who walked a hair short on the left, at the hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:185:The route was the only thing she had ever made. She thought of that on the road, where there was time. She had not been given it. There had been no round when she crossed the Line, only a rumour in a ferry-town that the holds had no hands; and she had gone to the first gate, and mended what was there, and asked where the next fence was. It had been four holds, the first year. She had put the fifth on in her third round, and the sixth for an old woman's sake. The seventh she had added because of a child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:201:It was not her hands, then. She had half thought, on the hill out of Oxhollow, that something in them might have begun to fail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:209:She would be at Thornwater on the ninety-third, and he would be fifteen days from the break. She would have her two days at that gate, and the carter's hand, which she owed, and whatever was on the bench; and on the ninety-fifth the round would take her out by the far road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:211:The hand troubled her more than she liked. A split across the palm is a small thing on the day, and she had left it clean and wrapped, and told the man to keep it out of a rein. He would not have kept it out of a rein. He had a load for Thornwater and one pair of hands, and he had given her the news of a late crew when he might have held his tongue and had his mending. By the ninety-third that hand would be fifteen days old. She owed it to him as she owed nothing else on the round, and she meant to pay it before she sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:213:So he would be whole four days after she had gone. She would be two gates on by then, and the round would not bring her back to Thornwater for twenty days more; and a crew off a board does not keep whole for twenty days. He would be on some other road, and as like as not broken in some other place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:225:She took it out of the round. It was five days from the ninety-fifth to the hundredth, and she went through them one at a time, as she went along a bench. Four she could spend at Thornwater's own table without lying to anybody. The grain was moving; carters came through that yard from every hold up the valley, and a bench that filled twice in two days would fill again in four. They would make her late at six gates all the same, and she knew every one of the people she would be keeping waiting. But they were work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:235:It was still the eighty-fourth day, and the light was going off the hill above Oxhollow, when Teague counted his three. He had done it before the healer's mule was out of hearing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:249:But it was a day he had spent and a thing he had hoped for, and he could not set it down against five names. Pike had struck a ford off his hold and given it to them; that had cost nothing, because he had chosen the pass himself. The healer's lateness was not theirs on any ground he had read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:251:The road came round the shoulder of the hill, and Oxhollow lay under him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:257:The mild one was by the gate-post. Teague found him at once, this time, and was pleased with himself for a breath, until he saw that the man had been looking at him since the shoulder of the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:259:The big one sat on the well's coping with a box-rope coiled over his knee. His left wrist had a cuff of clean linen on it, tied with a knot that Teague knew, since he had seen it on his own people. Mended, then, and under orders. The girl was on a trough. She had one boot, and the other foot was bare and strapped, crosswise, by somebody who had been shown how. It was on its whole sole. He had read those feet in an inn as the quickest thing in the room, and he would not have laid a copper on them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:265:The old keeper was on his bench by the stone house, with a tally-stick, and he gave Teague the rest in the time it took two mules to pass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:273:Teague went on down to the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:281:The second cost Teague something, and he had known that he would pay it since the fifty-seventh day. The ford had been good work. To say so aloud was to say that the slip had gone where it ought, and that he had no claim left on it, even in his own head. It was true, and a true thing was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:291:The boy was sitting on the gravel at the water's edge, alone, with a book on his knee. He held one arm in a little against his left side, and under the shirt, when he breathed, something drew a line across his ribs. There was a healer's tape on him. That meant ribs; and ribs, by her rule, were three weeks under linen, counted from whichever day the mountain had done it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:293:Teague still could not tell what he was. A Force stood one way and a Wind another, and there was no Path in the set of that boy that a man could name, and no want of one. He went down the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:303:"You cleared Thornwater's ford with five and lost a cart on the high bench with five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:311:There was no contempt in it. He said it as Pike said a line, level, so that it could be called wrong if it was wrong; and he waited, as Pike waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:313:Cael went through it for the place where he could say so. He thought of a smith in Lowmarch, and six marks owed, and a tail-board that had not banged. He thought of what a ford-keeper had counted out, and what the ford-keeper had said he was paying for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:325:*Eighty-fourth day. Oxhollow, at the ford. The map is delivered: sixty-three lines, and the last one says no. Seventy marks, counted. Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and sixteen. Six owed to the smith for a cart that is in a gorge.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:329:*The nine: held, on a mountain and off it. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:331:*Teague priced us. He's right about the cart. Oryn read me three times and got nothing three times and wants a fourth. Two people out here measuring me, and neither of them has a seal. I keep waiting to mind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:9:"That's all it does," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:23:Oryn tucked the roll into her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:53:"There are forty-four," said Seln, "and they are not in a line. Do not go by the look of a stone; go by the mark. Several without one look a good deal better than their neighbours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:57:Oryn had loaded the mule in the hollow. Two of the boxes hung from the pack-saddle, one on either flank, and her own panniers were lashed on top with the crossbow in its cover. She took the animal by the cheek-strap and walked it to the edge of the break, and there she changed her hand and put herself on the outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:61:"If she slips with me on the inside, she goes that way with nothing to stop her. This way she's got something to lean on." Oryn clicked her tongue. "She's a mule, and she'd sooner lean than fall. Most things would."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:63:They went out onto it together, the brown beast with her near flank brushing the wall and the square woman at her shoulder on the open side, a boot's length from the rim. The mule set each small hoof down and tried it. The woman's eyes were on the scratches; and where a marked stone lay under the animal's line she moved its head a little with her hand, and the foot came down there. At the far end Seln received the cheek-strap, and the boxes came off, and she brought the mule back as steadily as she had taken it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:77:He said it across the gap, to her, before she had opened her mouth. Oryn considered the box, and the rope that went round it and over his chest, and his two hands, which were open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:91:Anchor asked, as he had expected. Here was a rim thirty yards long with a broken boy on it. *There? There?* Each stone under his boot was a place where a point might be fixed and told to stay, and at each one he said no, and moved his foot to the next mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:99:He stood still, with the gorge breathing cold up his back, and he knew what he was choosing between. On the one hand was what he could see for himself. On the other was a line the length of a finger, cut with the point of a knife by a mild man who had come this way in the rain and stood on forty-four stones, one at a time, to learn which would have him. Cael had not asked for that report. He had been given it, since it bore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:107:"So everybody says." Oryn was looking at his colour, and then at his side. "You breathed to the tape the whole way, which is more than most do. Most don't believe it till they've cried out once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:131:"I can tape it." Oryn pulled the lace tight. "I told you on the hill. Tide runs from the healer into somebody else, and there's nowhere in me to run it from. A healer with a broken leg lies in a barn six weeks like anyone, and hopes there's another healer on the next road. There isn't, out here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:151:They slept that night beside a seep of good water, halfway down, in a fold of the hill where somebody had once built a wall of loose stones against the wind. Seln had found it, and he had a fire lit when they came up, and enough dead heather gathered to keep it till morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:157:The track had come down by then onto the last long slant above the valley, and Lira's right foot hung from the pack-saddle at about the height of the healer's shoulder, bare, and wound with the strip of linen that it had worn since a meadow. Oryn went beside it for the better part of a mile. Cael, two paces behind, saw her eyes go to it and come away, and go again, as a person's go to a thing that somebody else has done badly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:169:Oryn kept her pace. She put up her left hand and took the heel in her palm as it swung, and went on so, with another person's foot in her hand and her lips moving; and it took her a good deal longer than ten. She turned the foot outward. It went a little way, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:175:She did her sum aloud, walking, as plainly as Pike did his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:191:They halted at noon, on a floor of turf beside a stream, with the road in sight half a mile off, two pale ruts going north-west; and there she showed her. It was a craft, like any other, and Cael watched it as he would have watched a new guard. One turn went round the shin above the ankle, for an anchor; then a strip ran from the inside of that, down under the heel, and up the outside, pulled hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:207:"That's all tape ever does," said Oryn. "Take it off. It's your turn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:215:Karis had been studying the healer across the flames for some time, with her notebook on her knee. She could hold a pencil again, for as long as it took to write a line, and she had spent her line that evening on the foot of the long leaf. Cael had seen it: *Sixty-three. The ledge. Gone, for thirty-one paces. Cart: no.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:219:"Twenty days," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:231:"That's a carter at the foot of a hill who had a split hand, and told me while I was looking at it that a crew was late off the top. I said I'd do his hand that evening, and then I went up to you." There was no reproach in it. "He's got it in a rag still. He hauls to Thornwater, and I'll find him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:235:"They feed me, and they feed her." She nodded at the mule. "One of them shoes her. One gave me this coat, the year I came, off a peg that nobody was using any more. I get a bed for two nights and a table to work at, and nobody's put a coin in my hand since I crossed the Line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:239:"The day." Oryn looked into the fire. "I said I'd be at a gate on a day. There's a child three stops on with a chest that I listen to every round, and most rounds it's nothing. One round it won't be nothing; and if I'm five days late for that one, then that's what five days cost."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:255:"I remember who I've had my hands on," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:269:"You're Iron, Rank Four; you said so on the hill. That's three above me, and I was thought good." She was picking the words. "A Tide of that rank could sit in any city on the continent and have people come to her. Why are you walking round seven fences with a sore heel?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:271:Oryn was slow to answer; and when she did, though her voice had not changed, Cael thought that it was the first thing she had told them which she had not said a hundred times before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:287:"I know what I am. I'm twelve times late." Oryn reached for her boot. "Go to sleep. That leg knits at night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:289:Cael lay awake a while after the others. He had supposed, for eighty days, that he was the only person past the Line who had walked out from under a measure, and that the rest had merely been born beyond it. Here was another. She had not been thrown out, and she had not run; she had looked at a list that was kept in the wrong order, and gone to find a place where she could keep it in the right one, and all she had asked of that place was a road to do it on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:295:Oxhollow lay in the bend of a broad shallow river, and they came down to its ford in the middle of the eighty-fourth morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:297:The road had brought them along a valley for a day and a half, through alder and rough pasture, with the mountain standing over their right shoulders the whole way. From below it was a long grey wall with a notch in it, and he had looked back at the notch a good many times. On the evening of the eighty-third day he had seen a line of smoke ahead, and in the morning a fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:299:The hold stood on the far bank, in a hollow of the hill, behind a palisade of split logs with a gate in it, and between the gate and the water there was a low stone house with a turf roof. The ford itself was a hundred yards of pale gravel under a foot of quick water, wider than any he had crossed past the Line. On the near side a yoke of red oxen stood in the shallows with a chain between them, chewing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:301:"For the wheels," said Oryn. "He hooks on and draws them through, at a copper a wheel, and nothing for feet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:307:"You'll be the Lowmarch crew that took my paper. A carter said the one-armed man had given it to five." He looked past them, at the empty road, and at the river. "Where's your cart?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:309:Pike had said that he would ask it at his gate, and Cael had had the answer ready for six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:351:He counted it onto the board in tens, seven heaps of thick worn silver, beside the foot of the fan. Then he took a burnt stick and made his mark at the bottom of Pike's slip, pressing hard: a horned head, broad between the horns, over three wavy strokes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:357:"Sixty-three lines," said Lira. She was leaning in the doorway on her taped foot, looking at the heaps. "And seventy marks. That's a mark a line, and seven over. What are the seven for?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:359:"For the last line," said Karis. "It's the only one that says no."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:19:Karis wrote it down as he had said it. *Owed: one rope, sixty yards, four-strand, to the ferryman. No price.* She put the purse away uncounted, and Cael thought that it was the first debt she had ever entered without a number beside it, and that she did not like the look of the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:21:They went over with the Oxhollow cart, a copper a wheel and a copper a beast out of the carter's own bag. Halfway across Brom lifted his head. In the round shed on the eastern bank something brown was walking its circle, steadily, with one white foot coming round and round into the light, and Brom watched that foot for the rest of the crossing with the face of a man who has seen a child of his doing well at a trade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:25:Pike saw them from the landing road. He looked, twice over, at a strange cart with three boxes on it, and at a girl in one boot of her own and one the size of a bucket, and at how Cael held his left arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:35:"So I'd heard, from a man who came in last night." Pike set his pen in its pot. "I'll say your line when I've read your paper. Read mine first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:37:Lira was already reading. She had gone to the left-hand post, from habit, and she was standing in front of two slips that had not been there on the seventieth morning. One was bark and one was a shingle, and on each of them, under a hold's mark and a line of words, stood a word in the square black hand, in capitals, which Cael had seen on this plank only once before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:47:Pike had not raised his head, and he said it level, as he would have told her the price of standing-money.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:51:"Who they ought to have. One's a bull wants bringing down off the top grazing before the snow shuts it, and he's killed a dog. The other's a ford that wants staking. They'd each of them heard a line said in this meadow about a river and five dead hounds." He blotted something. "I told them you were six days off on a hill. They said they'd wait a fortnight. After that they'll have Teague."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:55:He had been written on a good many boards. A woman in Ardenmere had chalked him up with a question mark, and a panel had posted him at a rate that it had scattered on purpose, and for two years a house had put his name on a card every week because a schedule told it to. Nobody had ever before gone to the keeper of a book and asked who was good, and been given him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:57:He had stood in an inn, forty days ago, and heard Pike say that he could not send a hold a crew that had never yet had anything go wrong. Something had gone wrong for them twice since then, and they had come back from it both times, and here it was on a shingle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:65:It had come down a mountain inside her shirt and six days along a valley, and it was soft at the folds. Pike laid it on the oak and put two fingers on it, and read it to himself from the careful writing at the head to the foot, where five strokes stood side by side over a horned head and three wavy lines; and then he read it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:75:Pike nodded slowly. He opened the book at the leaf with five names, and set the flat stone on its upper corner, and turned his shoulder to the meadow. There were perhaps thirty people within hearing, and Cael saw the hide-woman among them, with her knife on her thigh, and under the ash an old carter who lifted his chin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:77:"High traverse, Oxhollow to Thornwater. Mapped. Delivered at the ford. Carts go round."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:83:It went down the meadow in that level carrying voice, past five fires. A woman looked at Lira's boot. A man by the nearest tent said something to his neighbour, low, and the neighbour looked at the strange cart, and then at the empty place beside the smith's shed where a hired one had stood. The Oxhollow carter had halted his mules at the edge of the grass to listen. At *carts go round* he had nodded slowly, like a person who hears his own errand read out to strangers; and at *cart lost* he looked at his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:85:"Is it wrong?" said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:87:He put it to all five, as he put it to anybody. Cael went through the line a word at a time, for the place where he could say so. *Mapped* was true, and *delivered*; carts would go round, for an old man with a tally-stick had said it in those words. There had been a cart, and there was none, and three of them had been hurt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:89:He looked for the thing that the line left out, and found it at once, and it was everything: a ledge that had shown no crack, and a page marked derived, and eight seconds of fire. None of that was in Pike's column, and he had known since a river bank that it would not be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:93:Pike wrote, and the pen went to the horn pot and came back four times. When the line was on the page he did a thing that Cael had not seen him do before; he dropped the nib to the space beneath it and wrote four words more, smaller, and drew a short rule under them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:101:It was Brom who said it. He had come up to the oak, and he was looking at the small words under the line as he had looked at a smith's cut axle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:105:Pike straightened, and gave the whole of it to the meadow a second time, from the first word to the last, with the six taken off at the end; and this time nobody along the fires murmured. He did not go to the iron box, since there was nothing in it of theirs. He drove the soft paper down onto the spike beneath the plank, among the dead slips, and that was the end of a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:109:He turned that over, since he had expected to. On the bank at Oxhollow he had written that he kept waiting to mind, and he had gone on waiting for six days of road. The line said that they had lost a cart, and it said so to thirty strangers, twice. And he stood under an eave with five bands of linen round his chest and felt only that a thing had been counted, by a person who counted everything alike; it was the seventh line under their names, and it was worth sixty-four, and both of those were so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:113:"I want one thing straight, for my own page," she said. "The seventy is in here. I counted it at a ford, and it hasn't altered on the road. Earned stands at a hundred and thirty-two. What you have written under that line is another figure, and I don't believe that it is money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:115:"It's what I'd tell a hold you were worth for that job, if a hold came and asked me," said Pike. "The six you'll count out to a smith yourselves, before dark, if I know you. I only take it off where the meadow can see it go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:125:He had been reading the plank, as he read it every time, from the left-hand post to the right, and there were nine slips besides the two with their names on. He had sorted them and found nothing among them that weighed much, and his eye had come to rest, for want of anything better, on Pike's flat stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:127:It was a grey river stone the size of a loaf, and it lay on the upper corner of the open book, as it always lay. Something was under its far edge: a corner showed there, toward Pike's side of the oak, no wider than three fingers, and it was neither bark nor hide. It was paper, of a good cream weight, such as a clerk writes on at a desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:129:There was a curve of dust across the corner, and he looked at that for a breath before he understood it. The dust followed the rim of the stone exactly, a fine pale line, as a tide leaves its line on a beach; so the paper had not been slid there this morning. It had lain under that stone, in that one place, for long enough to take the stone's shape.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:131:Every other slip that Pike had was on a tack, and he read them all out to anybody who was frying dough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:133:Cael leaned a little over the oak. The writing ran away from him, toward the man with the book, and he read it upside down, as he had once read a bailiff's list on a counter in Denvash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:135:*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Fallow road. East of Thornwater; the second day; where the road leaves its line at the rise and comes back to it. One hundred marks (one hundred).*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:139:He read it a second time, to be sure of the figure. Then he reached across the oak and drew the paper out from under the stone by its corner, and the dust-line broke. He turned the sheet round and held it out to Pike, in his fingers, saying nothing, as any crew holds out a slip that it wishes to hear said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:141:Pike's face stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:147:"That's the Fallow Ring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:149:He said it to the paper. Lira had turned round from her two slips, and Brom from the spike. None of them answered him, since the name meant nothing to any of the five, and Pike raised his eyes and saw that it meant nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:151:"That's quiet ground."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:159:Pike laid the cream sheet down on the oak, face upward, and put his hand on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:161:"Quiet ground's where the sigil goes to sleep. Yours. Everyone's. You walk a road, and there's a place on it where your Arbiter stops — not slows; stops — and you walk on past and it's back. There's a handful of them. Along the void roads, all of them. Carters walk round them, and have as long as there've been carters. The dogs live in them. Stillhounds. Nobody's asked them why. Why any of it, nobody knows and nobody's needed to, because the rule's *don't go in*, and the rule's never once failed anybody who kept it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:173:"I've walked past the Fallow Ring two hundred times. Never once stepped over the line. Thirty years on the roads and I've never met a practitioner who did it twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:175:"What's the line?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:179:"You'll know," he said. "Your Arbiter tells you. It stops."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:189:Cael knew the place. East of a square with a dot in it, two days of ticks out, a double line left its straight and went out to the left in a curve and came back to itself, as a path goes round a pond; and inside the curve a carter had drawn nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:193:"He has drawn that. A road that leaves its line and comes back. He drew it for his own wheels, and never drew what was in the middle, since he had no business there. And she said that everybody knew it, and that nobody went in." She took the finger away. "I have had those two for a month and could do nothing with either. This is the third, and it has a price on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:195:She looked up at Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:199:"It's a paper with a hundred on it," said Pike. "I'd not call it more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:205:"I said it. I'm bound to say it; twice, the day it came, to the whole grass, with the price." He turned the cream sheet a little with one finger. "The broker's clerk brought it up from the landing the morning after the last sack, sealed as you see it, with a hundred marks in a bag, counted. I've had that seal on bales for thirty years. I never had it before on a slip that sends a crew two days past Thornwater."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:211:He said that last quite mildly, and Cael understood that he had been told something about the man. Pike wrote down whatever was put in front of him and read it to anyone; but he had found, in thirty years, one paper that he would not hang up between two fences and a wheel, and he had kept it where a person must go looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:219:"That I couldn't tell you," said Pike. "It's not a trade I've ever seen come over my ferry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:227:"I'll not mark it tonight," said Pike. "That's not a thing I'd say of a fence. Take it in with you, and put it on your boards, and look at it with a lamp. If you bring it me in the morning I'll say it and write your strokes." He pushed the paper across the oak with one finger, until it lay in front of Cael. "And if you don't, it goes back where it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:249:The account was square, as far as coin could square it. There remained a rope with no figure against it, and a line in a book that said *cart lost*, and neither of those could be paid off; they could only be added to, by whatever the five of them did next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:253:She was standing where the eave's shadow stopped, with her toes on the line of it, her own boot and the great one side by side, and she was looking east. The saddle road went up there, pale in the last light, toward a wash, and a ford, and a fence; and beyond the fence there was a road that none of them had walked, with a bend in it. The cold was coming up off the river behind them. Along the meadow the fires had drawn in to red, and somebody was calling a child home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:267:"He counts what he hasn't done," she said. "That's all that is. Two hundred times he didn't. You'd have gone round it the first time, and the second, and you'd have written both down. And the third time you'd have stood there with your book shut, working out what it would cost to look." She scuffed the great boot along the shadow's edge. "I've watched you price things since a lane in Ardenmere. You've said *not yet* to me, and *not here*, and *no* a hundred times. You don't say *never*. I don't think you know how."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:275:"So do I." She said it low, and she was still looking at the road. "He said your Arbiter tells you. It stops. I've spent three months not doing the one thing I'm good at, on purpose, a stride at a time, and it went off anyway when a ledge fell. I'd like to stand somewhere that I couldn't. I'd like to know what's left when it isn't me doing the *not*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:13:He did not count. He had the book open against the shaft, with the leaf turned to the sixty-third line, and he had written the number and nothing after it. Above them the slope was in shadow still. Thirty grey heaps lay along the upper shelf with white on their backs, and not one of them had yet begun to scrape.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:15:It was the last morning of the job, and he let himself know it. Three lines remained of a table that had sixty-two. After the ledge there was a shoulder, and a hillside going down into a brown valley, and a man at a ford who would count seventy marks into Karis's purse. He had walked a mountain for seven days with nine things shut in him, writing down what a cord and a clock and a girl's feet could tell him, and not one line of it had wanted anything that he was. He thought that he would be sorry to come down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:43:It was Wind, and it was hers, and it was the whole of it. He had not heard that sound from her since a painted floor at Norhold, and she had never, there or anywhere, let it go so hard. She was not on the falling stone. She was twenty feet east of it, in the air, and then she was standing on sound ledge with both arms flung wide. Behind her a hundred feet of shelf was going down into the blue with a roar that grew as it fell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:45:Wind stood up in his own feet to answer hers. He put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:61:Then the shelf began to come away in front of the leaders. He saw the edge of it sag, and a grey back lurch outward, and after that there was no shelf and no line, only the whole upper face of the bay beginning to move. The animals had turned downhill, since downhill was the only way that the ground was going. They came in a front, eighty yards wide and three or four deep, and the mountain's loose skin came with them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:97:She had no breath to spare for it. She had both hands lifted a little in front of her, the palms with their pink lines turned toward the hill, and she had begun to do the thing that Ternhall had taught her before she was fifteen. *Points first, always. Joins last.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:115:He gave it no more than a breath's looking, and that was enough. The front had come a third of the way, and it was no longer grey; it had a brown smoke of dust standing over it, and out of the smoke came the sound, a deep steady grinding with the hail of the plates on top. He passed Brom going the other way, and Brom's eyes went to him and to the cart and he did not waste his wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:123:All nine of them were up. He had never felt them so, on the stretch or at the ford; there the offers had come one behind another, and here they came in a single shout. Wind wanted his feet and had the whole distance measured. Storm showed him the air over the bay, torn already, as a lane. He put them down as he ran, without words, since there was no time for words, and they got up again at every stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:127:He saw it at the edge of his left eye and could not help turning his head. Across the face of the mountain, above the pale rock, a line of fire had stood up out of the shale. It ran from a point high over the wall's middle down and outward on either hand, straight as two rules, yellow-white, the height of a man's knee; and it was sixty yards from end to end. She had joined them. Nine points and eight channels, no join longer than eight paces, laid on frozen stone in the time it takes to lace a boot, and every one of them burning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:133:He could see the animals now, the foremost, with their heads down and their plates flying; and he saw them feel it. The middle of the line broke. The ones dead above the arrowhead's point flung themselves left and right as if the ground there had been snatched away, and the ones beside them went with them, and in the space of two breaths a front eighty yards wide had torn down the middle into two streams. Eleven paces above the rock there was nothing coming at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:139:He was outside her line. Its eastern foot stood a little ahead of him and uphill, burning, and the eastern stream was pouring down past the end of it, and he was in the edge of where that stream was going to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:143:Compression had the thing measured already. It had waited since a river for a blow worth taking, and it showed him how: a plated shoulder at a run, met square, and sent down through his frame into the slab beneath the shale. He would have stood. Anchor offered him the other way. *There.* One fixed point against the rock under his left boot, and told to stay, and a boy with a box would have been a post that a river goes round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:147:It would have gone off in front of animals that were running from one already. They were pouring away from her fire, down and east; if a second loud thing stood up in their road, the leaders would check, and the rest would pile on them from behind, and a stream that is dammed goes over its bank. Its bank was sixty yards of fire held by one small person who had never held a third of it. They would come back through her line and over the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:157:Then he was in the air, going the way he had chosen, with both arms still round the box. He came down on his left leg in the shale at the foot of her fire, and slid, and the edge of a plate opened his calf along the old line as a thumb opens a pod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:245:So she bound the ankle in the dark, on the seventy-eighth morning, and ate nothing, as she had eaten nothing before a card. The foot was what it was. It bent less than the other and later, and she had stopped being angry with it somewhere on the bench and begun simply to allow for it, as one allows for a wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:259:She had been told at Fenmark that a burst was a choice, and at Norhold she had made a rank of choosing well. For sixty days on gravel bars and roads she had refused it a stride at a time, on purpose, and she had been proud of how good she had got. Not one of those refusals was anywhere near her now. The ground went, and her body did what a body with Wind in it does when the ground goes, and it did not send up to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:287:Wind said *now*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:311:She was outside the line. She saw that with great clearness. Ten yards more would put her under the rock, and she did not have ten yards; she had perhaps six, and a foot that was a beat behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:313:*Now*, said Wind, for the last time, and it was not reasonable any longer. It was begging.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:317:Something the size of a cart went behind her so close that the wind of it turned her half round. She kept her legs. She was three strides from the stone, with her arm already out for it, when the shale that the thing had brought down with it took her ankles from under her like a wave on a beach, and she went onto her face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:Oryn had hobbled her on the western side, with a full net and the two panniers still roped on, and the long shape in its oiled cover lashed along the top of the pack as it had been since a mountain. The healer looked at that cover for a breath before she turned away. Then she walked across the line with her hands open at her sides, and Cael saw her fingers close once, hard, as the count went out of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:11:"East, along the old line of the road," said Karis. "I want the cord laid from this stone to the middle, a length at a time, as we laid it round the edge. If I have my sum right, the tenth will end among those stones. If I have it wrong, I should like to be told so by the ground and not by a table."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:He had his nine fragments as he always had them, and he held them. Wind leaned, since Lira was in front of him. Anchor had fallen quiet at the stake and had not asked since. He had opened nothing after a boulder, and he did not open anything now; but he walked those two hundred yards as the only one of the six who knew that he could have, and it was a strange thing to carry at the back of a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:31:"Ten cords," she said at last. Her voice was not altogether steady. "I worked it at the stone from the round, with a pencil. And I have walked it now with a rope, from the edge, in a straight line, and there is not a full stride between the two. I have never in my life had a figure come home like that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:81:She tipped what was left of the chips into her lap, thirty of them or more, and sorted them by eye into three heaps on the floor. There were the honey-coloured stones, such as the walls of Ostrand were built of. There were the dark close ones, like a district hall's, and a pale kind with a sparkle in the break. She chose the freshest-broken from each heap and held the three against the grey together, in the slanting light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:83:"No." She let them fall. "Not the colour, and not the grain; it has no grain at all. I have a piece of every sort of dressed stone between Denvash and the Line in this box, with a date to each, and not one family of them comes near it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:87:He put both palms on the floor. It was smooth, and very slightly rough under the skin, like the shell of an egg. He moved his hands outward over it as far as his ribs would let him, feeling for the thing that every floor he had ever knelt on possessed somewhere: a crack, or a line where one piece met the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:93:It was the same thing that he had done at the line, done now for work, and he did not count it a third. For a pace and a half round his knees, and a pace and a half straight down, he had the weight of what he was kneeling on, and it was one thing. There was no bed under it, and no joint through it, and no place where it changed. The short upright stood within his reach, and he took in the foot of that as well. It went down into the floor as a horn goes into a skull, and he could find no line between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:127:There were no instruments. There was a stake on the line with a registry's stamp on it, and that was the only thing within a quarter of a mile that a surveyor had ever touched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:131:"Then it stays shut," said Brom. "We've walked four days for a line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:133:"For eleven sheets and a line," said Karis, without lifting her pencil. "I should not undersell the sheets."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:153:Oryn had sat down on the western lip with her boot off. She had watched the whole afternoon from there, pressing the strip on her heel now and then with her thumb, and she had not once asked what any of it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:161:"I do, though. That's what it's like. The year before my Kindling I used to run the lanes at Fenmark against the big girls, with nothing in me, just legs, and I'd forgotten there was ever such a person." She shifted her palm for the pencil. "I didn't think that I'd meet her again. I'm not sure I mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:167:She numbered the last corner and rolled the sheet, and for once she did not stop to look at what she had got. Brom had set the box at the foot of the tallest stone, on the eastern side, an hour ago, to keep ten sheets of paper out of the north wind. He knelt there now with his back to it and reached behind him for the strap, and Cael went to steady the weight onto his shoulders, since that was a thing that could be done with one arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:191:Nothing was in them. He checked that twice. The hollow was empty, as far as light went, and no sound came out of it, and the wind brought him only the smell of cold earth and something rank and dry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:211:A stillhound hunted what went out of a person. Every hold in that country kept its Paths in its pockets on a road because of it; and here was a place, a quarter of a mile across, where a Path could not be taken out of a pocket by anybody. A deer that wandered over that line was a deer. A carter who did was a man with a stick. And five people who had cleared a ford with a declaration and a knife were standing at the door of the den with a hedging bill and two knives, and a healer whose hands had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:221:Then four of them looked at Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:237:*Ninety-sixth day. The Fallow Ring, at the eastern lip of the floor. Sunset.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:239:*The circle is perfect. The stakes are the registry's. The dogs are inside. Everyone's Path dies at the line and mine didn't, and I've told nobody, and I'm going to need to think about why I haven't. Not tonight. Tonight we're inside the one place on the continent where a stillhound can't lose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:243:There was a line that came next. He had written it at the foot of every entry since a shelf above a valley with no name, and for the last forty-three days it had ended in the same word. His pencil went to the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:245:He did not write it. It would not have been true, and he did not yet know what would be. He shut the book on an entry that stopped short, for the first time since he had crossed the Line, and put it away in Hesk's satchel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:247:Something lifted in him then, soft, behind the other eight. It came as it came at every dusk, in a meadow or on a road or at the turn of a carter's ruts. It was offering to take him out of the light. He stood ten cords and more inside the line, on the far side of a floor where a Shadow of fifteen years had found nothing to lift, and it came up in him exactly as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:17:"To Thornwater?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:67:"I should think somebody does, about once a day." He stood up carefully. "They went up this bank at dusk, with the light going and a river to get over, and there isn't a scuffed print among the four of them. Nobody turned to look back. Nobody stepped out of the line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:107:"Yes. That's her tape. I'd know where it was in a barrel." She tied the left boot to the cart's tail by its laces, where it hung all the way to Lowmarch, turning slowly, like a sign over a shop. "Don't anybody tell me what it looks like."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:117:A breath went in to a count of three before the tape spoke. On a level road he could take that breath at every fourth stride and never touch the edge. On a rise it wanted every third, and on the steepest of the valley's little hills every second, and there the breaths came so shallow and so quick that his head swam. He had always walked in fits. He had gone quick and then slow since he was a child on the lanes of Denvash, running to look at a thing and standing still to count it. He had watched a healer come down a mountain for a day and a half at one unbroken beat, and he had not been able to copy her for a hundred yards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:145:"I can see near enough. She didn't say near enough." He had his eyes on the rod's shadow, which lay along the grass to the north, as long as his hand and growing shorter. "She said a week from the middle of the eightieth. I was there. I saw where the rock's shadow lay when she took her hands off it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:147:So they ate, and the shadow drew in. Nobody spoke of it, but Cael noticed that nobody strayed far either. The carter chewed, and looked from the rod to the big man and back, with the mild puzzlement of a person watching the customs of a foreign hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:149:It took the better part of a quarter of an hour. The shadow came in to the width of three fingers and stood there, and stood, and then, by a hair that Cael would not have sworn to, it was on the other side of the rod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:227:It was Lowmarch's river. He knew its colour, and the way it bent. They had come back to it from the west, by a bank that he had never stood on; and somewhere down there in the dark was a round thatched shed with a drum in it, and a horse walking a circle that had once drawn their cart across a province. Seln had a fire going with his flint. The carter sat at it as a matter of course, and held his enormous feet to the warmth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:231:"Tomorrow is the ninetieth," she said. "The plank, and a smith. I have a slip in my coat with a horned head on it, and I want to hear what he does with a job that ended in a no." She turned a leaf. "And Thornwater on the ninety-third. That's four days, and two of them are road."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:233:"We haven't said we're going to Thornwater," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:259:*Eighty-ninth day. The west bank, a morning short of the ferry. The boxes rode from Oxhollow on the keeper's grain cart, for nothing, since the map said no. In hand, a hundred and fourteen; a boot. Six owed to the smith and a rope to the ferryman.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:263:*A crew went down this road a day ahead of us and never left a mark out of its line. I read them at every halt. They'll have been at the plank tonight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:265:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:3:The ferry brought its sack at noon, and Pike gave out the mail as he gave out everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:7:"*Cael,*" said Pike. He was frowning at a sheet folded very small. "And under it, *past the Line.* I've had that direction once before." He looked over the heads, twice. "I've the one Cael."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:11:"Stand where you are," said Pike, and his hand was in the sack again. "There's another. Same name, same three words, and a different hand altogether."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:17:He had sent one letter west to her, on the fourth day, by a courier with a split lip, and it had carried no place to answer to; to the other he had sent none at all. Both of them had written into the air, as an old man in Denvash had, and the air had brought both to the one plank that there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:They spent the rest of that day getting ready for a road. The Oxhollow carter had his off mule at the smith's for a shoe. Brom went over the three box-ropes a hand's breadth at a time, since they were all the rope that the five now owned. Seln was gone from the meadow between the mail and dusk, and came back with pale dust on his boots, and said nothing; and Cael, who had made a promise at that plank, looked at the dust and then at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:65:*Withrow's tenure holds. That is the whole of the sentence. I asked whether there was a longer one and was told there was not, in a way that meant there had been, for a week.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:67:*A query came down to the registry from somewhere above it. Bracken would not say where and I did not ask twice. It wanted former enrollees' last known direction of travel. Bracken answered it. He gave them a map reference to the Line and nothing past it — which is true, and which Bracken enjoyed being true. He told me so at the plaque wall with his hands behind his back, and then told me he had not told me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:71:*Rooke says the yard's quieter. He didn't say it was worse. He also didn't say it wasn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:75:A small wind came down the wash and lifted the corner of the sheet, and it was some little time before any of them found a word to put after that narrow upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:83:"Bracken. A map reference to the Line and nothing past it. Somebody up a stair wanted to know which way we'd gone, and he gave them a place that everybody already knew we'd been, and wrote it out properly, I expect, with a number." She was grinning. "I never liked him. I'd like to shake his hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:85:"He'll have enjoyed it for a week," said Karis. "It is the only kind of joke that a registrar is allowed." But she was smiling too, in her small tight way; and then she sobered. "Withrow holds. I had wondered. They had a week of something, by that, and it went no further."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:89:"He's quoted Rooke," he said at last. "That's Rooke to the life. He'd not tell you a thing was good, and he'd not tell you it was bad; he'd tell you what he'd seen, and you could carry it home and find out. *Quieter.* I'd like to know which of us he meant."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:93:Cael had read the last lines but two again, to himself. *I still lose to you every time I run it.* He could see the bout as it had gone, on sixty feet of sprung oak, with a blade's edge declared against him in earnest and nothing held back on either side; and he could see a tall, exact person walking it through afterward in an empty yard, alone, looking for the place where it turned. Something in Cael stirred at the thought, quick and bright, and asked *Edge?* of the nearest arris, which was the lip of the wash. He told it no. It was Ephram's, that question. He carried it for him, unspent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:115:They stood round it, and it was a handsome thing. The registry had printed it from a plate, in black, on a sheet as long as Brom's arm, and the whole of a continent was there: every district with its bound ruled round it, and every chartered road a firm double line, and the towns in three sizes of letter by their standing. Cael found Ardenmere, and a river he had crossed on a bridge, and a bluff above Ostrand with a small square on it. Somebody had written *ridge* beside a pass in the east, in pencil, and a little farther on, *Shelf; a pine; water at 36*, in the same small exact hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:119:"There's the Line," said Karis. Her finger went down a ruled stroke that crossed the sheet from top to bottom. "And here is what they have printed east of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:121:It was a river or two, laid in without their bends, and a scatter of hills, drawn all alike; and it was seven roads. They left the Line at seven places down the length of the sheet, and each was drawn as a row of short dashes, with no name beside it; and each ran out into the white paper for the length of a thumb, or a finger, and stopped. There was no town at the end of any of them. The dashes simply came to an end, as a person's voice does who has forgotten what he meant to say, and beyond them the sheet was clean to its margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:125:"I make it the third from the top, by where it crosses the first water. They have drawn the crossing and left out the ferry. They have left out Lowmarch." Karis put her little finger on the last dash of the third road. It ended in nothing, three fingers east of the river. "That is about the saddle. By this sheet we walked off the end of everything that is known the first time we climbed out of that meadow, and we have been standing on blank paper ever since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:137:She showed them. The first they knew: it was the double line that left its straight, two days beyond Thornwater, and went round a patch of bare skin and came back, and she had ringed the patch in pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:145:"Could you rule a line through them?" said Lira. "You've got that brass thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:149:"Their map has no roads. His has no quiet ground. Between them you get the country."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:155:They came up to Thornwater's gate an hour after noon on the ninety-third day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:171:"That's one," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:191:He did not make a sentence of it. He gave it as he would have given Pike a line: a paper off the plank, under a broker's seal; something to be fetched from a place on the Fallow road, two days east, at a rise; out by the east gate at first light. He said what the place was called, and what Pike called it, and he asked her for nothing. He had no more right to that sentence than he had had at a well, and he found that it was easier to leave unsaid the second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:193:Oryn ate, and heard him out, and scraped the bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:203:"The other's mine." Oryn looked at her own right hand. "Every carter on my round has told me the same thing about that place, for thirty rounds. That a healer's hands are no good in it. Not one of them's seen it. They had it from their fathers. I've let it go by me all that time, since I'd no business in there and a list to keep." Her mouth went hard. "I don't like a thing about my own hands that I've only been told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:221:It was the place where he had written on his first stay at Thornwater, and on his second. Two of the boxes stood behind him among the barley sacks, and the third, with the corner that was worn white, waited by the ladder's head for the morning. Down in the yard the Oxhollow carter was backing his mules to an empty bed. He would load grain at first light and go home by the ferry, sixteen days in all, and he had said goodbye to nobody; but Cael had seen him stand for some time looking at a boot that hung from his cart's tail, and then untie it, and carry it over, and stand it carefully outside the barn door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:227:There was a bench under its window, and by dusk there were seven people on it, three of the hold and the rest come in with the east carts, and one of them a child. Oryn came out when the shadow of the fence had crossed the well, and washed to the elbow, and went along them as he had seen her go along eight at Oxhollow; and she chose two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:231:Oryn glanced at the lamp, and then across the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:233:"You. Ember."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:245:"That's honest." Oryn jerked her head at the sill. "This is a yard, and I want a light. Come here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:255:Oryn had two fingers on her wrist already, and two on her neck, and her lips moved to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:261:Cael sat on in the loft door until the cold drove him in. Tomorrow there would be an east gate, and a road with a bend in it; and for the first time since he had crossed the Line, six people would go out by a gate together where there had always been five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:7:There could be no lamp. She had lit one in a yard two evenings ago and been absurdly glad of it, and she would not have set a point here for all the paper in Ternhall. She knelt down in the frozen grass on the western side of the stone and laid the notebook open on her knee. The stars were out. She could see the white of the page and not much else, and she resolved to write large, one line to a leaf, and sort it by daylight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:9:First she called her sigil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:17:There was no flicker in it, and no fading. She had half expected a lamp turned down, and it was a lamp that had never been in the room. She stood a stride inside the line with her heart going hard, and looked for the place behind her breastbone, and found the place. There was nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:29:She had been empty for nine days. She had spent every point she owned on a mountain in eight seconds, and afterward she had not been able to strike a flint. But in all those nine days the sigil had been there. She had called it on the first night under the pale rock, and it had come, faithful as ever, and when she asked it for a point it had simply had nothing to give her. That had been a counter with a clerk behind it and an empty till.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:33:*Empty: sigil present, reserve gone.* She wrote it large, by feel. *Here: reserve present, sigil gone.* She turned the leaf. *These are not the same condition and must not be entered under one word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:39:She tried next to make the line finer than a stride, and could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:41:A hand held out across the stone did nothing at all; the sigil hung where it was. She had watched the healer stand so at dusk, for a long while, and had not asked her what she felt. An arm did nothing. But when she stood astride the stone itself, with one boot on either side, she had the sigil or had it not according to how she leaned, and Karis would not write down a lean. She put: *Narrower than one stride of mine. No finer figure tonight. Wants daylight, and a second person to watch my feet.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:47:Five people had walked up to this place in a row, an arm's length apart. She was an Ember, and Lira a Wind, and Brom an Iron Skin; there was a Shadow among them of a higher tier than any of the rest, and a Tide who had been trained in a healing house. Karis had sat at lectures on what an Arbiter was. She had copied out, in a restricted room, a good many pages on the subject, and on one point every page agreed: it was a person's own. It woke for that one person, on one morning, and named a single gift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:73:"Because yours sits nearest the skin of any of us. You had your hand up before the rest of us had finished the stride." Karis shook out the long cord, with its scraps of red wool. "I walk on the line itself, with this end. Brom has the other, at the last mark. When the cord comes tight between us I stop, and find the line under me, and that is a station. Brom cuts a turf there with the bill and turns it over, so that we leave a black mark in the grass at every cord. Then he comes up to me, and we do it again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:79:"What do I do?" said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:81:"You keep the stone. Somebody must be standing at the place where we began, so that we know it when we come round to it from the other side. And I should like one of us to be outside the whole time, with the mule, where an Arbiter is an Arbiter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:95:At the second station Karis did a new thing. She turned round and looked back along the cord to the first turf, and beyond it to the stone; and then she waved Seln forward along that same line, until he stood a full cord ahead of her with a peeled wand held upright.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:97:"That is where the next mark would fall if the line ran straight," she said. "It won't. Watch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:99:They walked the third cord. Lira kept to her stride inside, and Karis to the line, and when the cord came tight Karis was standing well to the right of Seln's wand. She had her short cord out already, and she laid it on the grass from the wand to her own boots and read the knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:105:It was the third line of a new table, and it was of another kind from the sixty-three that he had written on a mountain. There a cord and a clock had shown what anybody could check. Here the only instruments were two people's Arbiters, going out and coming back; he had none to offer, and so he held the pencil for the people who had, and it surprised him a little that he did not grudge it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:117:Lira had come out across the line to look, and she crouched and tipped her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:129:Seln had come back down the line with his wand, and he stood over the stake and looked at it without bending. His mild face told Cael nothing whatever, and by now Cael knew that for a kind of news.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:131:Something in Cael had turned toward the thing as well. Anchor asked now, more quietly than it had ever asked, as one craftsman takes note of another's work. *There.* It was not a question. Here was a point that somebody else had fixed, and told to stay, and it had stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:135:He had laid two things side by side at a wash four days ago, and had refused to rule a line between them for want of a third. A query had come down to a registry from somewhere above it, asking which way certain people had gone. A paper had come over a river with a hundred marks, in the hand of somebody who had learned to write at a ruled desk. And here, on the edge of a place where a carter would not put a wheel, was a bar of iron with a registry's stamp on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:137:That made three. He could rule the line now, and it would be straight. What he could not do was say which way along it he was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:145:They went on. Cael walked the fourth cord with his head turned back over his shoulder. The bar stood in the grass where they had left it, brown and small, beside a square of black turf; and he thought that it was a strange thing to have crossed a Line, and a mountain, and ninety-six days of road, and to be met at the far end of all of it by two numerals that somebody at a counter had signed for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:157:They went round the north side with the ruts beside them. He could see where the carts had gone, forty yards off, keeping the same distance from the line the whole way, as exactly as if the carters had had a cord of their own; and he supposed that in a sense they had. A man who walked in front of a mule here for twenty-two years, with a Path in him, would have learned to the yard where he ceased to be comfortable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:159:From the north the ring looked different. The eleven uprights had closed up and overlapped, and between two of the tallest he had his first clear sight of what lay among them. It was a floor, grey and pale and perfectly level, raised a little above the grass like the top of a low table; and the morning light lay on it without a shadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:161:The ruts came back to their old line on the eastern side, and went away from them over the next swell. Seln was waiting there, a cord ahead, and he was down on one knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:165:There were paths in the grass, and Cael would not have found them. They were no wider than his two hands, and the stems had not been broken, only pressed aside, low down, by something that went under them rather than through. Three of them crossed the line within twenty yards of one another. Where the nearest ran over a patch of bare ground there was a print in the frost-softened earth, as broad as a palm, with no mark of a claw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:191:They had come up from the south, with the grey stone in plain sight ahead and a square brown figure standing beside it with a mule. Karis reached the end of the sixty-second cord, and stopped, and found the line, and Brom cut his turf; and the stone was still some way off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:193:She coiled the cord over her shoulder, and walked the rest on the line, in her own short steps, counting under her breath, until she set her last foot down against the stone itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:207:"From any mark on it to the middle is ten cords. I can work that without a table. Ten cords from this stone, on the old line of the road, and one would be standing in the centre of those."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:213:It lay in the air a breath. Lira was looking at the stones, and Brom had the bill over his shoulder and was counting his turfs. Oryn, who had stood at that stone since first light, was watching five people come toward her along a line that she could not see. Nobody took the words up, and Karis did not seem to look for anyone to do so. She had entered a property of the figure, and she bent to tie the cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:229:"I do not know what the number counts, and I shall not guess." Seln rose. He looked from the stake to the ring, and back along the line of turfs to the stone, as he would have looked over a room that somebody had left in a hurry. "It stands a boot's width from where she stopped. She found that place with her feet, this morning, knowing nothing. Whoever drove this found the same place to the same boot's width."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:235:Cael looked at it. A stake marked the far end of a measure; somebody had stood at another place, with the other end of a line in his hand, and had paid it out until it reached exactly here.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:237:He looked along the only line there was. It ran from the stake's foot, across two hundred yards and more of pale grass, to the middle of eleven stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:15:He looked for the leader without thinking. A pack had a first mover, and a crew had a captain, and every yard he had ever sat above had held one fighter round whom the rest arranged themselves. He went along the line from the east end to the west for the animal that the others watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:41:It was deeper than the first, a long low cave of a place where a bed of softer stone had weathered back, and from its mouth the whole line of the upper shelf could be seen against the evening sky. He had laid a ring of stones on its floor, and put nothing in the ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:49:"Hear me first. Pike's *loud* has three things in it, as the country's rule has, and I don't know which of them matters to those." She tipped her head at the roof, and at what was above it. "Of the three, a fire is the one I can try this evening and then take back. It's struck with a flint, it will be the size of my two hands, and if a head comes up, Seln puts his coat over it. A Path I can't take back, and a shout I'd sooner not try on purpose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:75:"That's what I'd have argued. I had four sentences ready." Her eyes had the brightness in them that he had seen over a charcoal river. "I shall want a second pair of eyes, since I can't watch both ends of a line three hundred yards long; and a hand, for when mine stops."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:91:The second thing took her until the sun was on the shelf. High on the face, a little west of the leading animal, a vein of white stone ran down the slope as broad as a door, and at the first hour by the clock the leader's beak had been a cart's length short of it. At the second hour the white vein was behind the leader's tail, and the whole line, thirty animals and all their gaps, lay forty yards farther along the mountain than it had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:95:"I saw the sixth come up a pace, and twice more I saw a leg lift. I never saw the line go anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:101:The sun had been on the crags above the upper shelf for an hour, and whatever the frost had been holding there it let go. He heard a crack, a long way up, and then a hollow knocking that grew; and a rock the size of a keg came down the face in great slow bounds, turning over in the air, and went straight through the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:105:Karis had not flinched, and her eyes had never left the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:125:He had the west end, and he had it faithfully. His eyes were on the last animal of the line, a big one with a broken plate on its near shoulder, which stood a little beyond a rib of the hill, so that from where he sat he could see it and it could not have seen its own fellows at the eastern end. He had been counting the slabs along its spine to keep himself awake, and he had got to seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:129:It did so suddenly, and nothing on that shelf had moved so quickly in two days of his watching. Its off foreleg came up off the shelf and went down on the slope above, and the hind leg followed, and then both again, three steps, with a clatter of horn that reached him a moment later. Beside it the next did the same, and the next. He swept his eyes east along the shelf, and there was not one grey back in the whole line that was where it had been; all thirty had gone up the hill by the length of their own bodies, the calves scrambling inside them, and stood now on the open slope with their heads lifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:157:"There wasn't. I've got good eyes, and I've watched a starting line since I was small. I know what it looks like when one goes and the rest follow, because you can see it run along the row, and this didn't run along." Lira sat up. "It was like one animal thirty carts long."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:193:"Yes." Karis's eyes rested on her, and then on Brom. "I have watched Iron Skin start at your wrists when a rope snatched, and go down again, and you told me in a loft that something leaves you when it comes. A burst went off by itself on a gravel bar, in our first week, because a foot slipped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:197:"I don't say that they have. I say that I know of one thing in this country that reaches every animal of a kind in the same instant, through rock, and it's the thing those hounds turned to. I'm not entitled to invent a second until I've used up the first." She drew a line across the new page. "Whatever a frightened shale-back gives off, the herd has a sense for it, and that's the same sense, or its cousin. Now look what they did with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:201:"Uphill, and all together, with no first animal to wait for. The hounds go to the thing, and these go from it." She wrote four words and underlined them. "*Same sense. Opposite answer.* And there is Pike's word to lay beside it, which is *loud*; and when we asked a ford what a carter meant by that, the answer was a Path."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:205:"*Shale-back. Seventy-fifth day. One: it does not answer fire at three hundred and fifty yards, nor sound, nor a blow on its own shelf. Two: it answers something given off by one of its own in fright, at once, the whole herd together, by moving away. Three: by that, and by the carters' rule, it will answer a declaration in the same way, and a declaration is a great deal larger than a frightened animal.*" She stopped. "*Derived. Not tested.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:229:"The other one I gave you because I couldn't hold a pencil. This I can write myself, and I have. But it's derived, and a derived thing in one hand is an opinion." She was looking at the leaf, and not at Hesk's book, which she had never read. "You had the far end of the line. If you'll put down what you saw there, in your own words, then it's two people's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:245:"Oxhollow's side," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:29:There were three ribs. He knew the number without touching them; he had heard it. They were low on the left side, and they let him breathe in to a certain depth and then they stopped him, quite firmly, as a hand stops a door. The calf had opened along the line of the old bite. Seln had taken off his own neckcloth and bound it, and the cloth was already dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:51:"You don't know that, and I don't know it either. I've never spent it all before; nobody at Ternhall would have let me." Her voice had no fright in it, and that was worse than fright. "Nine points and eight channels, held for eight seconds across sixty yards. I should be able to give you the cost of that to a decimal. I can't find the figure. I can't find where I keep figures."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:59:The herd was still there, though he could not have said how many of it. They stood here and there on the level, among the spilled shale, grey on grey, and through the first hours of the dark he heard them moving, a few slow steps and a clatter of horn and then quiet. By the middle of the night the sounds had spread themselves out along the foot of the face into something very like a line, and toward morning one of them began to scrape.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:91:He read it through, and he was not ashamed of it, for it had been true when the pencil made it; but he could find no sentence to set beneath it that was a thing counted, and he would not put down one that was only a thing felt. He shut the book, and that day got no line either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:107:He went for water first. The seep was at the bay's eastern corner, a hundred and thirty yards from the rock, and every yard of the way lay under the stripped face, where the plates were still coming down singly, with no warning but a small dry tick. Cael watched him cross it without once hurrying. He stopped whenever a slide began, and judged its line, and stood still until it had gone by, sometimes within a few feet of him; and he came back in the rain with the skin full and grey grit to his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:181:"They've kept it. That's all they were asked to do." She glanced up at Lira's face, as one looks at the head of a horse before going round behind it. "Name's Oryn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:185:"Tide. Healer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:217:"There," said Oryn. "That's it over. You'll not get worse than that from me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:221:There was nothing to see. He had half expected a light, or a colour, as there was grey in Iron Skin and yellow in a point; and there was a square brown woman kneeling in the dust with her hands on a girl's leg, breathing slowly. A minute went by, and another, and the only thing that changed was that the sweat came out on her forehead, though the air was cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:229:"What she mends with. Everybody's got it. It goes round you like water round a mill, and when you break a bone it does the knitting, if you leave it be for six weeks." Oryn spoke in pieces, between breaths, with her eyes shut. "At a break it stands, since it can't get by, and it piles up, and that's the heat you felt in this last night. I find where it's standing. Then I open the way with mine, and hers goes through, and I hold it open while it does the six weeks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:237:"That's right," said Oryn. "Lie still and let it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:239:It took the greater part of an hour, and he counted it by the shadow of the wall, which crept the width of two hands across the floor. After the first of it the hollow was silent. Brom held Lira as he would have held a full pail, and Seln stood at the mule's head with the rein in his fingers; and Cael watched a stranger's face, and saw it grow a little older while he looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:245:Oryn did not get up. She turned herself about on the floor, slowly, and let herself down with her back against the rock, a little at a time, as a carter sets down a load whose weight he has known for years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:251:"One mending. A Tide has a reserve, like a purse, and it's spent at the rate of what it mends. Mine holds three in a day, if I've slept under a roof and somebody's fed me, and on a road it holds two." She wiped her face with her sleeve. "After that the reserve's gone, and I'm a woman with a mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:283:"Ember. And nothing. You're empty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:293:Oryn looked at that for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:313:Oryn did not answer at once. She leaned over and set her thumb on the place where the wrong turn had been, and pressed, hard, with her eyes on Lira's face and not on the leg. Lira's face did nothing whatever.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:327:He counted with her. *One. Two.* It was the first time since he crossed the Line that anybody had laid a hand on him to learn what he was. *Three. Four.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:363:"That's not an answer," Oryn said. "That's a *finding*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:369:"I can't mend what's in you, since I can't find where it's standing," she said. "So you get what a carter gets. Cloth, and tape in the morning." She knotted the end. "A man at the foot of this hill told me the mountain had come down two days back, and that a crew off the Lowmarch plank had gone up it with a cart and not come off the other side. He said you were late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:383:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:11:"And outside the line nobody tries anything," said Seln. "It is a road. I should like that kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:15:The sun stood as high as it would go at that time of year, pale and small, and their shadows lay short on the frosted grass. They had gathered on the western side of the grey stone, with the turn of the ruts at their backs and the ring two hundred yards and more in front. Oryn had the mule by the cheek-strap a few yards behind the rest, and her free hand tucked into her armpit for warmth; she had watched the whole of the morning from that stone without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:21:Cael saw her shoulders tighten as her foot came down, and ease again; she had done this nine times in the dark, she had told them at first light, and it plainly did not grow pleasanter. She stood a stride inside the line, with her back to the ring, and her eyes went slowly along the slope in front of her from left to right, as he had seen them go over a district floor before a bout.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:35:"Does it hurt?" said Oryn, from behind the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:49:He wrote it, and looked at it a breath longer than a line needed. He had sat in a whitewashed room for the better part of a year while that small exact person laid points on a wall for him to study, and he carried one of them still, the fifth of his nine; and he had not known until this noon that there was a place anywhere where her hand could make that dip and have nothing come of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:75:He looked about him, and his eye fell on the middle box, which sat on the grass just outside the line where he had put it down. He leaned across the stone and took it by its two beckets, and lifted it over, and stood holding it against his chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:81:"Weight. I wanted to know whether a hundredweight was a hundredweight in here, with nothing behind me." He set it down inside the line, quite gently. "It is. So that's what I've got. Write that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:121:She sat down on the grass outside the line and took off the great boot, and unwound the whole of a spare shirt, and stood up on her own taped foot with the frost under it. She was to run on nothing for a fortnight yet, and nobody reminded her of it; this was one stride, and she had plainly weighed it against a healer's month before she touched the lace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:123:"I want all of it," she said. "Not a lean. I'm going to ask for the whole thing, the way I'd ask on a card with a panel watching, and I'm going to ask along the line, that way, north. So if the old man's wrong I come down in grass and not on top of one of you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:125:"And if he is wrong," said Karis, very quietly, "it will be heard at Thornwater."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:131:He did not watch her go. If a one-armed man had been wrong for thirty years, the answer to it would come out of the grass on the eastern side, a quarter of a mile off, where three runs crossed a line; and somebody had to have his eyes there when the air broke. He fixed them on that place and kept them on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:133:So it was his own feet that told him. Wind stood up in them to go with hers, harder than it had leaned in three months, and he put it down; and in the same instant the grass behind his shoulder hissed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:He turned his head. She was a yard and a half along the line from the place where she had planted herself, with the left foot forward and the right drawn up beside it, looking down at the pair of them on the frost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:153:"I'd always thought of it as mine. They told me at Fenmark that it was a thing I *did*, and I believed them, and I was proud of doing it well. And every piece of the doing's still here. I can feel it. I could do that step again exactly." She set her right foot forward on its whole sole, and drew it back. "There's just nothing out there to take hold of."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:155:"Come out," said Oryn. "You've a bare foot on frost."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:185:"Now me," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:191:He held it out. She took it between both of hers, as she had taken a carter's palm on a plank at Thornwater, with two fingers of her right hand laid along the inside where the pulse was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:201:It stopped. It did not trail away; it stopped as a word stops when a door is shut on the speaker. She stood a stride inside the line with Brom's forearm between her palms, and her mouth was a little open, and there was no count in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:205:"Out," said Oryn hoarsely.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:211:"Is it Brom?" said Karis. "Forgive me; I must ask it. His Arbiter stopped at that stone, and yours did. If the thing that you read is his current, and his current is shaped by his Path, then perhaps there was nothing in him to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:213:"That's the third." Oryn had not let go of the wrist. "You. Stand where you are, this side. Give me the arm across."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:Brom stood on the western side of the stone, where he was an Iron Skin with his Path in the next room. He stretched out his left arm. Oryn kept her two hands on it, and she stepped over the stone by herself, so that she stood inside and he outside, with the mended wrist held between them across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:225:The wind went over the grass. Nobody at the stone had anything to put beside that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:235:"So you'll all hear this, and you'll hear it once," said Oryn. "I'm coming in with you. I said I'd see him whole to the ninety-ninth, and I can't do that from a stone. But understand what you've got. If one of you puts a leg through something in there, I can pull it straight. I can splint it, and I can carry my end of you. I can't mend it. Not a scratch. Not till you're this side of that." She pointed at the stone with her chin. "Whatever happens in there happens to six people with no healer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:245:Cael watched it happen. Five people had each lost a thing, a stride from where they stood, and had it handed back, and every one of them had turned inward to look at it. Oryn was hobbling the mule, with her lips moving in a count that she did not seem to know she was keeping. Brom knelt by the middle box. Karis sat with her eyes shut, and Lira walked up and down on the great boot, a little apart, and Seln stood looking east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:247:There were five rows to be ruled fair before the leaf went into a box. He took it a cord along the line to the north, to Brom's first turf, where there was a flat place to kneel; and nobody marked his going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:263:He was a pace short of the line. He walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:269:The turf under his left boot was outside the line, and the turf under his right was within it, and he had the weight of both as he had the weight of his own hands. Nothing dimmed. He took another step, and a third, and the ground went on telling him what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:277:At the twentieth stride he let Wind have his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:279:It was a long way short of a burst. It was the smallest piece of what Lira's fighting had taught him, the lean that takes a body off a line a hair before the blow arrives; and he asked it for a foot. His weight went over without his deciding it. The boulder, which had been in front of him, was on his right hand, and he was past it, and the grass had hardly stirred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:283:He stood on the far side with the tape drawn tight across his ribs and something going out of him into the cold air, small, as a breath goes out. He listened, and there was the wind, and the hiss of seed-heads, and far off toward the east a bird that got up and settled again. Nothing whatever came through the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:285:He shut them both. He put Wind down, and closed the read as one closes a book, and they went back to where the other seven lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:289:He walked back along the line, and stepped out over the first turf, and came down to the stone with the leaf in his hand.

