/bin/zsh -lc "rg -n '\\b(Hall|Hesk|Withrow|Ephram|Vell|Ostrand|Denvash|Ardenmere|Fenmark|Ternhall|Norhold|Storm|Compression|Anchor|Kindling|sigil|stillhounds?|shale-backs?|void roads?|quiet ground|Crown yard)\\b' editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-{16..30}.md" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:15:Brom leaned on his rope through all of it, and Cael thought that he had never seen him so content. He had stood idle in a yard for five days and in a meadow for eleven. Here he had found a way of putting his whole weight to use that a woman with a needle could not have forbidden, and he had found it by himself. Iron Skin had not once risen to meet a load on that shelf. Cael had his own things to keep down there, for Compression rose whenever the rope snatched, and Wind leaned at every yard that Lira ran, and he paid the rent on both fragments and spent neither.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:41:Something in him had found the lip before his eyes did. Anchor had asked at every sill and coping since a river, and at every one of seven narrowings, and it had never asked as it asked now. *There?* It showed him the place, under the crust, where a fixed point would sit: one point, set against the sound rock and told to stay, and that plank of stone would have carried a loaded wagon. It would have cost him nothing that he could not have paid by supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:177:*The nine: held, the whole length of it. Anchor asked at the seventh as it has never asked. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:63:They gave it half an hour by Hesk's clock, with the fire built up to the size of a hat, and thirty beaks rasped on in the dark overhead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:197:"I don't say that they have. I say that I know of one thing in this country that reaches every animal of a kind in the same instant, through rock, and it's the thing those hounds turned to. I'm not entitled to invent a second until I've used up the first." She drew a line across the new page. "Whatever a frightened shale-back gives off, the herd has a sense for it, and that's the same sense, or its cousin. Now look what they did with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:229:"The other one I gave you because I couldn't hold a pencil. This I can write myself, and I have. But it's derived, and a derived thing in one hand is an opinion." She was looking at the leaf, and not at Hesk's book, which she had never read. "You had the far end of the line. If you'll put down what you saw there, in your own words, then it's two people's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:19:"I asked a question at the plank, two mornings ago, and I have carried the answer for a day and a half, since it concerned nothing that we had yet seen. I asked what a shale-back was, and whether a crew had ever come to harm by one." He inclined his head toward the horn. "He gave me the carters' rule first, and he gave it twice, and I shall give it as he did. 'Never be below them. Never be *loud* below them.'"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:133:He walked. Nine fragments had come up at the snatch, and he went on down the slope with the rope in his hands, putting them down one at a time. Compression had felt the jerk come along the hemp and had offered to take it through his frame into the hill. Anchor had been asking about the island since first light. *There?* A point fixed against that notch, and told to stay, would have been a better thing than any log; it would not have walked, or worn, or needed a man's weight to hold it. He owned the craft of it. He told it that the log was Brom's, and to leave it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:139:It was not only the hold. A reading taken with that thing could be checked by nobody, and Cael had lived since his Kindling under figures that could not be checked by the person they concerned. What Karis did with a knotted cord, any carter could do after her, at the same place, and get the same number or call her a liar. That was the map the man was buying. The other would only have been a boy on a hillside saying that he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:155:"Four, and a piece," said Karis, with Hesk's clock in her hand. "I heard it land, and then I heard it land again." She looked at the far wall with respect. "I shall have to take the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:185:"Seventy lines." Lira began to laugh, low, mindful of the rule. "Seventy marks. He's being paid by the line." She pointed at the book in his hand. "You've written in one of those every night since a lane in Ardenmere, and nobody ever gave you a copper. Now there's a man with a ford who'll give you a mark every time you rule one off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:17:He had sent one letter west to her, on the fourth day, by a courier with a split lip, and it had carried no place to answer to; to the other he had sent none at all. Both of them had written into the air, as an old man in Denvash had, and the air had brought both to the one plank that there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:37:He put them both inside Hesk's book, between the leaves, where a cream sheet had lain the night before. It was a small thing to have decided; but he had gone a long time with nothing in front of him except what he was holding down, and he liked the weight of two unbroken seals a good deal more than he would have liked knowing what was under them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:63:*The Crown yard has a new cohort captain. The intake came up the bluff a fortnight ago, forty-one of them, and they've put a third-year over them who was a second-year when you left and whose name you would not know, so I'll not spend the ink. He is fair. He counts slower than you did. They stand where he puts them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:65:*Withrow's tenure holds. That is the whole of the sentence. I asked whether there was a longer one and was told there was not, in a way that meant there had been, for a week.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:85:"He'll have enjoyed it for a week," said Karis. "It is the only kind of joke that a registrar is allowed." But she was smiling too, in her small tight way; and then she sobered. "Withrow holds. I had wondered. They had a week of something, by that, and it went no further."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:93:Cael had read the last lines but two again, to himself. *I still lose to you every time I run it.* He could see the bout as it had gone, on sixty feet of sprung oak, with a blade's edge declared against him in earnest and nothing held back on either side; and he could see a tall, exact person walking it through afterward in an empty yard, alone, looking for the place where it turned. Something in Cael stirred at the thought, quick and bright, and asked *Edge?* of the nearest arris, which was the lip of the wash. He told it no. It was Ephram's, that question. He carried it for him, unspent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:115:They stood round it, and it was a handsome thing. The registry had printed it from a plate, in black, on a sheet as long as Brom's arm, and the whole of a continent was there: every district with its bound ruled round it, and every chartered road a firm double line, and the towns in three sizes of letter by their standing. Cael found Ardenmere, and a river he had crossed on a bridge, and a bluff above Ostrand with a small square on it. Somebody had written *ridge* beside a pass in the east, in pencil, and a little farther on, *Shelf; a pine; water at 36*, in the same small exact hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:149:"Their map has no roads. His has no quiet ground. Between them you get the country."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:55:He had been written on a good many boards. A woman in Ardenmere had chalked him up with a question mark, and a panel had posted him at a rate that it had scattered on purpose, and for two years a house had put his name on a card every week because a schedule told it to. Nobody had ever before gone to the keeper of a book and asked who was good, and been given him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:133:Cael leaned a little over the oak. The writing ran away from him, toward the man with the book, and he read it upside down, as he had once read a bailiff's list on a counter in Denvash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:151:"That's quiet ground."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:161:"Quiet ground's where the sigil goes to sleep. Yours. Everyone's. You walk a road, and there's a place on it where your Arbiter stops — not slows; stops — and you walk on past and it's back. There's a handful of them. Along the void roads, all of them. Carters walk round them, and have as long as there've been carters. The dogs live in them. Stillhounds. Nobody's asked them why. Why any of it, nobody knows and nobody's needed to, because the rule's *don't go in*, and the rule's never once failed anybody who kept it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:267:"He counts what he hasn't done," she said. "That's all that is. Two hundred times he didn't. You'd have gone round it the first time, and the second, and you'd have written both down. And the third time you'd have stood there with your book shut, working out what it would cost to look." She scuffed the great boot along the shadow's edge. "I've watched you price things since a lane in Ardenmere. You've said *not yet* to me, and *not here*, and *no* a hundred times. You don't say *never*. I don't think you know how."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:17:"Those stay," she said. "The chips come. I have a piece of every sort of dressed stone that I could reach between Denvash and the Line, and a date for each, and if there is so much as a doorstep at this place, I mean to know which of them it matches."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:45:They had halted on a level of short turf where a spring came out. Brom had been helped off with the box and lay on his back beside it with his arms spread. The others had gone to the water. Lira had not; she had sat down next to Cael, and stretched out both legs, and was looking at Hesk's book where it lay on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:57:*Filed with your record. It reads: left in good standing, undefeated on paper. I've kept this ledger forty years and never had a line like it. The session still stands. The road still runs through Ardenmere. — V.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:73:He had thought about that on the forty-ninth night, and it came back to him now on a turf bank two days past a fence. There was a woman in Ardenmere who kept a straight ledger of a fighting yard, and a one-armed man in a meadow who kept a straight book of a plank; and neither had the least idea that the other was alive. He was written in both. He did not think that any registry on the continent could have said as much of one of its own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:91:He looked at the leaf. *The road still runs through Ardenmere.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:93:"So does Ephram."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:185:He counted strides, since somebody would want the number. And he did what a one-armed man had told them to do, though it had not been said to him. He turned his attention inward, to the place behind his breastbone where a small bright thing had gathered on the morning of his Kindling. He had gone looking for it a few times since, in the first months, on bad nights. He looked now. It was as it had always been: a dark place, like a niche in a wall where a lamp once stood lit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:221:The nine fragments lay in him where they had lain since a ford. He was sure of that as well. When Lira's hand went up, Wind had leaned in his own feet to go with hers, as it had leaned every day for two years, and he had put it down without thinking; and that had been on the far side of where the stone now stood. Anchor was asking about the stone already. *There?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:37:Pike had sat down at last, on the hearth bench, under his own handwriting, with the ledger open beside him and a cup that somebody had put into his hand. Cael took his own cup and went over, as he had gone over once before to ask about a ledger-keeper in Ardenmere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:211:There were five. He drew the cream sheet toward him, and folded it once along its old fold, and put it inside Hesk's book for the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:117:A breath went in to a count of three before the tape spoke. On a level road he could take that breath at every fourth stride and never touch the edge. On a rise it wanted every third, and on the steepest of the valley's little hills every second, and there the breaths came so shallow and so quick that his head swam. He had always walked in fits. He had gone quick and then slow since he was a child on the lanes of Denvash, running to look at a thing and standing still to count it. He had watched a healer come down a mountain for a day and a half at one unbroken beat, and he had not been able to copy her for a hundred yards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:153:"I'd always thought of it as mine. They told me at Fenmark that it was a thing I *did*, and I believed them, and I was proud of doing it well. And every piece of the doing's still here. I can feel it. I could do that step again exactly." She set her right foot forward on its whole sole, and drew it back. "There's just nothing out there to take hold of."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:He had his nine fragments as he always had them, and he held them. Wind leaned, since Lira was in front of him. Anchor had fallen quiet at the stake and had not asked since. He had opened nothing after a boulder, and he did not open anything now; but he walked those two hundred yards as the only one of the six who knew that he could have, and it was a strange thing to carry at the back of a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:81:She tipped what was left of the chips into her lap, thirty of them or more, and sorted them by eye into three heaps on the floor. There were the honey-coloured stones, such as the walls of Ostrand were built of. There were the dark close ones, like a district hall's, and a pale kind with a sparkle in the break. She chose the freshest-broken from each heap and held the three against the grey together, in the slanting light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:83:"No." She let them fall. "Not the colour, and not the grain; it has no grain at all. I have a piece of every sort of dressed stone between Denvash and the Line in this box, with a date to each, and not one family of them comes near it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:161:"I do, though. That's what it's like. The year before my Kindling I used to run the lanes at Fenmark against the big girls, with nothing in me, just legs, and I'd forgotten there was ever such a person." She shifted her palm for the pencil. "I didn't think that I'd meet her again. I'm not sure I mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:211:A stillhound hunted what went out of a person. Every hold in that country kept its Paths in its pockets on a road because of it; and here was a place, a quarter of a mile across, where a Path could not be taken out of a pocket by anybody. A deer that wandered over that line was a deer. A carter who did was a man with a stick. And five people who had cleared a ford with a declaration and a knife were standing at the door of the den with a hedging bill and two knives, and a healer whose hands had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:239:*The circle is perfect. The stakes are the registry's. The dogs are inside. Everyone's Path dies at the line and mine didn't, and I've told nobody, and I'm going to need to think about why I haven't. Not tonight. Tonight we're inside the one place on the continent where a stillhound can't lose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:245:He did not write it. It would not have been true, and he did not yet know what would be. He shut the book on an entry that stopped short, for the first time since he had crossed the Line, and put it away in Hesk's satchel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:43:It was Wind, and it was hers, and it was the whole of it. He had not heard that sound from her since a painted floor at Norhold, and she had never, there or anywhere, let it go so hard. She was not on the falling stone. She was twenty feet east of it, in the air, and then she was standing on sound ledge with both arms flung wide. Behind her a hundred feet of shelf was going down into the blue with a roar that grew as it fell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:97:She had no breath to spare for it. She had both hands lifted a little in front of her, the palms with their pink lines turned toward the hill, and she had begun to do the thing that Ternhall had taught her before she was fifteen. *Points first, always. Joins last.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:123:All nine of them were up. He had never felt them so, on the stretch or at the ford; there the offers had come one behind another, and here they came in a single shout. Wind wanted his feet and had the whole distance measured. Storm showed him the air over the bay, torn already, as a lane. He put them down as he ran, without words, since there was no time for words, and they got up again at every stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:143:Compression had the thing measured already. It had waited since a river for a blow worth taking, and it showed him how: a plated shoulder at a run, met square, and sent down through his frame into the slab beneath the shale. He would have stood. Anchor offered him the other way. *There.* One fixed point against the rock under his left boot, and told to stay, and a boy with a box would have been a post that a river goes round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:259:She had been told at Fenmark that a burst was a choice, and at Norhold she had made a rank of choosing well. For sixty days on gravel bars and roads she had refused it a stride at a time, on purpose, and she had been proud of how good she had got. Not one of those refusals was anywhere near her now. The ground went, and her body did what a body with Wind in it does when the ground goes, and it did not send up to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:91:Anchor asked, as he had expected. Here was a rim thirty yards long with a broken boy on it. *There? There?* Each stone under his boot was a place where a point might be fixed and told to stay, and at each one he said no, and moved his foot to the next mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:109:He turned round, since the others had. From the western ledge the whole bay lay open behind them. He could see the pale rock standing across its floor, and the two black lines above it that came to a point, and the wide scoured strip of the hill; and along the foot of the face, in a row, with a length and a half between each, the shale-backs were mowing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:335:"It isn't there any longer," said Cael. "It went on the morning we came to it. There's a lip of rock thirty-one paces long that a mule can be led over, by somebody who knows how, and there's no road." He put his own finger on the margin, where Karis had written small. "And that's the herd. They're shale-backs, on the face over the bay. If anybody uses a Path beneath them, they run, and the hill comes with them, and that's how your cart went into the gorge. She's written that it's derived."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:7:There could be no lamp. She had lit one in a yard two evenings ago and been absurdly glad of it, and she would not have set a point here for all the paper in Ternhall. She knelt down in the frozen grass on the western side of the stone and laid the notebook open on her knee. The stars were out. She could see the white of the page and not much else, and she resolved to write large, one line to a leaf, and sort it by daylight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:9:First she called her sigil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:29:She had been empty for nine days. She had spent every point she owned on a mountain in eight seconds, and afterward she had not been able to strike a flint. But in all those nine days the sigil had been there. She had called it on the first night under the pale rock, and it had come, faithful as ever, and when she asked it for a point it had simply had nothing to give her. That had been a counter with a clerk behind it and an empty till.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:33:*Empty: sigil present, reserve gone.* She wrote it large, by feel. *Here: reserve present, sigil gone.* She turned the leaf. *These are not the same condition and must not be entered under one word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:41:A hand held out across the stone did nothing at all; the sigil hung where it was. She had watched the healer stand so at dusk, for a long while, and had not asked her what she felt. An arm did nothing. But when she stood astride the stone itself, with one boot on either side, she had the sigil or had it not according to how she leaned, and Karis would not write down a lean. She put: *Narrower than one stride of mine. No finer figure tonight. Wants daylight, and a second person to watch my feet.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:131:Something in Cael had turned toward the thing as well. Anchor asked now, more quietly than it had ever asked, as one craftsman takes note of another's work. *There.* It was not a question. Here was a point that somebody else had fixed, and told to stay, and it had stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:51:"You don't know that, and I don't know it either. I've never spent it all before; nobody at Ternhall would have let me." Her voice had no fright in it, and that was worse than fright. "Nine points and eight channels, held for eight seconds across sixty yards. I should be able to give you the cost of that to a decimal. I can't find the figure. I can't find where I keep figures."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:89:He had the book, for Hesk's satchel had been on his shoulder when the mountain came, as it was always on his shoulder, and it had gone under him when he fell and taken no harm. He opened it once, in the grey of the morning, and found the page with the last entry. It ended on the word *Sleep*, and above that he had written that the job had no fight at the end of it.

